ription_html":"<p>Connect to Employment Hero API for integration with themes.<\/p>","description_plain":"Connect to Employment Hero API for integration with themes.","icon":"plug","author":{"name":"Benjamin Currey","email":"benjamin@currey.com.au"},"dependencies":[{"name":"grav","version":">=1.6.0"}],"homepage":"https:\/\/github.com\/Currey\/grav-plugin-employment-hero","docs":"https:\/\/github.com\/Currey\/grav-plugin-employment-hero\/blob\/develop\/README.md","demo":"http:\/\/demo.yoursite.com","keywords":"grav, plugin, api, employment, careers","bugs":"https:\/\/github.com\/Currey\/grav-plugin-employment-hero\/issues","license":"MIT","tag_name":"1.0.0","date":"2026-07-21T16:43:38+10:00","release_type":"stable","zipball_url":"https:\/\/getgrav.org\/download\/plugins\/employment-hero\/1.0.0","repository":"https:\/\/github.com\/Currey\/grav-plugin-employment-hero","slug":"employment-hero","install_path":"user\/plugins\/employment-hero","content_sha256":"7c70fc6dc0ec1e79a0a0bfa84ab33ad5aa27f8dbb1ca9eb0bf30eb0d158dd107","changelog":{"1.0.0":{"date":"2026-07-21","content":"1. [](#launch)\n    * Plugin launched.\n    * Basic functionality\n    * Base template returns simple results: `title`, `city`, `application_url`.\n"},"0.1.0":{"date":" 2026-07-07","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * ChangeLog started..."}}},"kahunacart":{"name":"KahunaCart","version":"1.6.5","description":"Native ecommerce for Grav 2.0 \u2014 products, cart, checkout, orders, and a provider ecosystem","description_html":"<p>Native ecommerce for Grav 2.0 \u2014 products, cart, checkout, orders, and a provider ecosystem<\/p>","description_plain":"Native ecommerce for Grav 2.0 \u2014 products, cart, checkout, orders, and a provider ecosystem","icon":"shopping-cart","author":{"name":"Trilby Media, LLC","email":"hello@trilby.media"},"dependencies":[{"name":"grav","version":">=2.1.0"},{"name":"api","version":">=1.0.31"}],"compatibility":{"grav":["2.0"]},"homepage":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com","docs":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/docs","keywords":"grav, premium, commerce, ecommerce, shop, cart, checkout, orders, payments","bugs":"https:\/\/github.com\/getgrav\/grav-premium-issues\/labels\/kahunacart","license":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/license","tag_name":"1.6.5","date":"2026-10-26T03:50:03Z","release_type":"stable","zipball_url":"https:\/\/getgrav.org\/download\/plugins\/kahunacart\/1.6.5","repository":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com","premium":{"vendor":"KahunaCart","validator":"kahunacart","license_product":"kahunacart","checkout_url":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/shop\/product\/kahunacart","button":"Buy Now","filename":"grav-plugin-kahunacart"},"slug":"kahunacart","install_path":"user\/plugins\/kahunacart","content_sha256":"6ab4de77c0af448efee5f33c2ab3c4addd488070bf7b62099a88b32b14cc598c","changelog":{"1.6.5":{"date":"10\/25\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **An add-on choice can sell another product.** A lamp's \"Add a warm white LED tealight\" tick box now puts the real LED in the cart as a line of its own, beside the lamp, so its stock is counted, its sale and quantity tiers apply, and a \"buy 10\" deal sees it, exactly as when it's bought from its own page. Pick the product under **Sells** on any add-on choice (a tick box, a dropdown or pills), and say how many go with one. The product page shows the LED's price for this shopper and greys it out with the reason when it can't be had. When the LED can't be added the lamp is added anyway and the cart says the LED was left out; a required add-on is refused instead. Two choices that sell the same LED are counted together against its stock, and the lamp and its LEDs go in together or not at all. Removing the lamp leaves the LED. The LED's editor lists the products that sell it, and deleting it, or restoring a revision of it without that variant, leaves the choice off the page with a dashboard alert; a page drawn before the delete still adds the lamp. An add-on whose choices sell a product can't be switched to a text box, or to a tick box while it has more than one choice. The storefront API, the counter cart and orders typed into the admin add it too, and their answers carry `left_out` when something was left out. Restored carts, cart links and renewals put lines back as they were and add nothing twice. See docs\/products-and-catalog.md (helpdesk #17)\n    * **`$kahunacart->addonOffers()->forProduct($productId, $currency)`** answers a product's add-on options the way `kahunacart_addons()` does, in the currency you name, with each choice that sells a product priced and checked. `kahunacart_addons()` is now built on it. `forCart($productId, $orderId)` answers the same for an open cart, pricing each choice that sells a product the way that cart will charge for it (its customer's groups and price lists, quantity tiers, sales and coupons), and the Point of Sale till's add-on picker reads it for a counter cart. See docs\/storefront.md\n    * **A tax rate can depend on what each item costs.** New York takes no state tax on clothing under $110 an item and taxes an item at $110 or more on its whole price; Massachusetts and Rhode Island exempt clothing up to $175 or $250 an item and tax only the part above. Give a rate \"Only on items from\" with a price, and choose \"Tax the whole price\" or \"Tax only the part above\". The price compared is what each item really costs after its discounts, so a coupon can take an item under the line, and the delivery on exempt items is exempt too. The usual rate keeps its one row on the order, the per-line rates skip or cut each line, and the rates on the order's shipping or taxable total ignore it. In another currency the rate uses the amount set for that currency, and with none it taxes every item. The CSV export and import carry two new columns, `unit_threshold` and `threshold_mode`, and an older file without them still imports and leaves thresholds alone. A rate with no threshold charges exactly what it did. See docs\/tax.md (helpdesk #21)\n    * **Pick the background, header and text colors of every email.** Customer Emails in the settings now has Email Background, Email Header Color and Email Text Color beside the link and button colors, all five with a color picker. The panels, the thin lines, the softer text and the header's lettering are worked out from them, so a store on a blue or dark theme no longer sends sand and brown emails, and nobody has to copy the whole email layout into a theme to repaint it. Leave a color blank and that part keeps KahunaCart's own, so nothing changes for a store that sets none. The writing on the buttons turns dark when the button color is light, so a yellow button no longer carries white text. Templates can read every shade through `kahunacart_email_palette()`, `action_ink` included. See docs\/emails.md\n    * **A customer group's discount can come off on top of a sale.** Give the group **With sales: On top of the sale** and its percentage comes off each item's price after its sale, before any code: a $50 shirt on a 20% sale is $40, the group's 10% takes it to $36, and a $10-off code to $26. **Better of the two**, where an item on sale gets whichever takes more off, stays the default, and every existing group keeps it. The group's part is its own discount row on the item, so receipts, reports, refunds and tax see it like the group's other rows. If a code beats a sale set to best of, the sale comes off and the group's percentage is worked out on the full price. The API, the MCP tools, store exports and seed files carry it as `discount_mode`, `best_of` or `on_top`. See docs\/customers.md (helpdesk #20)\n2. [](#improved)\n    * **Prices print properly on servers without PHP's intl extension.** Without intl every price came out as \"USD 28.00\", in every language. KahunaCart now writes prices from a table of ICU's own answers for every locale and every currency in use, so a store without intl prints \"$28.00\", \"1.234,50 \u20ac\" or \"\u20b912,34,567.50\", exactly as intl would. Checked against ICU for all 906 of its locales and every current currency. Dates and currency names still need intl. See docs\/multilanguage.md\n3. [](#bugfix)\n    * **A sale that only has quantity tiers gets a sale badge on the product's picture.** \"$10 each, $5 each for 3 or more\" takes nothing off one of the thing, so the badge needed money off at a quantity of one and never showed. It now gives the sale's name, or \"Buy 3+ and save\" when the sale has none, with no price struck through, in every theme that draws badges through KahunaCart (helpdesk #19)\n    * **A quantity price tier counts the variant across the whole cart.** Two lines of one variant, such as a set's part and a loose one, or two kept apart by different add-on answers, each counted only themselves, so six and four never reached \"2.00 each from 10\". Both are priced at the rung for ten now, the way a sale's quantity ladder already counted. A line an add-on priced still isn't counted. See docs\/pricing.md\n    * **Renaming or removing a choice in the product editor's Add-ons panel works.** The panel called an address with no add-on in it, so both failed with \"not found\". Adding an add-on no longer marks the product as having unsaved changes either\n    * **Store Currency is a list of real currencies**, shown as \"USD \u00b7 US Dollar\". It was a text box, so a symbol like \"$\" could be entered, and then prices didn't show (helpdesk #18)\n    * **Extra Currencies and How a Shopper's Currency Is Chosen only take what they list.** Extra Currencies suggests currencies as you type (\"eu\" offers EUR \u00b7 Euro) and refuses anything that isn't one, and the three detection steps are named in plain words instead of `choice`, `address` and `header`. Needs Admin2 2.2.1; an older admin still takes typed text, and a code that isn't a currency is still left out when the store reads it\n"},"1.6.4":{"date":"10\/24\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **`kahunacart_price_state(product, variant)`** answers whether a price is reduced and by how much: `on_sale`, `kind` (`sale` or `compare`), `now_minor`, `was_minor`, `percent`, the sale's `label`, the sale badge's words in `badge`, and the `offer` itself. A card's struck-through price and its sale badge now come from one answer, the same in every theme. See docs\/storefront.md\n2. [](#improved)\n    * **The sale badge is worked out by KahunaCart, the same in every theme**: `kahunacart_badges()` and `partials\/kahunacart-badges.html.twig` badge a product when a sale with money off covers it or when its compare-at price is above its price, in the same words everywhere (\"Summer Sale \u00b7 30% off\", or \"25% off\" for a compare-at price). Until now each theme decided for itself, so a compare-at price got a badge in Kahuna's product grid and nowhere else. A theme can still pass `sale` to word it its own way, or `sale: ''` for no badge. Themes that pass nothing get the badge with no change (helpdesk #16)\n    * **The shop's own product card and hero section strike through a compare-at price and say how much is off**, the same as a sale, and a sale's line under the price now gives the reduction after the sale's name. The product page draws the sale badge above the gallery with the merchant's badge\n"},"1.6.3":{"date":"10\/23\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Star screens into the admin sidebar**: a star beside each row of KahunaCart's nav puts that screen under KahunaCart in the Admin2 sidebar, one click away from anywhere in the admin. Every store screen and every add-on screen can be starred, and Orders, Products and Customers show until you star your own. The pencil at the foot of the Admin2 sidebar offers the same stars. Needs Admin2 2.2.0 and API 1.1.0 for the stars; older admins just don't show them\n"},"1.6.2":{"date":"10\/23\/2026","content":"1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **Downloads whose file is gone aren't listed.** Deleting a release, or a file, keeps the grants that pointed at it as a record of what was sold. Those grants still showed up on the account's Downloads page, the order page and the order emails as rows with the product's name and no file, one for every retired build, so a customer who had been sent twenty builds saw twenty empty rows above the one they could download. Only grants with a file to download are listed now\n"},"1.6.1":{"date":"10\/22\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Stars on a product card.** `kahunacart_rating(product)` answers a product's average, review count, the maximum and the lit share of the row (`{average: \"4.3\", count: 12, max: 5, percent: 86.0}`), or null when reviews are off or nothing is approved yet, so a card prints nothing rather than \"0 out of 5\". It reads the cached aggregate the product page uses. `partials\/kahunacart-rating.html.twig` draws it: five stars lit to the average over five faded ones, in the reviews' star color, with \"12 reviews\" beside them. The shipped card is unchanged; a theme includes the partial where its card wants stars. See docs\/storefront.md\n"},"1.6.0":{"date":"10\/22\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **A theme can set its card picture's shape.** `kahunacart.card_size: [width, height]` in the active theme's config crops product card pictures (and the wishlist's) to that box instead of 600 \u00d7 450, so a theme with square cards no longer has every picture cropped twice and loses its sides. See `docs\/theming.md`\n    * **Themes extend the storefront instead of copying it.** The plugin's templates are now also registered under the `@kahunacart` Twig namespace, so a theme's `kahunacart-checkout.html.twig` can be `{% extends '@kahunacart\/kahunacart-checkout.html.twig' %}` plus the blocks it changes. Everything it doesn't override stays the plugin's, so later releases reach the theme without it being touched. A copied template still works exactly as before\n    * **Every storefront page is split into named blocks.** Checkout, cart, product, order, listing, category and search have a block per region, a `_layout` block for laying the regions out in columns with `{{ block('\u2026') }}`, and `_class` blocks that add classes to the wrappers the scripts read without redrawing them. The processing page has a block per state, so a theme that wraps it keeps the declined, held and timed-out pages. See docs\/theme-blocks.md\n    * **One card, one cart line, one receipt line, one delivery method are partials.** `partials\/kahunacart-product-card.html.twig`, `kahunacart-cart-line.html.twig`, `kahunacart-cart-set-row.html.twig`, `kahunacart-order-line.html.twig` and `kahunacart-shipping-method.html.twig`: override one small file to restyle every instance, including the filter swap\n    * **Attribute blocks on the forms themes wire scripts to.** The search box, the estimate form and the bundle suggestion's two forms have an empty block inside the opening tag for htmx and the like\n    * **The country select takes an `id` and a `class`**, so a theme's `<label for>` can point at it\n    * **More class and region blocks where Kahuna needed them**: the currency question on the currency page, and the product grid's list class\n    * **Blocks on the account overview**: its heading, its section's classes, and an empty block above the panels for a row of figures, with the store credit balance (`credit_balance_minor`) handed to the page beside the order and download counts\n    * **`kahunacart_quick_add(product)` tells a product card whether it can add straight to the cart.** It answers the variant to post for a product with nothing to choose, and null for one with options, an amount to name, a date or a plan, which a card links to instead. The same rule the cart's cross-sells already used\n    * **`kahunacart_category_image(category, width, height)`** draws a category's picture at any size, from a `kahunacart_categories()` row or a node of the listing's tree, for category tiles over the shop\n    * **Five more theme tokens**: `--kahunacart-font`, `--kahunacart-font-display`, `--kahunacart-shadow`, `--kahunacart-gap` and `--kahunacart-focus` set the storefront's type, the shadow under its cards, the gap between them and the focus ring. Left unset, everything looks as it did\n    * **Themes can list a product's releases.** `kahunacart_releases(product, limit)` answers a product's published releases newest first, or with `null` the latest across every product a shopper can see: the version, the date written in the store's language, the notes as safe HTML, whether it is the product's newest, and each file's label, name and size (never a download link). It lets a theme draw a release ticker, a changelog or a \"what's new\" strip. See `docs\/storefront.md`\n    * **Gotify notifications.** A new channel kind for store-owner messages, for a [Gotify](https:\/\/gotify.net) server you run yourself: the server's address and an app token, then **Send test**. Messages carry their priority on Gotify's scale (quiet 2, normal 5, urgent 8, emergency 10), and tapping one in the Android app opens the admin link. The token goes in the `X-Gotify-Key` header, never in the URL. See [Gotify](docs\/notifications.md#gotify)\n    * **Whole amounts.** A new **Whole Amounts** switch on the General tab (`storefront.whole_amounts`, off as shipped) prints `$55` instead of `$55.00` on the storefront. A price with cents keeps them and is never rounded, and prices shown together (a sale's old and new price, a compare-at price, a \"From\" price and its top, a tier ladder) are printed alike, so `$55.00 $49.50` never turns into `$55 $49.50`. Currencies with no minor unit are unchanged. Emails, invoices, credit notes, receipts, reports, feeds and the API keep full amounts. `kahunacart_price()` takes a `with` argument naming the prices beside it, and `kahunacart_whole_amounts()` answers the setting\n    * **A cart or order summary is printed as one set.** With Whole Amounts on, every amount in the cart (the lines' prices and totals, the subtotal, the discounts and coupon rows, shipping, tax, fees, store credit, a deposit and its balance, and the total) keeps its cents if any one of them has cents, and drops them only when every one is whole, so a cart no longer reads `$48 \u00b7 -$5.76 \u00b7 $6 \u00b7 $48.24`. The same goes for the checkout summary with its delivery methods and payment fees, the pay page, and the storefront receipt (the invoice still prints full amounts). The pages carry the list as `price_set`, each price in the plugin's partials passes it as `with`, and a theme that overrides a partial does the same with `price_set|default([])`. `kahunacart_price_set(vars)` builds the list for a summary a theme draws itself, such as a mini cart. The shipping and tax estimate is a table of its own, and each order in the account's order list is its own set. Messages the storefront words in PHP follow the setting too, each amount on its own: the order minimum and maximum, a pay-what-you-want refusal, the restore page's prices then and now, and the bank transfer instructions on the receipt. Emails keep full amounts\n    * **Cart, bag or basket.** A new **Name for the Cart** select on the General tab (`storefront.cart_word`) makes every shopper-facing string that names the cart say bag or basket instead: the buy button, the cart page's title, the empty cart, the currency, coupon and wishlist messages, the restore page and the back links from payment. The language file carries one set of strings per word under `CART_WORDS`, a translation without them keeps its own cart wording, and add-ons and themes can ship sets of their own. The admin and emails keep saying cart. New strings for a theme's header and mini cart follow it too (`CART_LINK`, `VIEW_CART`, `BACK_TO_CART`, `CART_COUNT_ONE`, `CART_COUNT`, `ADD_NAMED_TO_CART`), and `kahunacart_cart_word()` answers the word\n    * **Pre-orders.** Give a product an **Out on** date in the product editor (`release_at`, in the store's timezone) and it sells as a pre-order until then, then becomes an ordinary product on its own with nothing to switch. Checkout takes payment in full as usual and stock works as it always does, so a limited pressing still sells out. The shopper sees **Pre-order** on the buy button (a set's or kit's too) and the cart's cross-sells, \"Out Friday, November 13\" beside the button, a Pre-order badge on cards and the product page, and \"Pre-order \u00b7 out Nov 13\" on cart and checkout lines. Each pre-order line keeps the date it was sold with, so the receipt, the confirmation email and the admin say the same thing if you move the date later, and the order list and order page show a Pre-order pill until it is out. A pre-order's download links are handed out at purchase but open on the release day (the receipt, the account's Downloads and the emails say \"Soon \u00b7 Nov 13\" until then), and when the date passes the worker sends each order that waited one \"It's out: your download is ready\" email with its links, never two. The date goes through the API and MCP, the catalog file, the CSV import and export, store export and restore, Duplicate and revisions, and the feeds and structured data say `preorder` with the release date. See docs\/products-and-catalog.md\n    * **`kahunacart_preorder(product)` for themes.** It answers `active`, `release_at`, `date_label` (\"Out Friday, November 13\"), `short_label` (\"Pre-order \u00b7 out Nov 13\"), `date` and `short_date` while a product is a pre-order, in the store's timezone and the shopper's language, and an empty array for anything else. The shipped buy button already says Pre-order and prints the date, so a theme that includes it gets both for free. See docs\/theming.md\n    * **Digital variants and files on any product.** A variant of a physical product can be digital: tick **Digital (no shipping)** on it in the variant editor (`is_digital`, migration 0130). A record sold as an amber LP, a black LP, a CD, a cassette and a download is now one product, and a line of its Download format is treated in every way as a digital product's line: no shipping and no weight, no address when everything in the bag is digital, digital tax and payment treatment, no fulfilling, the returns policy's download rules, and no shipping stated in the feeds and structured data for that variant. A mixed bag ships only the physical lines. Product files now work on physical products too: a file with no variant goes to every buyer of the product (each pressing comes with the download), and a file pinned to a variant with the new **For** select goes to that variant's buyers. Grants follow `downloads.grant_on` as before, **Give to past buyers** works the same, pre-orders hold them until their date, and a line refunded in full loses its downloads. The switch goes through the API, MCP, the catalog file, the CSV import and export, store export and restore, Duplicate and revisions; `update_product_file` takes `variant_id`. A product changed from digital to physical keeps granting its files. Code that completes orders outside checkout can freeze their pre-order lines with `CheckoutService::freezePreOrderLines($orderId)`. See docs\/digital-products.md\n    * **`kahunacart_downloads(limit)` for themes.** The signed-in customer's downloads, newest first, for a theme's own library on a home page or account: each with `title`, `product_title`, `product_slug`, `variant_title` (the format it was bought in), `image` (the product's first picture), `url`, `held`, `soon_label` and `order_number`. A file a pre-order still holds has `url` null, `held` true and `soon_label` \"Soon \u00b7 Nov 13\". It answers an empty list when nobody is signed in or anything goes wrong, and never throws. See docs\/theming.md\n    * **A coupon can apply itself to visitors from an ad.** Type the ad's `utm_campaign` into **Ad campaign tag** on the coupon (`utm_campaign`, migration 0129), and every visitor landing on any page with that tag holds the code just as a `?coupon=` link gives it: same checks, cookie, banner and days. An ad that is already running gets a discount without editing its URL and going back through review, and the code on the order shows the sale came from the ad even when the buyer returns days later. The URL keeps its tracking, a code the visitor already has stays, and nothing is said when no code is live. In the coupon API and the MCP coupon tools. See [Codes that apply from an ad campaign](docs\/coupons.md#codes-that-apply-from-an-ad-campaign)\n2. [](#improved)\n    * **The \"Buy more, save more\" ladder says what counts in a sentence.** Under the rungs it used to print the admin's one-word option label, so most ladders ended in a lone \"items\". It now reads \"Mix and match: any items in the sale count.\" or \"Each one must be a different product in the sale.\" (`SALE_TIER_COUNTS_UNITS` \/ `SALE_TIER_COUNTS_PRODUCTS`); the admin's option labels are unchanged\n    * **Choosing a collection point puts the delivery methods away.** While a counter is the chosen option the delivery list is hidden and disabled and an empty method is posted in its place, so a shopper collecting in store isn't shown, or charged for, a courier. Choosing delivery again brings the list back. Kahuna has done this for a while; now every theme gets it\n    * **Collecting in store ticks \"ship to the billing address\"**, as there is no delivery address to ask for\n    * **The delivery step's \"still needed\" line is left off printouts**, like the same line under the other two steps\n    * **The cart's remove forms are outside every block**, drawn before the cart table instead of after it, so no layout of the page can leave a remove button that does nothing\n    * **A test holds the block names to the docs.** Every `kahunacart_*` block in the templates must be listed in docs\/theme-blocks.md and every listed one must exist, because a block name is something themes depend on\n    * **The storefront holds its own under themes built on Pico, such as Quark2.** Lists the plugin draws (address cards, gallery thumbnails, tags, reviews and the rest) no longer pick up the theme's square bullets, the star-rating labels stay on one line with every bar starting at the same place, the account tabs sit on their underline, and the add-address card loses a stray chevron. The search box keeps its button beside the field: its landmark is now a `<search>` element around the form instead of `role=\"search\"` on it, which Pico draws as one pill-shaped group with the button stretched to half the row\n    * **Payment methods and collection points are drawn as cards at checkout**, like providers and delivery methods: the radio beside its name, the price at the far edge, the description under it. The counter's address reads a line at a time and its hours as a two-column week, on the receipt as well. A theme that draws its own cards keeps them\n    * **Store-owner rows on the Notifications screen only show the channels you have.** The greyed \"Telegram \u2014 set up\" chips for every kind not yet set up are gone, so a row lists only the places a message can actually go\n    * **Add a channel is a set of cards.** On the Channels page each kind is a card with a line about it and **Set up \u2192**, grouped into email and chat, push to your phone, text message and other, and a kind you already have says how many. Picking one shows its form with **Choose another kind** to go back. `#\/notifications\/channels?add=<kind>` still opens straight on a kind's form\n    * **The few storefront strings that said basket now say cart**, like the rest, so a store set to cart never mixes the two: a swapped bundle, a free gift that ran out, a price that changed, the restore page and two delivery reasons\n    * **The demo sign-in keeps its Account tab.** A protected demo login (`demo.protected_customers`) used to lose the Account tab and a theme's \"Password and security\" menu item, so a visitor trying the demo couldn't see that a customer can edit their profile. The tab and the item are back, and the page draws the profile form read-only (disabled, no buttons) under the \"its details are fixed\" notice. Changing anything is still refused on the server, as before\n    * **The account's front page is headed \"Account overview\".** It matches its Overview tab, so it no longer reads like the Account tab beside it (the profile). The heading and the browser tab use the new `ACCOUNT_OVERVIEW_TITLE` string; links that say \"Your account\" are unchanged\n    * **Rating a product is a row of stars.** The review form's five \"N out of 5 stars\" radio buttons are now five stars that light up to the one under the pointer and stay lit to the one picked, in the theme's star color for reviews. It is still a radio group underneath, so the arrow keys, the required check and a page with no script work as before, and each star still says \"4 out of 5 stars\" to a screen reader. New classes `kahunacart-review-rating-stars`, `kahunacart-review-rating-input`\n    * **A deposit is plain from the bag to the invoice.** On a stay or booking paid in parts, the bag now shows \"Due today\" and \"To pay by \u2026\" under the total (it used to start at checkout), and the screen and PDF invoices show \"Paid so far\" and what is left, by when. The amount paid today (\"Due today\" before paying, \"Paid so far\" after) sits in a tinted band under the total rather than reading like one more line. The invoice's date is in the store's date format\n    * **Account tabs a customer has nothing in are left out.** A software store's customer no longer sees empty Bookings and Stock alerts tabs: with the new **Hide Empty Tabs** setting on (Accounts tab, on by default), Downloads, Stock alerts and any add-on tab that says how to tell (`$registry->showsWhen($id, $check)`, see docs\/customer-accounts.md) show only for a customer with something in them. Bookings, Licenses and Subscriptions do this from their next releases. Each page still opens at its address, and the tab shows while you are on it. Turn the setting off to show every tab to everyone\n    * **Ships its license.** A `LICENSE` file now comes with the plugin, and the blueprint links to the KahunaCart License at kahunacart.com\/license instead of the Grav Premium license\n3. [](#bugfix)\n    * **The checkout's collection point no longer prints the counter's name twice.** The partial's `heading: false` was read through `default(true)`, which turns false into true\n    * **Structured custom fields no longer print raw under \"More details\".** A public field holding a list or a map, such as a record's track list in a json field, printed as a line of JSON on the product page. The shipped partial leaves those out (`row.structured`), since a theme draws them itself\n    * **The quantity box no longer says \"Limit 240 per order\" when no limit is set.** The hint beside it was worded after the box's maximum had been capped at the variant's stock, so a pressing with 240 on the shelf read as a per-order limit of 240. It now says only the merchant's own rule; the box still stops at what's in stock\n    * **Return and refund dates follow the store's language.** The return deadline (\"You can ask until \u2026\") and the request dates printed day first (\"19 November 2026\") on every store. They're written with the new `kahunacart_date(at, form)` helper now, which uses the store's locale and timezone: \"November 19, 2026\" in US English, \"19 November 2026\" in British English\n    * **A pre-order is not \"In stock\".** `kahunacart_stock()` now answers `preorder` and `in_stock`: `in_stock` is true only for a tracked variant with some left that is not low, not on backorder and not a pre-order, so a stock line that prints \"In stock\" when `in_stock` is true says nothing under a pre-order's price. \"Only 9 left\" and sold out print as before. A theme that printed \"In stock\" for every tracked variant should test `stock.in_stock` instead\n    * **Dates a shopper reads follow the store's language everywhere.** Review dates (\"18 October 2026\" on a US English store), the account's order dates (\"2026-10-21\"), a shipment's \"Sent on\", a download's expiry, a release's date, the credit balance's rows, the quote, pay and payment-failed pages and emails, a deposit's balance due date and the gift-card, set-password and balance-request emails now go through `kahunacart_date()`: \"October 21, 2026\" in US English, \"21 October 2026\" in British English. Invoices, credit notes, packing slips, the till receipt and reports keep the year-month-day form. Email templates now call `kahunacart_date()`, so a theme that overrides one of them needs the function too (it is registered with the rest)\n    * **\"1 review\", \"3 reviews\".** The count under a product's stars was \"3 review(s)\". It is now `REVIEWS_COUNT_ONE` or `REVIEWS_COUNT`, the way the other counts are\n    * **The cart page's title uses the cart word in sentence case.** \"Your Bag\" is \"Your bag\", \"Your Basket\" is \"Your basket\" and \"Your Cart\" is \"Your cart\", matching \"Your bag is empty.\"\n    * **More shopper-facing strings follow the cart word.** \"Added to cart.\" after an add is now \"Added to your bag.\" (`ADDED_TO_CART`, with a set for bag and basket), and so are the coupon refusal \"This coupon does not apply to anything in your cart\" (`COUPON_ERROR_SCOPE`, which was a fixed English sentence), \"Your cart is empty\" when checkout is pressed on an empty cart, and \"This cart can no longer be copied\" on the currency page. Emails and the JSON API still say cart\n    * **The account's order list shows when a pre-order is out.** An order with a pre-order line that is not out yet carries a \"Pre-order \u00b7 out Nov 13\" chip beside its status chips, on the account overview and the order history, the way the admin's order list shows its Pre-order pill\n    * **A pre-order can't be returned before it is out.** A pre-order line whose release date is ahead leaves the returnable lines (it is listed as \"Not out yet\"), so the receipt and the order history no longer offer **Return items** on an order whose only shippable lines are pre-orders; the order offers the refund request instead. The line can be returned once the product is out\n"},"1.5.18":{"date":"10\/19\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Disputed orders are marked in the order list.** An order a customer disputed carries a red **Disputed** badge in the Orders list and on the order screen, and the list has a **Dispute** filter (open, won or lost) once any order has had one. The order's own statuses are not changed, because a dispute is not a refund. Orders whose history already holds a dispute are marked open when the store updates\n    * **Open disputes in Needs attention.** The strip at the top of the KahunaCart admin says how many orders have an open dispute, in red because each one has a deadline, and **Review the disputes** opens the order list filtered to them (`#\/orders?dispute=open`)\n    * **A dispute that is settled says how it ended.** When the provider reports it, the badge turns into **Dispute won** or **Dispute lost**, the order's history says which, and the new **Dispute won or lost** message goes to the channels ticked for it. Stripe and PayPal report settlements from their next releases. For Paddle, Mollie, or a dispute reported wrong, pick the outcome from the select beside the badge on the order screen (`POST \/kahunacart\/orders\/{id}\/dispute`, MCP tool `set_order_dispute`)\n    * **Each payment method's card says how it stands as the Payments screen opens.** Under the name, a line says Connected, Needs attention, Not connected or Not checked yet, when it was last checked, whether the webhook endpoint is registered, and when the last webhook arrived. A card whose last check is over an hour old checks again in the background, and the answer is kept, so the screen never has to be pressed to find out. Bank Transfer and Store credit, which no platform sends webhooks for, show none of the webhook parts\n    * **Check connection looks at the webhook too.** A check that passes with something wrong in the webhook is **Needs attention**, with the reason written out: no endpoint at this store's address, an endpoint switched off at the platform or missing events the driver handles (a Stripe endpoint without `charge.dispute.created`, say), or three or more failed webhooks in a row. Stripe, Polar, PayPal, Square, Paddle and Lemon Squeezy look their endpoint up, from their next releases; Mollie, SumUp and Moneris send this store's address with each payment, so the delivery log speaks for them\n    * **Recent webhooks on every card.** Lists the last ten webhooks from that platform: when, whether it succeeded, what it was, the answer the store gave, the order it was about and any error\n    * **`bin\/plugin kahunacart webhooks --check`** runs the same check from a shell, for one method with `--provider` or for all of them, and exits 1 when any fails or needs attention\n    * **\"$5 shipping, free from $20\" as one method.** A shipping method with a free-shipping minimum has a new switch, **Charge under the minimum** (`free_minimum_charges_below`, migration 0127): under the minimum the method is offered at its own cost, with \"Free from $20.00\" under its price at checkout, and from the minimum the same method costs nothing. Until now a $5 flat rate and a free method gated on $20 both stayed on offer above $20, because a minimum only decides when its own method appears. The minimum follows **Compare before discounts** and **Count only items that ship** as before, a live carrier's quote can't put the postage back, and the order's shipping line records the rate it would have cost. Off on every existing method. Offers carry `free_from_minor` for themes. See [Charging under the minimum](docs\/shipping.md#charging-under-the-minimum-5-free-from-20)\n    * **A customer group's Discount (%) now comes off prices.** It was stored and shown but never applied. The cart now takes it off as a sale of that much on everything for the group's members while they're signed in (or attached to a till or admin order), and for guests when the group is the store's default. A customer in several groups gets the highest, a line already on sale gets whichever saves more, codes come off the discounted price, and the totals show one line named after the group. Product pages still show the ordinary price; use a price list with **Show as a discount in the catalog** for prices members see while browsing. **A store that typed a percentage on a group will see it applied after updating**, so check **Customers \u2192 Groups** first. See [A group's discount](docs\/customers.md#a-groups-discount)\n    * **A badge of your own over a product's pictures.** **Badge text** and **Colour** on the product (\"BOGO\", \"Last one\", in accent, red, green, amber, blue or dark; `badge_text` and `badge_tone`, migration 0128) put a badge over the product's pictures in the shop and on its page, beside the sold-out, sale and download badges. Translatable, in the product API, the MCP product tools and the catalog file. Every badge is now drawn by one partial, `partials\/kahunacart-badges.html.twig`, with fixed classes (`kahunacart-badge--{kind}`, `kahunacart-badge--tone-{tone}`) and `kahunacart_badges()` behind it, so each theme only styles them. See [Badges on the picture](docs\/products-and-catalog.md#badges-on-the-picture)\n    * **Each cart line links to its product**, opened on the line's variant (`?variant=`), so a medium red shirt in the cart opens the product page on medium red. Lines carry `url`, empty for a product that is gone or unpublished\n2. [](#improved)\n    * **`onKahunaCartDisputeClosed` for driver and add-on authors.** Fired once per dispute and outcome by a driver whose platform reports a settlement, with `outcome` `won` or `lost`, and with `manual` true when a person set it on the order. Every order row carries `dispute_status` and `disputed_at`, and `GET \/kahunacart\/orders?dispute=open` filters by it. See the provider developer guide's Dispute event\n    * **`ProviderWebhookStatusInterface` for driver authors.** A driver whose platform can list webhook endpoints implements `webhookStatus($url)`, read only, and answers with `WebhookStatus::forEndpoint()`, so every card words the same problem the same way. See the provider developer guide\n    * **`GET \/kahunacart\/providers` answers each method's last check, its deliveries and how its platform finds the store** (`last_check`, `deliveries`, `webhook_mode`), and `POST \/kahunacart\/providers\/{slug}\/check` answers `state`, `warnings` and `webhook` beside what it always did\n    * **`GET \/kahunacart\/webhooks` answers each delivery's `order_number`**, so a link to the order works\n"},"1.5.17":{"date":"10\/18\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **A new account starts with the customer's addresses.** When a guest's account opens (the box at checkout, the offer on the receipt, or an invitation from the Customers screen), the billing and delivery addresses from their newest orders go into the address book as the defaults, one entry when they're the same. Only an empty book is filled, and an address with no street isn't filed. An account that already has an empty book sees the addresses from its last order on **Your addresses** with a **Save to my address book** button. See [Addresses from earlier orders](docs\/customer-accounts.md#addresses-from-earlier-orders)\n    * **Addresses suggested as you type.** With a Mapbox or Google key under **Address Validation**, every street box lists matching addresses after three letters, and picking one fills in the street, town, state, postcode and country and moves to the second address line. At checkout, on the account's **Add an address**, and in the admin's customer address book, New order form, **Pickup locations** and the shipping add-on's ship-from addresses. It keeps to the country already chosen, and an address from another country changes the country and its state list. The key never reaches the browser: the store asks the provider through `{base}\/address-lookup` (nonce-checked) and `GET \/kahunacart\/address-lookup`, and the typing and the pick share one session token, so each address typed is one session on your provider's bill. Rate limited per visitor (`address_lookup`, 60 a minute) and for the whole store (`address_lookup_store`, 3,000 requests a day). Google keys need Places API (New) switched on. A form set to a country whose post is written in one Latin-script language is answered in that language, so towns read the way the post prints them (Wien, K\u00f6ln, Milano). Turn it off with **Suggest Addresses as You Type** (`checkout.address_validation.lookup`). See [Suggestions as you type](docs\/address-validation.md#suggestions-as-you-type)\n    * **Attach a file to one order.** An order's **Downloads** panel has **Attach a file**, for a file that belongs to that order alone, such as a certificate of analysis for the batch a customer was sent. The customer sees it on their receipt and in their account's downloads straight away, and with **Email the customer** ticked (the default) they're sent the new **New file for the customer** email (`download-added`) with the link and your message above it. It takes the store's default download limit and expiry, **Revoke** takes it back, and the order's history records it. Works on physical orders too. Over the API: `POST \/kahunacart\/orders\/{id}\/downloads` (multipart, `kahunacart.orders.manage`). The file is kept under `orders\/<id>\/` in the files root and carries its order in the new `order_id` column (migration 0125), so nobody else is given it. A store export carries it with the orders (the new `order_files` set, under the orders permission) and never with the catalog's files, and anonymising the order deletes it. See [Files for one order](docs\/digital-products.md#files-for-one-order)\n    * **Give a new product file to past buyers.** Tick **Give to past buyers** beside **Upload** on a product's files (`grant_past_orders=1` on the upload) and everyone who already bought the product, or the variant the file is for, gets that file too (and only that file), newest 500 orders first. Only orders that went through and kept their money are given it: a sale status, a payment not refunded or voided, and paid when `downloads.grant_on` is `paid`. See [Giving a new file to past buyers](docs\/digital-products.md#giving-a-new-file-to-past-buyers)\n2. [](#improved)\n    * **The admin's address forms say what's missing before you save.** The customer address book, the New order form and **Pickup locations** mark every box the country requires (the same set checkout uses, from Settings \u2192 Countries) with a line under it, instead of refusing the save. **Pickup locations** now has country and state lists like the other address forms. Add-on sections get the same form through `ctx.ui` (`countries()`, `addressFields()`, `checkAddress()`, `bindAddressForm()`). See [Admin sections \u2192 An address](docs\/admin-sections.md#an-address)\n    * **`GET \/kahunacart\/countries` answers each country's required address fields and whether address lookup is on**, and is open to `kahunacart.settings` and `kahunacart.shipping.manage` as well\n    * **The KahunaCart admin on a phone opens on the screen, not the nav.** Under 900px wide the sidebar used to sit above every screen, so each one started with thirty nav rows to scroll past. It's now a drawer: a bar at the top names the screen you're on (Store \u00b7 Orders), and tapping it slides the nav in from the right, clear of the admin's own menu on the left. Picking a row, tapping outside, the \u00d7 or Escape closes it. A desktop that keeps the nav as an icon rail still gets full labels in the drawer\n    * **The three download upload endpoints share one set of checks.** A product's files, a release's builds and an order's files refuse the same uploads (none, too big, a type that isn't stored) in the same words, through `AdminController::downloadUpload()` and `storeDownloadUpload()`\n3. [](#bugfix)\n    * **The state field on a new address was a text box in a store with a default country.** The account's **Add an address** form, and the delivery estimate on the cart and wish lists, showed the default country already chosen beside a text box for the state, and only offered the list of states after the country was changed. The state list now follows the country the form opens on\n"},"1.5.16":{"date":"10\/17\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Cross-sells in the cart.** Under the basket, **Goes well with your order** lists the cross-sells you picked for the products in it: the most recently added product's first, in your order, leaving out anything already in the basket, single-variant products that are sold out, and anything past the fourth. A product that needs nothing chosen (one variant, no add-on options, no pay what you want, no booking dates or plan, no minimum above one) gets an **Add** button that puts one in the basket and comes back to the cart; anything else gets **View**. On by default with the new `cart.cross_sells` setting, and a store that has never picked a cross-sell sees nothing. Themes get `cart_cross_sells`, and override `partials\/kahunacart-cart-cross-sells.html.twig`. See [Cross-sells in the cart](docs\/products-and-catalog.md#cross-sells-in-the-cart)\n    * **The shopper picks a part's variant in a grouped product.** Each part in the product's **What is in it** panel now has a **Variant** select for a child with more than one variant: **Default**, a named variant, or the new **Shopper picks**. A picking part puts a plain select on the set's buy form (no JavaScript needed), with sold-out variants disabled and backordered ones marked the way the product page marks them, and the cart refuses a set whose pick is missing, from another product or not buyable, naming the part, with nothing added. One pick covers every unit of a part that holds more than one. A set is now its product plus the picks, so a red easel set and a blue one are two rows in the cart, each with its own quantity box and \u00d7, and adding the red one again raises the red set; a set with no picking parts works exactly as before. The bundle price counts any variant of a picking part towards a set, at the same price whichever is picked; such a set is counted one set at a time, each taking the units worth most to it, so the same basket comes to the same total whatever order it was filled in, and a set with no picking parts is counted exactly as before. The storefront API (`parts` on `POST \/cart\/items`, sets by key on `\/api\/cart\/sets\/{key}`), an agent checkout's line items, counter carts and orders staff build take the picks too. Stored as `shopper_picks` on the part by migration 0124 and returned in `kit_items`. See [Which variant a part sells](docs\/products-and-catalog.md#which-variant-a-part-sells)\n"},"1.5.15":{"date":"10\/17\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **The cart draws a grouped product as one row with its parts under it.** The lines a grouped product's buy button adds are headed by a row of the product's own, with its picture, a quantity box for how many sets, what the set adds up to, and one \u00d7 that takes the whole set out. Changing the set's quantity moves every part by what one set needs and leaves any spare part where it is. By default a part keeps its own quantity box and \u00d7, and changing one part so the cart no longer holds a full set stops a bundle price from applying. Tick the new **Lock the parts in the cart** (`lock_parts`) on the product and the parts change only through the set's row: no box and no \u00d7 on a part, and a change to one part on its own is refused from the cart, the storefront API and an agent checkout. A set already in a basket keeps the rule it was added under. A kit's parts (an add-on type's) are always locked. A part of a set and the same thing bought on its own are now two lines, so a spare panel isn't folded into the easel. Pricing, stock, the bundle price, tax and refunds are unchanged, and the checkout, receipt and emails still list the parts. Themes get `cart_rows`, and the storefront API gives each line a `set` and has `PATCH`\/`DELETE \/api\/cart\/sets\/{product_id}` for a whole set. See [Kits and grouped products \u2192 In the cart](docs\/products-and-catalog.md#in-the-cart)\n    * **Pictures on cart lines.** Each line in the cart shows a small picture: the one its variant shows on the product page (the variant's own, else its colour's, else the product's first), or none when the product has no pictures. Themes get it as `item.image`\n2. [](#bugfix)\n    * **Removing a free gift through the storefront API answered with a server error.** `DELETE \/api\/cart\/items\/{id}` on a promotion's gift now answers `422` with the sentence saying why it stays, like the cart page\n3. [](#improved)\n    * **The agent checkout and the cart pick a line's picture the same way.** Both go through `KahunaCart::lineImages()`\n"},"1.5.14":{"date":"10\/17\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **A shop page puts the shop in your menu.** The shop answers at its route without a page of its own, so the menu label, its place and whether it shows were the theme's to decide. Add a page at the store's address with the new **Shop page** template (`kahunacart`) and the shop is in the page tree like any other page: its `menu:` is the label, its folder number its place, and `visible: false` hides it. Every storefront page hangs off it, so the menu marks it active anywhere in the shop. The product listing takes its title, its metadata and its content as an intro, and the sitemap plugin lists it, which puts the shop's front door in the sitemap for the first time (left out while the store is coming soon). The address is still the store's `route`: a shop page anywhere else redirects to the store, and the new **Shop page** check in `bin\/plugin kahunacart check` and on the Health screen says how to fix it. Themes read `kahunacart_shop_page` to leave out their own Shop link. A site without a shop page is unchanged. See [The shop page](docs\/storefront.md#the-shop-page)\n"},"1.5.13":{"date":"10\/17\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **A free-shipping minimum can count only the items that ship.** A merchant's \"free shipping on $10 or more\" was given to a cart of a $5 download and $8 of prints, because the minimum added up the whole cart. Each shipping method with a minimum now has **Count only items that ship** (`free_minimum_shipped_only`) beside **Compare before discounts**: on, downloads and services are left out of the count, so that cart counts as $8 and pays for postage, and the \"spend more\" message names the $8. Every line that ships still counts when shipping profiles split them into separate parcels. Off by default, so every existing method still compares the whole cart. Migration `0123` adds the column; the admin API and the MCP shipping tools take the field. See [Counting only the items that ship](docs\/shipping.md#counting-only-the-items-that-ship)\n"},"1.5.12":{"date":"10\/16\/2026","content":"1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **Dragging a gallery picture often took two tries.** A drop only counted when it landed on top of another card, so letting go in the gap between two cards, which is where \"put it between these\" naturally lands, or in the empty space after the last card did nothing at all. The whole list takes the drop now, and the card nearest the pointer decides where it goes.\n"},"1.5.11":{"date":"10\/16\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Drag gallery pictures into order.** Drag a card in the Media panel onto another to move it there; a bar on the edge shows where it lands. Dragging a picture to the front makes it the main image, and each card's \u22ef menu has **Make main image**, **Move earlier** and **Move later** for anyone who'd rather not drag. Samples reorder the same way. The up and down arrows are gone, and **Edit** sits on the card. The new order is saved in one call, `POST \/kahunacart\/products\/{id}\/media\/reorder`, which renumbers the whole list in one transaction, where the arrows used to send two separate calls per step. See [Product media](docs\/products-and-catalog.md#adding-pictures)\n2. [](#bugfix)\n    * **The Main image badge skipped a gallery that opened with a video.** The first gallery *image* is the main picture, and the badge now says so even when a video sits in front of it.\n"},"1.5.10":{"date":"10\/16\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Add a whole folder of pictures to a product at once.** The Media panel has a dropzone now: drop pictures anywhere on the panel, or click it and choose as many files as you like. They upload one after another and join the gallery in filename order, so `IMG_2` comes before `IMG_10` and `red-back` before `red-front`. Files dropped while a batch is still going join the end of it. A file that's refused (too big, not a picture) is named in a message and the rest still upload, and audio or a PDF is turned away before anything is sent, with a pointer to **Add a sample**. Each picture's description is added on its card afterwards. The old one-at-a-time **Add to gallery** form is gone. See [Product media](docs\/products-and-catalog.md#adding-pictures)\n2. [](#bugfix)\n    * **The CSV importer's dropzone put its title and its hint side by side.** It's one column again, title over hint.\n"},"1.5.9":{"date":"10\/14\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Conditions on coupons.** A coupon takes the same conditions a sale and a payment method take, so a code can be kept to a customer group (`customer_group is trade`) or to a cart that holds a given product or variant (`Cart (by variant) contains` the full-price clip). A code that fails one is refused with a sentence naming it, such as \"This code is only for customers in Trade.\" What is in the cart is judged on the cart page, and so is the customer when they are signed in; the country, and the customer for a guest, wait for checkout. A customer's groups, email and order count count only when the shopper is signed in as that customer, so a guest who types a Trade member's email at checkout is judged as a guest and can't use a Trade-only code. Set them in the coupon editor's new **Conditions** panel, or as `conditions` on `\/kahunacart\/coupons` and the `create_coupon` and `update_coupon` tools. A campaign's template carries them onto every code it mints, store export and restore carry them, deleting a coupon deletes them, and a product card draws no deduction for a code that has them. See [Coupons \u2192 Conditions](docs\/coupons.md#conditions)\n2. [](#bugfix)\n    * **A signed-in account could take over another person's customer record at checkout.** Typing someone else's email address while signed in tied that customer record to your account, so the account area showed their orders, addresses, downloads and licence keys, and a coupon kept for a customer group treated you as them. An account is now tied to a customer record at checkout only when the address typed is the account's own, and an existing record is claimed only when `account.claim_by_email` is on. The order still goes to the customer the address names. A customer record that has a store sign-in of its own is never claimed by a Grav account with the same address, at checkout or in the account area. Store accounts are not affected. See [Customer accounts](docs\/customer-accounts.md#grav-accounts-and-the-email-claim)\n    * **A condition with a value that couldn't be compared was saved and then let everyone through.** A group whose `children` wasn't a list, a subtotal of \"not-a-number\", a true or false where an id or group goes, a product, variant, category or customer group that doesn't exist, and `is none of` with an empty list were all accepted and acted as no restriction. Sales, coupons, payment methods and campaign templates now refuse them with a sentence naming the problem, and write nothing. See [Rules and conditions](docs\/rules.md#attribute-vocabulary)\n    * **\"Orders this customer has placed\" always read 0.** A first-order-only code or sale could never tell a newcomer from a regular. It now counts the customer's completed or paid orders, leaving out carts, drafts, cancelled and refunded orders and test orders, and only when a condition asks for it. A guest has placed none\n    * **Merging a store export kept the old ids inside conditions.** A condition on a product, variant, category or tag, on a coupon, sale or campaign template, now moves to the target store's own ids. An id the target has no match for is dropped, a condition left with nothing is left out, and the restore result says so\n    * **A payment method restored into another store lost its conditions.** Store export and restore now carry a payment method's conditions, with the Coupons & sales set, and a merge moves the product and variant ids inside them to the target store's own, the same as coupon and sale conditions. That set restores after the store configuration and the catalog. See [Export and restore](docs\/export-and-restore.md)\n    * **A text comparison on a customer group was refused.** `customer_group starts_with \"tra\"` (or contains, ends with, does not contain) needed \"tra\" to be an existing group code. The existence check now applies only when a condition names the group itself, with is, is not, is any of or is none of\n    * **\"Left blank\" couldn't be written as a rule.** A checkout field rule such as `field_billing_company eq \"\"` was refused for having no value. Equals and not-equals on a text attribute now take empty text; the other operators, numbers and ids still need a value\n    * **A checkout field's rule accepted a variant that doesn't exist.** The checkout-field editor checked a condition's product, variant, category, tag and customer group ids against nothing, so a rule naming variant 999999 was saved. It now gets the same check the sale, coupon and payment method writers do\n    * **Editing a coupon campaign switched its future codes on.** Saving a campaign always sent the codes it mints as enabled, so a campaign set to mint codes switched off minted them on after any edit. An edit now keeps what the campaign had, and only a new campaign starts on\n    * **A cached product card could show one customer's price to another.** On a store with a price list that is shown in the catalog and ruled on the customer (a Trade price, a returning-customer price), a page with `[kahunacart_products]`, `[kahunacart_sections]`, `[kahunacart_product]` or `[kahunacart_categories]` in it was kept in Grav's page cache once, so the first visitor's price went to everybody after them: a member's 9.00 to a guest whose own price was 11.00. Those pages are now drawn for each visitor, and a signed-in customer's copy goes out `private, no-cache`. A store with no such list keeps its caching. See [Shortcodes](docs\/shortcodes.md).\n    * **A free gift for a customer group never arrived.** The free-gift check read the cart with no customer groups and no past orders, so a signed-in Trade member got nothing from a gift sale kept to Trade, and a gift for returning customers never went to one. It now reads the customer the way sales and coupons do: the signed-in shopper's groups and past orders, a guest's default group, and a customer staff attached to a till sale or an admin-built order.\n    * **Customer-based pricing (groups, email, domain, order count) now needs the customer signed in.** Price lists, sales, payment methods and checkout fields give a customer their group, and read their email and email domain, only when the shopper is signed in as that customer, the way coupons already did. A guest who types a member's email at checkout is priced with the store's default group, and one who types an `@acme.com` address doesn't get the \"Acme employees\" price list, sale, payment method or checkout field, because an address typed at checkout proves nothing. A coupon with `allowed_emails`, or a customer group, is the way to reach a known list of people. A till sale or an order made in the admin still counts the customer staff attached. A trade customer signs in to get trade prices. Payment methods and checkout fields limited to a customer group now see the signed-in customer's group, which they never saw before. Price lists, payment methods and the checkout form also count the signed-in customer's past orders now, where they always read 0, so a \"returning customer\" price list or payment method reaches a returning customer. Signing in also prices again a cart that guest checkout had already linked to the customer and one that came back from an earlier visit, where only a cart the sign-in itself claimed was priced again. Checkout fields are judged for the customer the submit has just put on the order, rather than for the order as it was read before the email changed\n3. [](#improved)\n    * **Grav accounts no longer adopt a guest's order history by email unless you turn it on.** `account.claim_by_email` is now off by default, because Grav lets a user change the email on their profile without verifying it, so the address on an account isn't proof. A Grav account still sees the orders it places while signed in, and the record checkout makes for its own address is still its own. A store whose registration and profile changes verify email addresses can turn it on under Settings \u2192 Accounts, and a store that relied on Grav accounts picking up their guest history needs to turn it back on after updating. See [Customer accounts](docs\/customer-accounts.md#the-trade-off-stated-plainly)\n"},"1.5.8":{"date":"10\/14\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **A buyer's VAT number and the reverse charge from a merchant of record.** When the buyer gives a VAT number on Polar's checkout and Polar applies the reverse charge, the number and a line saying Polar applied it now print on the receipt, invoice, invoice PDF, credit note, order emails and the admin order screen. A provider reports them as `buyer_tax_id` and `reverse_charge` on the payment it settles, and the order's totals never change. An order's own VAT number or **Don't charge tax** still wins. See [Provider contract](docs\/provider-contract.md) ([kahunacart-issues#3](https:\/\/github.com\/kahunacart\/kahunacart-issues\/issues\/3))\n2. [](#bugfix)\n    * **Printing the order page, the cart or any shop page gave blank sheets.** The print rule that hides the theme around an invoice applied to every page the storefront stylesheet loads on, so only the invoice printed. It now applies only to a page that holds an invoice ([kahunacart-issues#3](https:\/\/github.com\/kahunacart\/kahunacart-issues\/issues\/3))\n3. [](#improved)\n    * **Shop pages print cleanly.** Printing the receipt, cart, checkout, a product, the catalog or an account page leaves buttons, forms, filters, pagination and the account offer off the paper, and prints the shop in black on white with whole table rows and plain links. Parts a theme draws itself need the `kahunacart-no-print` class; see [Theming](docs\/theming.md) ([kahunacart-issues#3](https:\/\/github.com\/kahunacart\/kahunacart-issues\/issues\/3))\n"},"1.5.7":{"date":"10\/14\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Test orders.** **Mark as test order** on the order screen keeps an order you placed to try the store out of every report: revenue, order counts, items, top products, breakdowns, refunds and the CSV export. Use it for rehearsals with a 100% coupon, comps, and cards run through a live provider. Nothing else about the order changes, the Orders list shows it with a **Test order** badge, the mark is recorded on the order's history, and **Count in reports again** takes it off. The closed days the order touches are recomputed straight away. Also `POST \/kahunacart\/orders\/{id}\/test` and the MCP tool `mark_test_order`. See [Test orders](docs\/reports.md#test-orders)\n    * **Counter sales.** Core now carries what a till needs, ready for the Point of Sale add-on. `create_order` with `channel: pos` rings up a sale at a pickup location: taxed at that location, no postage, no address, and a walk-in sale needs no email at all. The signed-in user is kept as the staff member. Every order now has a `channel` (`web`, `manual`, `pos` or `agent`, filled in for past orders by migration 0120 as far as the store can tell), and the order list, exports and reports filter and segment by channel and location. See [Counter sales](docs\/counter-sales.md)\n    * **Tenders.** `POST \/kahunacart\/orders\/{id}\/tenders` (the `add_order_tender` tool) pays an order one tender at a time: cash with change and cash rounding, store credit, a gift card, or a pay link shown as a QR code. A sale paid several ways is part paid until it is covered and completes once. Cash is recorded through a new built-in `cash` provider\n    * **Card readers.** A payment provider can now take a card on a reader at the counter: the `card_present` capability and `ProviderTerminalInterface` (readers, pairing, start, status, cancel), with routes under `\/kahunacart\/providers\/{slug}\/readers` and `\/kahunacart\/orders\/{id}\/reader-payments`. A reader payment lands on the same order as any other tender, settles from the webhook or a status check, and is reconciled when a start or cancel comes back unclear. The Dummy provider has simulated readers for trying it out. See [Card readers](docs\/provider-contract.md#card-readers-optional)\n    * **Till receipt.** An 80 mm receipt for thermal printers with the order number as a Code 128 barcode, at `GET \/kahunacart\/orders\/{id}\/receipt.html?layout=till` and behind the customer's signed receipt link with `?print=till`. `pos.receipt_logo` adds a logo. The template is `kahunacart-till-receipt.html.twig`\n    * **Counter returns.** `POST \/kahunacart\/orders\/{id}\/counter-return` (the `counter_return` tool) opens, approves and receives a return in one step and refunds it back the way it was paid, in cash or to store credit. A sale paid several ways is refunded one leg per payment, and the return closes only when every leg has gone through. A paid counter sale is recorded as handed over at the counter, so it never waits in the to-ship list\n    * **The counter cart.** A till prices the basket live as items are scanned: `$kahunacart->counterCart()` (and `\/kahunacart\/counter-carts` with an MCP tool per step) opens a cart at a pickup location, adds and changes lines, applies coupons, attaches or detaches a customer, and completes it into the same counter sale `create_order` would have built. Staff discounts, by percent or amount on a line or the whole sale and labelled with their reason, are worked out where coupons are, so tax follows them. Counter carts never reach abandoned-cart recovery, reminders, cart links or the pay page\n    * **Pair card readers from the admin.** A payment provider that takes cards on a reader has a **Card readers** button on its card on the Payments screen. It lists the paired readers, pairs a new one from the code the reader shows (or shows a code to type into a Square Terminal) and forgets one\n    * **Give up on a card payment.** When a reader never says whether a card went through, `POST \/kahunacart\/orders\/{id}\/reader-payments\/{tid}\/abandon` asks once more and, only if there is still no answer, lets staff give up on it so the sale can be paid another way. A driver whose platform cannot tell how a payment ended (a Square Terminal checkout that timed out) says so, and the till shows it as checking and offers the same choice. A payment the platform has reported as taken is never given up\n    * **Counter sales in the admin.** The Orders list filters by `#\/orders?channel=pos`, with a chip to take it off, and Settings \u2192 Commerce has a Counter Sales section for cash rounding and the receipt logo\n    * **Cart contains product or variant.** Rule conditions can now ask what is anywhere in the cart: `cart_product` and `cart_variant` (**Cart (by product)** and **Cart (by variant)** in the admin) match when the cart **contains** one of the values, and with **doesn't contain** or **contains none of** when no line is. So a sale can take \u00a310 off a second-quality item only while the full-price one is in the cart. A free gift a promotion added doesn't count, and where there's no cart (a product card) the condition matches neither way. Works in sales, free gifts, payment methods, price lists and checkout fields. See [Rules and conditions](docs\/rules.md)\n    * **Bundle price for grouped products and kits.** Tick **Sell as a set** in the **What is in it** panel and the product's own price becomes the price of a full set of its parts, however they reach the cart: through the group's button or added one by one. The parts stay separate lines and each takes its own stock, and the saving shows as one discount row named after the group, spread over the parts so tax and refunds follow it. A sale never applies on top of a set, and the customer gets whichever of the two saves more; a coupon comes off after the bundle price. Each currency takes its own price, and a currency without one quotes the parts. See [Bundle price](docs\/products-and-catalog.md)\n    * **Grouped products show what the set costs.** Product pages, cards, hero and compact sections show the bundle price with the parts struck through, or what the parts add up to, never the grouped product's own unused price. `kahunacart_set_price()` and `partials\/kahunacart-set-price.html.twig` are there for themes. The products API and MCP take `sell_as_set` and answer `set_price`, and product feeds list a set at its bundle price\n    * **Look up variants by id.** `GET \/kahunacart\/variants\/search?ids=24,25` answers those variants, in the order asked\n2. [](#bugfix)\n    * **A payment method condition using \"is one of\" or \"is none of\" matched only its first value.** It saved its values as one line of text; it now saves a list, and an old condition is fixed the next time it's saved\n    * **The What is in it panel never appeared.** Drawing it threw an error on any store with products, so a grouped product's parts couldn't be added from the admin, and a new product hid it until the first save. It now shows on new and existing products, and a grouped product can be created with its parts and bundle price in one save\n    * **Payments that arrive together are settled once, against what is really owed.** Every payment on an order (checkout, a webhook, a deposit or balance, an agent charge, a refund, a tender or a card at the counter) is now decided under the order's lock from what has really been captured, so two arriving at the same moment can no longer take more than the order's total, mark an order paid before it is, or leave it fully refunded while it still holds money. The paid event, the payment-received email and what follows them are written together with the change to paid and delivered by a job, so they still go out if the request dies half way, and events wait until the change is saved\n    * **No refund while a card payment is still on the reader.** A refund of any kind is refused with a sentence saying to finish or cancel the card payment first\n    * **The sales report PDF printed `%1$s` in place of the currency** on a store that sells in more than one currency, in the lines saying figures were converted and how many orders or refunds had no recorded rate. The PDF is rendered in an admin request, where those lines were filled in the wrong way. A test now checks every document, receipt and email template for the same mistake\n    * **A return refunded by hand or to store credit stayed open.** Recording a refund made outside the provider, or refunding to store credit, now takes the return it belongs to (`return_id`) the way the refund route does, so the return closes as refunded once its money has gone back\n    * **An add-on that fails when an order is placed no longer costs the customer their receipt.** The order confirmation and any gift card codes were queued after the `onKahunaCartOrderCompleted` listeners, so one listener throwing an error left the order placed with no confirmation and a gift card whose code nobody was sent. Every step now runs whatever an earlier one throws, and all of it waits until the order's transaction has committed, so nothing is announced for an order that was rolled back\n    * **Top products, the Products report and product breakdowns counted lines at full price.** A coupon or sale discount is recorded against the line it applies to, and the product figures ignored it, so a product sold with 50% off showed twice what it took and one given away with a 100% coupon showed its full price, while Revenue above it was right. A product's revenue is now what its lines took after their discounts. Migration 0119 rebuilds the report rollup once so past days are corrected too\n    * **Semantic search embeddings never ran on a worker kept inside the site's PHP-FPM pool.** A host that runs `bin\/plugin kahunacart work` from a small entry script in the site's own pool, so its limits and identity apply, has a PHP SAPI of `fpm-fcgi`, and the `search.embed` job took that for a web request and put itself back every minute, so products never got their embeddings. The worker is now recognised by `GRAV_CLI` as well as by the SAPI, and the same goes for the rest of core's checks for the command line: the drain after a response, and rebuilding the product search index in place ([getgrav\/grav-premium-issues#626](https:\/\/github.com\/getgrav\/grav-premium-issues\/issues\/626))\n3. [](#improved)\n    * **One set of condition controls.** The condition editors in sales, price lists and payment methods now share the same controls. A variant is picked by searching its product name, variant name or SKU, and a saved condition shows names, not ids. Price lists show attribute names instead of their keys. Conditions about what is in the cart read **contains**, **doesn't contain**, **contains any of** and **contains none of**\n"},"1.5.6":{"date":"10\/13\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Returns.** A customer can send items back from their order page: they pick the lines and how many of each, give a reason, and choose by mail or in store when you take both. Each return gets a number such as `R1042-1`. You approve it on the order screen with a note and, if you like, a return label (upload a PDF, or paste a link and a tracking number), and the customer is emailed the number, your instructions and the address. When the parcel arrives, **Mark received** records how many came back and how many are damaged, puts the sound ones back in stock, and opens the refund already filled in for you to check and confirm. Damaged units are never restocked, and the refund never restocks twice. You can also decline a return with a note, cancel one that never arrives, or mark it refunded if you refunded it another way. See [Returns](docs\/orders-and-admin.md#returns)\n    * **Your published return policy now runs the return form.** `policies.returns` (returns accepted, the window in days, by mail or in store, and the fee) used to feed only Google and the product feeds. It now decides which orders can be returned: physical items that are not marked **Final sale**, on a paid order, inside the window counted from delivery (or shipping, or completion). A fixed return fee comes off the suggested refund. Two new settings, **Return instructions** and **Return address**, go in the approval email and on the order page\n    * **Customers see where their return has got to** on the receipt and in their account: requested (with a **Withdraw** button), approved with the return number, instructions, address and label, received, refunded, declined with your note, or cancelled\n    * **Returns on the Orders screen.** The refund-request filter is now **Returns & refunds** with **Open**, **Awaiting goods** and **Received**, and the dashboard alert also counts received returns still waiting for their refund\n    * **Four new emails**: return requested, return approved, return declined and return received, each switchable and editable like the others. You hear about a new return through the existing refund-request notification, which now lists the items\n    * **For developers:** `GET \/kahunacart\/returns` and receive, cancel, mark-refunded, label and label\/buy routes under `\/kahunacart\/orders\/{id}\/refund-request\/`, each an MCP tool; the refund route takes `return_id`; events `onKahunaCartReturnRequested`, `onKahunaCartReturnApproved`, `onKahunaCartReturnReceived`, `onKahunaCartReturnCancelled` and `onKahunaCartReturnRefunded`; webhook topics `return.requested`, `return.approved`, `return.received`, `return.cancelled` and `return.refunded`; and two label events, `onKahunaCartReturnLabelProviders` and `onKahunaCartBuyReturnLabel`, so a shipping add-on can offer **Buy return label**. Store export and restore carry returns and their labels\n2. [](#improved)\n    * **A store that accepts returns shows the return form instead of the refund request on orders with physical items.** Orders with nothing to send back (downloads, services, bookings, subscriptions), and every order on a store with returns switched off, keep the refund request exactly as before\n    * **Erasing a customer blanks the message they wrote on a refund request or return, and your decline note,** and deletes any return label file. The amounts and statuses stay, because they are part of the order's money record\n    * **An order with an active return can't be edited** until the return is finished, so the return's lines stay the lines that were bought\n3. [](#bugfix)\n    * **On MySQL and MariaDB, two refund requests sent at the same moment could both open.** Opening a request now locks the order and checks again on every database\n"},"1.5.5":{"date":"10\/13\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Filter the orders list.** A row of filters under the search box: **Date** (Today, Last 7 days, Last 30 days, This month, Last month, This year or Custom\u2026), **Product** with **Variant**, **Status**, **Payment**, **Fulfillment** and, on a store that sells in more than one currency, **Currency**. They are answered over every order instead of the page on screen, each status shows how many orders it would give with the other filters on, and **Clear filters** turns them all off. The filters work together with search, sorting, invoices, bulk actions and **Export\u2026**, and they are kept in the address so a filtered list can be bookmarked. The search box now says what it searches: order number, name, email, company, city or phone. See [Filtering orders](docs\/orders-and-admin.md#filtering-orders)\n    * **See who bought a product.** Each row on Reports \u2192 Products, and each variant row, has an **Orders** link that opens the order list for that product in the report's dates, and the product editor has an **Orders** button for every order it was ever bought in. An export with one row per line from that list holds only that product's lines, so the file is the buyers with their emails, dates, quantities and prices. See [Who bought a product](docs\/orders-and-admin.md#who-bought-a-product)\n    * **A customer's orders on the Orders screen.** **Open in Orders** on a customer's record opens the list filtered to them, shown as a **Customer:** chip, so their orders can be narrowed by date, product and status too\n    * **`GET \/kahunacart\/orders` takes `product`, `variant`, `from`, `to`, `customer`, `status`, `payment` and `fulfillment`**, with the same keys on the MCP tool `list_orders`, and answers with `facets` (counts per status over the filtered list) and `filter` naming the product, variant and customer. `POST \/kahunacart\/orders\/bulk\/export` and `export_orders` take them as `filter`. A new index on order lines by product (migration 0117) keeps the product filter fast on a big store\n2. [](#bugfix)\n    * **Works on hosts that disable `getmypid()`.** Hardened PHP-FPM pools often turn off `getmypid()` and `gethostname()`, and KahunaCart called them without a check: the job drain after each request, refunds from the admin and from webhooks, settlement recording, catalog index rebuilds and exchange-rate refreshes all failed with \"Call to undefined function\". It now falls back to `0` and `unknown` (grav-db-kit 1.0.6), and so do the worker heartbeat and `bin\/plugin kahunacart work --daemon` [getgrav\/grav-premium-issues#625](https:\/\/github.com\/getgrav\/grav-premium-issues\/issues\/625)\n\n"}}},"kahunacart-stripe":{"name":"KahunaCart Stripe","version":"1.2.12","description":"Stripe payments for KahunaCart \u2014 hosted Checkout, subscriptions, webhooks, and refunds","description_html":"<p>Stripe payments for KahunaCart \u2014 hosted Checkout, subscriptions, webhooks, and refunds<\/p>","description_plain":"Stripe payments for KahunaCart \u2014 hosted Checkout, subscriptions, webhooks, and refunds","icon":"credit-card","author":{"name":"Trilby Media, LLC","email":"hello@trilby.media"},"dependencies":[{"name":"grav","version":">=2.0.0"},{"name":"kahunacart","version":">=1.5.18"}],"compatibility":{"grav":["2.0"]},"homepage":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com","docs":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/docs\/providers\/stripe","keywords":"grav, premium, commerce, ecommerce, payments, stripe, checkout, subscriptions","bugs":"https:\/\/github.com\/getgrav\/grav-premium-issues\/labels\/kahunacart-stripe","license":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/license","tag_name":"1.2.12","date":"2026-10-22T23:32:47Z","release_type":"stable","zipball_url":"https:\/\/getgrav.org\/download\/plugins\/kahunacart-stripe\/1.2.12","repository":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com","premium":{"vendor":"KahunaCart","validator":"kahunacart","license_product":"kahunacart","checkout_url":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/shop\/product\/kahunacart","button":"Buy Now","filename":"grav-plugin-kahunacart-stripe"},"slug":"kahunacart-stripe","install_path":"user\/plugins\/kahunacart-stripe","content_sha256":"ccddbe756cccf4ca5f7d485c7e441a2b555fe9ab401c080e22ece9007e7408a9","changelog":{"1.2.12":{"date":"10\/22\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **Ships its license.** A `LICENSE` file now comes with the plugin, and the blueprint links to the KahunaCart License at kahunacart.com\/license instead of the Grav Premium license\n"},"1.2.11":{"date":"10\/19\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **A settled dispute says how it ended.** Stripe's `charge.dispute.closed` is written on the order as **Dispute won at Stripe** or **Dispute lost at Stripe**, the order's dispute badge follows, and KahunaCart sends its Dispute won or lost message. An inquiry that closed without becoming a chargeback counts as won. Each outcome is reported once however often Stripe delivers it. The endpoint now subscribes to `charge.dispute.closed`; press **Set up webhook** once on an existing store to add it (the Payments card says **Needs attention** until then)\n    * **Check connection says whether Stripe's webhook endpoint is right.** After the key checks out, the driver looks up this store's endpoint at Stripe, changing nothing, and the Payments card warns when there is none at this store's address, when Stripe has disabled it, or when it is missing events the driver handles, such as `charge.dispute.created` on an endpoint set up before disputes were reported. **Set up webhook** fixes each of these. Old endpoints at another address on this store's route are listed\n2. [](#improved)\n    * Needs KahunaCart 1.5.18\n"},"1.2.10":{"date":"10\/14\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Card readers with Stripe Terminal.** With KahunaCart 1.5.7 and the Point of Sale add-on, a counter sale can be paid on a Stripe reader (WisePOS E or S700). Pair a reader with the registration code it shows; the first pairing creates a Stripe Terminal location from your store address. A payment goes to the reader and settles from Stripe's webhook or from the till's status check, whichever comes first, exactly once. A decline, a cancel on the reader and a timeout are told apart, Cancel at the till clears the reader, and a refund goes back through the ordinary refund route. The driver only reports what Stripe has confirmed: a command that times out is checked against the reader and the payment before anything is retried, so a card is never charged twice. Test mode works with Stripe's simulated readers. Press **Set up webhook** once on an existing store to add the two reader events. Interac in Canada is not supported yet. Needs KahunaCart 1.5.7\n"},"1.2.9":{"date":"10\/13\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **A dispute at Stripe is written on the order and tells you.** When a customer disputes a charge, the order's history says \"Dispute opened at Stripe\" with the amount, Stripe's reason and the date your evidence is due, and KahunaCart sends its Dispute opened message to the channels you have ticked. Affiliates holds the partner's commission on it. The order itself is not changed. Each dispute is reported once however often Stripe delivers it, a dispute that arrives before its payment is recorded is asked for again rather than dropped, and a dispute on a charge that is not one of this store's orders is left alone. The endpoint now subscribes to `charge.dispute.created`; press **Set up webhook** once on an existing store to add it\n"},"1.2.8":{"date":"10\/10\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Tells KahunaCart what Stripe paid out.** On a store that sells in more than one currency, each payment's balance transaction is read once on the job worker, and Stripe's exchange rate and the net that reached your balance after Stripe's fee are recorded on the order (\"Paid out as $100.90 at 1.1202\" under a \u20ac94.00 charge). Works for one-off payments, captures, a subscription's first period and its renewals (an invoice paid in several payments records nothing, rather than one payment's payout as the whole invoice's), with the key of the account the payment was taken on. Needs KahunaCart 1.4.10\n1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **A held payment captured in Stripe before the customer came back is recorded at what Stripe took.** When part of a held payment had been captured in Stripe's own dashboard by the time the customer returned to the store, the order was marked paid at the full amount that was asked. The driver now reports the intent's `amount_received` (also on a capture, a saved-card charge and an agent payment), and KahunaCart 1.4.10 no longer marks such an order paid in full on the return: it leaves it for Stripe's webhook, which settles it at what was taken\n"},"1.2.7":{"date":"10\/06\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **A Stripe account for each payment method.** On a KahunaCart store with more than one selling company, each payment method can carry its own Stripe account: account ID, keys, signing secret, Managed Payments settings, tax code and API version. Hosted checkout and every later action on the order (capture, void, refund, webhook) use the account the order was paid through. Needs KahunaCart 1.4.2\n    * **A webhook per account.** Each account gets its own endpoint and signing secret from its **Set up webhook** button. The default account's endpoint and requests are unchanged\n    * **Customer references are kept apart per account**, and an account stays on file for its old orders after its method is edited or deleted\n    * **A fresh installation can set up its first payment method account** without default credentials. A default account that isn't configured stays unavailable at checkout\n"},"1.2.6":{"date":"10\/04\/2026","content":"1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **Discounted Managed Payments invoices record and refund the amount paid.** The first-invoice coupon is subtracted from Stripe's subtotal before calculating the pre-tax ledger amount, tax breakdown and partial refund ratio\n    * **Subscription payments include the payment method fee.** Recurring prices use the renewal order total, including the fee and its tax; any difference on the first order is charged or discounted on its initial invoice only. Managed Payments receives the net price including the fee and lets Stripe add tax. Methods without fees keep their existing requests\n    * **Orders in Icelandic kr\u00f3nur and Ugandan shillings are charged the right amount.** Stripe's API counts ISK and UGX in hundredths, the decimal part always `00`, although they have no minor unit: 5 ISK is an `amount` of `500`. The driver sent the store's whole-unit figure as it was, so a 5,000 ISK order was a checkout for 50 ISK. Amounts now go to Stripe at its own scale for these two currencies in checkouts, subscription prices, captures, saved-card charges and refunds, and come back to the store's scale in settlements, holds, captures, refunds and subscription invoices, so a payment is recorded for the amount the order was for. Other currencies keep their existing conversion except MGA, corrected below\n    * **Malagasy ariary orders are charged and recorded at the right scale.** Stripe counts MGA in whole ariary while KahunaCart stores hundredths. Checkouts, subscription prices, captures, saved-card charges and refunds now convert both ways; fractional ariary amounts are refused rather than rounded\n    * **Authorization webhooks keep the currency Stripe reports.** A hold reported in a different currency from its order now reaches KahunaCart with that currency, so the ledger keeps the requested order amount and the mismatch is reported\n1. [](#improved)\n    * The README has a Currencies section and the Instructions tab a step on selling in more than one currency: the plugin keeps no list of its own, because which currencies a Stripe account can take depends on its country and payment method, so a store that takes a currency today does not lose it after an update\n"},"1.2.5":{"date":"09\/25\/2026","content":"1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **The download no longer carries PHPUnit.** Every release until now committed a development `vendor\/`: PHPUnit and about thirty packages only the test suite uses, some 1,700 files in a plugin that needs no Composer package at run time. `vendor\/` is now a `--no-dev` install holding just the autoloader, and `psr\/http-message` is declared under `replace` because Grav core already supplies it. A test fails if a development install is ever committed again\n"},"1.2.4":{"date":"09\/23\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * Included subscription fee choices keep fee-free request bodies, while Added keeps the agreed fee and tax\n    * **Agent payments: Google Pay through UCP.** `StripeProvider` implements KahunaCart's new `AgentPaymentInterface`, so an agent platform such as Google AI Mode or Gemini can complete a checkout with a card the buyer picked in Google Pay, without a redirect. The driver declares `agent_payments` when a publishable key is set and Managed Payments is off, advertises `com.google.pay` with Stripe's Google Pay gateway parameters (`stripe:version` `2018-10-31`), turns the Stripe token Google mints into a PaymentMethod with `card[token]`, and confirms a PaymentIntent with `error_on_requires_action`, so a card that wants a 3-D Secure challenge fails with a sentence the agent can relay rather than waiting for a browser. Manual capture holds the money as a two-step checkout does, both calls carry an idempotency key made from the order and the platform's own key so a retried completion never charges twice, and the intent carries the usual order and transaction metadata plus `kahunacart_source: agent`. The token goes to Stripe in one call and never into a log, a ledger row or a result, and the `payment_intent.succeeded` delivery that follows settles nothing a second time. **Needs the base plugin at 1.3.0**\n    * The test suite can load KahunaCart core from another checkout: set `KAHUNACART_TEST_CORE_ROOT` to its folder\n    * **3-D Secure on a Google Pay agent charge becomes a handoff to the buyer.** The PaymentIntent is confirmed with `return_url` (the store's payment return page) instead of `error_on_requires_action`, and a `requires_action` intent answers KahunaCart with Stripe's hosted authentication page and the intent id, which KahunaCart 1.2.15 turns into UCP's `requires_escalation` with the page behind the checkout's `continue_url`. A `use_stripe_sdk` action is followed only to a page on Stripe's own host; anything else still fails with a sentence the agent can repeat.\n    * **An agent's `payment_intent.succeeded` is reported by its transaction hash.** It settles a challenged payment and a charge whose answer the store never recorded; after an ordinary charge core's ledger gate makes it a no-op. An agent's `payment_intent.payment_failed` no longer looks for a Checkout Session that was never there.\n1. [](#improved)\n    * Composer's installed-package record carries the plugin's current package name, `kahunacart\/grav-plugin-kahunacart-stripe`, instead of the old `trilbymedia\/grav-plugin-commerce-stripe`\n"},"1.2.3":{"date":"09\/16\/2026","content":"1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **The wallet button no longer disappears on a dark storefront.** It painted itself `#111111` on every theme but `light` and `outline`, so on the dark side of a theme like Kahuna the Apple Pay \/ Google Pay \/ Link button was a near-black rectangle on a near-black page, and `outline` was no better \u2014 a black edge and a black label with nothing behind them. The base plugin's `express.theme` has grown an `auto` value and made it the default, and this button now draws it the way the provider contract asks: the fill, the label and the edge come from `--kahunacart-express-auto-bg`, `--kahunacart-express-auto-fg` and `--kahunacart-express-auto-border` on the region wrapper, which the storefront stylesheet resolves from the page's own ink and ground. Dark button with a light label on a light page, light button with a dark label on a dark one, and it flips with the page rather than with a setting. `outline` follows the page as well now \u2014 the fill stays transparent and the border and the text take the page's own foreground, because picking an outlined button is a choice about the button's edge and never a choice to have it vanish. `dark` and `light` are unchanged and still absolute, so a merchant who picked one gets exactly that. **Needs the base plugin at 1.2.10**, which is where `auto` and the wrapper's colours come from\n"},"1.2.2":{"date":"09\/10\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **One shopper is now one Stripe Customer.** Every checkout used to go over with `customer_email` and `customer_creation: always`, which gives Stripe nothing to match a returning buyer on, so it opened a second Customer for every repeat order: two billing histories, two sets of saved cards, and a dashboard showing two strangers who each bought once. KahunaCart 1.2.3 keeps Stripe's own customer id per customer and hands it to the driver, which now sends `customer` with it instead, on one-time checkouts, authorisations and subscription checkouts alike. Never both parameters in one request, because Stripe refuses that pair outright. The id is reported back from the session Stripe answers with and from the `checkout.session.completed`, `payment_intent.succeeded` and `charge.captured` deliveries, so a store starts building the mapping from its very next order\n    * A held id that Stripe no longer knows, which is what a merchant who rotated keys onto another account or moved a store from sandbox to live is holding, opens a fresh Customer on the email path instead of failing the checkout. One line goes to the Grav log and the new id replaces the old one, so it corrects itself on the first order and nobody has to clean anything up\n    * Under Managed Payments the store still never asks Stripe to open a Customer, since Onelink is the seller there, but an id Stripe itself reported is sent back the same way\n"},"1.2.1":{"date":"09\/10\/2026","content":"1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **Every request to Stripe now names the API version, so Managed Payments works on an account with an old default.** The driver used to inherit the account's default version, which is whatever version the account was opened on. On a 2020 default Stripe refused every Managed Payments checkout with \"not supported on API version 2020-03-02\", and the merchant's only way out was to upgrade the whole account. Each call now carries `Stripe-Version: 2025-09-30.clover`, the version the driver's references were checked against, which is what Stripe recommends for an integration anyway. Webhook payloads are unchanged: the endpoint is still registered without a version and the handlers read both layouts\n"},"1.2.0":{"date":"09\/08\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * Stripe registers its own webhook endpoint. `StripeProvider` implements `ProviderWebhookSetupInterface`, so **Set up webhook** appears on the Stripe card under Payment methods: it finds or creates the endpoint for this store's URL, subscribes it to every event this driver acts on, and writes the signing secret Stripe hands back into the active mode's field. That replaces the step that breaks stores, a merchant copying a secret between two browser tabs and ticking eighteen event boxes, every mistake in which is silent, because checkout keeps working and paid orders simply never settle\n    * Pressing it twice adopts the endpoint already registered for the URL instead of creating a second one that would deliver everything twice. An adopted endpoint whose event list has fallen behind is corrected rather than merely reported, and one Stripe disabled after a run of failed deliveries, what a store that was down for a weekend comes back to, is switched back on in the same call\n    * Stripe puts `secret` in the answer to the create call and nowhere else, so an adopted endpoint has none to report. A store that already holds one is told its secret is left alone; a store that does not is told the two ways out, which are copying it from the dashboard or pressing **Reset webhook**. Reset deletes the endpoint at this exact URL and registers a fresh one, and deletes nothing else on the account: endpoints on this store's route at a previous address are reported so the merchant can remove them, never removed for them\n    * The endpoint is created with no `api_version`, so it follows the account default instead of being pinned for years to whatever version this driver was written against\n    * A restricted key that cannot manage webhook endpoints now fails with a sentence the merchant can act on, pointing at the Instructions tab. Stripe's own answer to that case is a developer's sentence with the key quoted inside it, and this result is rendered onto an admin screen and into whatever logs that screen, so neither half of it goes through. A key Stripe rejects outright gets its own sentence, since the fix is a different one\n    * `checkConnection()`, which Stripe did not have. It reads `GET \/v1\/account` with the saved key and reports the account that key actually belongs to, the country and default currency, whether Stripe is letting the account take payments, and what this store will do at checkout. A live key sitting in the test field is named outright, Stripe puts the mode in the key, and that is the one mistake here that charges real cards while a merchant believes they are rehearsing. A missing webhook signing secret fails the check rather than passing with a warning nobody reads, because a store in that state takes the customer's money and records none of it\n    * A `mode` setting is passed to the provider. Stripe serves both modes from one API host, so nothing about a request says which key pair it is carrying, and the driver has to be told: the signing secret it writes back after registering an endpoint belongs in that mode's field and writing it into the other one would leave a store rejecting every delivery\n    * An **Instructions** tab in the plugin settings, between General and API Keys. It walks a merchant through the whole sequence in order, the Stripe account or sandbox, the secret key for the mode, saving it, pressing Set up webhook, the manual fallback with the endpoint URL and both halves of the event list, the test cards and the Stripe CLI, and the three things to check afterwards. Written for somebody doing this for the first time rather than for somebody who already knows what a signing secret is\n    * The README describes the button, what adoption and reset each do, and points at the tab\n    * The two manual-capture events, `payment_intent.amount_capturable_updated` and `payment_intent.canceled`, are on the list the button subscribes and in the manual list in the README and the Instructions tab. The driver had translated them since 1.1.0 but no list told a merchant to tick them, so a hold placed or released in Stripe's own dashboard never settled the ledger on a store set up by hand\n    * A test reads the event types `mapEvent()` dispatches on straight out of the source and fails when the subscribed list differs, so the next event a release adds cannot go unheard\n    * A **Checkout Label** setting, like every other driver has: the customer-facing name of the payment option, empty for the default \"Credit \/ Debit Card (Stripe)\"\n    * A capture made in Stripe's own dashboard now reaches the store. `payment_intent.succeeded` and `charge.captured` are translated and are on the list the setup button subscribes, in the README and in the Instructions tab. Manual capture is a two-console arrangement and the dashboard is the obvious place to take the money for a merchant already there reviewing the payment, and without these the order sat at `authorized` for ever with the money taken, the admin went on drawing a Capture button, and pressing it answered \"this PaymentIntent has already been captured\" and wrote a failed capture row. A partial capture records the figure taken and the figure Stripe released\n    * The connection check resolves the Billing Portal configuration when one is set, and fails naming it when Stripe does not know it. Stripe keeps configurations per mode, so an id copied from a sandbox into a live store used to pass every check the store had and then fail for every subscriber who pressed the portal button, which meant the customer met the problem before the merchant did\n    * Every refusal from the webhook endpoint names itself. `verifySignature()` grew a `signatureProblem()` behind it that answers `no_secret_configured`, `missing_headers`, `stale_timestamp` or `bad_signature`, and the two other refusals answer `unparseable_body` and `not_an_object`. Nine distinct causes used to write one identical log row, and the log is where somebody debugging a webhook actually looks: clock skew, a secret pasted with a trailing newline and a proxy truncating bodies have completely different fixes. `no_secret_configured` earns its own code because it is the store's own setting rather than the sender's mistake, and it is the commonest reason a fresh store's paid orders never settle\n    * Every `ignored()` carries a reason and the platform's own id for the object, so the delivery log tells \"an event type we do not handle\" apart from \"a payment we refused to act on\"\n    * The store's own tax can be billed on a subscription. When the caller supplies the period's tax, the subscriber's price is minted at the taxed per-period total and the breakdown goes back on `SubscriptionResult::$taxLines`, so the add-on stores it on the row and every renewal order carries the same lines. A taxed price belongs to one jurisdiction and is deliberately not written back onto the plan\n    * A one-time checkout carries the caller's `description` to Stripe, so a shopper's Stripe receipt names what they bought instead of reading \"Order payment\", and it fills `client_reference_id` and the metadata with this store's own customer id. It also asks Stripe to create a Customer for the sale (`customer_creation: always`, and not under Managed Payments), so a merchant reconciling in Stripe's dashboard has something to click through. The README says what that costs on a store with a lot of guest sales\n\n2. [](#bugfix)\n    * **A Managed Payments refund was scaled on the store's current setting rather than on the marker Stripe put on the session.** Both refund legs read the `managed_payments` config, so a merchant who trialled the mode and turned it back off refunded merchant-of-record orders light by the tax, and every refund made in Stripe's dashboard was written into the pre-tax ledger heavy by it. On a live order that is 100 net going back as 100 when the customer paid 106, and 106 landing in a ledger that counts in nets. The setting says how a store sells today; the session says how one order was sold, and they part company the moment the flag moves with live orders on the books. The session's marker now decides in both directions, so an ordinary store is unaffected beyond one extra read per refund\n    * **A refund larger than the whole charge was blamed on the store's settings.** Stripe refuses that with a different sentence from the one it uses for a refund larger than the remainder, and only the second was mapped. The first fell through to the handler that reads a named parameter as a misconfigured store, so the merchant was sent to check payment settings that were fine, with no reason code, which also meant the base plugin's refund balance notice never fired. Both refusals now say the same thing about the amount and carry the same reason code\n    * **A card Stripe kept was not written down here.** The completion delivery asked the store's `save_payment_methods` setting whether to read the method back, and that is the setting as it stands when the webhook arrives, not the one the checkout was created under. A merchant who turned saving off in that window left a card held at Stripe with no local row, which the shopper can neither see in their account nor remove. The checkout now stamps a marker on the session and the completion reads that, so the row is written on settlement whatever the setting says by then\n    * Two merchant sentences were in British English. The Billing Portal check and the unknown-payment refusal both said \"does not recognise\"; both are rewritten as \"has no record of\", which reads better than either spelling\n    * **A refund the store made settled in the ledger at zero under Managed Payments.** The driver reported `amount_minor: 0` on every refund event to mean \"do not open a row from this\", which was safe only while nothing read that key on the settlement path. Since the base plugin's `markSuccess()` gained a settled amount it is read, so a \u20ac100 refund confirmed by `refund.updated` rewrote its own ledger row to \u20ac0.00: the order never reached `refunded`, no credit note was raised, the reversal that revokes licences and download grants never ran, and the admin offered the same \u20ac100 as refundable again. The key is now omitted, which the base plugin reads as \"the driver did not say\"\n    * **A refund issued in Stripe's dashboard could not be matched to any order.** Two causes, and between them real refunds went back to customers with the ledger knowing nothing about it. Refund events named the charge (`ch_\u2026`) while the purchase row holds the PaymentIntent (`pi_\u2026`), so the lookup compared two id families and found nothing, every time, on every store; and on API version `2026-08-26.dahlia` the Charge in a `charge.refunded` event carries no refunds list at all, so the one event that had the order hash on it was ignored. Every refund event now carries `payment_ref` with the PaymentIntent id, which is the reference the purchase row holds, with `charge_id` beside it rather than instead of it. A refund delivery for a refund this store issued is logged against its order too\n    * **Managed Payments could not be turned on.** `http_build_query` writes a PHP `true` as `1`, and Stripe answers a boolean parameter carrying `1` with \"Invalid boolean: 1\", so the very first API call was refused and every checkout on a store with the flag on failed with a message that meant nothing to the merchant. Booleans are converted once, in the encoder, so the next one cannot be written the same way\n    * **A `customer.subscription.deleted` was recorded as a cancellation scheduled for the period end.** The add-on decides immediate-versus-scheduled from `ended` and `at_period_end`; Stripe's own field is `ended_at`, a timestamp, so both hints read null and the add-on scheduled it. A subscriber Stripe had already cut off kept their licences, downloads and entitlements for up to a full billing period nobody was paying for, and the storefront showed them a cancellation date Stripe had already passed. Both keys are sent now, and a scheduled cancellation says `ended: false` beside its `at_period_end`\n    * **A customer who pressed \"Renew subscription\" in the Billing Portal was never heard.** Stripe reports the undo as an ordinary update and the driver had a branch for a cancellation being scheduled and none for one being unscheduled, so the delivery was answered 200 `ignored`: Stripe agreed the customer was staying and went on charging them while KahunaCart cut off their access on the date it still had stored. Reported as `resumed` carrying `uncanceled: true`, on both the classic and the flexible-billing-mode portal payloads\n    * **A plan repricing was ignored at Stripe.** A stored price id was reused because it existed, so a merchant raising a plan from 6.00 to 7.00 saw the storefront, the order, the subscription row and the customer's own page all say 7.00 while Stripe went on charging 6.00 for the life of every new agreement. The stored price is read back and compared on amount, currency and interval before it is reused, and a fresh one is minted when they disagree. Nothing is archived: the old price is still billing live subscriptions\n    * **A guest's card was kept.** The driver asked \"is there a customer id\", and this store writes a customer row for every checkout keyed on the email, so the answer was yes for a shopper with no account anywhere. The card was stored against a record nobody can sign in to: never asked for, never visible, and impossible to withdraw. The base plugin answers the real question now and the driver asks it\n    * **A failed subscription read during activation was answered 200.** The read failed soft, so the activation arrived with no period and the add-on dated the agreement by arithmetic on the store's own clock, and a subscription that started on a trial was recorded as active because the trial flag is read off the subscription that could not be read. Stripe never redelivers a delivery it was told was handled, and the empty catch left not even a log line. That read now throws, the base plugin answers 500, and Stripe tries again; the payment half of the apply is idempotent, so the retry costs nothing\n    * Stripe's raw error text no longer reaches a shopper or a merchant. Every failure path goes through one sanitiser: a message carrying any part of an API key is replaced outright, a schema field name such as `line_items[0][price_data][tax_code]` becomes a sentence about the store's settings, and Stripe's object ids are taken out of what a merchant reads. There used to be two error formatters here and the one wired to the shopper was the one that passed everything through, so a merchant using a restricted key could have a partially redacted key shown to a customer at checkout and written onto a failed transaction row that outlives it\n    * A refused 3-D Secure challenge reads \"The bank did not authorize this card. Try again, or pay with a different card.\" Stripe's own sentence for it names a PaymentIntent and tells the shopper to try confirming again, which means nothing to somebody holding a card. An ordinary card decline still shows Stripe's own wording, which is written for that person and is better than anything this driver would invent\n    * The three refund refusals a merchant actually meets are said in words that name what to do next, and none of them quotes a Stripe id. A subscription's first period is refused before Stripe is called at all, with a sentence saying to refund it in Stripe against the invoice and record it here, rather than Stripe's \"No such charge\" with a 66-character session id in it. A refusal for exceeding the platform's balance carries `RefundResult::REASON_EXCEEDS_BALANCE`, so the base plugin can raise a notice naming both figures\n    * A refund reports what it sent. The settled amount is the net the store asked for, with the gross and the tax beside it on the row, so an accountant preparing a VAT return can see that a \u20ac100 refund cost the business \u20ac119 of which \u20ac19 is not coming back. The row also keeps Stripe's own refund status, the currency, and both ids for the money it came out of\n    * `refundRequest()` is implemented, so a Managed Payments refund that empties the order sends the charge's own remainder rather than a scaled proportion. Refunding \u20ac100 in three goes used to send \u20ac118 back against a \u20ac119 charge, leaving the customer a cent short and a euro at Stripe that nothing would ever claim\n    * A settlement always reports its currency, not only under Managed Payments. The base plugin has a complete currency-mismatch path and it compares against that key, so omitting it did not make the check lenient, it switched it off for every store not running Managed Payments\n    * The webhook signature timestamp window is enforced in both directions. A future-dated delivery used to be accepted with no upper bound, and the everyday value is diagnosis: a store whose clock is ahead of Stripe's now refuses with `stale_timestamp` instead of `bad_signature`\n    * A JSON list posted at the endpoint is refused with a 400 rather than answered 200 `ignored`. `is_array()` in PHP is true of `[1,2,3]`, so a scanner's junk used to produce log rows indistinguishable from ordinary unhandled traffic\n    * An empty credential is refused in the transport, before any network call, so every entry point behaves the same way. A store set to live mode before the live key was pasted in used to spend a round trip on every checkout to be told \"Invalid API key provided\", and the shopper read that sentence\n    * A webhook delivery's reads are bounded. A five-second connect timeout and a ten-second read, against Stripe's own thirty-second delivery window: both were thirty, and this driver can make two reads inside one delivery, so a slow minute at Stripe could cost sixty seconds and a run of those is how an endpoint ends up disabled\n    * A superseded Checkout Session is expired at Stripe before the replacement opens, and a session left payable by a decline is expired too. A shopper who hesitated, went back and started again left two Stripe pages payable for the same order, and paying both is not hypothetical when the old tab is still open. Wrapped, so a Stripe that will not close the old page cannot stop the new one\n    * The webhook setup button adds missing events and no longer removes surplus ones. An event a merchant subscribed that endpoint to themselves, `charge.dispute.created` for their own logging say, used to be stripped off the next time anybody pressed it. A reset on a store with no endpoint at this URL no longer claims to have deleted one\n    * `provider_customer_ref` rides on every recurring event, under the contract's name with Stripe's own spelling beside it. Three subscription branches carried no customer reference at all, and a subscription whose activation was lost and which is then cancelled through the portal reaches the store with none on the row, so there is no Billing Portal URL to build and the customer can neither undo the cancellation nor replace their card\n    * A scheduled cancellation is dated by Stripe rather than by local arithmetic. `SubscriptionResult::$effectiveAt` carries `cancel_at`, falling back to Stripe's own period end, so a customer's account page and their card statement cannot name different days\n    * In test mode the connection check no longer says Stripe is not letting the account take payments. `charges_enabled` is false on every sandbox until live onboarding is finished and test charges work regardless, so the sentence was untrue and it sat inside a green tick\n    * The README's claim that manual capture removes iDEAL, Bancontact and the SEPA debits is withdrawn. On a US account the same cart under both modes offered an identical set of methods with nothing dropped; the European methods were never on offer to that account either way, so the claim is untested rather than confirmed, and the file now says so\n    * A delivery whose `type` is a JSON list is ignored instead of being cast to a string, which was a 500 with debug on and the word \"Array\" without\n    * A subscription checkout reports the Checkout Session id, so the store has something to put on the purchase row it opened for the first period. That row's reference comes from the result and the only field there was is `providerRef`, which becomes the subscription row's `provider_ref` and is what every later webhook and the customer's own cancel and pause buttons are matched on, so the session id could not go there and the row was written empty: a merchant looking at a subscription checkout somebody walked away from had no id to quote at Stripe and nothing to search the dashboard for. It now goes back on the base plugin's new `SubscriptionResult::$transactionRef`, and `providerRef` stays free for the Subscription id that arrives on `checkout.session.completed`\n"},"1.1.0":{"date":"09\/01\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * Two-step capture. `capture_mode: manual` creates the Checkout Session with `capture_method: manual` on the PaymentIntent, so checkout holds the card instead of charging it and the order stands at `authorized` until the merchant presses Capture or Void. `StripeProvider` implements `AuthorizeCaptureInterface` in full: `capture()` resolves a Checkout Session reference into its intent and posts `amount_to_capture`, `void()` cancels the intent, and `completeAuthorize()` reads the session Stripe stamped onto the return URL and reports what the intent's state actually says\n    * The README says plainly what the mode costs before a merchant switches it on: Stripe narrows a checkout to the payment methods it can hold, so iDEAL, Bancontact and the bank debits stop being offered to European customers while it is on. The mode is the merchant's decision and this driver will not make it for them \u2014 there is no store-wide capture setting in KahunaCart and there never will be, because a cart that hardcoded one is how another store lost those methods for months without noticing\n    * `payment_intent.amount_capturable_updated` and `payment_intent.canceled` are translated, so a hold and its release settle the ledger whether they were made here or in Stripe's own dashboard. The authorization carries `amount_capturable` \u2014 what Stripe actually held, which is the figure the merchant captures against, rather than the amount that was asked for\n    * Saved cards. `save_payment_methods` adds `setup_future_usage: off_session` to the Checkout Session, and `TokenizationInterface` describes and detaches what Stripe keeps. What is stored here is Stripe's reference, the brand, the last four digits and the expiry, and there is nowhere in KahunaCart for anything more. A removal detaches at Stripe first and the local row goes only once Stripe says it has let go; a method Stripe has already forgotten counts as removed, and a Stripe that cannot be reached fails loudly rather than claiming success\n    * Off-session charging through `OffSessionChargeInterface::charge()`, which hands the caller's idempotency key to Stripe's own `Idempotency-Key` header rather than inspecting it, so a retried renewal is one charge on a statement instead of two. The customer id is read off the method because Stripe refuses to charge an attached method without naming its customer, and a method that needs 3-D Secure fails with Stripe's reason rather than answering a redirect nobody is there to follow\n    * An express-pay wallet button, registered on `onKahunaCartRegisterExpressButtons` and drawn to the store's own style contract. It asks Stripe.js whether the browser has Apple Pay, Google Pay or Link and appears only when it does, so nobody is shown a wallet button that would do nothing. It posts nothing: pressing it hands the shopper to Stripe's hosted page, which is where this driver's payments have always happened\n    * Publishable-key settings for both modes, public by design and used only by that probe. Without one the express capability is not declared at all, which is better than declaring a button that cannot ask the browser anything\n    * `encryptedSettings()` names both secret keys and both webhook signing secrets, so KahunaCart stores them encrypted against a key that lives in the store's database and not in any file \u2014 a config file copied to staging or attached to a ticket no longer carries the Stripe key. Both modes are named rather than only the active one: a store that switched to live must not leave its test secret in plaintext beside it. The publishable keys are deliberately excluded, since they are printed into the page\n"},"1.0.0":{"date":"08\/31\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * A completed Checkout Session now reports `amount_minor` \u2014 Stripe's `amount_total` for that session \u2014 so the base plugin settles the pending ledger row at what Stripe actually took rather than at the amount the row was opened with. A `mode=subscription` session that opened on a free trial arrives as `no_payment_required` with a total of `0` and is recorded as `0`, which is the difference between a trial and a full-price sale nobody paid for. A session naming no total at all omits the key rather than claiming a free sale, and the merchant-of-record split still reports its pre-tax subtotal where it applies\n    * Recurring billing: Stripe declares the `Subscriptions` capability and implements `RecurringBillingInterface`, so the KahunaCart Subscriptions add-on can sell plans through Stripe Billing. A plan checks out as a Checkout Session in `mode=subscription`; the plan's Stripe price is created on its first sale \u2014 product and all, in one idempotent call \u2014 and handed back so it is reused by every later subscriber, or supplied by the merchant and used untouched\n    * Recurring webhook translation on the same endpoint payments already use: `checkout.session.completed` in subscription mode is a paid order *and* an `activated` event carrying the period and the order hash; `invoice.paid` with `billing_reason: subscription_cycle` is a `renewed` event with the period Stripe billed, the amount, and the invoice id the add-on dedupes on; `invoice.payment_failed` is `payment_failed`, plus `past_due` when Stripe has moved the subscription there; `customer.subscription.updated` is `paused`\/`resumed`, `cancelled` (at period end), `payment_method_updated`, or `expired` when dunning leaves the subscription unpaid; `customer.subscription.deleted` is `cancelled` or `expired` depending on Stripe's cancellation reason; and Stripe's own `customer.subscription.paused`\/`.resumed` are translated too. Every decision is made from the delivery itself, so a redelivery produces identical events\n    * Both API-version layouts of the objects that moved in `2025-03-31.basil` are read: `current_period_start`\/`end` on the subscription or on its items, and `invoice.subscription` or `invoice.parent.subscription_details.subscription`. A webhook endpoint carries its own API version independently of the keys, so both still arrive\n    * A cancellation scheduled from the Billing Portal is caught in either billing mode \u2014 flexible mode, the default since `2025-09-30.clover`, records it on `cancel_at` and can leave `cancel_at_period_end` untouched\n    * Cancel at period end or immediately, pause with `pause_collection[behavior]=void` (nothing billed and nothing owed for the periods the customer did not use), resume by clearing it \u2014 which is also the uncancel, since the base contract has no `uncancelSubscription()`: the subscription is read first, and a scheduled cancellation is cleared with `cancel_at_period_end=false` in classic billing mode or `cancel_at=` in flexible mode, never both in one request, while a subscription with neither a pause nor a scheduled cancellation is refused rather than written to \u2014 and a Billing Portal session URL for card updates and invoices, with an optional `billing_portal_configuration` setting for stores keeping more than one portal configuration\n    * Trials: `trial_days` becomes `subscription_data[trial_period_days]`, and the `no_payment_required` checkout Stripe completes a trial with is treated as a paid order so the subscription actually activates\n    * Managed Payments sells subscriptions as well \u2014 Stripe stays merchant of record on every period, not just the first. The eligible digital-goods tax code and the explicit `tax_behavior: exclusive` move onto the plan's price and product, since subscription mode has no inline line item to carry them\n    * Recurring API writes carry an `Idempotency-Key`, so a retried checkout replays its session rather than opening a second one and two customers racing to be a plan's first subscriber cannot mint two prices. The key folds in the request it belongs to, so an attempt that genuinely differs \u2014 the base plugin opens a fresh ledger row each time payment starts \u2014 gets a key of its own instead of failing on Stripe's changed-parameters check\n    * Metadata the caller puts on the subscription context is carried through to Stripe's own, within Stripe's limits and never over the driver's four keys. That is how `transaction_hash` reaches the completion webhook, which is what settles the order against the ledger row the base plugin had already opened\n    * Managed Payments now declares the `DigitalGoodsOnly` capability, since Stripe's merchant-of-record programme accepts only products carrying an eligible digital-goods tax code. Checkout stops offering Stripe for carts holding a shippable line while the flag is on; ordinary Stripe is untouched and still sells anything\n    * Initial release: first-party Stripe provider for KahunaCart\n    * Hosted Checkout Session flow \u2014 `initiatePayment()` creates a Stripe Checkout Session and redirects, with order and transaction hashes attached to both session and payment-intent metadata\n    * Webhook handling with self-contained `Stripe-Signature` verification (HMAC-SHA256, 300s replay window, constant-time comparison, no SDK)\n    * Event mapping for `checkout.session.completed`, `checkout.session.async_payment_succeeded`, `checkout.session.async_payment_failed`, `payment_intent.payment_failed`, `refund.created`, `refund.updated`, `refund.failed`, and `charge.refunded`\n    * Full and partial refunds through `\/v1\/refunds`, by payment intent or charge reference\n    * Async refund lifecycle: a refund Stripe answers `pending` or `requires_action` is reported as pending and settled later by `refund.updated` \/ `refund.failed`, keyed on the same `re_\u2026` reference the refund call returned; `failed` and `canceled` release the held amount for a retry\n    * `charge.refunded` is now acted on only for a refund the charge already shows as settled, since it fires at refund creation and cannot confirm one Stripe is still working on\n    * Stripe Managed Payments support behind a `managed_payments` flag \u2014 Stripe becomes merchant of record, calculating and remitting sales tax, VAT, and GST itself and issuing the receipt and invoice, while KahunaCart's own tax engine stands down. Not a separate API: the same Checkout Session call with `managed_payments[enabled]`, an eligible product tax code (`managed_payments_tax_code`, default `txcd_10000000`), an explicit `tax_behavior: exclusive` so an account-level \"Include tax in prices\" setting cannot reinterpret a pre-tax total, and the statement descriptor dropped because Stripe rejects it in this mode\n    * Managed Payments declares `MerchantOfRecord`, which is what has the base plugin's `MerchantOfRecordTaxStage` price the order pre-tax; refunds convert that net figure to the gross Stripe's charge is measured in by reading the Checkout Session's `amount_subtotal` \/ `amount_total`, so refunding a whole order returns the tax with it. Refunds issued in the Stripe dashboard are not recorded locally under Managed Payments \u2014 Stripe puts no tax breakdown on a Refund or a Charge \u2014 and the gross is reported beside the row instead\n    * Test\/live mode toggle with separate key pairs; the provider registers only when the active mode's secret key is set\n    * Optional statement label applied to the payment intent\n    * Unit test suite covering signature verification, event mapping, Checkout Session parameter building, and the whole recurring path \u2014 every subscription webhook type, price reuse against price creation, cancel, pause, resume, portal requests, Managed Payments plans, and redelivery producing identical events"}}},"kahunacart-polar":{"name":"KahunaCart Polar","version":"1.1.15","description":"Polar payments for KahunaCart \u2014 hosted checkout, merchant of record tax, webhooks, refunds, and catalog sync","description_html":"<p>Polar payments for KahunaCart \u2014 hosted checkout, merchant of record tax, webhooks, refunds, and catalog sync<\/p>","description_plain":"Polar payments for KahunaCart \u2014 hosted checkout, merchant of record tax, webhooks, refunds, and catalog sync","icon":"globe","author":{"name":"Trilby Media, LLC","email":"hello@trilby.media"},"dependencies":[{"name":"grav","version":">=2.0.0"},{"name":"kahunacart","version":">=1.5.18"}],"compatibility":{"grav":["2.0"]},"homepage":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com","docs":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/docs\/providers\/polar","keywords":"grav, premium, commerce, ecommerce, payments, polar, checkout, merchant-of-record","bugs":"https:\/\/github.com\/getgrav\/grav-premium-issues\/labels\/kahunacart-polar","license":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/license","tag_name":"1.1.15","date":"2026-10-22T23:32:53Z","release_type":"stable","zipball_url":"https:\/\/getgrav.org\/download\/plugins\/kahunacart-polar\/1.1.15","repository":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com","premium":{"vendor":"KahunaCart","validator":"kahunacart","license_product":"kahunacart","checkout_url":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/shop\/product\/kahunacart","button":"Buy Now","filename":"grav-plugin-kahunacart-polar"},"slug":"kahunacart-polar","install_path":"user\/plugins\/kahunacart-polar","content_sha256":"0ebfa62758cb6a31cc82eaf834dfcbab102038989f7c6c66e781fd5ac69cdd60","changelog":{"1.1.15":{"date":"10\/22\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **Ships its license.** A `LICENSE` file now comes with the plugin, and the blueprint links to the KahunaCart License at kahunacart.com\/license instead of the Grav Premium license\n"},"1.1.14":{"date":"10\/19\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Check connection says whether Polar's webhook endpoint is right.** After the key checks out, the driver looks up this store's endpoint at Polar, changing nothing, and the Payments card warns when there is none at this store's address, when Polar has switched it off, when it is missing events the driver handles, or when it is on an API version other than 2026-04. **Set up webhook** fixes each of these. A token without `webhooks:read` is reported, not warned about\n2. [](#improved)\n    * Needs KahunaCart 1.5.18\n"},"1.1.13":{"date":"10\/14\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **The buyer's VAT number reaches KahunaCart.** The driver reports the VAT number a buyer gives on Polar's checkout, and the reverse charge when Polar charged no VAT on a taxable order, so KahunaCart 1.5.8 prints both on the receipt, invoice and order emails. Only a VAT-type number counts as a reverse charge ([kahunacart-issues#3](https:\/\/github.com\/kahunacart\/kahunacart-issues\/issues\/3))\n    * **Fill in older orders.** `bin\/plugin kahunacart-polar tax-ids:backfill` asks Polar for the VAT number on orders paid before this release and adds it. `--dry-run` shows what it would do, `--order=1036` does one order, and running it twice changes nothing\n2. [](#improved)\n    * Needs KahunaCart 1.5.8\n"},"1.1.12":{"date":"10\/13\/2026","content":"1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **The Polar API version message now has a row you can tick channels on.** The message sent when Polar stopped serving the pinned version was recorded under a name that was never registered, so only a catch-all channel in the config file ever received it. On KahunaCart 1.5.3 or later the existing alert is the message: **Polar API version problem** appears under Notifications \u2192 Store owner, goes out when the problem starts and again as \"Cleared\" when it is fixed, and also covers Polar not answering and a webhook endpoint that could not be moved. It can arrive up to six hours after the fallback first happens. **Warn me when the API version pin stops working** still switches it off. An older KahunaCart behaves as before\n"},"1.1.11":{"date":"10\/11\/2026","content":"1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **Works with a shop mounted at the site root.** With KahunaCart's `route: '\/'` (core 1.5.0) the webhook endpoints Polar is checked against are `https:\/\/example.com\/webhook\/polar` rather than `https:\/\/example.com\/\/webhook\/polar`\n"},"1.1.10":{"date":"10\/04\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * Document that Included subscription fee choices keep Polar available and Added fee agreements are refused\n    * **A store that sells in more than one currency can take payments through Polar in its verified presentment currencies.** Which currencies Polar takes depends on the Polar organization; the plugin offers the presentment currencies verified in Polar's source. A checkout Polar rejects over the currency now says \"Polar does not take payments in EUR for this store\" and suggests another way to pay, instead of repeating Polar's validation message. The README and the settings screen's instructions tell the merchant to switch each currency on in the Polar organization. A paid order or a refund Polar reports in another currency than the order's reaches KahunaCart with that currency, and a payment or refund that names none reports none. Needs KahunaCart 1.4.0.\n2. [](#improved)\n    * A subscription plan's product name in Polar shows its price at the currency's own decimal places, so a yen plan reads \"JPY 1,999 \/ month\" and not \"JPY 19.99 \/ month\". Dollars, euros and pounds read as before.\n3. [](#bugfix)\n    * **Subscription payment methods with a fee are refused.** Polar cannot charge an independent first invoice adjustment, so KahunaCart no longer opens a subscription that could collect less than its order. Use a fee-free method for Polar subscriptions; one-time fees still work.\n    * **ISK, UGX and MGA payments and refunds use Polar\u2019s units.** Checkout and subscription prices, webhook payments, refunds and catalogue sync now convert both ways, including tax amounts. Refund ceilings compare amounts at the same scale, and a refund in a different currency from its Polar order is refused. Fractional MGA amounts and currencies without a verified Polar payment scale are refused\n    * **Catalog sync no longer labels every price as US dollars.** A push without a currency used to send \"usd\" whatever the store sold in, so a euro store's prices reached Polar as dollars. The push now sends the store's base currency, which KahunaCart 1.4.0 gives it with every product, and refuses a product that arrives with none.\n    * **Catalog sync no longer imports a Polar price in another currency as the same number of base-currency units.** A pull used to read each price's amount and ignore its currency, so a euro price on a dollar store came in as dollars. Each pulled price now says which currency Polar has it in, and KahunaCart skips one that is not in the store's base currency.\n"},"1.1.9":{"date":"09\/29\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Other plugins hear about deliveries for orders this store never made.** Polar sends every order event on an organization to every endpoint on it, so a store sharing its organization hears about orders another app sold. Those were logged as unmatched and dropped. A verified `order.*`, `refund.*` or `subscription.*` delivery that carries none of this store's references now also fires the Grav event `onKahunaCartPolarWebhookUnmatched`, with the event type, the decoded payload, the Polar order id and the delivery id, so a plugin that imported the old app's licences can revoke one when its order is refunded. The answer to Polar stays 200, and a listener that fails is logged rather than retried\n2. [](#improved)\n    * **Polar's checkout names the store, not the plugin.** The private product every cart is charged against is the title a buyer reads above the amount on Polar's page, and it was created as \"KahunaCart Order\" on every store. A new one is now named after the store (\"Grav Premium Order\"; \"KahunaCart Order\" only for a store with no name). One that already exists is found by its metadata as before, so it keeps whatever name it has in Polar and can be renamed there, and the connection check now reports that name instead of the plugin's default\n3. [](#bugfix)\n    * **The driver only touches its own webhook endpoint.** An endpoint counted as this store's when its URL merely ended in `\/webhook\/polar`, so on a Polar organization shared with another store or another app, the daily API version job would move that app's endpoint to this plugin's version and Reset would delete it. An endpoint is now this store's only when its URL is exactly one of the store's own webhook URLs, one for `system.custom_base_url` and one for each Site Addresses entry, with scheme and host compared without regard to case and a trailing slash ignored. Set up webhook, Reset, the daily job and the dashboard alert all use that one test. Other endpoints on the organization are left alone, not reported as stale, and counted under *Other endpoints* in the Set up webhook result. A store that names no address of its own has the daily job move nothing, with a log line saying what to set\n"},"1.1.8":{"date":"09\/25\/2026","content":"1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **The download no longer carries PHPUnit.** Every release until now committed a development `vendor\/`: PHPUnit and about thirty packages only the test suite uses, some 1,700 files in a plugin that needs no Composer package at run time. `vendor\/` is now a `--no-dev` install holding just the autoloader, and `psr\/http-message` is declared under `replace` because Grav core already supplies it. A test fails if a development install is ever committed again\n"},"1.1.7":{"date":"09\/23\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * Composer's installed-package record carries the plugin's current package name, `kahunacart\/grav-plugin-kahunacart-polar`, instead of the old `trilbymedia\/grav-plugin-kahunacart-polar`\n"},"1.1.6":{"date":"09\/11\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **The webhook endpoint follows the plugin's API version on its own.** A daily job moves every endpoint on this store's Polar webhook route onto the version the plugin reads, so an upgrade carries the endpoint with it without anybody pressing Set up webhook, and an endpoint left on a version Polar has since removed is rescued even after Polar has stopped delivering to it. It rides the KahunaCart worker, keeps exactly one run queued a day ahead, and logs what it moved. It also notes an endpoint still signed with Polar's original scheme, and Set up webhook shows the scheme beside the version; both schemes verify, and a secret generated fresh at Polar moves an endpoint to Standard Webhooks. A run that fails, over a token without the webhooks scope or a Polar outage, logs once and tries again tomorrow\n\n    * **You are told when the pin stops working.** A line at the top of the store admin says Polar no longer serves the pinned version and the store is selling on Polar's current one, or that Polar is not answering at all, or that an endpoint could not be moved; it rechecks every six hours and names what to do. The first time the fallback fires, one message goes to the store's notification channels, once per version. Both are behind a new *Warn me when the API version pin stops working* setting on the Polar screen, on by default; off, the Grav log still records it\n\n2. [](#improved)\n    * **A pin Polar has stopped serving no longer stops the store.** Polar removes an API version nine months after it opens, and from that day every pinned call answers a bare 404. The driver now reads the first one as the pin having expired: it logs that, retries the call with no version, which Polar answers in whichever version is current, and floats that way for the rest of the request. That is the behaviour the driver had before it pinned anything, so a store on an old plugin keeps selling while the log says to update it. A bare 404 with no version on it is still thrown, and the message names both things it can be, a wrong path or a removed version, rather than asserting one\n"},"1.1.5":{"date":"09\/11\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **Every request to Polar is pinned to API version 2026-04.** Polar now versions its API by date: a new version opens each quarter, a request that names no version gets whichever one is Current that day, and 2026-10 becomes Current on October 1. Before this the driver named no version, so its contract with Polar would have changed on that day without a release. The version is one constant, sent as `Polar-Version` on every call; moving it is a deliberate release\n    * **A 404 from Polar's router is no longer read as a verdict on the resource.** Polar answers a missing product or order with `{\"error\":\"ResourceNotFound\"}`, and a path or API version it does not serve with a bare `{\"detail\":\"Not Found\"}`: the same status, told apart only by the `error` key. On the day a pinned version is removed the bare answer comes back for everything, and read as data it would make every product gone, every order unknown and every stored customer id stale. The driver now treats it the way it treats Polar being unreachable, with a message that names the version and says to update the plugin\n    * **The webhook endpoint asks for its payloads in the same version.** Set up webhook registers the endpoint with `api_version` 2026-04, and pressing it on an endpoint that carries another version, or none, moves it and says so. The endpoint's version is shown in the result beside its events. A delivery that arrives in a different version is still handled, and a warning in the Grav log names the version and says to press Set up webhook\n\n2. [](#bugfix)\n    * **Webhook secrets generated before 8 September 2026 verify again.** Polar changed its signing scheme on that date: newer secrets sign with the decoded key, the way Standard Webhooks specifies and the way 1.1.4 reads them, but older secrets sign with the printable `whsec_` string itself. A store whose endpoint predates the change was refusing every delivery with `bad_signature` since 1.1.4. Both keys are tried now, the way Polar's own SDKs do\n"},"1.1.4":{"date":"09\/11\/2026","content":"1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **Polar webhooks are verified against the right key, so paid orders settle.** A Polar signing secret is `whsec_` followed by the key in base64, and Polar signs with the decoded key. The driver hashed with the printable string instead, so every real delivery was refused with `bad_signature`, the order never moved off pending, and the return page span forever. The secret is decoded now, the way Standard Webhooks specifies. A secret pasted without the prefix still works as it did\n"},"1.1.3":{"date":"09\/10\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * Polar is told which customer it is looking at. A checkout for somebody who has bought here before now carries Polar's own customer id alongside the store's `external_customer_id`, and the id Polar names on the checkout it creates and on the paid order goes back to the store so it is there for the next one. Before this, a hosted checkout built from an email address gave Polar nothing to match on, so a shopper who bought twice ended up as two customers in the Polar dashboard, with two billing histories and two sets of saved cards. An id Polar no longer recognises, after a move between organizations or from the sandbox to production, is dropped and the checkout goes again without it, so the shopper sees a working checkout and the new id replaces the stale one\n"},"1.1.2":{"date":"09\/10\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **The README and the Instructions tab now say to set Polar's Default tax behavior to Exclusive.** The provider hands Polar the order's pre-tax total as one ad-hoc fixed price and records Polar's reported net as what was paid, so the organization setting under Payments has to treat that amount as the price before tax. Inclusive would carve the tax out of it and leave every order short; Location-based does the same for customers in some countries and not others. Nothing in the code changed\n"},"1.1.1":{"date":"09\/08\/2026","content":"1. [](#bugfix)\n    * A subscription checkout reports the Polar checkout's own id, so the pending purchase row for the first period is no longer left with an empty reference. The only field a driver had was `providerRef`, which becomes the subscription row's `provider_ref` and is what every later webhook and the customer's own cancel and pause buttons are matched on, and there is no subscription to name yet because Polar opens one on the first `order.paid`. The id now goes back on the base plugin's new `SubscriptionResult::$transactionRef`, which is the same id a one-off payment has always written to its own ledger row, so a merchant looking at an abandoned subscription checkout has something to search Polar for\n    * A plan product Polar already holds is renamed in place when the plan's own title differs from it, so a store upgraded from a release that never sent the title stops selling as \"KahunaCart Plan N\" on the hosted page and the card statement; the carrier product's old merchant-facing description is rewritten for the buyer the first time a checkout finds it. A rename Polar refuses never stands between the customer and the checkout\n    * A refund clamped down to what Polar still holds now reports the figure it actually sent, and KahunaCart writes the row from that. The ledger row is opened at the amount the admin typed, and Polar's side can hold less \u2014 a discount the customer took on the hosted page, or a refund the merchant already issued in Polar's dashboard \u2014 so a refund that went out smaller used to leave the books saying money went back that never left: the order flipped to fully refunded, the restock and the download revocation ran, a credit note was issued for the full figure, and the customer was short the difference with nothing anywhere saying so. The refund's gross and tax ride along on the same keys a refund webhook writes, so a refund settled on submission and one settled by webhook now leave the same record behind \u2014 which in a VAT jurisdiction is the only note of what the customer actually got back\n    * Every recurring event carries the Polar customer id as `provider_customer_ref`, which is the key the Subscriptions add-on reads. It was written under `customer`, which nothing looks for, so the local subscription row never got a customer reference and the billing portal button was never drawn \u2014 and Polar's portal is the only place a card can be replaced, so a `past_due` customer had no way to recover and was revoked 21 days later. `customer` is kept beside the new key for the audit trail\n    * The connection check reads a configured checkout product back from Polar and reports the name Polar holds for it. It used to print a constant beside whatever id was configured, so a mistyped, stale or wrong-environment id produced a green light and an invented product name next to an id that does not exist \u2014 and the next real checkout failed with \"Product does not exist\". A missing or archived product now fails the check in a plain sentence, on the screen the merchant is already looking at\n    * A subscription checkout carries `transaction_hash`, as a one-time checkout always has. Without it the webhook has to attach the money to the order's most recent pending purchase row, which is the wrong row the moment a customer has two attempts open \u2014 and a customer whose first attempt was refused is exactly that customer\n    * `initiatePayment()` and `refund()` refuse an empty access token in plain words instead of making a real unauthenticated call and handing back Polar's \"Unauthorized\". The connection check and webhook setup already did; one guard on the request path makes the whole driver behave the same way\n    * A JSON list body is no longer read as an event. `[1,2,3]` decodes to a PHP array and walked straight past the \"is this an object\" gate, arriving as a 200 with nothing done; `array_is_list()` says what that gate meant\n    * Tidying up at Polar can no longer fail a checkout. Two housekeeping calls run while a checkout session is being created, rewriting the carrier product's old merchant-facing description and renaming a plan product whose title has changed, and both talked to Polar without catching a transport failure. A product endpoint that was briefly slow while the checkout endpoint was fine, which is the ordinary shape of a partial outage, meant the shopper waited out the connect timeout and was told the store could not reach Polar. No session was ever attempted, and the sale was lost again on the next try, for a cosmetic rewrite nobody asked for\n    * `revoke_benefits` is decided by KahunaCart, not hard-coded true and not worked out from the driver's view of one Polar order. A local order can be paid by more than one Polar charge, and the driver only ever sees the one it is refunding, so a refund that emptied one charge of two read as \"this empties the order\": Polar pulled the customer's licence keys, downloads and Discord access while KahunaCart still showed the line as delivered and the order as only partly refunded. The store said the customer still had the goods and Polar said they did not. The base plugin now hands the driver a `RefundRequest` carrying `exhaustsBalance`, which is whether this refund takes the last of the local order's refundable balance, and that alone decides the flag. A caller that cannot say revokes nothing\n    * A verified delivery whose `type` is a JSON array no longer answers 500. Casting an array to a string is a warning on a production error handler and an exception on a store with debug on, so the same delivery was read as the literal word \"Array\" on one store and answered 500 on the other, and a 500 is what tells a platform to send it again until it gives up. Anything that is not a string is not an event type, and it is refused as `ignored:malformed`\n    * A JSON list as an event's `data` is no longer announced as a completed payment. A list is an array in PHP, so it walked past the \"is this an object\" gate one level down from the one that already catches it on the envelope. Nothing settled, because there was no hash and no reference on it, but a garbage delivery reading as a payment is noise in the one table a merchant opens when they are already worried about money\n    * The connection check fails on a store with no webhook secret instead of passing with a warning. Checkout works perfectly without one and then no paid order ever settles: the customer pays, Polar takes the money, every delivery is rejected, and the order sits in the cart for ever. The sentence was already the clearest one in the driver and it is unchanged, but `ok` is what the admin badge is drawn from, and that badge was green on a store that could take money and could not record it. Everything the check did prove still shows beside the refusal\n    * A plan's Polar product carries its price and interval in the name on every mint, not only on the invented fallback. A repricing cannot reuse the old product, so it mints a second one, and the merchant was left with two rows in their own Polar catalogue both called \"Wave Log Spreadsheet\" with only a metadata key telling them apart, which Polar's dashboard does not show. Every later repricing added another. Names now read \"Wave Log Spreadsheet ($7.00 \/ month)\", and the lookup is unchanged: a product is still found by the `kahunacart_slug` this plugin stamps on it, whatever it ends up called\n    * A configured checkout product id that was never a Polar id is refused in this plugin's own words, without a round trip. Polar answers a malformed id with its own schema complaint about UUIDs and invalid characters, and that was repeated to the merchant on top of this plugin's sentence, telling them about Polar's validator instead of about the box they typed in\n    * Polar's UUID complaint no longer reaches the merchant on the refund path either. A refund against a reference Polar does not recognize answered \"Polar rejected the checkout details: Input should be a valid UUID, invalid character: found `v` at 10\", which names Polar's schema validator and a call nobody made. It now reads \"That does not look like a Polar id\", and every other refusal on that path names the order lookup it came from rather than a checkout\n    * A scheduled pause dates itself from Polar's period end rather than the store's. Polar leaves `pauses_at` null until the pause actually happens, so the driver read no date at all and the store fell back to its own `current_period_end`. On a store whose copy had drifted a month that meant telling the customer billing paused on 3 November while Polar was going to pause on 3 October. The scheduling flag plus the period end in the same PATCH response is Polar's own answer and is what is reported now, and a scheduled cancellation with no `ends_at` reads the same way\n\n2. [](#improved)\n    * Polar's refusals are written for the person reading them. A rejected access token said `invalid_token` on the merchant's settings screen, and a checkout Polar would not accept handed the shopper its raw Pydantic validation list \u2014 the same complaint three times over, naming Polar's own schema fields. Now a token refusal says to check the token on the settings screen, an email address Polar will not take says exactly that, and any other validation list is cut to its first message. A refund that cannot read its Polar order names the order it could not read, instead of answering \"Refund failed: Not found\". Anything Polar already wrote in plain English is passed through untouched\n    * The carrier product's description is written for the buyer. Polar's hosted page prints it under the amount, so every shopper paying through the store was reading a note to the merchant \u2014 \"Carries checkout payments from this KahunaCart store. Each order overrides the price below.\" \u2014 that mentions a price they cannot see. It now names the store, taking the name from the invoice settings and falling back to the site title\n    * Catalog sync leaves two kinds of Polar product alone. This plugin's own private products \u2014 the carrier every cart is charged against, and the recurring product minted for each plan and again on each repricing \u2014 were pulled in as ordinary catalog rows, so a fresh store's first checkout dropped a draft \"KahunaCart Order\" priced at its $1 placeholder into the merchant's catalog. And a genuinely recurring Polar product pulled in as a one-time item, because the normalized product structure carries no interval: a $5\/month plan became a $5 thing a shopper buys once and is never billed for again\n    * A rejected webhook says why in the store's webhook log: `no_secret_configured`, `missing_headers`, `stale_timestamp`, `bad_signature`, `unparseable_body` or `not_an_object`, because a wrong secret and a clock that has drifted are different jobs and both used to read as \"rejected\". The reason is always a code this driver chose; nothing from the request is repeated into it\n    * A Polar outage fails fast. The connect timeout drops from 30 seconds to 5, inside an unchanged 30-second total, so a checkout submit making three sequential calls on a cold request no longer holds a customer's POST for a minute and a half against a `max_execution_time` that is commonly 30 or 60. A slow answer is still an answer; only the wait for a machine that is not there is cut\n    * An `order.paid` whose order says it is not paid is ignored rather than settling the order on the strength of the event's name, and the refusal names itself and names the order. Polar will not send one, but this driver is the reference the others are written against\n    * A refund this store submits carries the order's metadata, so the order and transaction hashes are on the refund and a `refund.updated` redelivered after a crash can find the row it settles. A refund issued in Polar's own dashboard still cannot be matched, because Polar copies nothing onto it \u2014 that stays documented as the limitation it is\n    * The base plugin dependency moves to 1.2.0, which is the release that gives `RefundResult` a settled amount and a payload and `WebhookResult::invalid()` a reason \u2014 the three things this release reports through\n    * The README covers the refund amount rule, benefit revocation, the customer reference, what the connection check now proves, what catalog sync skips and why, and what Polar's refusals say. It also records that Polar publishes no event at all for a declined card, so an order that was attempted and refused looks the same as a cart nobody tried to pay\n    * `no_secret_configured` is kept apart from `missing_headers`. An empty signing secret is the store's own setting and not something the sender did, it is the commonest reason a fresh store's paid orders never settle, and the merchant reading that log row needs to be sent to the settings screen rather than off looking at what Polar posted\n    * Refunds report the payment they are against as `payment_ref` as well as `order_id`, on both the create call and the refund events. `payment_ref` is the contract's own name for that value and the first key the base plugin tries when a refund arrives with no local row to match, and no shipped driver was sending it, so the name the contract tells a new driver to use was satisfied by nothing in the tree\n    * A second attempt at a cart no longer tries to close the session the first one opened, and the README and the provider contract now say why. Polar has no way to expire a checkout: `DELETE \/v1\/checkouts\/{id}` answers 405, `PATCH` takes the request and ignores a `status` key, and a session runs to its own `expires_at` about an hour out. The call this driver was making did nothing, silently, and passed its own unit test while doing it, because a returned 405 is not a thrown error. What catches a customer who finishes the old page is KahunaCart's superseded-row bookkeeping, which does work: the retired row still settles a late webhook so the money is recorded, the order history and a merchant notice name both references, and the refundable balance counts both charges so the second can be handed back\n    * A scheduled pause or cancellation reports Polar's own date for it. The store used to work the date out from its copy of the period end, which can drift, so a customer's account page could say billing pauses on the 14th beside a card statement showing a payment on the 15th. That disagreement is what turns into a chargeback rather than a support ticket. Polar does not fill in `pauses_at` until the pause actually happens, so a freshly scheduled pause is read from `pause_at_period_end` plus the `current_period_end` in the same response, which is the platform's own date and not the store's copy of it\n    * The driver declares the address fields Polar will not complete a checkout without: a state for the US and Canada, which is what its tax calculation needs. KahunaCart only asks for a full address when the cart holds something physical, so a shopper buying a single download sailed through the store's checkout, typed their card in, pressed Pay, and got a bare \"This field is required\" under an empty State box on a page the merchant does not control\n\n3. [](#new)\n    * An **Instructions** tab in the plugin settings, after General, that walks a merchant through setup in order: the account or sandbox, which credential to copy and from where, saving it, the webhook (button or manual steps with the endpoint URL and the events), test cards or sandbox accounts, and what to check afterwards\n"},"1.1.0":{"date":"09\/01\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * `captureMode()` is declared explicitly as `Automatic`, and there is deliberately no setting to change it. Polar's API is products, prices, checkouts, customers, subscriptions, orders, benefits, license keys, the customer portal, usage meters and webhooks \u2014 no authorisation resource, no capture call, no void call. Polar is the merchant of record and Stripe is *its* processor rather than the store's, so the only party who could authorise without capturing is Polar itself and it does not offer that to a seller; by the time an order exists to read back the money has moved and the tax has been computed and remitted against it. `Authorize`, `Capture` and `Void` are undeclared with it, so KahunaCart never draws a Capture or Void button on a Polar order\n    * `encryptedSettings()` names both access tokens and both webhook signing secrets, so KahunaCart stores all four encrypted against a key that lives in the store's database rather than in any file. A token can spend from the organization and a secret would let anyone holding it sign an event this plugin cannot tell from a real one. Both environments are named rather than only the active one, so a store that went live does not leave its sandbox credentials in plaintext beside the encrypted production ones. A value written in plaintext before this reads correctly and is rewritten encrypted on the next save, with nothing for a merchant to do. The organization ids and checkout product ids are left in the clear on purpose \u2014 neither is a secret, an organization id appears in Polar's own dashboard URLs, and both are what a merchant reads back when an order lands against the wrong product\n    * The README says plainly what this driver does not claim, and why `Subscriptions` being declared is not a contradiction. Polar does keep a card on file and charges it, but on a calendar it runs itself: there is no vault a payment method could be stored in from here, no method id to hand back, and no way to charge one on this store's own initiative, so `Tokenization` and `OffSessionCharge` stay undeclared. Its checkout takes wallets, but only on Polar's own hosted page after the redirect, so there is no SDK to mount an express button with either\n    * The base plugin dependency moves to 1.1.0, which is the release that introduced `captureMode()`, `encryptedSettings()` and the `CaptureMode` enum this driver now imports\n"},"1.0.0":{"date":"08\/31\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * Webhook setup now repairs an endpoint it adopts instead of only reporting on it. A store that pressed **Set up webhook** before the subscription events were added has an endpoint subscribed to a list that has since fallen behind, and nothing about that store looks broken \u2014 checkout works, orders settle, and only the newer events go unheard \u2014 so being told which ones are missing is no use when the button that could add them is the one that just declined to. An adopted endpoint whose events differ from the set this provider handles is now updated to exactly that set through Polar's `PATCH \/v1\/webhooks\/endpoints\/{id}`, which replaces the list wholesale, and the summary names what was added and anything dropped that this provider never handled. Only `events` is sent, so the endpoint keeps its URL, and the secret is untouched \u2014 Polar regenerates one only through a call of its own, `PATCH \/v1\/webhooks\/endpoints\/{id}\/secret` \u2014 which means deliveries already in flight stay verifiable and the stored secret is still the right one. An update Polar refuses fails the setup with Polar's own reason rather than reporting success because an endpoint exists; an endpoint listed without an id, which cannot be addressed, is still adopted as it stands with its gaps named\n    * A completed Polar order reports `amount_minor` only when Polar named an amount. It is still the order's `net_amount` \u2014 the store's own takings, Polar being merchant of record \u2014 and a zero-total `subscription_create` order, which is what a free trial produces, is now recorded as a `0` rather than settling the pending ledger row at the full plan price. An order naming neither a net nor a total leaves the key off entirely, so the base plugin falls back to the amount the row was opened with instead of reading silence as a free sale\n    * Recurring billing. Polar declares the `Subscriptions` capability and implements `RecurringBillingInterface`, so a KahunaCart plan priced through Polar is billed by Polar: it advances the period, charges the stored card, retries a failed renewal on its own schedule, and reports each step to the one webhook endpoint this provider already owns. Each plan gets a private recurring Polar product created on first checkout and recorded back on the plan \u2014 checked before it is reused, because a merchant who reprices a plan in KahunaCart would otherwise leave that id pointing at a product still charging the old figure, and a mismatch mints a fresh product rather than repricing the one every existing subscriber sits on. All four of KahunaCart's intervals are Polar intervals and Polar counts them, so nothing has to be refused for want of a counterpart; trials are sent per checkout, where they override whatever the Polar product carries, and a plan with no trial says so explicitly so a dashboard-configured trial cannot be given away. Cancel at period end and cancel now, real pause and resume \u2014 Polar has both now, and pausing is scheduled for period end because the customer has already paid for the period they are in \u2014 with resume doubling as the uncancel, since the base contract has no `uncancelSubscription()`: a scheduled cancellation is undone with Polar's own plain `cancel_at_period_end: false` update and no `revoke` in it, one intent per request because Polar's update schemas are mutually exclusive, and a subscription with nothing to undo is refused in plain words rather than in Polar's \u2014 plus a customer portal link minted per visit for replacing a card, which is the only way a past-due subscription recovers\n    * Renewals are recorded in the same net currency as every other Polar amount. Polar is merchant of record for a renewal exactly as it is for a one-time sale, so the base plugin's tax stage never runs for one and the ledger holds pre-tax figures throughout; a renewal is therefore reported as Polar's `net_amount`, with its tax beside it as `tax_minor` and the customer's charge as `gross_amount_minor`. A renewal is also read from `order.paid` and never from `order.created` or `subscription.cycled`, both of which fire before the money has arrived \u2014 and a renewal delivery never reports a payment against the original order, which matters because Polar copies a subscription's metadata onto every order it raises and that first order's hash rides along on all of them\n    * Failed renewals, without a failed-payment event to read them from. Polar publishes none; what it publishes is a subscription moving to `past_due` and an unpaid renewal order left open with `next_payment_attempt_at` set to the next retry, so that is what a decline is read from. When the retries run out Polar revokes the subscription and says nothing about why, so the cause is inferred: a subscription nobody cancelled that had entered `past_due` is reported as expired, and everything else \u2014 a scheduled cancellation reaching its date, a stated reason, a merchant's immediate revoke \u2014 as cancelled. A customer who chose to leave is never reported as having lapsed\n    * The webhook setup button now registers the recurring events alongside the payment ones \u2014 `order.updated` and the six `subscription.*` events the provider acts on \u2014 whether or not the store sells plans today, so a store that starts selling them later has nothing to come back and tick. `subscription.updated` and `subscription.cycled` are deliberately not among them: Polar announces most transitions twice, once on the catch-all and once on an event that says which change it was, and acting on both would report every transition twice\n    * The checkout now carries who the customer is, not just their email. `external_customer_id` is the KahunaCart customer id, so a Polar customer can be matched back to this store without guessing at an address; `customer_name` and `customer_billing_address` hand over what the customer already typed. The address matters beyond saving them typing it twice \u2014 Polar is merchant of record, so the country on it is where its tax calculation starts, and two records disagreeing about that is a tax position assembled from a guess. Polar normalises regions to ISO 3166-2 itself (`AZ` becomes `US-AZ`). US customers are still asked to confirm a full address whatever we send, which is Polar's own requirement\n    * Reset support and stale-endpoint reporting for webhook setup, plus the fix that made deletion work at all: a `204 No Content` \u2014 which is exactly what Polar answers a `DELETE` with \u2014 was read as a failed connection, so a deletion that had already succeeded was reported as an outage and the caller would have retried against something already gone. Empty bodies now only mean an outage when the status is 0\n    * Polar registers its own webhook endpoint. One button creates it with this store's URL and all six events the provider actually handles \u2014 `order.paid`, `order.refunded`, `refund.created`, `refund.updated`, `product.created`, `product.updated` \u2014 and keeps the signing secret Polar returns. Polar's `WebhookEndpointCreate` accepts no secret of ours, but the endpoint it answers with carries the one it generated, which removes the copy-paste rather than moving it. Pressing it again adopts the endpoint already registered for that URL instead of creating a second one that would deliver everything twice, and an adopted endpoint subscribed to the wrong events is brought up to date (above). Needs `webhooks:write` on the token\n    * **Check connection** in the KahunaCart admin's Payments section. It walks what a sale walks \u2014 resolve the organization, then find or create the carrier product \u2014 so it exercises the token, the `organizations:read` \/ `products:read` \/ `products:write` scopes, and the account the token is actually pointed at, then reports each of those back. A store with no webhook secret set passes with a warning rather than a clean bill: checkout works perfectly without one and then no paid order ever settles, which is exactly the failure worth catching before a customer finds it\n    * `organization_id` is no longer sent with an organization token (`polar_oat_\u2026`). Polar rejects the combination outright \u2014 \"Setting organization_id is disallowed when using an organization token\" \u2014 so the field is now omitted for those, including when a merchant has configured one, since a configured id cannot override what the token is already scoped to. Personal and OAuth tokens are unaffected and still send the discovered or configured organization. Found by the new connection check on its first real run\n    * Setup is now an access token and a webhook secret. The organization is discovered by asking Polar which organizations the token can act on, so `{mode}_organization_id` can stay empty \u2014 which, with the carrier product already found-or-created on first sale, leaves nothing else to copy out of a dashboard. A token reaching several organizations is the one case that still has to be answered by hand: KahunaCart refuses to guess and names the candidates, because picking wrong would sell through the wrong catalogue, the wrong payouts and the wrong tax registration. A token that cannot list organizations at all is unaffected \u2014 that is what an organization-scoped token looks like, and the product endpoints read the organization off the token. Wants the `organizations:read` scope\n    * `DigitalGoodsOnly` capability: Polar has no shipping, no delivery address and no physical fulfilment, so checkout no longer offers it for a cart containing anything that has to be posted\n    * Initial release: first-party Polar provider for KahunaCart, the reference hosted merchant-of-record + two-way sync implementation\n    * Hosted checkout flow \u2014 `initiatePayment()` creates a Polar checkout session priced with an ad-hoc fixed price equal to the order total, so any cart checks out without per-product mapping\n    * The carrier product is either configured per environment or resolved once from a private \"KahunaCart Order\" product the plugin finds by metadata and creates on first use\n    * `MerchantOfRecord` capability: Polar computes and remits tax at checkout, so KahunaCart's own tax estimate steps aside\n    * Webhook handling with self-contained Standard Webhooks verification (HMAC-SHA256 over `id.timestamp.body`, base64 signatures, 300s replay window in both directions, constant-time comparison, no SDK)\n    * Event mapping for `order.paid`, `refund.created`, `refund.updated`, `product.created`, and `product.updated`\n    * Full and partial refunds through `\/v1\/refunds\/`, with benefit revocation for one-time purchases. Polar's `amount` is net of tax \u2014 its ceiling, `Order.refundable_amount`, is documented as \"net, before taxes\" \u2014 and so is a merchant-of-record order's local total, so the amount passes through unconverted. The order is read first and the request clamped to what Polar says is still refundable, so a Polar-side discount code or a refund issued in Polar's dashboard cannot turn a full refund into a rejected one\n    * Refund and payment webhooks report the net amount to the ledger, with Polar's tax carried beside it as `refunded_tax_minor` \/ `tax_minor` and the customer-facing total as `gross_amount_minor`. Reporting net + tax would overstate a dashboard-issued refund by the tax on a ledger that never held it, and enough of those would push the refunded total past the captured total, mark the order fully refunded, and revoke the customer's downloads with money still owed back\n    * Async refund lifecycle: a refund Polar answers `pending` is reported as pending and settled later by `refund.updated`, keyed on the same refund uuid the refund call returned; `failed` and `canceled` release the held amount for a retry\n    * `order.refunded` is no longer acted on \u2014 it carries the order, so the only reference it can name is the Polar order id, which is the payment row's reference rather than any refund's\n    * Two-way catalog sync: `pushProduct()` creates or updates a Polar product idempotently by `kahunacart_slug` metadata, `pullProducts()` walks the paginated catalog into normalized product arrays\n    * Sandbox\/production toggle with separate access token, webhook secret, organization id, and checkout product per environment; the provider registers only when the active environment's token is set\n    * Unit test suite covering signature verification, event mapping, checkout parameter building, product push\/pull request and response mapping, and transport-failure degradation"}}},"kahunacart-paypal":{"name":"KahunaCart PayPal","version":"1.1.8","description":"PayPal payments for KahunaCart \u2014 hosted checkout, webhooks, and refunds","description_html":"<p>PayPal payments for KahunaCart \u2014 hosted checkout, webhooks, and refunds<\/p>","description_plain":"PayPal payments for KahunaCart \u2014 hosted checkout, webhooks, and refunds","icon":"paypal","author":{"name":"Trilby Media, LLC","email":"hello@trilby.media"},"dependencies":[{"name":"grav","version":">=2.0.0"},{"name":"kahunacart","version":">=1.5.18"}],"compatibility":{"grav":["2.0"]},"homepage":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com","docs":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/docs\/providers\/paypal","keywords":"grav, premium, commerce, ecommerce, payments, paypal, checkout","bugs":"https:\/\/github.com\/getgrav\/grav-premium-issues\/labels\/kahunacart-paypal","license":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/license","tag_name":"1.1.8","date":"2026-10-22T23:33:10Z","release_type":"stable","zipball_url":"https:\/\/getgrav.org\/download\/plugins\/kahunacart-paypal\/1.1.8","repository":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com","premium":{"vendor":"KahunaCart","validator":"kahunacart","license_product":"kahunacart","checkout_url":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/shop\/product\/kahunacart","button":"Buy Now","filename":"grav-plugin-kahunacart-paypal"},"slug":"kahunacart-paypal","install_path":"user\/plugins\/kahunacart-paypal","content_sha256":"288882d16f3a3500dcd3a794effe0b507f63f3034f264ad66fd97efea306e350","changelog":{"1.1.8":{"date":"10\/22\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **Ships its license.** A `LICENSE` file now comes with the plugin, and the blueprint links to the KahunaCart License at kahunacart.com\/license instead of the Grav Premium license\n"},"1.1.7":{"date":"10\/19\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **A settled dispute says how it ended.** PayPal's `CUSTOMER.DISPUTE.RESOLVED` is written on the order as **Dispute won at PayPal** or **Dispute lost at PayPal**, the order's dispute badge follows, and KahunaCart sends its Dispute won or lost message. The customer winning is lost; you winning, the customer withdrawing, or PayPal paying out of its own protection is won. Each outcome is reported once however often PayPal delivers it. The webhook now subscribes to `CUSTOMER.DISPUTE.RESOLVED`; press **Set up webhook** once on an existing store to add it (the Payments card says **Needs attention** until then)\n    * **Check connection says whether PayPal's webhook is right.** After the credentials check out, the driver looks up the webhook at this store's address, changing nothing, and the Payments card warns when there is none, when it is missing events the driver handles, or when its ID is not the one in the settings, which makes every delivery fail verification. **Set up webhook** fixes each of these. A webhook missing only `CUSTOMER.DISPUTE.CREATED` is now that warning rather than a sentence in the check's summary, and the check's rows show the webhook ID on its own\n2. [](#improved)\n    * Needs KahunaCart 1.5.18\n"},"1.1.6":{"date":"10\/13\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **A dispute at PayPal is written on the order and tells you.** When a customer opens a dispute, the order's history says \"Dispute opened at PayPal\" with the amount, PayPal's reason and the date you must answer by, and KahunaCart sends its Dispute opened message to the channels you have ticked. Affiliates holds the partner's commission on it. The order itself is not changed, each dispute is reported once however often PayPal delivers it, and one that arrives before its payment is recorded is asked for again rather than dropped. The webhook now subscribes to `CUSTOMER.DISPUTE.CREATED`; on an existing store, press **Set up webhook** once to add it (**Check connection** still passes until then, and says so)\n"},"1.1.5":{"date":"10\/10\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Tells KahunaCart what PayPal paid out.** On a store that sells in more than one currency, each capture's receivable breakdown is read once on the job worker, and PayPal's exchange rate and the amount it credited to your account after its fee are recorded on the order. A capture still pending is asked about again later, and a payment held first and captured afterwards is followed from its authorization to its capture, and records nothing when the authorization was captured in several parts or the one capture is for another amount or currency than the payment, or does not say which. Needs KahunaCart 1.4.10\n"},"1.1.4":{"date":"10\/04\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **PayPal is no longer offered for a currency it cannot take.** The driver now tells KahunaCart which currencies PayPal's REST API accepts, the 25 on PayPal's currency codes page, so checkout and the pay link leave PayPal out of an order in KWD, INR, ISK or any other currency off that list instead of letting the shopper pick it and fail at the end. BRL, CNY and MYR stay on the list although PayPal takes them only for in-country accounts, because the plugin cannot see where the account is and a store with a domestic account takes them today. **Needs the base plugin at 1.4.0**, which is where the question is asked\n1. [](#improved)\n    * A currency PayPal refuses at pay time is named in the sentence the shopper or merchant reads, \"PayPal does not take payments in BRL\", where it used to say \"this store's currency\", which means nothing on a store that sells in several\n    * The README and the Instructions tab say which currencies PayPal takes, why the three in-country ones are included, and that HUF, JPY and TWD are whole-unit currencies\n"},"1.1.3":{"date":"09\/25\/2026","content":"1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **The download no longer carries PHPUnit.** Every release until now committed a development `vendor\/`: PHPUnit and about thirty packages only the test suite uses, some 1,700 files in a plugin that needs no Composer package at run time. `vendor\/` is now a `--no-dev` install holding just the autoloader, and `psr\/http-message` is declared under `replace` because Grav core already supplies it. A test fails if a development install is ever committed again\n"},"1.1.2":{"date":"09\/23\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * Composer's installed-package record carries the plugin's current package name, `kahunacart\/grav-plugin-kahunacart-paypal`, instead of the old `trilbymedia\/grav-plugin-kahunacart-paypal`\n"},"1.1.1":{"date":"09\/08\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * A **Checkout Label** setting, like every other driver has: the customer-facing name of the payment option, empty for the default \"PayPal\"\n    * An **Instructions** tab in the plugin settings, after General, that walks a merchant through setup in order: the account or sandbox, which credential to copy and from where, saving it, the webhook (button or manual steps with the endpoint URL and the events), test cards or sandbox accounts, and what to check afterwards\n    * **Set up webhook** and **Reset webhook** on the PayPal card in the admin. `PayPalProvider` implements `ProviderWebhookSetupInterface`, so the button registers this store's endpoint on the REST app, subscribes it to the ten events the driver acts on, and writes the webhook ID back into the active mode's field. PayPal's equivalent of a signing secret is that ID, and unlike a Stripe signing secret it can be read back at any time, so the ID is stored whether the webhook was created or adopted \u2014 a store that lost its config gets it back by pressing the button\n    * The button asks PayPal which event types the app is offered before it registers anything. PayPal refuses the whole call over one name it does not recognise, and `event_types[6].name \/ Not a valid event name` gives a merchant no way to tell which of ten names it meant, so an unavailable event is left out and named in the result instead of failing the registration\n    * Pressing it twice adopts the webhook already registered for the URL rather than creating a second one that would deliver everything twice, and patches in any of the ten events it is missing. Events the merchant subscribed it to themselves are kept \u2014 the patch sends the union, not this driver's list\n    * **Reset webhook** deletes the webhook at this exact URL and registers a fresh one. Webhooks on this store's route at a *different* address, which is what a site that moved off a tunnel leaves behind, are reported and never deleted: one REST app is commonly shared between a staging store and a live one\n    * `WEBHOOK_URL_ALREADY_EXISTS` and `WEBHOOK_NUMBER_LIMIT_EXCEEDED` are translated into something a merchant can act on, rather than passed through as PayPal wrote them\n    * **Check connection** on the PayPal card. `PayPalProvider` implements `ProviderConnectionInterface`: it asks PayPal for a token with the saved credentials \u2014 the same call every other request makes first \u2014 reports the app id they belong to, and names the refund or webhook-management scope when an app is missing one. There is no second \"read my merchant account\" call because PayPal has none for a plain REST app\n    * The check reads the configured webhook ID back from PayPal, and fails on a store that has none or has one PayPal does not recognise. That is the state where checkout works, the customer's money moves and nothing settles, and a sandbox webhook ID left in the live fields is the easiest way into it. It also fails, naming them, when the webhook is not subscribed to every event the driver handles\n    * Two events joined the registered set that the docs only ever suggested: `PAYMENT.AUTHORIZATION.CREATED` and `PAYMENT.AUTHORIZATION.VOIDED` are now registered whether or not the store is on manual capture, so switching capture mode later needs nothing added at PayPal\n\n1. [](#improved)\n    * The webhook ID is no longer declared a secret, and the docblock that claimed an attacker could forge with it is corrected. PayPal's verification call is authenticated with the merchant's own OAuth token, so the ID alone forges nothing and gets PayPal to sign nothing \u2014 it is the equivalent of a Stripe endpoint's `we_\u2026` id, which Stripe's driver prints, rather than of a signing secret, which it does not. The driver was calling it a secret and then rendering it on the connection check, in the webhook setup result and on the CLI, and of the two halves that is the one worth keeping: a merchant needs to read the ID back and compare it against their dashboard. `encryptedSettings()` now names the two client secrets and nothing else\n    * The PayPal order carries the cart's own lines and a description, so the approval page and the buyer's own PayPal record name what was bought rather than only an amount \u2014 the record they will look at in six weeks, and the one PayPal shows in a dispute. The lines are only sent when the arithmetic is honest: PayPal validates the breakdown against the charge and refuses the whole order when it does not add up, so a cart discount goes on as `discount` and a cart whose lines come to *less* than the charge \u2014 tax or shipping added on top, and nothing says which \u2014 sends the description alone rather than labelling somebody's VAT as a handling fee\n    * A refusal a shopper or a merchant reads is written for them. The refusals a real store meets \u2014 an unsupported currency, a declined funding instrument, a restricted account, a refund larger than PayPal has left \u2014 are translated into a sentence naming what to do next, and everything else falls back to PayPal's own sentence with the `debug_id` stripped. The debug id is kept on the connection check and the webhook buttons, which are the screens a merchant pastes into a support ticket\n    * Every `invalid()` and `ignored()` carries a reason, in the contract's own vocabulary. A merchant whose orders stop settling used to open the delivery log and find a column of bare `invalid` with nothing to tell \"our webhook ID is wrong\", which is on their own settings screen, from \"somebody found the endpoint and is posting junk\", which needs no action. `no_secret_configured` earns its place on this driver in particular, because the ID is typed by hand\n    * A JSON array at the top level is refused before the verification call rather than after it, so a stranger posting one cannot spend an outbound call to PayPal to find out it was ignored\n    * Payment and refund events carry the payment reference under `payment_ref` and `order_id`, the names the base plugin reads, so a delivery can be joined to its order in the log. Across the September 2026 sandbox run, 228 PayPal deliveries reached a store and three of them carried an order id\n    * An amount PayPal sent that this plugin will not parse is kept on the payload as `amount_unparsable` rather than being dropped. \"PayPal named no amount\" and \"PayPal named one I could not read\" used to be the same answer, after which the row settled at the figure it was opened with and nothing anywhere said so\n    * A settled refund reports what PayPal said it refunded, and keeps PayPal's `seller_payable_breakdown` \u2014 gross, fee, net, and the running total refunded against the capture. Those are the figures a VAT return and a reconciliation want and they cannot be recovered later; the same refund settled by the webhook has always kept them, so a refund recorded different things depending on which leg got there first\n    * The currency on a webhook payload comes back uppercased, which the contract asks for and PayPal happened to satisfy by accident\n    * A capture PayPal refuses names the issue in the message rather than repeating PayPal's field vocabulary, and a refusal that repeats this store's own credentials back at us \u2014 which PayPal does not do today, and is free to start doing \u2014 is replaced rather than rendered\n\n1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **HUF and TWD were charged a hundred times over.** The exponent table was doing two jobs at once: how many decimals PayPal accepts, and how many minor units KahunaCart counts in a major unit. Those are the same number for almost every currency and they are not for these two \u2014 ISO 4217 gives both an exponent of 2, which is what the ledger uses, and PayPal transacts both in whole units. So a store meaning 9,74 Ft asked PayPal for 974 forint, PayPal held 974, and the settlement parsed it back at the same wrong exponent and agreed with itself, so every mismatch check in the base passed and the only place the real figure existed was PayPal's dashboard. Refunds repeated it exactly. There are now two tables and a conversion between them. An amount PayPal cannot represent at all \u2014 1 000,50 Ft, which a percentage coupon on a HUF store produces \u2014 is **refused with a sentence** rather than silently rounded to 1 000 or 1 001\n    * **A refund event named the payment on `capture_id`, and the base plugin only reads `payment_ref`.** The value was always right \u2014 the driver digs the capture id out of the refund's `up` link \u2014 so every PayPal refund event was logged `refund_completed:unmatched`, a refund issued in PayPal's own dashboard never reached the order, and a refund whose response was lost stranded a pending row forever. `payment_ref` is now the name, with `capture_id` beside it because it is PayPal's own word for the same id\n    * **A capture PayPal refused vanished.** `CHECKOUT.ORDER.APPROVED` fires the capture call, and the result was thrown away and the delivery answered a bare `ignored`. An `INSTRUMENT_DECLINED` in the seconds between approval and capture is an ordinary PayPal outcome, and it left an order sitting in the cart table with no failed transaction, no notice, no history, no log line, and no second delivery coming \u2014 because 200 is the one answer that stops PayPal asking again. The three outcomes are now told apart: captured answers `ignored:captured` carrying the PayPal order id; a refusal is reported as the payment failure it is, so the pending row is marked failed and the order's timeline says what happened; and a call that never landed, or a PayPal 5xx, throws, so the base plugin answers 500 and PayPal redelivers\n    * **A capture PayPal refused was believed too readily, and the belief cost the sale.** The fix above read any 4xx as final, and that is not what a 4xx means. Twice in the September 2026 sandbox run PayPal answered one to a capture that the very next call completed normally, on properly approved orders \u2014 and a refusal fails the purchase row, tells the shopper their payment method was turned down, and is answered 200, so PayPal never asks again and a sale that was fine is lost while the store believes it handled everything correctly. Finality is now named rather than inferred: the driver refuses only on the issues PayPal's reference documents as refusals of the payment itself \u2014 the funding instrument, the payer, this store's ability to take the money, the amount or currency, and the order's own state \u2014 and reads anything else as worth another delivery. `PERMISSION_DENIED` is on the retry side on purpose: it means the REST app is missing a scope, which is a mistake in the configuration rather than anything about the payment, and a merchant who fixes it still has three days of PayPal redeliveries in which the money can move\n    * **A refused capture left nothing behind that said what PayPal had answered.** PayPal's status and issue went only into the sentence written for the shopper, which by design carries neither \u2014 so the two refusals above could not be identified afterwards, and the next occurrence would have been no more diagnosable than they were. Both outcomes that are not the money moving now put `HTTP 422, INSTRUMENT_DECLINED` and the debug id on the delivery-log row, which is the table a merchant already opens when an order did not settle. The shopper's sentence is unchanged and still carries no debug id\n    * **PayPal's raw validation text and a debug id reached a shopper.** With the store currency set to one PayPal does not take, a customer read \"If the currency supports decimals, only two decimal place precision is supported. (PayPal debug id: f34810357889a)\" \u2014 a sentence about a JSON field, shown to somebody holding a basket, that was not even right about the cause\n    * `REFUND_AMOUNT_EXCEEDED` and `CAPTURE_FULLY_REFUNDED` are classified as `RefundResult::REASON_EXCEEDS_BALANCE`, so the base plugin's drift line and its notice on the order both fire. Both mean PayPal holds less on the payment than this store thinks it does, which is what a store looks like after somebody refunded in PayPal's dashboard, and it was the only signal a merchant could get that the two ledgers had come apart\n    * The README and the Instructions tab said a refund issued in PayPal's dashboard could not be matched to an order, and that the Webhooks Simulator does not verify. Both were tested in the September 2026 sandbox run and both were wrong: the dashboard refund was the `capture_id` bug above, and the simulator verifies against the real webhook ID and reaches the event handlers \u2014 its limitation is the canned payload. The \"first six events are the minimum\" sentence was wrong too; the minimum for a store that sells and refunds is `CHECKOUT.ORDER.APPROVED`, `PAYMENT.CAPTURE.COMPLETED`, `PAYMENT.CAPTURE.REFUNDED` and `PAYMENT.REFUND.FAILED`, two of which were outside the six. The README also claimed KahunaCart >= 1.0.0 where the blueprint says 1.1.0, and the blueprint is right\n    * A refund now asks PayPal for the whole object back (`Prefer: return=representation`). PayPal answers a refund with an id and a status and no amount at all, and it silently **clamps** a refund larger than the capture has left rather than refusing it \u2014 so a $5.00 refund against $3.00 remaining came back `201` and the ledger recorded $5.00 as money the merchant never paid back. The row now says what PayPal actually returned, and the seller breakdown arrives in the same response\n    * Settlements in HUF and TWD record their amount again. The read path was parsing against the wire exponent \u2014 how many decimals PayPal accepts **from us**, which is none for those two \u2014 but PayPal is under no such rule and echoes them back with two, so `\"1000.00\"` was refused and a settled forint order recorded no amount at all. Reading is not writing: what counts is the precision the string actually carries, and precision beyond what the store holds is accepted only when it is all zeros, so `19.99` on a yen order is still refused rather than silently rounded\n"},"1.1.0":{"date":"09\/01\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * Two-step capture. `capture_mode: manual` creates the PayPal order with `intent: AUTHORIZE`, so the customer's approval places a hold and the order stands at `authorized` until the merchant presses Capture or Void. `PayPalProvider` implements `AuthorizeCaptureInterface`: `capture()` posts to `\/v2\/payments\/authorizations\/{id}\/capture` with `final_capture` so a partial capture releases the rest rather than leaving it held for another three days, `void()` releases the authorization, and `completeAuthorize()` reads the order PayPal names on the return URL and reports what its authorization actually says\n    * An approval on a manual store calls `\/v2\/checkout\/orders\/{id}\/authorize` rather than `\/capture`, because an order created to be authorized cannot be captured directly and PayPal refuses the attempt. The call is idempotent the same two ways the capture already was \u2014 `PayPal-Request-Id` collapses concurrent retries, and a redelivery that arrives after the first one comes back `ORDER_ALREADY_AUTHORIZED`, which means the hold exists\n    * `PAYMENT.AUTHORIZATION.CREATED` and `PAYMENT.AUTHORIZATION.VOIDED` are translated, so a hold and its release settle the ledger whether they happened here, in PayPal's own dashboard, or by expiry. The authorization carries what PayPal actually held, which is the figure the merchant captures against\n    * A capture PayPal answers `PENDING` is reported as a failure rather than a sale, because a capture under review has moved no money and the completion event settles it when it does\n    * A reference that names the order rather than the authorization is resolved rather than refused, which covers a merchant who captures before the authorization webhook has landed\n    * `encryptedSettings()` names both client secrets and both webhook ids, so KahunaCart stores them encrypted against a key that lives in the store's database and not in any file. The client ids are deliberately excluded: they are not secrets, PayPal prints them into its own SDK tag, and a merchant needs to read one back to check it against their dashboard\n    * The README says plainly what this driver does not claim. PayPal has Vault v3 and it has Smart Payment Buttons, and neither `Tokenization` nor `ExpressPay` is declared because neither is implemented \u2014 KahunaCart draws its buttons from these flags, so a flag claiming something that is not there would put a control in the admin that could never work\n"},"1.0.0":{"date":"08\/31\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * Initial release: first-party PayPal provider for KahunaCart\n    * Hosted redirect flow on Orders v2 \u2014 `initiatePayment()` creates a PayPal order and redirects to the approval page, with the transaction hash on `custom_id` and the order hash on the purchase unit's `reference_id`\n    * Capture runs from the webhook: `CHECKOUT.ORDER.APPROVED` triggers `POST \/v2\/checkout\/orders\/{id}\/capture`, and the resulting `PAYMENT.CAPTURE.COMPLETED` event completes the order \u2014 no return-URL handler required\n    * Webhook verification through PayPal's `verify-webhook-signature` endpoint, with missing transmission headers, an unconfigured webhook ID, and cert URLs off PayPal's own domains rejected before any call goes out\n    * Event mapping for `CHECKOUT.ORDER.APPROVED`, `PAYMENT.CAPTURE.COMPLETED`, `PAYMENT.CAPTURE.DENIED`, `PAYMENT.CAPTURE.DECLINED`, `PAYMENT.CAPTURE.REVERSED`, `CHECKOUT.ORDER.DECLINED`, `PAYMENT.CAPTURE.REFUNDED`, `PAYMENT.REFUND.FAILED`, and `PAYMENT.REFUND.PENDING`\n    * Full and partial refunds through `\/v2\/payments\/captures\/{id}\/refund`\n    * Async refund lifecycle: a refund PayPal answers `PENDING` is reported as pending and settled later by `PAYMENT.CAPTURE.REFUNDED` or `PAYMENT.REFUND.FAILED`, keyed on the same refund id the refund call returned; `CANCELLED` and `FAILED` on submission are refused rather than counted as refunded\n    * `PAYMENT.CAPTURE.REVERSED` stays a payment failure rather than a refund failure \u2014 PayPal reversing a capture is money pulled back over the store's head, not the failure of a refund the store submitted\n    * OAuth2 client-credentials tokens, cached per request lifecycle and never persisted\n    * Idempotency keys on both order creation and capture, so a redelivered webhook cannot double-charge\n    * Sandbox\/live mode toggle with separate credential sets; the provider registers only when the active mode's client id and secret are both set\n    * Exact integer and string conversion between minor units and PayPal's decimal amounts at each currency's own precision \u2014 zero-decimal (JPY, KRW\u2026), two-decimal, and three-decimal (KWD, BHD\u2026) currencies all convert correctly, with HUF and TWD following PayPal's whole-unit handling rather than ISO 4217, and unrecognised formats rejected rather than rounded\n    * Unit test suite covering webhook verification, event mapping, capture-on-approval, order and refund body building, money conversion, and transport failure"}}},"kahunacart-dummy":{"name":"KahunaCart Dummy","version":"1.2.9","description":"Test payment provider for KahunaCart \u2014 no money moves; the reference implementation for provider developers","description_html":"<p>Test payment provider for KahunaCart \u2014 no money moves; the reference implementation for provider developers<\/p>","description_plain":"Test payment provider for KahunaCart \u2014 no money moves; the reference implementation for provider developers","icon":"flask","author":{"name":"Trilby Media, LLC","email":"hello@trilby.media"},"dependencies":[{"name":"grav","version":">=2.0.0"},{"name":"kahunacart","version":">=1.4.0"}],"compatibility":{"grav":["2.0"]},"homepage":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com","docs":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/docs\/providers\/dummy","keywords":"grav, commerce, ecommerce, payments, testing, development, provider","bugs":"https:\/\/github.com\/kahunacart\/grav-plugin-kahunacart-dummy\/issues","license":"MIT","tag_name":"1.2.9","date":"2026-10-14T19:21:27Z","release_type":"stable","zipball_url":"https:\/\/getgrav.org\/download\/plugins\/kahunacart-dummy\/1.2.9","repository":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com","premium":{"vendor":"KahunaCart","validator":"kahunacart","license_product":"kahunacart","checkout_url":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/shop\/product\/kahunacart","button":"Buy Now","filename":"grav-plugin-kahunacart-dummy"},"slug":"kahunacart-dummy","install_path":"user\/plugins\/kahunacart-dummy","content_sha256":"5a18c18bf7438de62f9ef76cc7e6f814670572621755ab48ded2fc8caa2e481b","changelog":{"1.2.9":{"date":"10\/14\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Simulated card readers.** With KahunaCart 1.5.7 the test provider declares `card_present` and offers two simulated readers, so a counter sale can take a card on a reader with no hardware and no account. A payment approves after a short delay you can set, an amount ending in `.05` is declined, and one ending in `.09` times out, so every path a till has to handle can be tried. Any code starting `sim-` pairs another reader, a reader takes one payment at a time, and a cancel is confirmed at once while the payment is pending and answers too late once it is paid. On an older KahunaCart the readers are simply not offered. See [Simulated card readers](README.md#simulated-card-readers)\n"},"1.2.8":{"date":"10\/11\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **The test provider's subscriptions travel in a store bundle.** With KahunaCart 1.5.0's export and restore, `kahunacart_dummy_subscriptions` is carried as the `dummy.subscriptions` set and comes back on restore, linked to the restored orders and variants. It is the reference example for an add-on registering on `onKahunaCartRegisterExports`\n    * **An optional test key with a connection check**, stored encrypted, so a store bundle has a provider key to carry in tests\n1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **Works with a shop mounted at the site root.** With KahunaCart's `route: '\/'` (core 1.5.0) the simulated subscription webhook posts to `\/webhook\/dummy` rather than `\/\/webhook\/dummy`\n"},"1.2.7":{"date":"10\/04\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * Document that the simulator follows the subscription\u2019s saved Added or Included fee choice\n    * **The Test Provider's agreements table has a search box.** Type a few words and the agreements that do not hold all of them drop away as you type, in any order and whatever the case, looking at the agreement, the customer, the amount and the status. The whole list is on screen, so it searches in the browser. Needs KahunaCart 1.3.11 or later, which draws the box; on an older one there is no box, as before\n    * **Make the Test Provider refuse a currency.** New setting **Only Take These Currencies** (`only_currencies`, empty by default, which takes every currency as before). With codes in it, an order in any other currency is not offered Test Payment, and a store with nothing else on offer for that currency shows checkout's \"No payment method is available for orders in EUR.\" A payment posted by hand for such an order is refused too, with \"Test Payment does not take EUR\". So a test store can see what its shoppers see when a provider can't take their currency\n    * **Make a payment or refund report a different currency.** New setting **Report a Different Currency** (`report_currency`, off by default). Every payment and refund webhook the provider receives then says its amount is in that currency, whatever the order is in, so a test store can watch KahunaCart's currency mismatch handling on a live site: the payment is recorded for the amount it was opened with, what the provider reported stays on the transaction, and the store owner gets a notice. Only for testing that case\n\n1. [](#improved)\n    * The first hosted subscription payment records the order\u2019s payable total, including tax and payment-method fees, and shows it separately from the recurring amount\n    * **Payment webhooks say which currency their amount is in.** A posted `payment_completed`, `payment_authorized` or `refund_completed` that carries a `currency` has it passed on, in capitals, the way a real platform reports it. Without the key nothing changes. Needs KahunaCart 1.4.0, which compares it with the order's currency\n    * **The test pages and the agreements table show each amount in its own currency.** The hosted checkout, the billing portal and `bin\/plugin kahunacart-dummy subscription` printed every amount with two decimals, so a yen or dinar plan read wrong. They now use the currency's own decimal places, and the admin table prints each agreement in its own currency\n    * **Needs KahunaCart 1.4.0.** The provider now uses the currency check that arrives with it, so the plugin asks for 1.4.0 or later\n\n1. [](#bugfix)\n    * Subscription renewals and declined renewal attempts include the payment method's fee and its tax when Subscriptions supplies the renewal order total; the hosted checkout and billing portal show that payable recurring amount\n    * **Sorting the Test Provider's agreements by Amount keeps currencies apart.** The table sorts currency codes first and amounts within each currency, so yen, dollars and dinars are never compared as one amount\n"},"1.2.6":{"date":"09\/30\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **The download no longer carries the plugin's tests.** `tests\/` and `phpunit.xml` stay in the repository and out of the release zip, 27 files fewer on every install. Nothing the plugin runs is affected\n"},"1.2.5":{"date":"09\/30\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **The Test Provider's agreements table sorts by its column headings.** Press a heading to sort by it, and press it again to turn the order round. The whole list is on screen, so it sorts in the browser. Needs KahunaCart 1.3.8 or later, which draws the headings; on an older one the headings are plain as before\n"},"1.2.4":{"date":"09\/29\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Act as a merchant of record, for testing a store that will sell through one.** New setting **Act as Merchant of Record** (`merchant_of_record`, off by default). On, the provider declares the `MerchantOfRecord` capability, as Polar does, so a store rehearsing on it behaves the way it will in production: an order paid through it carries no local tax, and on a store where it's the only payment method on offer the cart and checkout say \"Any tax is added when you pay.\" instead of quoting a tax (that part needs the base plugin at 1.3.6). It still charges nobody. Off, nothing changes, because a store testing ordinary payments has to keep computing its own tax. A local site can turn it on from its `.env` without touching tracked config; see [Acting as a merchant of record](README.md#acting-as-a-merchant-of-record)\n"},"1.2.3":{"date":"09\/25\/2026","content":"1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **The download no longer carries PHPUnit.** Every release until now committed a development `vendor\/`: PHPUnit and about thirty packages only the test suite uses, some 1,700 files in a plugin that needs no Composer package at run time. `vendor\/` is now a `--no-dev` install holding just the autoloader, and `psr\/http-message` is declared under `replace` because Grav core already supplies it. A test fails if a development install is ever committed again\n"},"1.2.2":{"date":"09\/23\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * Composer's installed-package record carries the plugin's current package name, `kahunacart\/grav-plugin-kahunacart-dummy`, instead of the old `trilbymedia\/grav-plugin-kahunacart-dummy`\n"},"1.2.1":{"date":"09\/16\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Test express pay actually pays now.** The button drew itself to the store's express style and then linked to `\/checkout`, which on the cart page was a redundant link and on the checkout page was a button that did nothing at all \u2014 a placeholder where a wallet button goes, and a broken button is worse than no button. Pressing it now opens a fake wallet sheet drawn by this plugin's own script and stylesheet, over the page, looking like a wallet sheet rather than like part of the shop: the store's name, the total, a saved contact and address, the store's **real** delivery offers priced for that address with the first one chosen and a price on each, and one Pay button. Choosing a different method reprices the whole order \u2014 postage, tax and all \u2014 because the sheet asks the store rather than adding numbers up itself. **The purchase behind it is not faked.** Pay posts to this plugin's own endpoint, which places the cart through the base plugin's two new express entry points: the sheet's address, the chosen method priced by the store's own shipping engine with carrier rates in it where the shipping add-on is installed, the taxes, the plugin veto, this provider's test payment with whatever `outcome` is configured, and the store's own receipt at the end. An order bought this way is indistinguishable in the admin from one bought on the checkout page. **A store that cannot deliver to the wallet address gets a sentence and no Pay button**, for the same reason the placeholder had to go. The saved details are settings \u2014 **Plugins \u2192 KahunaCart Dummy \u2192 Express Pay** \u2014 defaulting to a US address any US shipping zone will match, so a store selling elsewhere can point them somewhere it covers. **Nothing about a payment passes through any of it**: no card field, no token field and no wallet payload in the button, the sheet or the endpoint, and the sheet prints `Test wallet \u00b7 no charge` where a real one would print a card. With scripting off the button is still a link to checkout. The whole flow is four short files, which is the point \u2014 this is the reference implementation, and a driver author should be able to read it start to finish. **Needs the base plugin at 1.2.10**, which is where `quoteExpressOrder()` and `placeExpressOrder()` come from\n\n1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **The test express button no longer vanishes on a dark storefront.** It painted itself `#111111` on every theme but `light` and `outline`, so on the dark side of a theme like Kahuna it was a near-black rectangle on a near-black page \u2014 you could tab to it, and that was about the only way to find it. The base plugin's `express.theme` has grown an `auto` value and made it the default, and this button now draws that value the way the provider contract asks a driver with no SDK of its own to draw it: the fill, the label and the edge come from `--kahunacart-express-auto-bg`, `--kahunacart-express-auto-fg` and `--kahunacart-express-auto-border` on the region wrapper, which the storefront stylesheet resolves from the page's own ink and ground. Dark button with a light label on a light page, light button with a dark label on a dark one, and it flips with the page rather than with a setting. `dark` and `light` are unchanged and still absolute \u2014 a merchant who picked one gets exactly that \u2014 and `outline` now follows the page as well: the fill stays transparent, and its border and its label come from `--kahunacart-express-auto-bg`, the page's own foreground, because a black outline on a dark page is as gone as a black slab. This provider is also the reference implementation, so the whole change is three short match blocks anybody writing a real driver can copy. **Needs the base plugin at 1.2.10**, which is where `auto` and the wrapper's colours come from\n"},"1.2.0":{"date":"09\/08\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **The Test Provider tab has a Settings screen, and it is the plugin's own form.** Every switch this provider has is a blueprint field, and until now reading one meant leaving the KahunaCart admin for the Grav admin's plugin list \u2014 the tab that drives the agreements could not tell you what `capture_mode` was set to, and the banner about unsigned webhooks sent you somewhere else to fix it. The tab now has two screens, **Agreements** and **Settings**, with Settings on a `settings` sub-route of its own so a reload and the Back button land where the address says, and KahunaCart draws the plugin's blueprint form on the second one: the manifest says `settings_plugin`, the screen renders the slot the base plugin forwards into, and the header's Save button saves it. Following **Plugins \u2192 KahunaCart Dummy** in the Grav admin lands here too, and the unsigned-webhooks banner now points at the tab beside it rather than at another page. Against an older base plugin the screen prints one line pointing at the Grav admin's page, so nothing is stranded\n    * **`bin\/plugin kahunacart reset` now empties the fake platform's ledger.** Every row in `kahunacart_dummy_subscriptions` was made by somebody testing, which makes the whole table test data by definition \u2014 and until now a pre-launch reset left it behind, so the fake platform still held live agreements for orders and customers that were gone and the next worker tick renewed them. The `ref` is derived from the order rather than random, so a leftover would also collide with the first real subscription that reused the number. The table is declared on the base plugin's new `onKahunaCartRegisterResetTables` under both the orders and the customers scope, so either flag takes it, and it appears in the plan the command prints with its row count before it asks. Deliberately not behind this plugin's `enabled` switch: the provider ships switched off, and a guard would hide the fake agreements on exactly the store that has finished testing\n"},"1.1.0":{"date":"09\/01\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **Cancel at period end is a switch.** The Test provider section drew it as a bare checkbox; it is the same track-and-knob switch the base admin uses for a standing on\/off setting\n\n1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **The provider declares the features it understands.** Every demo store has this provider installed, and it declared nothing, so Order versioning and the Catalog index could not be switched on anywhere it was present: the base plugin takes an add-on's silence as \"not ready\". It now declares `order_versioning`, `order_status_registry` and `catalog_index`, which is honest \u2014 it reads no order line, no status string and no catalog table; it is handed a payment context and answers with a result\n\n1. [](#new)\n    * Two-step capture: `capture_mode: manual` makes checkout hold the money instead of taking it, and the order stands at the `authorized` payment status until the merchant presses Capture or Void. `DummyProvider` implements `AuthorizeCaptureInterface` in full \u2014 `authorize()`, `capture()`, `void()` and `completeAuthorize()` \u2014 so a store can walk the whole lifecycle without an account anywhere\n    * `authorize_outcome: captured` reports a capture the platform made despite being asked to hold. Not a simulated bug but a documented rule with a real cause: some payment methods cannot be authorized without being taken, the contract asks a driver to say so in the returned status rather than hide the method, and a store that has never met the branch will meet it the first time somebody pays with one of them\n    * `capture_outcome: fail` and `void_outcome: fail` refuse those operations with fixed messages, so the failure paths \u2014 an authorization that stays live, money that stays held \u2014 can be read before either button is trusted\n    * `payment_authorized` and `payment_voided` webhook actions, so a hold and its release can be driven from curl exactly as a payment and a refund already could. A `payment_completed` quoting a live authorization is read by the store as a capture the merchant made in the platform's own dashboard\n    * Saved payment methods: `TokenizationInterface` with `describePaymentMethod()` and `deletePaymentMethod()`, and all three ways a method reaches the store \u2014 attached to a payment result with `save_method`, carried on a webhook body's `payment_method` block, or asked for through `storePaymentMethod()`. What is kept is a reference and four display fields, and nothing that resembles card data can be stored at all\n    * `token_outcome: forgotten` answers that the platform no longer holds a method, so a stale row is retired rather than offered; `token_outcome: refuse_delete` refuses the removal, which is the loud failure that stops a customer being told a card is gone while the platform can still charge it\n    * Off-session charging: `OffSessionChargeInterface::charge()` folds the caller's idempotency key into the reference it answers with, so a retried renewal produces one reference and one charge. It is the rule a driver most often gets wrong and it costs somebody two charges on one statement\n    * An express-pay button, `dummy-express`, registered into both express zones on `onKahunaCartRegisterExpressButtons`. It draws to exactly the height, radius, theme and minimum width the store's `express.*` settings ask for, so a theme's express region can be laid out and checked against the style contract before a real wallet button exists. Nothing about a payment passes through it: no form, no input, no token field\n    * `captureMode()` answers from the plugin's own config, which is the exception the contract allows and not the rule it warns against \u2014 the warning is about a store-wide key that overrides what a provider says, and this platform genuinely holds money either way\n    * `encryptedSettings()` declared explicitly as nothing, because this provider has no credentials: no API key, no secret key, and deliberately no webhook signing secret. Its docblock carries the two lines a real driver writes instead\n    * Capabilities grew to match: `Authorize`, `Capture`, `Void`, `Tokenization`, `OffSessionCharge` and `ExpressPay` are declared alongside the refund and subscription flags, each because the fake platform genuinely does the thing the flag means. `HostedCheckout`, `MerchantOfRecord` and `DigitalGoodsOnly` are still not declared, for the same reason as before\n    * README sections on two-step capture, saved payment methods and the express button, each with the curl bodies that drive them\n"},"1.0.0":{"date":"08\/31\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * Initial release: free test payment provider for KahunaCart, and the reference implementation for provider developers\n    * Config-selectable checkout behavior \u2014 `complete_paid` (instant capture), `complete_pending` (offline-style), `redirect` (bounces to the return URL and waits for a webhook), and `fail` (a fixed simulated decline)\n    * Unsigned webhook endpoint mapping `payment_completed`, `payment_failed`, and `refund_completed` bodies to the matching `WebhookResult`, so the whole payment lifecycle can be driven from curl\n    * Unsigned HTTP posts gated behind both the plugin's `enabled` and a separate `allow_webhooks` switch, each shipping off; a post that arrives while either is off is rejected with a 400\n    * Refunds and partial refunds always succeed, with references derived from the payment reference so an admin refund and a refund webhook agree on one ledger row\n    * Deterministic transaction references derived from the base plugin's transaction hash \u2014 stable across a retry, reproducible in a test, printable in a curl command\n    * Unit test suite covering the outcome matrix, webhook mapping, webhook gating, and refunds\n    * Async refund lifecycle demonstration: `refund_outcome: pending` makes refunds return pending so the ledger flow can be driven end to end, and the webhook endpoint accepts `refund_failed` to exercise the rejection branch\n    * Recurring billing: declares `ProviderCapability::Subscriptions` and implements `RecurringBillingInterface`, keeping its agreements in a table of its own (`kahunacart_dummy_subscriptions`, registered through KahunaCart's migrator) so a whole subscription lifecycle can be walked without a real provider\n    * A hosted checkout page of its own at `\/kahunacart-dummy\/subscribe\/{ref}` \u2014 deliberately outside the storefront and outside the theme, because that is what a hosted checkout is. Pressing Pay fires one webhook carrying the order's payment and the agreement's activation together, the way a first invoice really arrives; Cancel changes nothing. An `outcome: fail` store declines recurring checkout as it declines any other, writing no row and emitting no event\n    * Simulation controls for every event a real platform would eventually send \u2014 `renew`, `fail`, `past-due`, `cancel` (now or at period end), `expire`, `pause`, `resume`, `method-updated` \u2014 as `bin\/plugin kahunacart-dummy subscription <ref> <control>` and as buttons on a new Test Provider tab in the KahunaCart admin, gated on `kahunacart.settings`\n    * Every control delivers through this store's own `POST {route}\/webhook\/dummy` handler and into the same webhook log, so the real dispatch runs and nothing is a private path around the endpoint. In-process by default so it works from cron and CI, and so it needs no `allow_webhooks`; `--url` posts over HTTP for the times when the delivery itself is what is being tested, and meets the gate like any other post\n    * A read-only billing portal at `\/kahunacart-dummy\/portal\/{customerRef}`, so the account page's \"Update payment method\" button can be demonstrated on a store with no payment account anywhere\n    * Billing-calendar arithmetic that clamps rather than overflows: 31 January plus a month is 28 (or 29) February, and periods advance from where the last one closed rather than from the clock, so an event fired late still bills the period it was for\n    * Refuses simulated events a real platform could not have sent \u2014 renewing a cancelled agreement, resuming one with nothing to undo \u2014 so a test can only produce an event stream a store could really have received\n    * `resume` doubles as the uncancel, matching the real drivers and the base contract, which has no `uncancelSubscription()`. On an `active`, `trialing` or `past_due` agreement scheduled to stop at the end of the period it clears the schedule, leaves the status alone, and delivers the `resumed` event with `raw.uncanceled = true`; on a paused one it resumes billing as before; on one that is both, it undoes both. It is refused only when there is genuinely nothing to undo\n    * A `payment_completed` webhook now reports `amount_minor` \u2014 what this provider is pretending to have taken \u2014 and the store settles the pending ledger row at it rather than at the amount the row was opened with. The activation delivery a trial produces carries a `0`, so a free first period records no revenue; a body that names no amount leaves the key off and settles exactly as it always did\n\n1. [](#bugfix)\n    * The hosted subscription checkout and every simulation control now work on a store with `allow_webhooks` off. Those deliveries never leave the process, so they are marked with a PSR-7 request attribute the provider trusts by construction: a marker no HTTP request can carry, because headers arrive over the wire and attributes do not. Pressing \"Pay and subscribe\" on a default store used to move the provider's own row to `active` and then have its activation thrown away at the gate, leaving the store's subscription `incomplete`, the order in the cart, and the shopper on \"Processing your payment\u2026\" forever. `allow_webhooks` still gates unsigned posts arriving over HTTP, `--url` deliveries included, exactly as before\n    * The hosted checkout page names the plan after the product rather than printing `Variant #21`, adding the variant's own title when it says something the product's does not. A variant that cannot be resolved still falls back to its id"}}},"kahunacart-licenses":{"name":"KahunaCart Licenses","version":"1.1.6","description":"License keys for KahunaCart \u2014 issuance, activation seats, updates entitlement, and a public validation API","description_html":"<p>License keys for KahunaCart \u2014 issuance, activation seats, updates entitlement, and a public validation API<\/p>","description_plain":"License keys for KahunaCart \u2014 issuance, activation seats, updates entitlement, and a public validation API","icon":"key","author":{"name":"Trilby Media, LLC","email":"hello@trilby.media"},"dependencies":[{"name":"grav","version":">=2.0.0"},{"name":"kahunacart","version":">=1.4.4"}],"compatibility":{"grav":["2.0"]},"homepage":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/add-ons\/license-keys","docs":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/docs\/selling\/licenses","keywords":"grav, premium, commerce, ecommerce, licensing, license-keys, activation, updates","bugs":"https:\/\/github.com\/getgrav\/grav-premium-issues\/labels\/kahunacart-licenses","license":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/license","tag_name":"1.1.6","date":"2026-10-26T03:50:50Z","release_type":"stable","zipball_url":"https:\/\/getgrav.org\/download\/plugins\/kahunacart-licenses\/1.1.6","repository":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/add-ons\/license-keys","premium":{"vendor":"KahunaCart","validator":"kahunacart","license_product":"kahunacart-licenses","checkout_url":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/shop\/product\/kahunacart-licenses","button":"Buy Now","filename":"grav-plugin-kahunacart-licenses"},"slug":"kahunacart-licenses","install_path":"user\/plugins\/kahunacart-licenses","content_sha256":"24c506ffc92431c5b87300c8608dfb898f89f6d2727899465403443c189e2954","changelog":{"1.1.6":{"date":"10\/25\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **Buttons in these emails stay readable on a light button color.** The writing on a button was always white, so a store that picked a light Email Button Color got white on yellow. It now turns dark when the button is light, the same as KahunaCart's own emails. Needs KahunaCart 1.6.5 for the dark writing; an older KahunaCart keeps white\n"},"1.1.5":{"date":"10\/23\/2026","content":"1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **Which builds a download may have is decided by its own order's key.** The updates window a download was judged on came from any key matching the order's customer record or address, and a guest checkout files an order under whatever customer the typed address belongs to. Somebody holding an old grant could check out with a stranger's address and have the stranger's wider window decide which releases they could download. Only the keys the grant's own order bought count now. Every live grant has a key on its own order, so nobody loses a build they had\n    * **An add-on only goes on a key the buyer named or the order minted.** When an add-on line named no usable key (its \"license in this cart\" was taken out of the basket, or its rule changed after checkout), issuance put it on the newest key matching the order's customer or address. A guest who typed somebody else's address got their add-on on that person's key, and the add-on's download (a theme's ready-to-run store, say) with that person's key written in. A storefront or agent order now gets no guessed key: the problem is recorded on the order for the merchant. Orders staff entered in the admin or at a till keep the old fallback\n    * **\"The license in this cart\" has to be a line that mints one.** Checkout, the cart's \"lost its license\" note and the buy form's \"the one in this cart\" option now ask the same question issuance does: does the basket hold a line for that product with an enabled license rule? A base product sold with its rule switched off doesn't count, and owning a key already no longer lets the line through, so the buyer is asked to name a key (the buy form lists a signed-in buyer's keys)\n    * **A disabled variant rule no longer hides the product rule behind it.** The checkout gate read a line's rule including disabled ones, while issuance uses only enabled rules, variant before product. An add-on variant with its own rule switched off skipped every check at checkout and was then sold under the product's add-on rule. Both read the rule the same way now\n2. [](#improved)\n    * A key issued by hand with no order doesn't decide which builds a download page offers anymore, since downloads are judged only on their own order's keys. A grant whose order holds no key is left as the base plugin offers it, as for any keyless buyer\n"},"1.1.4":{"date":"10\/23\/2026","content":"1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **A package is stamped only with its own order's key.** A download looked for the buyer's keys by their customer record and their address as well as by the order. A guest checkout files an order under whatever customer the typed address belongs to, so somebody who bought a product with another customer's address could get that customer's key, and the add-ons it covers, written into their zip. Only the keys the download's own order bought or widened are written now. When the order has no key yet the package goes out with every gated folder removed and no key, as it did for a buyer with no key\n    * **An add-on's package carries the key it was bought for, and keeps the add-ons that key pays for.** A product sold as an add-on (a theme whose package is a whole store, say) has no key of its own, and its download looked for one anyway. It found none, so every gated folder came out of the zip, even ones the buyer had paid for, and no `licenses.yaml` was written. The download now uses the key the add-on's order widened, or the key bought in the same cart while the grant is still queued. The stamp and the gates are still the add-on's own rule\n    * **A stamped add-on package lists the right products.** The key's own product heads the `{#products}` list, with the add-on's catalog name and its included packages after it, so a theme rule that includes `vellum` puts a `vellum:` line in the file for Grav's package manager\n2. [](#improved)\n    * **A key can fetch the releases of an add-on granted against it.** The public `\/release` endpoint served only the key's own product. It now also serves the release files of every add-on on the key, judged by the key's updates window, and stamps them by the add-on's own rule\n    * **A key's card offers the packages of add-ons bought on later orders.** The downloads on a license card came only from the order the key was sold on. A theme bought later is on its own order, and its package now shows on the card too. Two packages that share a file name and a title are both listed\n    * **The `save_product_rule` tool takes `stamps`.** The REST route already accepted stamped downloads; the tool's schema now lists them, so an assistant can write a rule's stamps and gates\n"},"1.1.3":{"date":"10\/22\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **No empty Licenses tab.** On KahunaCart 1.6.0 with Hide Empty Tabs on (the default), a customer who owns no key doesn't get a Licenses tab in their account; the page still opens at its address. Older KahunaCart shows the tab as before\n"},"1.1.2":{"date":"10\/22\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Themes can show a product's editions, a customer's keys at a glance, and check a key from the page.** `kahunacart_license_rule(product)` answers a licensed product's editions (one per sellable variant, with its live price and what it includes in plain words: \"5 seats\", \"Yours to keep\", \"12 months of updates\"), the renewal with its on-time price, the add-ons that attach to it, and the packages a key opens. `kahunacart_licenses_overview()` gives a signed-in customer's keys, how many are active, expiring soon and expired, and a link to the Licenses tab. `kahunacart_licenses_api()` gives the absolute addresses of this site's public validate and latest endpoints. See `docs\/storefront.md`\n2. [](#bugfix)\n    * **The receipt's licence panel fits a phone screen.** The list of keys sized its column to its widest card, and a long path in the instructions (`user\/plugins\/kahunacart\/docs\/hardening.md`) had nowhere to break, so on a phone the receipt ran past the edge of the screen and scrolled sideways. The column now shrinks to the screen, and code and links in the instructions wrap. The same list on the customer's account page gets the same fix\n3. [](#improved)\n    * **Ships its license.** A `LICENSE` file now comes with the plugin, and the blueprint links to the KahunaCart License at kahunacart.com\/license instead of the Grav Premium license\n\n"},"1.1.1":{"date":"10\/19\/2026","content":"1. [](#bugfix)\n    * The renewal reminder sweep runs once an hour, as documented. It ran on every worker pass, about once a minute, because each sweep was booked under that hour's own key and the queue only holds a key back while a job is still waiting; it is now booked for the top of the next hour under one key. Nobody got a reminder twice either way: each reminder is recorded once when it is sent\n"},"1.1.0":{"date":"10\/19\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Renewal reminders.** A key's owner gets an email as the key's updates end or its license expires, on a schedule you set under **Licenses \u2192 Settings \u2192 Renewals**: a list of reminders you can add to and remove from, each a number of days before or after the date (30 before, 7 before and 1 after to start). Only keys a renewal product renews get one, never a revoked or suspended key or one a live subscription is renewing, and only the latest reminder due is sent, so switching this on doesn't send two emails at once or write to everyone whose key lapsed last year. Each reminder goes once however many workers run, and renewing starts next year's fresh. The email is **Renewal reminder** under Customer on the Notifications screen, appears in the email log, and its template can be overridden in a theme\n    * **The reminder's Renew button signs the owner in.** It opens the renewal product with their key already picked, so a customer whose price needs them signed in (a group price list, like a founders' rate) gets it without knowing to sign in first. It goes through KahunaCart's own sign-in link, so the confirm button, two-factor and disabled accounts all work as they do for any sign-in link. The link dies when the key is renewed or changes hands, and expires a month after the date\n    * **Late renewals keep the anniversary.** New setting **Late Renewals Start**: **From the anniversary** (the default) runs a late renewal from the date the old year ended, so updates that lapsed in September and were renewed in March run to next September; a key years behind gets the anniversary year it is in now. **From the day they pay** keeps the old behaviour. Early renewals are unchanged\n    * **On-time renewal discount.** New **On-Time Discount** and **Grace Days** settings take a percentage off a renewal paid before the key's date or within the grace days after it, shown as its own line on the cart and receipt. Off by default (0%), and never taken off a line a price list already prices\n2. [](#improved)\n    * The account's **Renew** link opens the renewal product with that key already picked in the key list\n"},"1.0.19":{"date":"10\/13\/2026","content":"1. [](#bugfix)\n    * The PHP licence client (`clients\/php\/KahunaCartLicense.php`) works on hosts that disable `getmypid()`. It named its cache temp file after the process id, which is a fatal error there; the name now ends in a random string\n"},"1.0.18":{"date":"10\/13\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Hear when a paid renewal or add-on does not land.** **A paid renewal or add-on did not land** (high) goes to your channels when a customer has paid and the line could not be applied: no key given, a key that is not yours, for another product or revoked, or no license for the add-on to attach to. Once per order line, with the order, the product and the reason, never the key\n    * **Know when a license runs out of seats.** **A license is out of seats** (low, switched off until you turn it on) says when someone tries to activate a license with every seat in use, once per license per day, with the product and the customer's name\n"},"1.0.17":{"date":"10\/11\/2026","content":"1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **Works with a shop mounted at the site root.** With KahunaCart's `route: '\/'` (core 1.5.0) the `{account_url}` placeholder in a licence file, the account links on the receipt, in emails and in the account area (`https:\/\/example.com\/\/account\/licenses`, or `\/de\/\/account`) and the admin's store links have one slash in them rather than two, and the stylesheet loads only on the shop's own pages rather than only on the home page\n"},"1.0.16":{"date":"10\/09\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **An add-on can't go into the cart with nothing to attach to.** On an add-on's product page, when the visitor has no licence in the cart and none on an account we can see, Add to cart now reads as unavailable until a key is typed into \"I already have a license key\", and pressing it brings the licence box into view instead of adding a line the cart then refused with \"Add-ons need a license to attach to\". The box itself is drawn as a callout, with a bold question and an accent border, so it is not missed. The button is blocked rather than disabled, because KahunaCart's options script already switches `disabled` for stock and variants. With scripting off the form posts as before and the cart still explains\n"},"1.0.15":{"date":"10\/09\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Merging one customer into another moves the keys too.** When KahunaCart merges customer B into customer A, every licence key filed under B is now filed under A, so they show on A's account page, A can rename them and manage their seats, and renewals and add-ons can be bought against them. Nothing else about a key changes: its order, product, seats, dates and activations stay as they were. A preview of the merge reports how many keys will move, and running the same merge again moves nothing. The keys go to the customer's current survivor, looked up when they are written, so a merge that finishes after its target was itself merged away files them under the final customer and not under the alias. Every run, for any pair, also moves keys still filed under a customer that was merged away in that chain, so a retry repairs keys an overlapping merge left behind. Needs a KahunaCart that fires `onKahunaCartCustomersMerged`\n"},"1.0.14":{"date":"10\/08\/2026","content":"1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **An unpaid order no longer promises a license key.** The keys panel said \"One more key is being generated and will appear shortly\" on any order with a licensed product, paid or not, so a customer whose payment had just been cancelled got a \"Your payment did not go through\" email promising a key that was never coming, and a bank-transfer order's receipt said the same before the money arrived. Keys are issued when an order is paid, so the receipt and every order email now mention keys only once the payment has gone through. Keys that already exist are still shown, even on an order refunded since\n"},"1.0.13":{"date":"10\/08\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * The licensing API and download links now take their address from KahunaCart's own `SiteUrl::api()` instead of building one here, so they agree with every other add-on about where the API answers. Needs KahunaCart 1.4.4 or later\n"},"1.0.12":{"date":"10\/04\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **The Licensed products table and the tables on a key have search boxes.** Type a few words and the rows that do not hold all of them drop away as you type, in any order and whatever the case: Licensed products by product or kind, a key's Activations by machine or label, and Also covers by product. The tables on a key only get a box once they have more than eight rows. The keys list keeps its own search. Needs KahunaCart 1.3.11 or later, which draws the box\n"},"1.0.11":{"date":"10\/01\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **Help text on the issue-a-key, key-details and product-rule forms now follows the admin's Help text setting.** It shows below the label or in a tooltip behind an info icon\n"},"1.0.10":{"date":"09\/30\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **The download no longer carries the plugin's tests.** `tests\/` and `phpunit.xml` stay in the repository and out of the release zip, 104 files fewer on every install. Nothing the plugin runs is affected\n"},"1.0.9":{"date":"09\/30\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **The tables in the Licenses screens sort by their column headings.** Press a heading on the keys list, on a key's activations and add-ons, or on the licensed products table to sort by it, and press it again to turn the order round. The keys list sorts on the server, so a heading orders every key that matches the search and not only the page on screen, and pressing one goes back to page one. `GET \/kahunacart\/licenses` takes `sort` (`key`, `project`, `product`, `status`, `seats`, `customer`, `order` or `expires`) and `dir`, a key with nothing in that column sorts last whichever way it runs, and any other `sort` is ignored, so the list is newest first as before. Needs KahunaCart 1.3.8 or later, which draws the headings\n"},"1.0.8":{"date":"09\/29\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **A bundle's key validates for every package the bundle ships.** A key covered its included packages on the licence card, in licence files and in stamped zips, but `\/validate` with `product` only passed for the key's own product and the add-ons bought against it, so a bundle key asked about one of its packages came back 409 `product_mismatch`. `product` now passes for any slug the key covers: its own product, its add-ons, and the Included packages on either product's rule, including a package that is only sold inside bundles and has no catalog row at all. `\/activate` checks `product` the same way. A slug the key does not cover is refused exactly as before, and every existing field and status code is unchanged. See docs\/api.md\n    * **`\/validate` answers `covers`, the sorted list of every slug the key is good for,** so a package manager or an installer holding one key for a bundle learns in one call which packages it may install\n    * **A licence check without an HTTP call.** Code running on the same site as the store, such as a download proxy or a package repository, calls `KahunaCartLicenses::instance()->validator()->check($key, $product)` and gets exactly the status and `data` the `\/validate` route would answer, `covers` included, with no rate limit and no second PHP worker tied up calling itself. `validate()` beside it returns the payload and throws the refusal instead. The route answers through the same object, so the two cannot drift apart. See \"Checking a key from the same site\" in docs\/api.md\n    * **Your own servers can skip the per-address rate limit.** A server that checks keys on its customers' behalf sends every check from one address, so the per-address bucket capped all of its customers together at 120 a minute. List such servers, as IPs or CIDR ranges, under **Licenses \u2192 Settings \u2192 Rate Limits \u2192 Your Own Servers** (`api.rate_limit.trusted`). They skip the per-address bucket and keep a per-key one at a limit of their own, 600 a minute by default (`api.rate_limit.per_minute_trusted`). Trust is decided on the address the store resolved, never on a header the caller sent. New strings `API_RATE_LIMIT_TRUSTED`, `API_RATE_LIMIT_PER_MINUTE_TRUSTED` and their help text. See \"Validating from your own server\" in docs\/api.md\n    * **Keys from another system can be imported as they are.** `Service\\LicenseImport` (through `KahunaCartLicenses::instance()->licenseImport()`) writes a licence with a key the customer already holds, in any layout, with its product, customer, order, status, dates, seats, issuer, external id, meta and original issue date. It fires no events, sends no email and writes no order. It is idempotent on the issuer and external id, so it can be run again and brings rows in line with the source, and a key that is already here under another identity is reported as a conflict rather than overwritten. It is the piece an import command or a CSV importer builds on; there is no admin screen for it. See \"Importing keys from another system\" in docs\/admin.md\n    * **Introductions can pluralise.** `{one|many}` in the email or Licenses-tab introduction picks the first form above one key and the second above several, so `Your {license key is|license keys are} below. Keep {it|them} somewhere safe.` reads right however many keys an order carries. The default email introduction now uses it. See \"Placeholders\" in docs\/storefront.md\n    * **A product rule's key format can say \"no prefix\".** The rule editor's custom key format has a **Prefix** choice, and **No prefix** drops the store's prefix from that product's keys, so a product can mint new keys that look like the ones another system issued (four blocks of eight hex characters, for example) while the rest of the store keeps its own. Over the API it is `\"prefix\": \"\"` in `key_format`; absent still inherits. The example key in the rule editor now also shows the store's prefix when the rule has none of its own, which it left out before\n    * **The Licenses tab is a list, and every key has a page of its own.** The tab drew every key as a full card with its instructions, files and forms, so a customer with fifty keys got fifty sets of instructions on one page. It now lists one card per key, newest first: the product, the project name, the status, the key with a copy button and the day it was bought, and a **Details** button that opens the key (the product name does too). The key's page (`{base}\/account\/licenses\/{ref}`, inside the tab, with a way back to the list at its top and bottom) has the key, what it covers, its licence files, a link to the order it came from, its ready-to-run packages and its instructions, then a quieter **Manage this license** card with only what can mean something for that key: terms that limit something, renewals, the Project box when an add-on can attach to the key, and the sites list when the key has a seat limit or a site on it. `{ref}` is 16 hex characters worked out from the key's stored hash (an HMAC, never the key, its hash or its id), so the addresses do not count up; it stays the same for the key's life and changes when the key is regenerated. It is only ever matched against the signed-in customer's own keys, so a key that is somebody else's or does not exist gets the same \"not found\" and a 404. A signed-out visit comes back to the key's page after sign-in. Forms on the page post to it and bring the customer back to it. Needs KahunaCart 1.3.6, whose account tabs can have pages below them. New partials `license-detail.html.twig`, `license-manage.html.twig`, `license-status.html.twig`, `license-renewal.html.twig`, `license-project.html.twig`, `license-sites.html.twig` and `license-instructions.html.twig`; the receipt's key card includes the shared ones and renders as before. A build on the key's page shows the product file's `title` and `description` when core has those columns, and its name otherwise. See \"The Licenses tab\" in docs\/storefront.md\n    * **Download all.** When the store's licence files include one with a fixed name that writes a line per product (a Grav store's `licenses.yaml`), the Licenses tab offers it merged across every active key the customer owns: one line per package, the newer key where two keys cover the same package, sorted by slug. Served from the account area at `{base}\/account\/licenses?download={name}`, behind its login. See \"Download all\" in docs\/storefront.md\n\n1. [](#improved)\n    * **A receipt leaves out seats, expiry and updates for a key that has none of them.** A perpetual key with unlimited seats and every future release used to print \"0 in use, unlimited seats \u00b7 Does not expire \u00b7 Includes all future updates\" on the receipt; the line now only appears when one of them says something\n    * **An add-on's own Included packages now count for the key it is attached to**, on the licence card, in licence files, in stamped zips and in `covers`, the same way a licence rule's always have\n    * **A key's admin screen lists its Included packages under Also covers**, marked \"Included\", beside the add-ons bought onto it. A bundle key used to read \"Just the product it was sold for\" although it validates for every package the bundle ships\n    * **A key with no updates window reads \"Every future release\" under Updates until**, not \"Never\", which read as the opposite\n    * **The receipt prints the install instructions once, not once per key.** An order with three keys printed the store's instructions three times, one copy under each key. Each key is now a short card (`order-license.html.twig`): the product, its status, the key with its copy button, what it covers, its files (the builds are in the receipt's own Downloads list, so the card no longer repeats them), and for a buyer who can sign in a **Manage this license** link to the key's own page. The instructions follow once, under all the keys (`order-instructions.html.twig`), and a store whose instructions name the key still gets each key's own, headed with its product. The order email and its plain-text part do the same. The panel carries `instructions` (from `LicenseView::instructionsOnce()`) and each key `page_url` and `has_terms`. `license.html.twig`, the full card, is still there for any page that includes it.\n"},"1.0.7":{"date":"09\/25\/2026","content":"1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **Installing through GPM no longer switches the add-on off.** The plugin loads its classes through Composer's `vendor\/autoload.php`, but `vendor\/` was left out of the repository and so out of the download GPM installs. Grav logged \"Plugin 'kahunacart-licenses' autoload failed: Failed opening required ...\/vendor\/autoload.php\" and turned the plugin off on every store that installed or updated it that way. The run-time half of `vendor\/` is part of the download now, and a test fails if it goes missing or if a development install is committed over it\n"}}},"kahunacart-subscriptions":{"name":"KahunaCart Subscriptions","version":"1.1.11","description":"Recurring billing for KahunaCart \u2014 plans, subscriptions, renewal orders, provider and invoice modes","description_html":"<p>Recurring billing for KahunaCart \u2014 plans, subscriptions, renewal orders, provider and invoice modes<\/p>","description_plain":"Recurring billing for KahunaCart \u2014 plans, subscriptions, renewal orders, provider and invoice modes","icon":"refresh","author":{"name":"Trilby Media, LLC","email":"hello@trilby.media"},"dependencies":[{"name":"grav","version":">=2.0.0"},{"name":"kahunacart","version":">=1.4.4"}],"compatibility":{"grav":["2.0"]},"homepage":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/add-ons\/subscriptions","docs":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/docs\/selling\/subscriptions","keywords":"grav, premium, commerce, ecommerce, subscriptions, recurring, billing, plans","bugs":"https:\/\/github.com\/getgrav\/grav-premium-issues\/labels\/kahunacart-subscriptions","license":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/license","tag_name":"1.1.11","date":"2026-10-26T03:51:03Z","release_type":"stable","zipball_url":"https:\/\/getgrav.org\/download\/plugins\/kahunacart-subscriptions\/1.1.11","repository":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/add-ons\/subscriptions","premium":{"vendor":"KahunaCart","validator":"kahunacart","license_product":"kahunacart-subscriptions","checkout_url":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/shop\/product\/kahunacart-subscriptions","button":"Buy Now","filename":"grav-plugin-kahunacart-subscriptions"},"slug":"kahunacart-subscriptions","install_path":"user\/plugins\/kahunacart-subscriptions","content_sha256":"8eaae037e811c87dd15bacb405a4c218e91a1392dd739d3d8bbc43205161a532","changelog":{"1.1.11":{"date":"10\/25\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **Buttons in these emails stay readable on a light button color.** The writing on a button was always white, so a store that picked a light Email Button Color got white on yellow. It now turns dark when the button is light, the same as KahunaCart's own emails. Needs KahunaCart 1.6.5 for the dark writing; an older KahunaCart keeps white\n"},"1.1.10":{"date":"10\/22\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **No empty Subscriptions tab.** On KahunaCart 1.6.0 with Hide Empty Tabs on (the default), a customer with no subscription doesn't get a Subscriptions tab in their account; the page still opens at its address. Older KahunaCart shows the tab as before\n    * **Plan prices follow Whole Amounts.** With KahunaCart 1.6.0's Whole Amounts on, the plans offered on a paywalled page said \"$19.00 every month\". They now print the way core prints its own prices: \"$19 every month\", never rounding cents away, and the offers on one page are one set, so if any of them has cents they all keep theirs. A billing option's own price in the buy box follows the setting too, the way the variant's price beside it does. Emails, invoices and the cart line's trial note keep full amounts. On an older core nothing changes\n    * **Ships its license.** A `LICENSE` file now comes with the plugin, and the blueprint links to the KahunaCart License at kahunacart.com\/license instead of the Grav Premium license\n"},"1.1.9":{"date":"10\/13\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Hear about subscribers as they come and go.** Under Notifications \u2192 Store owner there are four new rows. **A new subscriber** (low, switched off until you turn it on) says who has started, paid or on a free trial. **A subscription is cancelled** arrives when a cancellation is set to take effect and again when it ends. **A subscription is past due** (high) arrives once for each missed renewal, with what is owed. **A subscription expired** arrives when a past-due one runs out. Each names the plan and the customer's name, never their email, and links to the subscription. A failed renewal payment is already sent by KahunaCart as Payment failed, so there is no second row for it\n    * **Renewals waiting on a price reach your phone too.** The alert for renewals that cannot be built for want of a price in the subscription's currency is now also sent to your channels, once when it shows and once when it clears. Needs KahunaCart 1.5.3\n"},"1.1.8":{"date":"10\/11\/2026","content":"1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **Works with a shop mounted at the site root.** With KahunaCart's `route: '\/'` (core 1.5.0) a subscription's page is `\/subscription\/{hash}`, and its links, the links in subscription emails and the admin's link to the page have one slash in them rather than two. The stylesheet still loads only on the shop's own pages, and the page's Back to shop link and the account tab's Browse the shop link go to `\/`\n"},"1.1.7":{"date":"10\/08\/2026","content":"1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **A free trial is free with every payment method, not only Stripe and Polar.** A plan with a free trial bought through a payment method that cannot run subscriptions itself (Mollie, PayPal, Moneris, bank transfer and others) falls back to invoice mode, and that order used to be charged the whole first period. The first invoice at the end of the trial then billed the same period again. Now the plan costs nothing on the order that starts the trial, as the docs always said: with nothing left to pay the order completes as paid and the customer goes straight to the receipt without visiting a payment page, and a fixed payment method fee and its tax, or postage on a physical plan, is still charged through the method they picked. The first invoice is for the first whole period, starting where the trial ends. The same now happens when Stripe or Polar refuses a subscription and it falls back to invoice mode. Orders placed before this version are not changed; docs\/troubleshooting.md says how to put them right\n"},"1.1.6":{"date":"10\/08\/2026","content":"1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **Subscription links work on a site installed in a subfolder.** On a store at an address like `example.com\/store`, the links on a subscription's page and in the account's Subscriptions tab (the subscription itself, its orders, the product) pointed at the domain root and ended on the web server's 404 page. They now carry the store's own address, and the active language like every other storefront link\n2. [](#improved)\n    * Storefront links now come from KahunaCart's own `SiteUrl::store()` instead of being built here, so they agree with every other add-on. Needs KahunaCart 1.4.4 or later\n"},"1.1.5":{"date":"10\/04\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Choose whether a plan's price includes the payment method's fee.** A new setting, **Payment method fees on subscriptions**, is **Added to the price** by default: the subscriber pays the method's fee and its tax on every payment, the first and each renewal, and on a renewal invoice the fee follows the method they pick. **Included in the price** makes the plan's price what the subscriber pays every time, whatever the method, and the store absorbs the fee. Each plan can override the store's choice, and a subscription keeps the choice it started with. Subscriptions started before this version renew without a method fee, as they always did.\n    * **A plan can be sold in every currency the store sells in.** On a store that sells in more than one currency (KahunaCart 1.4), the plan editor has a box for each of the other currencies beside the plan's price, in that currency's own decimal places, and the Plans table lists them under the price. A plan with a price of its own can be bought only in the currencies that have an amount: in any other the buy button draws the option greyed out as \"Not available in EUR\", the form opens on the first option that can be bought, and the cart refuses it with a sentence naming the plan and the currency instead of charging the dollar number or nothing. A plan with no price of its own sells at the variant's price in that currency, as it did in dollars. Amounts travel as `currency_amounts` on the plan endpoints and the `create_plan` and `update_plan` tools, the same as every other price in the admin, and are saved with the plan in one transaction. Clearing a plan's own price clears its other amounts, and deleting a plan deletes them. See docs\/plans.md\n    * **A subscription renews in the currency it started in.** Each renewal is built as an order in the subscription's own currency at the amount the subscription was sold at, in invoice mode and for a platform's `renewed` event, and its page, its emails and the admin print its money in that currency with that currency's decimals. A renewal the store cannot price there (the variant has no price in it) is never built in another currency. It fails like a failed payment: a failed attempt and a note on the timeline, \"Waiting on a price in EUR\" beside the status in the admin, an alert on the admin's strip, and a high priority notification to the store owner that names the plan, the currency and the subscription number and nothing about the customer. A mark on the subscription keeps the invoice sweep and a platform's retries from repeating all of it, and it clears itself when a renewal goes through. In invoice mode the next sweep builds the invoice once the price is back, and **Renew now** says plainly that it cannot be done right now. A platform that bills in a different currency from the subscription's has that amount left out of the record, with one note on the timeline. Needs KahunaCart 1.4.0 or later. See docs\/subscriptions.md and docs\/troubleshooting.md\n    * **The Plans table and the orders on a subscription have search boxes.** Type a few words and the rows that do not hold all of them drop away as you type, in any order and whatever the case: Plans by plan, SKU or how often it bills, and a subscription's orders by order number or period. A plan's Move up and Move down still work with some rows hidden. The orders only get a box once there are more than eight of them. The subscriptions list keeps its own search. Needs KahunaCart 1.3.11 or later, which draws the box\n\n2. [](#improved)\n    * Subscription checkout gives providers the first order\u2019s payable amount separately from the recurring plan price, including its tax and payment-method fee\n    * **The subscribe panel on paywalled articles prices plans in the visitor's currency.** A plan with no amount in that currency is left out, and a product whose other plans are left out is named as the product of the one that remains\n    * **The admin never prints one currency's amounts with another's decimals.** A yen subscription in a dollar store shows whole yen, the plan editor takes yen without decimals, and a currency the store no longer sells in is still printed with its own decimals. Monthly recurring revenue is still counted per currency and never added together\n\n3. [](#bugfix)\n    * Stores with extra currencies disabled delete plans with the original single statement, while existing plans still retain their foreign-currency prices\n    * Concurrent plan edits and deletion cannot recreate prices for a deleted plan, and a currency-price edit checks the plan\u2019s latest own price before saving\n    * **Clearing a plan's own price clears its foreign prices in one save.** Inherited plans hide the foreign-price boxes, and typing an own price shows them again\n    * **Guest subscription payment and invoice links work from the manage page.** Receipt, invoice and renewal-payment links carry the access token required by KahunaCart\n    * **Immediate cancellation succeeds when the provider confirms it during the request.** The admin receives the cancelled subscription without an already-cancelled error\n    * **Existing subscriptions keep renewing in their own currency after it is disabled.** Invoice and provider renewals retain the agreed currency; a renewal can never use an amount from another currency\n    * **Deleting a plan also deletes its currency prices while extra currencies are enabled.** Cleanup applies to command-line purges too and happens in the same transaction as the plan deletion\n    * **Sorting subscriptions by amount keeps each currency together.** Amounts are compared only within their own currency\n\n    * The buyer pays the payment method fee and its tax on the first subscription payment and every renewal; provider agreements retain their agreed currency amounts, and methods that cannot collect them are not offered.\n"},"1.1.4":{"date":"10\/01\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **Help text on the plan editor now follows the admin's Help text setting.** It shows below the label or in a tooltip behind an info icon\n"},"1.1.3":{"date":"09\/30\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **The download no longer carries the plugin's tests.** `tests\/` and `phpunit.xml` stay in the repository and out of the release zip, 91 files fewer on every install. Nothing the plugin runs is affected\n"},"1.1.2":{"date":"09\/30\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **A plan's customer group also fills the subscribe panel on paywalled articles.** KahunaCart 1.3.8 can hide the rest of an article from anyone who isn't a member of a customer group and show a subscribe panel instead. The panel now lists the plans that grant that group: one button for each plan on a product that is published (listed or unlisted), open to the visitor and not sold out, with its price the way the product page states it (the first payment, then the renewal when that is different, or the trial). A product with Monthly and Yearly billing options gets a button for each, in the order the shop lists products and then the order the product page offers the plans. Nothing to set up: a plan with a **Customer group while subscribed** is offered on every article that asks for that group. It costs one database query however many products the shop has. Needs KahunaCart 1.3.8 or later. See docs\/plans.md\n    * **The subscriptions list and the orders on a subscription sort by their column headings.** Press a heading to sort by it, and press it again to turn the order round. The list sorts on the server, so a heading orders every subscription the filters leave and not only the page on screen, and pressing one goes back to page one. `GET \/kahunacart\/subscriptions` takes `sort` (`customer`, `plan`, `status`, `billing`, `amount` or `next`) and `dir`, a subscription with nothing in that column sorts last whichever way it runs, and any other `sort` is ignored, so the list is newest first as before. The Plans table has no headings to press, because its rows are in the order the buy button offers them. Needs KahunaCart 1.3.8 or later, which draws the headings\n"},"1.1.1":{"date":"09\/28\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **A plan can put its subscribers in a customer group while their subscription is live.** The plan editor has a new setting, **Customer group while subscribed**. Pick a group and everybody whose subscription on that plan is trialing, active or past due counts as a member of it, everywhere KahunaCart asks: price lists, coupon conditions, member-only products, and the `site.customer_group.<code>` permission a page's `access:` header can ask for. So a Downloads Pro page with `access: site.customer_group.members: true` opens for subscribers and closes the moment a subscription stops being live. Nothing is written to the group's member list: the store works it out from the subscription on each request, so a late webhook or a status set by hand can never leave somebody in a group they have stopped paying for. A cancellation at the end of the period keeps the group until that end, because the customer paid for it; a paused subscription grants nothing. Migration `subscriptions_0005_plan_customer_group` adds the column on SQLite, MySQL\/MariaDB and PostgreSQL. Needs KahunaCart 1.3.5 or later, the release that asks add-ons which groups a customer holds. See docs\/plans.md\n"},"1.1.0":{"date":"09\/25\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Billing options: several plans on one variant.** A membership sold monthly at $100, quarterly and yearly used to need three variants, and three variants kept three stock counts, so there was no single count of places left. One variant can now carry several plans, and all of them sell from its one stock count. Each plan can have its own price (leave it blank to use the variant's), a label for its choice on the buy button, and an order. The buy button shows the plans as a choice under **How often you pay**, as radio pills in the style of the variant options that work from the keyboard; picking one changes the price shown and the renewal terms, and the cart charges that plan's price through KahunaCart's line price event. A variant with one plan renders exactly what it did before. The Plans screen adds, reorders and edits a variant's options, and the variant picker no longer locks a variant that already has a plan. Migration `subscriptions_0004_billing_options` adds the columns and swaps the unique index on `variant_id` for a plain one on SQLite, MySQL\/MariaDB and PostgreSQL. See docs\/plans.md\n    * **When a subscription ends: its place stays used, or goes back to stock.** A per-plan setting for a store with a limited number of places. \"Its place stays used\", the default and the old behaviour, means twenty places is twenty subscriptions ever; \"Its place goes back to stock\" puts the unit back once the subscription has actually ended, so twenty places is twenty at a time. Never at a cancellation scheduled for the period end, never twice, and not when refunding the first order already put it back. The subscription's history records it\n    * A billing option's label follows the line's name to Stripe or Polar, so three options on one variant are three agreements that can be told apart in the platform's dashboard\n\n1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **A renewal now charges what the customer signed up at.** Every period is an ordinary order built through the cart, and the cart priced the line from the variant, so a renewal charged whatever the variant cost on the morning it was built. The price frozen on the subscription was recorded and never read: a customer who subscribed with a coupon lost it on the first renewal, and raising a variant's price repriced everybody already paying, which is the one thing the Plans docs say the store will never do. In provider mode the platform kept billing the frozen amount, so the store's renewal orders and the money disagreed instead. The renewal line is now priced at the subscription's own amount, with a \"Price agreed at sign-up\" row under it on the receipt, and a sale running on the day is not taken off it again. A plan with a renewal variant keeps pricing renewals from that variant, as it always did. Needs KahunaCart 1.2.11 or later, for its line price event\n    * **Taking a plan off sale no longer stops the renewals of the people already on it.** The cart asked the same question of a renewal line as of a customer's basket, found the plan switched off, and refused the renewal as \"no longer available\". In provider mode that happened after the platform had taken the money, so the subscription stopped moving forward while the card kept being charged. A renewal the store builds for itself is now let through, and a plan that is off sale is still refused to anybody new\n"},"1.0.3":{"date":"09\/25\/2026","content":"1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **Installing through GPM no longer switches the add-on off.** The plugin loads its classes through Composer's `vendor\/autoload.php`, but `vendor\/` was left out of the repository and so out of the download GPM installs. Grav logged \"Plugin 'kahunacart-subscriptions' autoload failed: Failed opening required ...\/vendor\/autoload.php\" and turned the plugin off on every store that installed or updated it that way. The run-time half of `vendor\/` is part of the download now, and a test fails if it goes missing or if a development install is committed over it\n"},"1.0.2":{"date":"09\/23\/2026","content":"1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **The storefront stylesheet is no longer added twice on subscription pages.** The add-on puts KahunaCart's own stylesheet on the page itself, and looked for the file one folder too high to stamp it, so its link never matched the one KahunaCart adds and the browser downloaded the same sheet twice. It now finds the file through Grav, and the page links it once.\n"},"1.0.1":{"date":"09\/15\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * The package name and the repository links now point at the kahunacart GitHub org rather than trilbymedia: `composer.json`, the security policy, and the links into the base plugin's provider contract and developer guide from `docs\/provider-developer-guide.md`\n"},"1.0.0":{"date":"09\/08\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **The mailing-list add-on is called Newsletters now, and the segment field follows it.** The event this plugin listens on is `onKahunaCartNewslettersRegisterSegmentFields` and the registry it checks for is `Grav\\Plugin\\KahunaCartNewsletters\\Segments\\Registry`. `subscription_status` still arrives in the segment editor the same way, and a store without that add-on still notices nothing. It needs Newsletters 1.0.0 or later; against the add-on under its old name the field is not contributed\n    * The subscription emails and the receipt panel read shorter and plainer, in US English with no dashes: the past-due, renewal and ended messages say what happened and what to do in one or two sentences, and the receipt panel's manage link is a real link with the address under it rather than a bare URL\n    * **The subscription page and the pay-link lookup read a customer's order history in a handful of queries.** Drawing a subscription used to cost a query for every renewal order behind it, so the customers who had been with the store longest waited the longest to see their own history, and the check for an unpaid renewal did the same walk again. Both read the orders together now\n\n1. [](#new)\n    * **`bin\/plugin kahunacart-subscriptions seed`, so an empty store is not what a merchant meets first.** Every screen this add-on adds opens on an empty table until somebody has been selling on a calendar for months, and the public demo's database is rebuilt every hour, so there was never anything on them to look at. The command writes what a small shop would have: two plans on the demo catalog, twenty subscribers across them, most running, two inside a trial, one whose invoice is late and inside its grace period, one paused, three who have gone, and a real order behind every period any of them was billed for \u2014 about sixty orders, each with the timeline entry that put it there. Idempotent on the subscription hash and the plan's variant, so the demo's reset runs it on a timer; `--purge` takes back exactly what it wrote, including the orders, and leaves a plan somebody is still subscribed to alone. Nothing is sent and nothing is left for the worker: orders are written without going near `OrderBuilder`, every live invoice-mode subscription renews further out than the longest rung of the reminder ladder, and the past-due one carries a complete invoice stamp, so a store running `bin\/plugin kahunacart work` finds no work in it. Dates are offsets from the moment it runs and the volume comes from a fixed seed, so two machines get the same people, the same billing dates and the same subscription URLs. See docs\/cli.md\n    * **A Settings tab, and the plugin's own form drawn on it.** \"Where do I change the reminder days\" used to be answered by leaving the KahunaCart admin for the Grav admin's plugin list, and nothing on the Subscriptions screen said so. The section now has a third tab beside Subscriptions and Plans, on the `settings` sub-route so a reload and the Back button land on it, and KahunaCart draws the plugin's own blueprint form inside it \u2014 the manifest says `settings_plugin`, the screen renders the slot the base plugin forwards into, and the header's Save button saves it. The invoice ladder, the grace period, the four emails and the admin switches are read and changed on the same screen as the subscriptions they govern, and following **Plugins \u2192 KahunaCart Subscriptions** in the Grav admin lands here too. Against an older base plugin the tab prints one line pointing at the Grav admin's page, so nothing is stranded. See docs\/admin.md\n    * **`subscription_status` in the Newsletters add-on's segment editor.** A store running both can now build a mailing-list segment out of what this add-on knows: everybody whose subscription lapsed last month, everybody active on a plan so the sales pitch leaves them out, everybody past due so the reminder goes to them and nobody else. Neither question is askable from the mailing list's own tables. The field is contributed on `onKahunaCartNewslettersRegisterSegmentFields` and is never a hard dependency \u2014 the newsletter is a separate add-on, the listener checks its registry class is there before touching anything, and a store running subscriptions without it notices nothing at all. The match is through the subscriber's linked customer record rather than by comparing addresses, so somebody who signed up to the newsletter with one address and bought with another correctly does not match: guessing that two addresses are one person is how a store emails somebody about somebody else's subscription\n    * **`bin\/plugin kahunacart reset` now takes the subscriptions with it.** Verified on a live store: a reset that deleted every order and every customer left the subscriptions behind, still active and still due, so the next worker tick tried to bill people who were no longer there. The add-on declares its tables on the base plugin's new `onKahunaCartRegisterResetTables` \u2014 the subscriptions, their events, their renewal orders and the billing tick's cursor \u2014 under both the orders and the customers scope, because a subscription belongs to the customer who holds it and is the sale that created it. `kahunacart_subscription_plans` is deliberately left out: a plan is what the merchant configured, and the reset promises the configuration survives. The tables appear in the plan the command prints, with their row counts, before it asks\n    * Recurring billing core for KahunaCart: plans, subscriptions, renewal orders and the subscription event timeline, with a state machine that is the only writer of a subscription's status, UTC period arithmetic with end-of-month clamping, guest subscriptions by hash, and a refunded renewal that rolls the period back \u2014 unless the refunded period is an older one the customer has already billed past, which is recorded as a note and changes no dates\n    * Free trials that are actually free: a plan with `trial_days` starts its subscription in `trialing` and its origin order is completed and paid with a total of zero, carrying a translatable \"Free trial (14 days)\" discount adjustment, with the ledger row settled at zero to match. The first paid period begins where the trial ends \u2014 14 free days and then a whole month, not a month that quietly started a fortnight ago\n    * Checkout: a plan sells through the ordinary buy button and checks out alone \u2014 one plan line, quantity one, nothing else in the cart, and a second subscription added to a cart that already holds one is refused with a message beside the button whether it is the same plan or a different one. Providers that run their own recurring billing (Stripe, Polar, the test provider) take the payment over and KahunaCart mirrors every later event from their webhooks: activation, each renewal minted as an ordinary paid order, failed payments, past due, pause and resume, a cancellation scheduled for the period end and the one that ends it, and expiry. A provider that cannot run the agreement falls back to invoice mode rather than refusing the sale\n    * Invoice mode, for any provider: KahunaCart keeps the calendar itself, builds each period's renewal as an unpaid order with a pay link, sends the reminder ladder (`invoice.reminder_days`, default 14, 3 and 0 days before), marks the subscription past due when the period ends unpaid, and lets it lapse after the grace period (`invoice.grace_days`, default 7, per-plan override). A payment during grace brings it back; paying early adds time rather than replacing it. Emails for the reminder, the due date, past due and expiry, all translatable and switchable\n    * Customer side: a Subscriptions tab in the account, and a page for every subscription at `\/subscription\/{hash}` (guests included) with the timeline, the renewal orders, cancel at period end, resume, update payment method where the provider offers a portal, and renew now in invoice mode. Receipts and receipt emails say when an order started or renewed a subscription\n    * Admin: a Subscriptions section with Plans (product and variant, interval, trial, billing mode, renewal variant, grace, per-provider references) and Subscriptions (filters, search, detail with timeline and orders, cancel now or at period end, pause, resume, extend by N days with a note, resend pay link, mark renewal paid), a customer panel listing that customer's subscriptions, and active, past-due and monthly-recurring-revenue figures. Gated on `kahunacart.subscriptions.view` and `kahunacart.subscriptions.manage`\n    * MCP tool manifest: `mcp.yaml` describes all seventeen admin API routes as MCP tools, so a `grav-mcp` client picks them up at startup with no code per plugin. Every tool carries the permission its route enforces, so a caller holding only `kahunacart.subscriptions.view` is offered the six read tools and none of the eleven writes, and the descriptions carry the rules a caller has to know: what the two billing modes mean, when pause, resume, cancel and uncancel are refused, that a renewal is an ordinary order marked paid through the base plugin's own route, and that invoice mode needs the store's scheduler running\n    * Documentation for plans, the two billing modes, the customer account, the admin section, troubleshooting, and a provider developer guide for implementing recurring billing\n    * A complete event reference in the provider developer guide: all fifteen events this plugin fires, each with its exact payload keys, when it fires and what a listener may usefully do, plus the five keys every subscription event carries. `onKahunaCartSubscriptionRenewalOrderBuilding` is documented as the question it is \u2014 fired through `ask()`, with a listener writing `lines` back onto the event \u2014 and the two order events the plugin listens to are described as the handlers they are rather than as events anybody can fire\n    * `SECURITY.md`: report privately to security@kahunacart.com, never in a public issue, with the base plugin's own policy as the full text\n    * Scheduled pauses. Polar and Stripe both stop billing at the end of the period the customer has already paid for rather than the moment the button is pressed, and the store now records that as what it is: a `pause_at_period_end` flag and a `pauses_at` date (migration `subscriptions_0003`), the row still live and still billing, \"Billing pauses on 3 October\" on the customer's page and a **Pausing** flag in the admin, a **Do not pause after all** button on both sides, and `pause_scheduled` \/ `pause_unscheduled` on the timeline. The status moves to `paused` when the platform's own event says billing has actually stopped. A driver that reports the agreement as paused is still honoured immediately\n    * A contract test that runs each shipped recurring driver's own event mapping \u2014 Polar and Stripe, skipped where the checkout is not beside this one \u2014 through the real webhook listener, so a driver whose raw keys the listener cannot read fails a test instead of quietly leaving customers with no billing portal\n    * **The store tells a customer when a provider-billed renewal is declined.** The past-due email belonged to invoice mode alone, so a Stripe subscription whose card failed moved to `past_due` and the store sent nothing at all: the customer found out by happening to open their subscription page, or not at all, while the retries ran out and the agreement expired. The same message now goes out on the delivery that moves the row, without a pay link because there is nothing here to pay, and pointing at the subscription page where **Update payment method** opens the billing portal. That button is the only place a card can be replaced. `emails.provider_past_due` switches the provider-mode half off on its own, for a store whose platform already emails about declined renewals\n    * **The tax a payment platform agreed to bill is kept and put back on every renewal.** A driver that is not the merchant of record now reports the breakdown it priced the subscriber's recurring price with, as `taxLines` on the `SubscriptionResult`, and the add-on writes it onto the subscription row, copies it onto every renewal order that subscription mints, and rebuilds it as ordinary tax adjustments through the calculator, in the same type, label and `source_json` shape a tax zone writes, so receipts, credit notes, refunds and the tax report read a renewal exactly as they read a first purchase. Renewal orders used to carry no tax at all, because a renewal order has no address for a zone to be matched on, so the first period said \u00a37.20, the second said \u00a36.00 and the platform charged \u00a36.00 for both. The rate is the one that was agreed rather than today's: a merchant who edits a rate changes what new subscribers are quoted, and existing agreements keep the figure they were sold until the plan is repriced. The stage stands down on a store running a flat rate, which already taxes every order including renewals\n    * **The store hands a driver the tax it charged on the first period.** The other half of the tax fix below, and without it that half could not fire: nothing told a driver what the store's own calculator had already charged, so a platform billing the plan's untaxed price had nothing to bill differently. `SubscriptionContext::$metadata` now carries `tax_minor` and `tax_lines` on every subscription checkout, computed from the origin order's own tax adjustments, and a driver that is not the merchant of record mints the subscriber's recurring price at the taxed total and reports the same lines back. Both keys are always sent, `0` and an empty list on a store that charges no tax, so a driver reads two keys rather than working out whether they are there. Tax the store prices inclusively is deliberately left out, because it is already inside the price frozen on the subscription row and sending it would charge it twice a period for the life of the agreement. A second attempt at an abandoned trial checkout falls back to the breakdown already agreed on the row, where the order in front of it has been discounted to nothing and has no tax left to read\n    * The driver contract test now covers cancellation as well as activation: a shipped driver whose `deleted` or `revoked` payload does not say `ended` fails, rather than leaving a revoked subscriber with a cancellation dated a month into the future\n\n1. [](#bugfix)\n    * A subscription checkout leaves an id on its purchase row. The pending purchase row for the first period took the agreement's reference, and on a hosted subscription checkout there is no agreement yet, because the platform mints one when the customer finishes paying, so the row was written with nothing on it and a merchant looking at a checkout somebody walked away from had no id to quote at the platform and nothing to search their dashboard for. A driver now reports the checkout's own id on `SubscriptionResult::$transactionRef` and the store writes that, leaving `provider_ref` free for the agreement, which is what every later webhook and the customer's own cancel and pause buttons are matched on. Stripe and Polar both report it; a driver that names no checkout goes on referencing the agreement exactly as before\n    * A refused transition reads as a sentence. The 422 was built from two raw status words, so a merchant who pressed Activate on a running subscription was told \"A active subscription cannot be active.\": wrong article, and a refusal denying the thing it is reporting is already true. Asking for the status a subscription is already in now says so (\"This subscription is already active.\"), every other pair gets the article its status word takes and the action it was actually refused (\"A cancelled subscription cannot be paused.\", \"An expired subscription cannot be renewed.\"), and `past_due` stops appearing as a column name in the middle of a sentence. The `from`, `to` and error code a caller branches on are untouched\n    * The billing portal link works: the return URL handed to the payment platform is now the page's absolute URL, where it used to be root-relative and Polar refused it with nothing in the log, so the \"Update payment method\" button sent the customer straight back with an error\n    * The billing portal is reachable again for every provider. `provider_customer_ref` on a subscription event is now read under the older `customer` spelling too \u2014 which is what every shipped driver was writing, so no Polar subscription ever had a portal \u2014 and a management call whose answer names the customer stamps it onto a row that has none, which is the only door open on a platform that has no customer until the first payment lands\n    * The portal button is drawn from the row rather than by minting a session on every render. Deciding whether to show it used to mean asking the payment platform for a one-shot portal URL on every single page view: the customer waited on that latency to read their own billing history, and a platform having a bad afternoon made the button quietly disappear. The session is now created when the button is pressed\n    * A subscription event the store could not apply is answered 500 so the platform redelivers it, instead of 200 and forgotten. A renewal whose order could not be minted used to leave the money taken, the period unadvanced and nothing but a log line; it now comes back until it lands, and the invoice reference keeps a redelivered renewal to exactly one order. A refusal the state machine will make again \u2014 an out-of-order `past_due` for a paused subscription \u2014 is still logged and answered, because retrying it could never help\n    * A subscription event for an agreement whose activation never arrived can find its row again. The order-hash fallback used to be for `activated` alone, so a store that missed the activation dropped every `past_due`, `cancelled` and `revoked` after it while the row sat at `incomplete` for ever. Any event carrying the hash may now open an `incomplete` row with no conflicting reference, and stamps the reference when it does\n    * A provider that reports a subscription the store has already ended as live again is written onto that subscription's timeline and into the Grav log, with the provider's own status, instead of being ignored in silence. Nothing is reactivated \u2014 a cancellation is final here \u2014 but a platform still billing a customer the store thinks has left is now something a merchant can find. Written once per agreement and event, so a redelivery does not fill the timeline\n    * A checkout the provider refused and the customer retried successfully is put back into provider mode, with the refusal note cleared and the platform that actually took it recorded. The row used to stay in invoice mode behind a live agreement, so the store's own tick would start emailing pay links for periods the platform was already charging the card for\n    * **Two changes to one subscription arriving at once no longer undo each other.** A merchant cancelling while the payment provider's renewal webhook landed could leave the cancelled subscription active again, with a new billing date the merchant had just taken off it. Both writers checked the status against their own copy of the row, and neither held anything while it wrote, so both were told the change was allowed. Every status change now locks the subscription, reads it back and checks the change is still allowed against what it finds before writing, so the second one is refused\n    * A subscription row opened by a refused or abandoned checkout picks up the corrected email address, and the customer id where a guest signed in between attempts. The account tab matches subscriptions to customers by email, so a stale address hid a subscription from the person paying for it\n    * An illegal pause is a 422 with `illegal_transition` whatever the billing mode. The transition is checked before the platform is asked anything, so a provider-mode subscription no longer answers 200 with the refusal buried in the response body\n    * The first period's link row is rewritten to the period the platform actually opened. It kept the period computed at checkout, so the customer's payments table printed dates their own receipt disagreed with \u2014 minutes usually, a whole day for anybody who opened a checkout at ten to midnight and paid after it\n    * A subscription delivery that named an agreement this store has no row for now says so in the delivery log. It used to be written as `subscription:past_due`, character for character the same row a delivery that updated a live subscription wrote, so a merchant whose add-on was switched off, or whose subscription rows were lost in a restore, read a log full of healthy-looking rows and had no way to tell that not one of them did anything. The listener reports the outcome back and the row reads `subscription:past_due:unmatched`\n    * A renewal for a subscription this store has already cancelled is written down instead of dropped in silence. The platform is saying it has taken money for a period, and the store both believes the agreement is over and used to keep no record that the message arrived: no timeline entry, no log line, and a delivery row reading `subscription:renewed` that looked exactly like one that worked. Nothing is reactivated, because a cancellation is final here, but the subscription's timeline and the Grav log now both say what the platform charged and that no renewal order was created, and the delivery log row reads `subscription:renewed:refused`. Written once per invoice, so a redelivery does not repeat it and a second billed period is said again\n    * The platform's id for the customer is taken off every event that carries one, not only the activation. A store that missed an activation delivery, or an agreement older than the release that started reading the key, had no billing portal for the life of the subscription, which is the only place a card can be replaced and the only way a past-due customer recovers. Polar sends the reference on its dunning notice, which is the delivery it matters most on. Only onto a row that has none: an event naming a different customer is a driver bug, not a correction to apply blind\n    * A scheduled pause is dated by the platform rather than by local arithmetic. The date the customer is shown came from this store's own `current_period_end`, which drifts from the platform's calendar, so the page could promise the 14th while the platform took one more payment on the 15th and stopped billing on the 16th. A driver that reports its own effective date now sets the date on the row, and the local period end is the fallback for one that does not\n    * Calling off a scheduled pause records the date that was cleared. The timeline entry carried an empty payload, so it could say a pause had been called off but not which day it was going to happen on, which is the one detail a customer asking about it will name\n    * **Each retry of a failed renewal counts.** Failures are deduplicated on the invoice reference so a redelivery cannot inflate the dunning counter, but a platform retrying the same invoice is not a redelivery: Stripe retried one invoice eight times over three weeks, delivered eight `invoice.payment_failed` events, and the row finished dunning reading `failed_attempts = 1` with one timeline entry for what was really three weeks of trying. The key now includes the try number where a driver names one (`attempt_count` in the event's raw payload, `attempt` accepted as a synonym); a driver that names none keeps exactly the old behavior\n    * **A store with more than two hundred subscriptions falling due at once invoices all of them.** A subscription that has been reminded is still due until it is paid or it lapses, so every sweep picked up the same first two hundred and the customers behind them were never reached at all. A sweep now carries on from where the last one stopped, and starts over from the earliest billing date once it reaches the end\n    * **A renewal invoice is matched to the order it belongs to.** A reference such as `invoice\/2026\/9` is kept inside the order's own stored data, and the way it was looked up could miss it, so a period the platform had already billed could be treated as a new one. The stored references are decoded before they are compared, which is exact\n    * **Two workers writing to one subscription at the same moment cannot lose one of the changes.** Different pieces of the same subscription's stored data, written at the same instant, could overwrite one another on PostgreSQL or fail outright on MariaDB. The update takes a lock on the row before it reads it"}}},"kahunacart-newsletters":{"name":"KahunaCart Newsletters","version":"1.1.17","description":"Email marketing for KahunaCart \u2014 subscribers with a real consent record, lists, branded campaigns, and open and click numbers of your own","description_html":"<p>Email marketing for KahunaCart \u2014 subscribers with a real consent record, lists, branded campaigns, and open and click numbers of your own<\/p>","description_plain":"Email marketing for KahunaCart \u2014 subscribers with a real consent record, lists, branded campaigns, and open and click numbers of your own","icon":"envelope","author":{"name":"Trilby Media, LLC","email":"hello@trilby.media"},"dependencies":[{"name":"grav","version":">=2.1.0"},{"name":"kahunacart","version":">=1.4.0"},{"name":"email","version":">=5.1.0"}],"compatibility":{"grav":["2.0"]},"homepage":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/add-ons\/newsletters","docs":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/docs\/newsletters\/overview","keywords":"grav, premium, commerce, ecommerce, newsletter, email, marketing, campaigns, subscribers","bugs":"https:\/\/github.com\/getgrav\/grav-premium-issues\/labels\/kahunacart-newsletters","license":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/license","tag_name":"1.1.17","date":"2026-10-22T02:30:43Z","release_type":"stable","zipball_url":"https:\/\/getgrav.org\/download\/plugins\/kahunacart-newsletters\/1.1.17","repository":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/add-ons\/newsletters","premium":{"vendor":"KahunaCart","validator":"kahunacart","license_product":"kahunacart-newsletters","checkout_url":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/shop\/product\/kahunacart-newsletters","button":"Buy Now","filename":"grav-plugin-kahunacart-newsletters"},"slug":"kahunacart-newsletters","install_path":"user\/plugins\/kahunacart-newsletters","content_sha256":"5673edb9c86b03d431997821abc1f909f63cc2191e448ec4e312525a27ed99d0","changelog":{"1.1.17":{"date":"10\/21\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Newsletters through their own provider.** With Email 5.3.3's **Newsletter Mail Engine** set, everything this plugin sends (campaigns, automations, confirmations, tests) goes through that provider, while the rest of the site's mail stays on the mail engine. Health names both: \"Sending newsletters through Resend (API). The rest of the site's mail goes through Cloudflare.\" With no newsletter engine set, everything goes one way as before (newsletters kit 1.0.13)\n    * **No campaigns through a provider that only allows transactional mail**, such as Cloudflare Email Service. While the newsletters go through one, starting, resuming, catching up, retrying or test-sending a campaign is refused with the reason, a queued campaign pauses before anybody is mailed, an automation's message fails with the reason, and Health warns (newsletters kit 1.0.13)\n1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **Health no longer asks for a webhook a provider cannot have**, such as Cloudflare's, which has no delivery webhooks (newsletters kit 1.0.13)\n    * **Skipped webhook events are no longer errors in the log.** An event type the store does not act on, or another site's mail on a shared Resend account, wrote an error line every time (newsletters kit 1.0.13)\n"},"1.1.16":{"date":"10\/21\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Proof that a provider's webhook works.** Set up now sends one small test message through the site's own mail transport straight afterwards, and each provider card has a Send a test button that does the same. The card's Test line then says Waiting, Received with the seconds it took, Arrived and refused (the signing secret is wrong), or after two minutes Nothing arrived, with the likely causes. The test goes to the provider's own test address where its plugin names one (Resend's `delivered@resend.dev`, the SES mailbox simulator, with email-resend 1.1.2 and email-amazon 1.2.1), otherwise to the store's From address, never to a subscriber. Only the provider the site sends through can be tested (newsletters kit 1.0.12)\n    * **Health says which provider is sending**, at the top: \"Sending through Resend (API).\" (newsletters kit 1.0.12)\n1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **The webhook health check no longer counts mail sent before the webhook existed.** Straight after Set up on a store with past campaigns, Health warned that no event had ever arrived although mail had gone out, and all of that mail had gone out before the webhook. Only mail sent since Set up counts now (newsletters kit 1.0.12)\n"},"1.1.15":{"date":"10\/21\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **Ships its license.** A `LICENSE` file now comes with the plugin, and the blueprint links to the KahunaCart License at kahunacart.com\/license instead of the Grav Premium license\n1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **A campaign bigger than one batch no longer stops after the first batch.** When a batch of 50 went out inside the run's time limit and under the per-minute cap, the send job counted itself done and nothing queued the next batch, so the campaign stayed on Sending with the rest of its list waiting. A finished batch with people still waiting now goes back in the queue a second later. With the default 60 a minute and a fast mail provider this hit almost every campaign over 50 people (newsletters kit 1.0.10)\n"},"1.1.14":{"date":"10\/14\/2026","content":"1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **The customer link sweep logs at info, not error.** \"linked 1 subscriber to a customer record\" is a record of the sweep doing its job, and it was written to Grav's log as an ERROR, which set off anything that watches the log for errors. It is now an INFO line. Real failures, such as a sweep that could not be queued, still log as errors\n"},"1.1.13":{"date":"10\/13\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **You hear when a campaign has finished sending.** A new **Newsletter Campaign Finished** row under Notifications \u2192 Store owner sends one quiet message when a campaign has gone out to everyone it was aimed at, scheduled ones included, with its name, how many were sent (and how many failed, if any) and a link to it. A campaign that pauses, is cancelled or still has work to do says nothing, and a campaign sent on to newcomers later does not send a second message\n    * **Five of the newsletter's alerts can reach your phone.** A campaign that stopped itself and a bounce rate over your limit arrive as urgent messages; a provider that has gone quiet, a webhook with no secret behind it and a failing deliverability check arrive as normal ones, each with a \"Cleared\" message when it is fixed. Switch them off or choose their channels under Notifications \u2192 Store owner (KahunaCart 1.5.3 or later). The Email plugin missing, no From address, stripped unsubscribe headers and a segment that would not count stay on the dashboard only\n"},"1.1.12":{"date":"10\/12\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Campaigns carry a postal address.** US law (CAN-SPAM) and the rules in many other places ask for a physical mailing address in marketing email, and campaigns used to go out without one. There is a new **Postal Address** box on the Sending tab of the settings, under the From address, and whatever is in it is printed at the bottom of every campaign in small type, one line per line, in the HTML and in the plain text part. Leave it empty and a store that has filled in the invoice address in KahunaCart's settings (`invoice.store_address`) gets that one automatically; empty in both, campaigns go out as they did. A `plain` campaign carries it once, in the engine's plain layout. The double opt-in confirmation is a transactional message and has no address in its HTML. The setting is `postal_address` in `user\/config\/plugins\/kahunacart-newsletters.yaml`\n"},"1.1.11":{"date":"10\/11\/2026","content":"1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **Works with a shop mounted at the site root.** With KahunaCart's `route: '\/'` (core 1.5.0) the public newsletter pages get the same `kahunacart_base` the shop's own pages do, and the seeded campaigns' store links no longer end in a slash that the paths after them doubled\n"},"1.1.10":{"date":"10\/09\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **Subscribers follow a customer merge.** When KahunaCart merges one customer into another, the merged-away customer's address keeps working as an alias of the survivor, and so does the mailing list. The subscribers already linked to the merged-away customer are moved to the survivor when the merge happens (and counted in the merge's dry-run preview as `newsletters`), and a new subscriber, a customers import row or an order on the old address links to the survivor instead of the alias. Only the link to the customer moves: the subscribers stay separate, so somebody on the list under two addresses is still on it twice, and no status, consent record or list membership changes. Running the same merge again moves nothing. The subscribers go to the customer's current survivor, looked up when the link is written, so a merge that finishes after its target was itself merged away links them to the final customer and not to the alias, and every run, for any pair, also moves subscribers still linked to a customer that was merged away in that chain, so a retry repairs links an overlapping merge left behind. A store on a KahunaCart without customer merging links exactly as before\n"},"1.1.9":{"date":"10\/04\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Tables have a search box.** Campaigns, a campaign's recipients, suppressions, an automation's people, automations, templates, the lists, tags and segments on the Audience screen, a campaign's links, a person's lists and what they were sent, past imports and every report get a box above the table that looks as each letter is typed. Every word has to be in the row, in any order and without regard to case. A table inside a panel only gets its box once it has more than eight rows, and the sample tables (who a draft would reach, the first rows of an import, the first ten of a segment) have none. A table that holds its whole answer hides the rows that do not match and says \"3 of 12\" beside the box; a table that pages asks the server and goes back to page one, because the row somebody wants is rarely on the page in hand, and a search that finds nothing keeps its box instead of saying there is nothing here. The subscribers table and the pickers keep the searches they had. `GET {prefix}\/campaigns` (name and subject), `\/campaigns\/{id}\/sends` (address, message id and error), `\/suppressions` (address, note and provider) and `\/flows\/{id}` (address and name, for the people) take `q`, applied before the sort, the count and the page, so `total` is the search's while the status counts stay over the whole table; a `%` or `_` is looked for as itself, and with no `q` each answers exactly as it did. The MCP tools `list_campaigns`, `list_campaign_recipients`, `list_suppressions` and `get_automation` take the same `q`. `Support\\ListSearch` reads it, beside `Support\\SortOrder`, and each repository names its columns in a `searchColumns()` next to its `SORTS`\n1. [](#improved)\n    * **Built on the newsletter kit 1.0.9.** The search boxes above come with it. Nothing about a store's setup changes\n    * **\"Total spent\" in a segment counts orders in the store's base currency only.** On a store that sells in more than one currency it added every order's total together, so 25.00 dollars, 50.00 euro and 120,000 yen made a customer who had spent 1,275.00. It now adds up the orders in the base currency, which is the figure KahunaCart's own customer screens show first, and the field is labelled \"Total spent, base currency orders only\" so nobody builds a segment on the wrong idea. Orders in other currencies don't count towards it. A store that sells in one currency sees no change at all\n    * **Needs KahunaCart 1.4.0.** The segment uses the totals-per-currency work that arrives with it, so the add-on asks for 1.4.0 or later\n"},"1.1.8":{"date":"10\/01\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **Built on the newsletter kit 1.0.8.** The change below comes with it. Nothing about a store's setup changes\n    * **Help under a field follows the admin's Help text setting.** On the campaign, segment, automation, list and import screens the help under a field now shows below its label or in a tooltip behind an info icon, whichever the admin is set to\n"},"1.1.7":{"date":"09\/29\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Built on the newsletter kit 1.0.7.** The change below comes with it. Nothing about a store's setup changes, and every address, route, permission and MCP tool name is the same\n    * **Newsletters name the store the way the store's own emails do.** Campaigns and the signup confirmation called the store by the site's title, so on a site where the store is one part of something bigger the footer read \"\u00a9 2026 Grav CMS\", the confirmation said \"start hearing from Grav CMS\" and `{{ store.name }}` gave \"Grav CMS\". They now follow KahunaCart's **Name the Store in Emails** (`emails.use_store_name`, KahunaCart 1.3.6): with it on, the header, the footer, the confirmation's subject and text, and `store.name` all use the store's name (`seller.name`, then `invoice.store_name`, then the site title), exactly as the order emails do. It's off by default, and on KahunaCart 1.3.5 and older, which have no such setting, nothing changes\n1. [](#improved)\n    * **One name on the Account page, not two.** The Account page had Profile's **Full name** (the account's) and, in Email preferences, **Your name** (the subscriber's), which saved to different places and drifted apart. For a signed-in customer the Email preferences panel no longer has a name box: the subscriber takes the account's name when the panel saves, and again whenever the profile is saved. An account with no name leaves the subscriber's name alone. The token preference center and signup forms keep their name box. `partials\/newsletter-account-tab.html.twig` no longer draws the field; a theme copy that still does is heard only when the account has no name\n"},"1.1.6":{"date":"09\/28\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Built on the newsletter kit 1.0.6.** The changes below come with it. Nothing about a store's setup changes, and every address, permission and MCP tool name is the same; the one new admin route has no MCP tool\n    * **See the email a campaign sent, and any one customer's copy of it.** A campaign that has gone out has a Show the email button, which opens the email on the campaign's screen with a sample subscriber. Every recipient it went to has a View button in the recipient table, and so does every row of a subscriber's \"What they were sent\"; either opens that person's own copy, with their name, the subject line a subject test gave them and any coupon code minted for them. The layout and their name are as they are today. Viewing it is never an open or a click, and the unsubscribe and preferences links in it do nothing, so nobody can be unsubscribed from the admin by accident\n1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **View in browser shows the reader's own copy.** The \"having trouble reading this?\" page showed a sample coupon code and always the first subject line of a subject test. It now shows the code minted for that reader and the subject line they were sent\n    * **The confirmation page's tab title reads properly.** After the signup link confirmed someone, the browser tab showed the page title as the theme wrote it in HTML, so a theme that separates the page and site name with `&middot;` showed \"You are on the list &middot; HB Tack\". It now shows \"You are on the list \u00b7 HB Tack\"\n"},"1.1.5":{"date":"09\/26\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Built on the newsletter kit 1.0.5.** The change below comes with it. Nothing about a store's setup changes, and every address, route, permission and MCP tool name is the same\n    * **Every table sorts by its headings.** Press a column's heading to sort the table by it, and press it again to reverse the order. Dates, counts and rates start with the biggest, and words start from A; an empty value, like a campaign nobody has opened yet, always goes last. Subscribers, a list's people, Campaigns, a campaign's recipients, Suppressions and an automation's people sort on the server, so the order covers everybody and not just the page on screen, and it stays in the address so a reload or Back keeps it. Every other table sorts where it is. Each table opens in its usual order until somebody presses a heading\n    * **The MCP list tools take the same sort.** `list_subscribers`, `list_campaigns`, `list_campaign_recipients`, `list_suppressions` and `get_automation` accept an optional `sort` and `dir`\n"},"1.1.4":{"date":"09\/26\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **Built on the newsletter kit 1.0.4.** The changes below come with it. Nothing about a store's setup changes, and every address, route, permission and MCP tool name is the same\n    * **A signup to more than one list says \"the lists\".** Checking two lists on the signup form, or naming two in the subscribe route's `lists` or the `newsletter` form action's `lists`, was answered \"...to be added to the list\" or \"You are on the list\". It now says \"the lists\"; one list checked is still \"the list\"\n    * **Rates keep their decimal.** Open, click, bounce and unsubscribe rates on the campaigns list, a campaign's own screen, the reports and the chart tooltips read \"8.0%\" beside \"0.2%\", rather than \"8%\", and the campaign performance report's tooltip has its percent sign\n    * **The Postmark card says what to do.** Before its server token is saved, Postmark has no Set up button, and its card had an empty gap where the card beside it says nothing is registered yet. It now says \"Add this address in Postmark by hand. The steps are below.\"\n"},"1.1.3":{"date":"09\/26\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **Built on the newsletter kit 1.0.3.** The changes below come with it. Nothing about a store's setup changes, and every address, route, permission and MCP tool name is the same\n    * **A signup's answer appears where the person is looking.** When the signup form takes an address, its fields and button give way to the answer in place, and the answer takes the focus so a screen reader reads it out; it used to be printed under the button, often out of view. A refusal scrolls into view and leaves the fields to correct. The partial's markup is unchanged; only its script moved, so a theme's copy of the partial keeps working as it did\n    * **Checkboxes are checked, not ticked.** The preference center and the account's Email preferences say \"Choose what you want to hear about.\", a signup refused for an unchecked consent box says \"Please check the box\", and the checkout opt-in note, the automations' per-run help and the no-events alert say checked, run and selected\n    * **One name for the preference center.** A subscriber's consent history and their Lists table now both say \"Preference center\" (the table said \"Preferences\")\n    * **Report charts write dates the way the rest of the admin does**, \"Jul 12\" on the campaigns report and on list growth (they read \"12 Jul\" and \"2026-07-12\"). The campaigns report's chart names its points by date (`Y-m-d`) for an MCP caller too\n    * **`#\/health` opens the Health tab**, the same as `#\/deliverability`\n    * **Under \"Sent to\", something the number does not already say**: the lists a campaign went to, or \"of 298\" while it is still sending, rather than \"298 total\". An automation's Left card reads \"Most often: marked it as spam\", its Moving card \"On a step the worker runs next\", and a webhook address wraps where its line is full rather than after the first hyphen\n2. [](#bugfix)\n    * **An automation names a tag by its name.** Its trigger read \"When the tag win-back is added\" and its tag steps \"Add the tag win-back\", the code the automation stores; they now use the tag's name, as the add-to-list step already names its list. The flows answers carry it as `trigger_name` and a tag step's `tag_name`\n"},"1.1.2":{"date":"09\/25\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **Built on the newsletter kit 1.0.2.** The fixes below come with it. Nothing about a store's setup changes, and every address, route, permission and MCP tool name is the same\n    * **American spelling throughout.** Everything a merchant or a subscriber reads, in the admin, the public pages, the emails, the command help, the MCP descriptions and the docs, now says center, color, canceled, enroll, recognize and percent (the preference center, \"Enroll somebody\", a canceled campaign, \"When a subscription is canceled\"). Translation keys, config keys, stored values such as the `cancelled` status and the `subscription_cancelled` trigger, and URLs are unchanged, so nothing a store saved or linked to moves. The demo seed's copy follows (\"in two new colors\")\n    * **The MCP manifest names every provider.** `generate_provider_secret` and `set_up_provider_webhook` take all seven providers (`mailersend` was missing from the first, and the second said SMTP2GO was the only provider whose API could create its webhook, when SendGrid, Mailgun, Resend, MailerSend and Amazon SES can with the key on file and Postmark with its server token), and `create_list` and `update_list` take `public`, which the API always accepted. `bin\/generate-mcp.php` now fills the template with the kit's own filler, with every capability and a store's words, and writes the same manifest otherwise\n2. [](#bugfix)\n    * **A signup form's own consent sentence is the one recorded.** A form given its own `consent_text` printed it, but the store's sentence was what got recorded against the person. The signup partial now carries the sentence it shows back to the route, signed with the site secret (a hidden `consent_form` field), and that sentence is recorded. A form with an empty `consent_text` shows the store's sentence while the store has one, rather than drawing no box, and the partial's script sends `consent` only when there is a box to tick. A theme's copy of the partial made before this keeps working and records the store's sentence; copy the new one to record a form's own\n    * **A new campaign starts from Track Opens and Track Clicks.** The campaign editor ticked both for every new campaign, whatever the settings said; it now starts from the settings\n    * **A campaign's cards and its chart count the same people.** Somebody who opened a message and then marked it as spam was counted by the chart and not by the Opened and Delivered cards. Both now count the delivered, opened and clicked stamps, and an open or a click after a complaint is counted like any other\n    * **A segment's editor says when the segments list shows another count.** The list shows the count last taken and the editor counts now, so the two could disagree with nothing on screen to say why; the editor now says so, and Recount brings the list up to date\n    * **The send-times heatmap gives the day names room**, so Wednesday no longer runs over the first hour, and a fresh report read in one millisecond says \"1 millisecond\"\n    * **An automation's steps read as the store would send them.** An email step shows its subject with the store's name filled in, and an add-to-list step names the list rather than its code\n    * **The Add people dialog's help describes both choices** rather than the one the list's setting picked, and what a bulk action or Add people did is drawn in the success colors when it worked\n    * **A draft campaign offers Delete, not \"Cancel it\".** Cancel is offered only to a campaign on its way (scheduled, sending, paused or stopped on failures)\n    * **The automations report names only the exit reasons there are.** Its definition listed \"no longer matching the automation's segment\", which never happens, and left out a suppressed address, a failed step and a deletion\n    * **The Welcome series recipe points at a trigger that exists.** Its note told a store without double opt-in to switch to \"When somebody joins a list\"; it now says \"When somebody signs up\"\n    * **The demo seed tells a whole story.** `bin\/plugin kahunacart-newsletters seed` now writes a consent history for every seeded person, the sends, opens and clicks of an automation's email steps, and dates nothing after the moment it ran; segments are counted after the automations have tagged people. Its rows differ from 1.1.1's for the same seed\n    * **Wording.** `export:csv --search` matches anywhere in an address or a name, and its help now says so (it said \"starting with\"); the From Name help says a campaign can use a different name in its editor, and a list through the API\n    * **Removed `unsubscribe.confirm_on_get`.** The setting was never read: the unsubscribe link has always opened a page with a button. It is gone from the Signup settings, `kahunacart-newsletters.yaml` and the docs, and a value left in a site's config does nothing, as before\n"},"1.1.1":{"date":"09\/25\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **A subscriber's page shows every list and edits tags in place.** Each list is drawn with where the person stands on it (subscribed, waiting to confirm, left, or not on it) with Add to list or Remove beside it, and a list they left themselves says so and offers nothing. Tags are chips you can take off one by one, with a box that adds an existing tag or a new one\n    * **Bulk actions on subscribers.** The Subscribers table has a tick box per row, one for the page, \"Select all N matching these filters\" and a bar that adds people to a list or takes them off one, adds or removes a tag, unsubscribes them from everything, or deletes them with a counted confirm. A big selection is worked through 500 at a time with a running count. The same is `POST \/kahunacart\/newsletters\/subscribers\/bulk` (`kahunacart.newsletters.manage`) and the MCP tool `bulk_subscribers`\n    * **A list has its own page.** Clicking a list on the Audience tab opens it with its name, description and settings, an Edit button, Add people (paste addresses, optionally with names) and its people in the subscribers table, with chips for where they stand on the list and the same bulk bar\n    * **Adding somebody asks how they agreed.** Every add from the admin (Add somebody, Add to list on a person's page, a selection, pasted addresses) offers \"Send them an invite to confirm\" or \"Add as subscribed, they already agreed\" with a required note saying where. The default follows the list's own \"Ask people to confirm\". Suppressed, bounced and complained addresses are never added, and anybody who left the list, or every list, themselves is skipped with the reason\n    * **The Audience tab's counts open the table they count.** A list's count opens the Subscribers table filtered to the people subscribed to that list, a tag's count to the people carrying it, and the filter is drawn so it can be seen and cleared; the filters travel in the address, so Back and reload keep them. `GET \/kahunacart\/newsletters\/subscribers` takes `list_status` beside `list`, and so does the MCP tool `list_subscribers`\n    * **Every change to somebody's consent is kept.** Each signup, confirmation, admin add or invite, unsubscribe, return to a list, import row, bounce, complaint and automation \"add to list\" writes one row into a consent history (who, when, which list, how, the admin's note, and the address and browser a person's own change came from), never capped or rewritten. It is shown under Consent on the subscriber's page, answered as `consent_log`, and deleted with the person. Migration `newsletters_0013_consent_events`\n    * **A campaign can go to several lists, or to every list with a segment.** The campaign editor keeps its \"Send it to\" list and gains \"Also send to\" boxes for the others and an \"All lists, with a segment\" choice, with a live count of who it would reach. Each person gets one email however many of the lists they are on, and the unsubscribe link takes them off the campaign's lists they are on and leaves their others alone. The API and the MCP tools take `list_ids`, and `GET \/kahunacart\/newsletters\/campaigns\/audience` counts a choice before it is saved. A campaign to one list is stored and sent exactly as before. Migration `newsletters_0014_campaign_lists`\n    * **`site.name` and `site.url` merge tags.** They read the same two values as `store.name` and `store.url`, and a template may use either spelling. The editors keep offering `store.*`\n    * **Export one person's data.** A subscriber's page has \"Export their data\", which downloads one JSON file with everything the newsletter holds about them: profile, lists, tags, consent history, every send with its opens and clicks, what the mail provider reported, automation walks, suppressions and the imports that named them. The same is `GET \/kahunacart\/newsletters\/subscribers\/{id}\/export` (`kahunacart.newsletters.manage`, like the CSV export) and the MCP tool `export_subscriber_data`\n    * **One signup can join several lists.** The public subscribe route takes `lists` beside `list`, the Form plugin's `newsletter` action takes a `lists` parameter, and `kahunacart_newsletter_form()` a `lists` option, which draws the lists as boxes to tick (named by their list names, none ticked for them) and sends only the ones ticked; at least one has to be. A form with one list draws exactly the markup it always did. Each list keeps its own double opt-in, one confirmation email covers them all, a code that names no list is left out, and one signup counts once against the rate limit\n    * **The import preview says what the run would do.** Before you press Start, the admin's import screen shows how many people would be new, already here, skipped and suppressed against the list you picked, and why (duplicates in the file, people who left the list themselves, suppressed addresses and so on), the same count `import:csv --dry-run` makes\n    * **An import can say where its people agreed.** A new \"Where did these people agree?\" field on the import screen (`basis` on the API, `--basis` on `import:csv`) is kept in each imported person's consent history. Left empty, the file's name is kept there, as before\n    * **A file headed plain `email` imports without picking a column.** Headings that plainly name a field (`email`, `E-mail`, `Email address`, `Name`, `First name`, `Last name` and the like) are picked for you, on the import screen and on the command line\n\n1. [](#improved)\n    * **One-click unsubscribe now leaves only the campaign's lists.** The Unsubscribe button a mail client shows beside a campaign (Gmail, Apple Mail and others) now takes the person off the lists that campaign went to and leaves their other lists alone, the same as the unsubscribe link at the bottom of the email. It used to take them off every list. Confirmation emails and automation emails still take them off every list.\n    * **Requires KahunaCart 1.3.2 or later.** KahunaCart 1.3.2 brings the trusted site address (Site Addresses, `security.site_urls`), which the double opt-in confirmation now uses: its link is built only on an address the store vouches for, and with none set the email waits on the queue, without using up its attempts, until there is one, rather than going out with a link nobody can press or one on whatever host a signup claimed. KahunaCart fills the address in from an administrator's own on their first dashboard visit\n    * **The public newsletter pages answer 503 while migrations are pending.** On a MySQL or PostgreSQL store, which migrates by hand, the subscribe, confirm, unsubscribe, preference, tracking and webhook addresses answer 503 with `Retry-After` until `bin\/plugin kahunacart migrate` has run, rather than a 404 or new code running over the old tables. A person gets a page saying to try again in a few minutes, a mail provider's one-click POST and webhooks get a bare 503 they retry, and the open pixel still answers its image. One line goes to the log. A store that is up to date pays nothing for the check\n    * **API changes a store's own scripts may notice.** `POST \/kahunacart\/newsletters\/subscribers` without a `mode` now follows the list's double opt-in (an invite on a list that asks), `mode: subscribed` needs a `note`, and it refuses (422) somebody who unsubscribed themselves. `POST \/kahunacart\/newsletters\/subscribers\/{id}\/resubscribe` needs a `note` and no longer takes them off the lists it was not asked about. The bulk route's unsubscribe or delete over an empty filter needs `all: true`. The MCP tools `add_subscriber`, `update_subscriber` and `resubscribe_subscriber` describe the new rules\n    * **Rate limits.** A mail client's one-click unsubscribe with a valid link is never rate limited, so a busy send no longer leaves people who pressed the button still subscribed. A signup through the Form plugin's `newsletter` action now counts in the same per-visitor subscribe limit as the public signup route (10 an hour by default)\n    * **A confirmation an admin or an import asked for waits for the click.** The confirmation page presses its own button for somebody who signed up themselves; for somebody an admin invited or an import put on a list, it now waits for them to press it, so a link scanner that runs scripts cannot sign up a person who never filled in a form\n    * **Mail provider events no longer keep the address.** A bounce, complaint or delivery report is stored without the email address (the hashed address was always kept for matching), an erasure now deletes the person's provider events too, and migration `newsletters_0015_event_addresses` takes the address out of the events stored before\n    * **A retried or replayed provider event counts once.** Providers retry webhooks, and every copy used to be a new row, so one temporary failure sent three times counted as three soft bounces and suppressed the address. The same event again is now recorded as already received and changes nothing, and `webhook:replay` says so instead of \"would suppress\". Migration `newsletters_0016_event_keys`\n    * **A signup through the site's own form is recorded as `form`.** A signup through the signup partial, with JavaScript or without, or through a theme's own form on the site, was recorded as an `api` signup. JSON from anywhere else (a script on another server) is still `api`, and a caller may name it with a new optional `source` field, `form` or `api`; any other value is ignored\n    * **What a subscriber reads.** The confirmation page's button says \"Yes, sign me up\" rather than \"Yes, keep me on the list\", the unsubscribe page says \"Done, you are off the list\", and the campaign's plain-text footer reads \"Example Shop (https:\/\/example.com\/shop)\"\n    * **Refusing a suppressed address says so plainly.** Adding, putting back or confirming a suppressed address now answers \"This address is on the suppression list, so it is never mailed. Remove it from Suppressions first.\", a string a site can reword in its language file\n    * **The Health tab's percentages.** \"0.3%%\" on the Health tab and in `deliverability:check` is now \"0.3%\"\n    * **Built on version 1.0.1 of the newsletter kit**, pinned exactly\n    * **No em dashes in the admin's words.** The admin screens' sentences that carried one (the Health tab's findings, the report definitions, the import and segment hints and a few more) now read with plain punctuation\n\n1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **Saving a subscriber who was waiting to confirm took them off their list.** The edit form's list boxes were ticked only for subscribed lists, so a pending one went up unticked and the save unsubscribed them from it. The form now saves only the name and the language; lists and tags have their own panels\n    * **Nobody who left by their own hand is put back by anybody else.** An admin's Add somebody, Add to list, the subscriber form's lists, a bulk add, an automation's \"add to list\" step and a CSV or customer import all skip a person who unsubscribed from that list, or from everything, themselves, and say why. An admin's Unsubscribe or a suppression pressed on somebody already gone no longer writes over how they left, which is what used to let the admin's own add put them straight back. \"Put them back on\" with a note saying they asked is the one way past it\n    * **The admin's CSV import did the opposite of its \"Ask everybody to confirm\" box.** Left off, as its hint says to for an export from another tool, it imported everybody waiting to confirm and emailed each of them a confirmation; ticked, it imported them as already agreed. It now does what the box says. The API and the command line were right all along\n    * **The campaign audience sample showed no names.** The people listed under a campaign's audience count now show their names\n    * **An import no longer rewrites how somebody joined a list they were already on.** Importing a person already on the list set that membership's source to `import`; it is now left as it was\n    * **The mail client's unsubscribe button works with a From address written with a name.** With the From email set as `Name <address>`, the `List-Unsubscribe` header carried the name inside its mailto, which no mail client can use. It now carries the address only; the one-click HTTPS unsubscribe was not affected\n    * **Somebody already subscribed who signed up for another list that asks people to confirm was never asked.** They were left waiting on that list with no email to answer, and anybody who typed their address into the form could put them there. They now get the confirmation email for that list and stay waiting until they press it; the page says the same as it does for anybody else\n    * **The customer import's preview counted people who had left the list themselves as already here.** The run skips them, so the preview now does too, against the list picked below it (it counts again when you pick another), with a \"Left the list themselves\" figure beside the others\n    * **The Subscribers table left out the lists somebody was waiting to confirm.** The Lists column now names them too, marked \"(pending)\", so somebody who has not confirmed yet is not shown as on no list\n"},"1.1.0":{"date":"09\/25\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **The newsletter engine is a shared package, bundled inside the plugin.** The engine now comes from Trilby Media's newsletter kit, which the plugin ships in `vendor-prefixed\/` under the same class names as before and wired to the database classes KahunaCart bundles, so it still shares KahunaCart's connection, migrator and job queue. Nothing changes for a store: tables, migrations, routes, settings, links already mailed and class names stay exactly as they were\n    * **Requires KahunaCart 1.3.0 or later and Grav 2.1.0 or later.** KahunaCart 1.3.0 is the first release that bundles the database classes the engine is built on, and it needs Grav 2.1.0. With an older KahunaCart the plugin stays off and says so once in the Grav log\n    * **KahunaCart's segment fields, flow triggers, merge tags, coupons, customer import and store recipes are added by the plugin's KahunaCart side.** The nine customer and order segment fields, the seven order, cart and subscription triggers, the three `customer.*` merge tags, coupon minting, the customer import (its two admin routes, its job and `import:customers`), the customer card route and the post-purchase and win-back recipes now reach the newsletter engine through a small registry, so the engine no longer reads a KahunaCart table itself. Field codes, trigger codes, tags, labels, routes, job types, recipe keys, their order in the editors and the SQL a segment runs are all unchanged, so saved segments, flows and templates keep working as they are. `GET \/kahunacart\/newsletters\/labels` also answers `capabilities`, saying which of customers, products, coupons and subscriptions the site has (all four on a KahunaCart store). The segment recount's \"last queued\" mark moved from KahunaCart's settings table to the newsletter's own state table, so the first worker run after updating recounts segments once.\n    * **The newsletter emails ship with the engine.** `emails\/newsletter-campaign`, `emails\/newsletter-confirmation` and `emails\/newsletter-plain-layout.html.twig` moved from `templates\/emails\/` into the engine's own resources, which the plugin adds to Twig after its own `templates\/`, and the demo recipe and newsletter page stub moved with them. Template names are unchanged, a theme's copy of any of them still wins, and every email renders byte for byte as before\n1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **The providers card shows the new state straight after \"Set up\".** The three settings fields shared one helper, and whichever loaded first built it for all three. When the transport or worker field loaded first, the providers card's refresh after setting up a webhook came back from the cache and kept showing the old state until the page was reloaded. All three fields now carry the same helper.\n    * **A signup through a Form plugin form records the same address as every other signup.** The newsletter form action stamped the consent record with Grav's client address, while the public signup route and the rate limiter used KahunaCart's, so on a store behind a proxy the two could disagree and the consent record could hold the proxy's address instead of the visitor's. The form action now uses KahunaCart's as well, which honours a forwarded address only from a proxy listed in `security.trusted_proxies`. A store behind a proxy or CDN that relied on Grav's `system.http_x_forwarded` setting (with a header such as `X-Forwarded-For` or `CF-Connecting-IP` switched on there, Grav took the address from that header on any request, whoever sent it) should list the proxy in KahunaCart's `security.trusted_proxies`, since consent records now use KahunaCart's trusted-proxy rule for both the storefront form and the Form-plugin action.\n"},"1.0.6":{"date":"09\/23\/2026","content":"1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **The storefront stylesheet is no longer added twice on the newsletter pages.** The subscribe, confirm, unsubscribe and preference pages put KahunaCart's own stylesheet on the page themselves, and looked for the file one folder too high to stamp it, so the link never matched the one KahunaCart adds and the browser downloaded the same sheet twice. It now finds the file through Grav, and each page links it once.\n"},"1.0.5":{"date":"09\/22\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **A failed send can be tried again, one at a time or all together.** A send the mailer refused, usually because the transport could not be reached for a moment, stayed failed for good, and the only way to reach that person was a new campaign. A failed row on a campaign's Recipients table now has a **Retry** button, and a sent campaign with failures has **Retry failed (N)** beside its other buttons. Only failed rows go back in the queue (bounces and complaints are never retried). The campaign runs again as the same campaign with the same report, nobody already mailed gets it twice, consent and suppression are checked again at delivery, and the counters are recounted when it finishes. `POST \/kahunacart\/newsletters\/campaigns\/{id}\/retry-failed` with an optional `send_id`, and the `retry_failed_campaign_sends` MCP tool\n\n1. [](#improved)\n    * **A scheduled campaign's screen leads with Edit, not Send it now.** The campaign is already on its way, so the orange button is **Edit** and **Send it now** is a plain button beside it. A draft keeps **Send it now** as the primary\n    * **The Sent to card on a scheduled campaign says who is queued and when**, as \"50 queued for Sep 22, 2026, 2:00 AM\" (`ADMIN_CAMPAIGN_QUEUED_FOR`). It read \"No list chosen\" even with a list on the campaign and the audience already taken. A campaign with a list but no count yet now says the audience is worked out when it starts, and \"No list chosen\" is kept for a campaign that really has none\n\n1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **A scheduled campaign now sends when its time comes.** Scheduling wrote the campaign as `scheduled` and queued its job for that moment, but nothing ever moved it on to `sending`, so the job woke up on time, found a campaign that was not `sending`, decided it was not its campaign and finished without mailing anybody. The job the campaign names now starts it when the moment has arrived, and waits if it wakes a little early. A job left behind by a reschedule still does nothing. Campaigns sent straight away were never affected\n    * **Editing a scheduled campaign shows when it is scheduled for.** The campaign editor always opened with an empty **Send at** box, so a campaign that had been scheduled looked like it had lost its time, and the button under the empty box read **Send it now**, one click from sending it hours early. A scheduled campaign now opens with its moment in the box in the browser's local time, and the button reads **Schedule it**. The schedule itself was always saved correctly\n    * **The Schedule it button lines up with the Send at box** instead of sitting halfway up its label\n    * **A campaign's Recipients chips name send statuses, not subscriber ones.** People queued for a campaign are `pending` sends, and the chip above them read \"Waiting to confirm\", the word for a subscriber who has not confirmed yet, even though everybody in the audience is confirmed. It now reads \"Waiting\", the same as the rows under it\n"}}},"kahunacart-shipping":{"name":"KahunaCart Shipping","version":"1.2.4","description":"Live carrier rates, boxes and packing, labels and tracking for KahunaCart","description_html":"<p>Live carrier rates, boxes and packing, labels and tracking for KahunaCart<\/p>","description_plain":"Live carrier rates, boxes and packing, labels and tracking for KahunaCart","icon":"truck","author":{"name":"Trilby Media, LLC","email":"hello@trilby.media"},"dependencies":[{"name":"grav","version":">=2.0.0"},{"name":"kahunacart","version":">=1.4.4"}],"compatibility":{"grav":["2.0"]},"homepage":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/add-ons\/shipping","docs":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/docs\/shipping\/overview","keywords":"grav, premium, commerce, ecommerce, shipping, carriers, rates, labels, tracking, shippo, easypost, shipengine, sendcloud","bugs":"https:\/\/github.com\/getgrav\/grav-premium-issues\/labels\/kahunacart-shipping","license":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/license","tag_name":"1.2.4","date":"2026-10-22T23:32:24Z","release_type":"stable","zipball_url":"https:\/\/getgrav.org\/download\/plugins\/kahunacart-shipping\/1.2.4","repository":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/add-ons\/shipping","premium":{"vendor":"KahunaCart","validator":"kahunacart","license_product":"kahunacart-shipping","checkout_url":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/shop\/product\/kahunacart-shipping","button":"Buy Now","filename":"grav-plugin-kahunacart-shipping"},"slug":"kahunacart-shipping","install_path":"user\/plugins\/kahunacart-shipping","content_sha256":"e3155e9b3b4f4ec62c59657bfd73fff22a90213385d6deabd6d5c06f065665ac","changelog":{"1.2.4":{"date":"10\/22\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **Ships its license.** A `LICENSE` file now comes with the plugin, and the blueprint links to the KahunaCart License at kahunacart.com\/license instead of the Grav Premium license\n"},"1.2.3":{"date":"10\/18\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **The ship-from address form works like the rest of the admin.** The country and the state are lists instead of boxes to type a code into, the street suggests addresses as it's typed on a store with address lookup set up, and pressing **Add this address** with something missing marks every box that needs filling in, all at once, instead of refusing one at a time. Needs KahunaCart 1.5.17; on an older KahunaCart the form is the plain one it was\n"},"1.2.2":{"date":"10\/17\/2026","content":"1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **Test the packer takes the order number on the receipt.** The panel on the Boxes tab asked for the order's database id, which is on no receipt, email or packing slip, so typing an order number answered \"That order has no lines to pack\" for every order. It now takes the number as printed, with or without its # and leading zeros (`1016`, `01057`, `#01057`), and still takes an id when no order has that number. A number that matches nothing says so instead. The route and the `preview_packing` MCP tool take it as `order`; `order_id` works as before\n"},"1.2.1":{"date":"10\/13\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Hear when automatic labels go wrong.** With Buy a label when an order is paid switched on, **No label was bought for a paid order** (normal) tells you when the parcels could not be planned, once per order per day. **A label could not be voided** (high) tells you when a label bought for an order that was then refunded or cancelled could not be refunded, whether the carrier said no straight away or later, the ninety days had passed or the request could not be made. Both name the order, give the reason in plain words rather than the carrier's own text, and say what to do\n    * **A webhook your store refuses is no longer silent.** A tracking delivery turned away for a wrong or missing secret, a wrong source, a bad signature or a driver that cannot read tracking now sends KahunaCart's Webhook rejected message, once per reason per day, instead of only appearing in the delivery log. Needs KahunaCart 1.5.3\n1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **A notification given its facts later no longer shares one dedupe key.** Raising an owner message with its data as a callable made the dedupe key from nothing, so the first message of the day for any order would silence the rest. No message sent before this release was affected\n"},"1.2.0":{"date":"10\/11\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Sendcloud is a fourth carrier aggregator.** Choose Sendcloud as the aggregator, paste the public key and secret key of a Sendcloud API integration on the new Sendcloud tab, and the store quotes, buys, cancels and tracks labels with the carriers your Sendcloud account offers, such as Royal Mail, DPD, PostNL, DHL and GLS. It works on Sendcloud's free plan. Quotes come from Sendcloud's own price list, a label's file comes back with the purchase, and tracking arrives through a webhook that Sendcloud signs with your secret key and every delivery is checked against. Register webhook on the Shipping carriers screen switches it on for your integration. Services delivered to a service point or a locker are left out for now\n    * **Sendcloud has no test environment, so test mode buys a free label.** In Test mode the quotes are the real ones, but every label is bought as Sendcloud's free \"Unstamped letter\", whatever service the sheet named. It is a real label that costs nothing and no carrier will carry, and the buy sheet and the label say so. Live mode buys the service that was quoted\n    * **A label bought twice is still one label.** Each Sendcloud purchase carries the store's own reference, so a purchase whose answer never arrived is found again rather than bought a second time, and a retry after a refused address goes out under a fresh reference\n    * **Customs declarations for Sendcloud** go out item by item with HS codes, and the Sendcloud tab has a VAT number and an EORI number for commercial exports, which Sendcloud asks for\n    * **Check connection warns when the store ships from the wrong country.** Sendcloud offers carriers from the country of your Sendcloud sender address, so a store shipping from anywhere else would be quoted nothing. The check names the integration and the sender country, and says so when they do not match, and a quote that comes back empty for that reason says why too\n2. [](#improved)\n    * **A customs declaration is asked for only at a customs border.** Parcels between EU countries, Monaco and Northern Ireland no longer need one, and the EU's own territories outside its customs area, such as the Canary Islands, Ceuta, Melilla, the \u00c5land Islands, the French overseas departments and Mount Athos, still do. The same rule decides it in the label planner and in every driver, so ShipEngine no longer refuses a label from France to Germany for want of a declaration\n    * **The Buy label sheet lists services cheapest first.** Services were listed in whatever order the aggregator answered in, so with Sendcloud the cheapest Royal Mail service could be eleventh of 33. They are now sorted by price, then by name, and when the order's method names no service the cheapest one is preselected\n    * **A webhook with nothing to act on yet is logged as ignored, not unreadable.** Sendcloud sends a status for every new parcel before the carrier has given it a tracking number. The webhook log on the Shipping carriers screen now says \"Read, nothing to act on yet\" for that, and keeps \"unreadable\" for a body that could not be read at all\n3. [](#bugfix)\n    * **A Sendcloud service code names its carrier correctly.** Service codes were split at the first underscore, which read Sendcloud's `royal_mailv2:tracked_24` as a carrier called `royal`. A colon is now looked for first, and Royal Mail and DPD UK on Sendcloud get core's own tracking links\n    * **A tracking update that arrives while its label is still being bought is no longer lost.** Sendcloud posts its first status before the purchase has answered, when the store does not yet know the tracking number, so that update was dropped. While a label from the same aggregator is being bought, such an update is now answered with a request to send it again, and the retry a few minutes later is accepted. This now covers a purchase tried again after a refusal, too, not only a new one\n    * **Two stores on one Sendcloud account no longer take each other's labels.** A copy of a store, or a test store that was reset, repeats order numbers and so the references Sendcloud knows a label by, and Sendcloud handed back the other store's label as this one. A label Sendcloud already has is now only taken when it is for the same order number, country and postcode; otherwise a new one is bought under the next reference, and the label says why\n    * **A Sendcloud label found again keeps the price it was quoted at.** Sendcloud does not say what a label cost, so a label whose purchase answer was lost and was found later was recorded at 0 with no currency. It now keeps the quoted price and its currency, and a free test label still costs 0\n    * **A full-page Sendcloud label found again is stored as a full page**, not as the smaller A6 label, and a PNG or ZPL label is fetched as itself\n    * **A Sendcloud cancellation that Sendcloud refused stays refused.** When Sendcloud said a label could no longer be cancelled and the parcel was still waiting to be sent, the label was shown as cancellation requested. It now shows Sendcloud's refusal, and only a shipment that says it is cancelled or being cancelled is shown as such\n    * **The store stops waiting on a queued Sendcloud cancellation 14 days after it asked for it**, counted from when it was asked rather than from the parcel's own dates, so a parcel with no dates on it is no longer waited on for ever\n    * **EasyPost and ShipEngine send a customs declaration at a customs border, not at a national one.** Madrid to the Canary Islands stays in Spain and still needs one, and now gets it; France to Germany crosses a border and needs none, and no longer gets one\n"},"1.1.8":{"date":"10\/08\/2026","content":"1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **The webhook address follows a changed API version.** It read a setting the API plugin does not have, so a store that had changed the API's version prefix was shown a webhook address, and a demo tracking link, still ending in `\/v1`\n2. [](#improved)\n    * The webhook and tracking addresses now come from KahunaCart's own `SiteUrl::api()` instead of being built here, so they agree with every other add-on. Needs KahunaCart 1.4.4 or later\n"},"1.1.7":{"date":"10\/04\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **The Labels list, Ship-from and Boxes have search boxes.** Type a few words and the rows that do not hold all of them drop away, in any order and whatever the case. Labels looks through the carrier, the service, the tracking number and the order number, and asks the server, so it finds a label on any page and the count and the pages follow the search; it works with the status chips and with the sort. Ship-from is searched by code or address and Boxes by code or name, as you type, and Ship-from only gets its box once it has more than eight addresses. `GET \/kahunacart\/shipping\/labels` takes `q` for the same thing. Needs KahunaCart 1.3.11 or later, which draws the box\n    * **A carrier account has a currency.** Each driver's tab has an **Account currency**, the currency that aggregator account is billed in, and left empty it is the store's own, which is what every store has always been quoted in. Rates are asked for in it and labels are bought in it, whatever currency an order was sold in. A store that sells in one currency with its account in that currency sees no change at all. There is a new Currencies page in the docs\n    * **A quote in another currency than the customer's cart is skipped, never relabelled.** KahunaCart 1.4 lets one store sell in several currencies, and there are no exchange rates yet to turn a carrier's price in one into another. So when the account is billed in a currency other than the cart's, the carrier is not asked at all, every method bound to it says \"This service cannot be quoted in the currency of this order\", and the merchant's fallback method carries the order. The skip goes in the log once per request at debug level, however many rates it covered. The request a driver receives already carries the account's currency, the order's and a place for the rate between them, so exchange rates have somewhere to go when they arrive\n    * **An order can be labelled from a carrier account in another currency.** Label prices used to be asked for in the order's currency and every other rate was dropped, so a euro order could not get a label from a dollar account. They are now asked for and bought in the account's currency, the label row keeps what the carrier charged and the currency it was charged in, and the Labels list, the label card, the Buy label sheet and the refund question print it in that currency with its own number of decimal places. The customs form on the same parcel stays in the order's currency\n    * **The demo carrier quotes in whatever currency the cart is in** when its **Currency it quotes in** is left empty, so a demo store can show carrier rates in every currency it sells in\n2. [](#improved)\n    * **A label's cost is read at the decimals of the currency it is in.** The purchase was told every currency has two decimal places, which is right for dollars and euros and would have read a yen price a hundred times too high. Needs KahunaCart 1.4.0 or later, which brings the base currency, the order builder's currency and the admin's money helper that takes a currency\n3. [](#bugfix)\n    * **Account-currency changes take effect in checkout immediately.** All four drivers include their account currency and selected carrier accounts in the quote cache key, so an old rate or currency refusal cannot linger for fifteen minutes after a setting changes\n    * **Sorting labels by Paid keeps currencies apart.** Labels are grouped by currency code before their costs are sorted, so yen, dollars and dinars are never compared as one amount. Labels in one currency keep their existing order\n    * **Demo carrier prices stay believable across currencies.** On stores selling in several currencies the demo formula uses reference USD cents and scales the whole charge with documented fixed demo factors and each currency's decimal places, rounding half up. Yen no longer reuses a cents amount as whole yen, and three-decimal currencies no longer reuse it as thousandths. These are invented demo prices, never live exchange rates; single-currency stores keep their original quotes byte for byte\n    * **The customs form says what currency the order is in.** It said USD on every store: the declaration looked for a currency on each order line, a line has none, and so the fallback won every time. A store selling in euros or pounds declared its parcels with the right amount and the wrong code beside it, to Shippo, EasyPost and ShipEngine alike. It now reads the order's own currency and flags a line that has none instead of guessing. A store that sells in dollars sees no change\n"},"1.1.6":{"date":"10\/01\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * Help text on the Shipping carriers screens now follows the admin's Help text setting, showing below the label or in a tooltip behind an info icon\n"},"1.1.5":{"date":"09\/30\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **The download no longer carries the plugin's tests.** `tests\/` and `phpunit.xml` stay in the repository and out of the release zip, 211 files fewer on every install. Nothing the plugin runs is affected\n"},"1.1.4":{"date":"09\/30\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **The tables on the Shipping screens sort by their column headings.** Press a heading on the Labels, Boxes or ship-from tables to sort by it, and press it again to turn the order round. The Labels list sorts on the server, so a heading orders every label and not only the page on screen, and pressing one goes back to page one. `GET \/kahunacart\/shipping\/labels` takes `sort` (`order`, `carrier`, `service`, `tracking`, `paid`, `status` or `bought`) and `dir`, a label with nothing in that column sorts last whichever way it runs, and any other `sort` is ignored, so the list is newest first as before. Needs KahunaCart 1.3.8 or later, which draws the headings\n"},"1.1.3":{"date":"09\/25\/2026","content":"1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **Installing through GPM no longer switches the add-on off.** The plugin loads its classes through Composer's `vendor\/autoload.php`, but `vendor\/` was left out of the repository and so out of the download GPM installs. Grav logged \"Plugin 'kahunacart-shipping' autoload failed: Failed opening required ...\/vendor\/autoload.php\" and turned the plugin off on every store that installed or updated it that way. The run-time half of `vendor\/` is part of the download now, and a test fails if it goes missing or if a development install is committed over it\n"},"1.1.2":{"date":"09\/23\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **Ready for KahunaCart 1.3, which bundles brick\/money under its own namespace.** A carrier's price is turned into cents by asking core's `Money::exponent()` for the currency's decimal places, instead of calling brick\/money's `Currency` class directly and falling back to a table of its own. From KahunaCart 1.3 the unprefixed `Brick\\Money\\Currency` does not exist, so the old code would quietly have used the table for every store. `Money::exponent()` has been in core since 1.0.0, so nothing changes on the KahunaCart this add-on already requires, and the plugin's own currency table is gone\n    * **The test suite runs against KahunaCart 1.3.** When it borrows the base plugin's dependencies it now also loads core's `vendor-prefixed\/autoload.php`, where brick\/money and YetiSearch live from 1.3. Against an older KahunaCart without that file nothing changes\n"},"1.1.1":{"date":"09\/21\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **Auto-buy waits until the order is paid.** It was built to buy the label the moment an order was placed, paid or not (it never actually did, see below), so a bank-transfer order sitting at pending, a card that was only authorised, or an order taken with a deposit at **Part paid** would all have got a label before the money was in. It now buys only once the order counts as paid: straight away for a card payment, and otherwise when the rest of the money arrives, whichever way it arrives \u2014 a pay link, a webhook, a capture, or the merchant marking it paid. An order paid at checkout gets one label, a payment delivered twice still buys one label, and a cancelled or refunded order voids a label bought later the same way it voids one bought at checkout. Buying a label by hand from the order screen works at any time\n2. [](#bugfix)\n    * **Auto-buy never actually bought anything.** The label planner hands the chosen service back beside the purchase options rather than inside them, and auto-buy passed the options on without it, so every automatic purchase was refused as incomplete before the carrier was asked and only a log line said so. The Buy label sheet and the demo seeder were never affected. There is now a test that runs auto-buy through core's real hook runner and the demo carrier and checks a label comes out the other end\n"},"1.1.0":{"date":"09\/16\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **The tracking webhook is two buttons now, on the Shipping carriers screen.** All three drivers could register their own webhook from the first release and nothing exposed any of it, so the only way to set inbound tracking up was four steps done by hand \u2014 one of which, on EasyPost, was running a `php -r` line to work out the value EasyPost has to sign with. There is a card under the driver's capability matrix now: **Generate secret** makes the random part of the address and saves it encrypted, **Register webhook** hands the address to the aggregator, and the card shows the address with the secret masked to its last four characters, a copy button carrying the real one, the last result and the last few deliveries. Registering adopts an existing webhook pointing at this store rather than adding a second, reports any older address still registered, and on EasyPost sets the derived signing key itself. A store nothing outside the building can reach \u2014 a `.test` hostname, a private address, plain `http` \u2014 is told so where the button is, rather than after registering something no carrier could ever deliver to. `bin\/plugin kahunacart-shipping webhook:secret` and `webhook:register [--reset]` do the same two things for a store whose setup is scripted, and there are three MCP tools beside them\n    * **A generated secret is encrypted the moment it is written, not the next time somebody presses Save.** It goes through core's own settings path rather than a second one of this plugin's, so the whole file is swept as it is written: the webhook secret the button generates and the API key typed into the tab beside it both end up encrypted at rest, and an already-encrypted value is left exactly as it is\n    * **EasyPost, as a second driver in the same plugin.** Pick it on the General tab, paste your keys into the EasyPost tab, and everything below the driver is exactly what it was: the same packer, the same boxes, the same labels table, the same tracking, the same screens. One account, one key, and rates from USPS, UPS, FedEx, DHL and the hundred-odd others behind it. It implements every interface the Shippo driver does \u2014 rates, carrier accounts, labels, voids, address validation, tracking, the inbound webhook and the connection check \u2014 and one thing Shippo cannot do at all. See docs\/carriers.md\n    * **EasyPost signs its webhook deliveries, and this checks every one of them.** `signed_webhooks` was in the capability matrix from the first release with Shippo answering **no**, because Shippo's only self-serve authentication on an inbound delivery is the secret in the address and a published list of source addresses \u2014 HMAC exists there but needs an account manager and ten business days. EasyPost signs by default: an `X-Hmac-Signature` header carrying an HMAC-SHA256 over the raw body, keyed on a secret set on the webhook at creation. Registration hands EasyPost that secret, and every delivery is checked before a byte of it is read \u2014 a signature that does not match is a **401** and a refused row in the delivery log, and so is a delivery with no signature at all. The card now prints a yes on that row for the first time, which is the whole reason the row is in the matrix\n    * **The signing key is derived from your webhook secret rather than being it.** That secret is also the token in the inbound address, and a URL is the part of a request that ends up in an access log, a referrer and a bug report somebody pastes into a ticket. So the key EasyPost actually signs with is worked out from the secret, deterministically, by both halves \u2014 the registration that hands it over and the check that runs on every delivery. One string for a merchant to manage and rotate, two different strings on the wire, and an address that leaks costs nothing but the address. Changing the secret means pressing **Register webhook** again, which is said in the field's own help text \u2014 and a store registering by hand instead has the one line that prints the derived value in docs\/tracking.md\n    * **Inches and ounces, converted once, at the driver's own boundary and nowhere else.** The store keeps millimetres and grams, because that is what a variant records and what Shippo takes directly. EasyPost is hardwired to inches and ounces to one decimal point. That conversion was the real test of whether the aggregator contract had been built around one API, and it passes: it is two functions inside the EasyPost driver, called by the parcel payload and the customs line and by nothing else in the plugin. They round half-up, and a measurement that is really there but rounds to nothing comes back as a tenth rather than a zero \u2014 a zero dimension is refused by every carrier, and \"no dimension\" is not what the merchant typed. There is a test for the edges\n    * **The service codes are the same on both sides, so changing aggregator does not mean rebinding a shipping method.** Shippo hands over `usps_priority` as one token; EasyPost hands over `USPS` and `GroundAdvantage` as two, and the driver joins them into `usps_ground_advantage` by breaking the token at its word boundaries. `usps_priority`, `ups_ground`, `ups_next_day_air`, `fedex_ground` \u2014 identical either way, with a test pinning each spelling. Paste the new keys, change one setting, press **Check connection** and **Refresh carriers**, and every method that was bound still resolves\n    * **EasyPost will only list your carrier accounts to a production key, and the add-on says so instead of failing quietly.** Its carrier account endpoints are production-only and refuse a test key outright, which is the one thing about setting EasyPost up that catches people. So **Refresh carriers** is asked with the production key whichever mode the store is in \u2014 which is also the right answer, because your carrier accounts are your carrier accounts and the same ids rate in test mode \u2014 and nothing else touches it: quoting, buying, refunding, tracking and address checks all use the mode's own key, and nothing spends money until Mode is Live. A test-mode store that has not pasted a production key yet gets a connection check that **passes** and tells it exactly that, rather than a red card about an endpoint it was never going to need until step three\n    * **A rate reference that carries two ids, and nothing above the driver knows.** EasyPost buys a label with a rate id *and* the shipment it belongs to, where Shippo needs only the rate. The contract has one opaque string for this, so the EasyPost driver's is `<shipment id>:<rate id>` and the composition stays inside the driver. A reference with no shipment on it \u2014 one recorded by an older version, or a rate that has gone stale \u2014 falls through to re-quoting the shipment and picking the service the merchant chose, which is exactly what the label request has always carried a service code as well as a rate reference for\n    * **One contract, three drivers, and a shared test that proves it.** `AggregatorContractTestCase` is the rules every driver keeps \u2014 a code the registry does not rewrite, a capability matrix that answers every case as a boolean, **every declared capability having the interface actually behind it**, a webhook parser that makes no outbound call, a signature verdict that matches what the driver declared, amounts in whole minor units, a rate's metadata carrying only sentences and the names core reserves, a connection check that never throws. Each driver extends it with five hooks. A second driver is the only thing that can prove an interface was written around a contract rather than around whichever API happened to be first, and a third is what proves the second was not a coincidence \u2014 so this is that proof rather than an argument for it\n    * **Three places where the first driver's name had been baked in, now reading under whichever driver is configured.** The API key was read from `shippo.test_token` whatever the aggregator was, the inbound webhook's secret from `shippo.webhook_secret`, and its optional address allowlist from `shippo.webhook_ips`. All three now read under the configured driver's own key, which is the rule the settings were always meant to follow \u2014 **a driver's settings live under its own key** \u2014 and a store that has pasted keys for both aggregators keeps both. The Carriers screen's \"paste a carrier key to begin\" now names the aggregator you actually chose, and a connection check on a store with nothing pasted answers in that driver's own vocabulary: EasyPost has an API *key* beginning `EZTK`, and telling an EasyPost merchant to look for a token beginning `shippo_test_` would send them hunting for a field that is not there\n    * **Every driver's secrets are declared for encryption, not just the configured one's.** A merchant who has pasted keys for both aggregators has both in `user\/config\/plugins\/kahunacart-shipping.yaml`, and a list that shrank to the driver of the day would leave the other one in plaintext the next time anything wrote that file\n    * **Carrier codes for EasyPost's spellings.** Shippo separates a carrier's words with underscores and EasyPost runs them together, because its tokens come from a class name: `DhlExpressAccount` becomes `dhlexpress`, and no amount of substituting hyphens gets that to core's `dhl-express`. The run-together spellings are now in the carrier map beside the underscored ones, so a parcel sent by DHL Express, Canada Post, Australia Post, Royal Mail or An Post through either aggregator gets a customer-facing tracking link to the right carrier's own website rather than falling through to **Other**\n    * **Written against EasyPost's published API with recorded fixtures, and honest about what that does and does not prove.** There was no EasyPost key on the machine that built this, so every branch is proven against a fixture written from EasyPost's own documented examples and none of it against the API itself. That is said out loud in the docs rather than glossed, and docs\/testing.md now carries a fifteen-step real-key campaign for the person who has a key \u2014 including the two steps a fixture can never stand in for: reading back the parcel EasyPost actually received to prove the unit conversion in the direction that matters, and `curl`ing the webhook endpoint with a wrong signature and with none to prove the 401 is real\n    * **ShipEngine joins Shippo and EasyPost in the same plugin.** Sandbox carrier accounts and services, live rates, PDF\/PNG\/ZPL labels, recovery after a lost purchase response, synchronous voids, tracking, address validation and customs, with mode checks before any API call. Webhooks require a derived shared-secret header and are honestly listed as unsigned by this driver.\n    * **ShipEngine setup and test coverage.** Three encrypted settings, an eight-step Instructions section, shared service codes, fixtures based on the published v1 API and a documented sandbox campaign. Sandbox tracking and genuine webhook delivery remain separate from fixture validation.\n    * **A bought label is drawn as a label.** The parcels panel beside an order used to print four lines of text with a small grey button reading Open the label under them, which was the quietest thing on a screen whose whole point is that file. It is now a card with the carrier's name on a colour band across the top and, under it, the service in big type, who the parcel is going to, the tracking number over a barcode rule with a copy button beside it that puts the number on the clipboard and opens nothing, and chips for the price and the format \u2014 and the whole card is the button that opens the file. The card, the line saying what you paid and the three verbs all start on the same left edge, and the ring around a focused card belongs to the keyboard \u2014 Tab to the card and you get one, click it and you do not. Reprint, Track and Void stay underneath as small text actions doing exactly what they did before, through exactly the same routes. A refunded label is stamped Voided and goes grey, a refused one gets a dashed card and the carrier's own words, a delivered one a tick. Carrier names are set in type and there are no carrier logos anywhere: a colour band with the name on it reads as USPS from across the room without borrowing USPS's trademark, and the demo carriers get invented colours so a demo store screenshots like a real one\n    * **And it shows the label itself beside those details where one can be had for nothing.** The picture sits at 4x6 proportions down the left of the card with the service, the address, the number and the chips next to it, so a merchant gets both and never one instead of the other; on a panel too narrow for two columns it moves above them. EasyPost renders every label as a PNG whether or not anybody asked, so a store printing 4x6 PDFs gets the picture as well for one download and no extra postage; the demo carrier draws its own with GD, banner and all. Shippo prints one file per purchase and has no way to re-render it, so its cards carry the details alone rather than buying a second label for a thumbnail. The picture is served by the same authenticated route as the label \u2014 it is the same document with the same customer's address on it \u2014 and labels bought before this have none, which is exactly the case the card was built to stand on its own for. See docs\/labels.md\n    * **The demo carriers now read like couriers a real shop would use.** Driftwood Post and Lagoon Express are **Kahuna Post** \u2014 Kahuna Ground Basic and Kahuna Air Mail \u2014 and **Kahuna Express** \u2014 Kahuna Express Saver, Kahuna Priority Overnight and Kahuna Express Worldwide. Still two carriers, because one cheap and slow against one dear and fast is the whole point of them, and still invented: they carry the store's own name rather than a real courier's, and the label they print still says NOT VALID FOR POSTAGE across the top. Every price, band, refusal, limit and tracking timeline is exactly what it was \u2014 only the names and the codes moved. A store seeded before this keeps its old parcels, because the driver answers to the five old service codes for this one release: an existing label still names its service and its carrier instead of reading Other, and a label bought against an old code comes back stamped with the new one. The one thing an alias cannot fix is a shipping method still bound to an old code \u2014 core matches a method to a rate by that code and no rate carries an old one now \u2014 so repoint those methods on the zone, or run the seeder's purge and seed again, which rewrites them. See docs\/demo-carrier.md\n    * **The demo store has two months of parcels behind it.** The seeder used to write eleven orders, all of them placed in the same second the demo reset ran, which read like a shop that had opened an hour ago however good the parcels on it looked. It now writes thirty-three more spread over the past sixty days: most of them delivered with a full history, five still in transit or out for delivery right now, one missed at the door and delivered on the second attempt, one gone back to the sender, two abroad with a customs declaration, two paid and waiting for somebody to buy a label, one refused at the counter, and a couple posted minutes ago so the compressed clock still has something moving while a visitor watches. A handful belong to the demo customer whose storefront password the demo prints, so signing in shows the customer's half of the add-on rather than an empty account. Every order, its parcels and its whole timeline are dated to the day it happened. See docs\/demo-carrier.md\n    * **A parcel bought before today took the days its service quotes, rather than half an hour.** The demo's clock compresses a whole journey into `demo_carrier.transit_minutes` so that something visibly moves on the screen, which is right for a parcel bought this morning and absurd for one bought six weeks ago. A parcel younger than a day still runs compressed; an older one ran at carrier speed \u2014 one day for Kahuna Priority Overnight, three for the Express Saver, five for Ground Basic, ten or fourteen for Air Mail, the same numbers on the rate sheet. Nothing is stored: the clock is still a pure function of when the label was bought, so a history written by the seeder and a history recomputed by a poll an hour later agree to the second\n    * **A parcel that is missed at the door and delivered on the second attempt.** The demo had one parcel that went wrong and it went wrong permanently \u2014 postcode `00000`, failed, returned to sender. `00001` is now the commonest thing that goes slightly wrong instead: out for delivery, \"Nobody home \u2014 a card was left\", out again the next day, signed for. One postcode per journey, which is the rule the address validator already used\n    * **A label the base plugin's own seeder left without one now gets a parcel.** `bin\/plugin kahunacart seed --account` writes a fulfilled order with a jumper on it and no label behind it, because core knows nothing about parcels \u2014 and on a store running this add-on that reads as a shop that posts things without printing labels. Found by what the order is rather than by a number, so whatever that seeder writes next is covered too, and **only for orders carrying core's own seed stamp**: a merchant's fulfilled March is never written onto. `--purge` takes those labels back off with their shipments and their history lines\n\n2. [](#improved)\n    * **The admin section is called Shipping carriers now, not Carriers.** The add-on is called Shipping everywhere else, so the sidebar entry under Add-ons and the heading on the screen say so too. The word *carriers* stays on the end because core already owns *Shipping* and *Shipping profiles* in the Setup band, and a third plain *Shipping* would be the worst thing on the screen. Nothing else moved: the route is still `#\/section\/carriers`, the permissions are the same, and the tab inside the screen is still **Carriers**, because that one really is the list of carrier accounts\n\n3. [](#bugfix)\n    * **Every label the seeder bought was stored with no file behind it, so Open the label opened nothing.** A demo label is a PDF and a PDF is a Twig template: core's renderer asks Twig for one, Twig asks the resource locator for `theme:\/\/`, and nothing registers that scheme on the command line until the theme has been initialised. It threw, the driver caught it the way it catches any renderer failing, and every seeded label came out bought and correct with an empty file path. A seeded demo now prints its labels\n    * **`--purge` left rows and files behind.** The shipment written for each seeded order stayed in the database pointing at an order id that no longer existed, the order's history lines stayed with it, and the PNG thumbnail beside each label stayed on disk under a name of its own. All three go now, so a purge really does leave the store it found\n    * **The seeder queued two dozen emails to invented customers.** Writing the back-dated parcels puts every one of them through the same tracking service a webhook and a poll write through, and reaching *delivered* is what queues the customer's delivered message \u2014 so a seed left twenty-odd of them sitting in the queue, where a demo box's jobs screen is meant to show the three it seeded on purpose, and where any store with a mailer wired up would have sent them. It also paged the merchant about parcels nobody posted. The seeder now writes through the same object with both of those taken off, so the history is still written by the code that writes a real parcel's and nothing goes out\n    * **The seeder fell over on a store that sells anything in pairs.** It wrote its order lines through the cart service, which enforces the store's own quantity rules, so a product with a minimum of two refused the single the seeder asked for and took the whole seed down with it under `set -e`. Quantities are now clamped the way the storefront clamps them before the line is added\n    * **ShipEngine sandbox findings corrected.** Ordinary boxes exclude carrier flat-rate packaging; domestic purchases save their recovery ID on a fresh matching quote; sandbox purchases omit the unrecoverable sample flag. Recorded responses cover recovery and exact-format downloads, and supplied PNG links feed the new label cards without another purchase.\n    * **An international EasyPost label could not be bought at all.** A customs declaration goes onto an EasyPost shipment when the shipment is created, and a shipment cannot be changed afterwards \u2014 so the shipment behind a rate reference, which a rate call made and a rate call has no declaration to send, had `customs_info: null` on it for ever. Every international purchase was a buy against that, and EasyPost refused every one with \"Customs info error: missing required field: ContentsType\". An international purchase now re-creates the shipment with its declaration, its label format and its paper size, and buys the rate the merchant chose off the fresh quote. Found on the first run against a real key; a domestic purchase is untouched and still buys the rate it was quoted\n    * **Every EasyPost label came back as a PNG, whatever format you asked for, and was written to disk under the wrong extension.** The 4x6 PDF setting was sent on the shipment the *label* code builds, and the shipment actually bought is the one the *rate* call built \u2014 which names no paper size, so EasyPost defaulted to PNG, `label_pdf_url` came back null, and the driver fell back to the PNG and saved it as `label-47.pdf`. A file that says PDF and is a PNG opens in nothing. The driver now asks EasyPost to render the format that was asked for whenever the bought shipment does not already carry it, which is EasyPost's own answer to this and buys no extra postage. ZPL works the same way, and a shipment that already has the right format costs no extra call\n    * **A purchase the carrier refused could never be tried again.** The key that stops a double-click buying two labels is built from the order, the service and what is in the boxes \u2014 and a refusal is almost never about any of those. An address USPS would not verify, a ship-from with a typo in it, an account with no balance: all fixed somewhere the key cannot see, so the second press produced the same key, the unique index refused it, and the merchant read **\"Already bought \u2014 a label already exists for this parcel\"** about a label that had never been bought. That parcel was locked out of that service permanently. A refused row is now taken back and tried again, with a conditional write so two merchants racing still produce one purchase; a pending, bought or voided row is still a duplicate, which is the whole double-click defence and has not moved\n    * **A test key could not quote anything.** EasyPost's carrier account endpoints are production-only, so a store in test mode with only a test key pasted can never have a carrier account row \u2014 and the bridge treated an empty account list as \"this carrier has nothing to ask\", so checkout quoted nothing at all. Meanwhile EasyPost's rate endpoint answers a test key perfectly well and needs no account filter. A driver can now say that an empty list proves nothing about it, and EasyPost says so exactly while no production key is pasted. Paste one and the ordinary rule applies again\n    * **Refresh carriers explained itself in EasyPost's words rather than in useful ones.** A test key answered `MODE.UNAUTHORIZED`, \"This resource requires a production API Key to access.\", which a merchant reads seconds after a connection check that passed. It now says which key to paste, that it is only ever read with and never bought with while the store is in test mode, and that the store quotes and prints test labels without it \u2014 with EasyPost's own sentence kept on the end. The Carriers screen was also dropping everything after the first full stop of any EasyPost refusal, because it only knew how to unwrap a Shippo one\n    * **Service codes did not match Shippo's for a carrier quoted through EasyPost's own shared accounts.** A rate bought through one of those names the shared account rather than the carrier \u2014 `FedExDefault`, not `FedEx` \u2014 which minted `fedexdefault_fedex_ground` where Shippo, and the same EasyPost account the day its own FedEx contract goes on, both mint `fedex_ground`. Three things broke at once: the codes stopped matching Shippo's, so a store moving between aggregators lost every method binding; a refusal could not be attributed to the carrier that made it; and the codes changed under a merchant who added a contract. The carrier token is now read the same way everywhere\n    * **The same address problem was printed twice.** EasyPost runs two checks over one address and both report it: a postcode that does not exist fails `zip4` and `delivery` with one \"Address not found\" each, and the merchant read it once beside the address and once under the street field. One fact, said once\n    * **A refused address offered a \"correction\" identical to what was typed.** A verification EasyPost cannot make answers with the submitted address copied back out, field for field, so the buy sheet put a button in front of a merchant that would change not one character. A correction now has to differ somewhere postal before it is offered as one \u2014 a name in capitals or a phone number with its punctuation taken out is not a correction\n    * **A tracking scan with no time on it landed at the first of January 1970.** EasyPost writes one of these for a parcel it knows nothing about yet, with an empty message and `datetime: null`, and there is nothing to invent a time from \u2014 the event's time is half of the index that makes a redelivered webhook a no-op. The scan is dropped and the tracker's own status reaches the merchant dated from its `updated_at` instead, which is the same fact told honestly\n    * **Every carrier account came back with no services, so a store with its production key pasted still had no service list.** EasyPost's carrier metadata filter is all or nothing: one name that endpoint does not know answers **422 over the whole request**, and `dhlecs` is one of those names. So all six carrier accounts on a real key came back with nothing on them, core read the catalogue as empty, the service box in the zone method editor stayed a free-text field, and the Carriers screen told the merchant to press **Refresh carriers** \u2014 which would fail in precisely the same way every time. A 422 now drops the filter and asks again for everything EasyPost publishes, keeping only the carriers this store holds. It is one extra call on a button pressed by hand, and only on the one status this can be: a refusal that is not about the request is left where it was, because asking twice would answer the same thing and cost another wait to hear it\n    * **DHL eCommerce is two names at EasyPost and only one of them can be asked for.** A carrier account says `DhlEcsAccount` and a rate bought through it says `DhlEcs`, so this store's token is `dhlecs` \u2014 and the published carrier metadata calls the same carrier `dhlecommercesolutions`. No amount of expanding or hyphenating gets from one to the other, so it is a map. The carriers are asked for under the name metadata knows them by and every answer is filed straight back under the token the account produced, because a service code minted from EasyPost's other spelling is one no rate from that account would ever match\n    * **A connection check with both keys pasted did not say which key had read what.** A test-mode store with a production key has two of them used in one check: the test key against the shipments endpoint, the production key against the carrier accounts, because that is the only key EasyPost will list those to. The summary said **\"Connected to EasyPost.\"** and left a merchant reading six carriers and a test-mode badge to work it out. It now names the mode in the sentence people actually read, says the count came from the production key, and says the thing anybody wants to hear about a production key on a test store: nothing is bought with it until **Mode** is Live\n\n"},"1.0.0":{"date":"09\/15\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Live carrier rates, from a key you paste once.** KahunaCart 1.2.0 published a carrier rate contract and shipped no carrier, deliberately \u2014 a first-party driver in core would commit core to maintaining somebody else's API version. This is the carrier. Paste a [Shippo](https:\/\/goshippo.com) key, press **Check connection**, pull your carrier list, bind a shipping method to a real service, and a customer's postage comes from USPS, UPS, FedEx, DHL or any of forty-odd others behind that one account. Shippo is an aggregator, which is the whole point of doing it this way round: a merchant who wants UPS and USPS gets both from one integration. EasyPost is the second driver and goes in this same plugin. To core the whole aggregator is one carrier with the code `shippo` and its services are `<carrier>_<token>` tokens \u2014 `usps_priority`, `ups_ground` \u2014 so the customer's tracking link still points at USPS and not at Shippo. See docs\/getting-started.md\n    * **A box catalogue and a packer, so a rate is for the parcel the order actually goes in.** Core packs an order into one package per shipping profile and says, in its own docblock, that working out which box things fit in is a carrier's job with a carrier's data. This is where that data arrives. A box is a code, a name, its outside measurements in whole millimetres and a maximum weight in whole grams; the packer expands each line into units, sorts them largest first, and puts each one into the first open parcel that will take it or into the smallest box it fits in \u2014 first-fit-decreasing, which is the standard answer and within a few per cent of optimal for the parcel counts a shop actually has. Nothing goes into a box that would take it over its maximum weight, the empty box counts towards its own limit because it goes on the scale too, and an item that fits no box at all travels on its own at its own dimensions rather than being refused, because \"this goes on its own\" is right far more often than \"this cannot be sent\". Each parcel's billable weight is the greater of its actual weight and its dimensional weight \u2014 length times width times height in centimetres over a divisor, 5000 by default, with per-carrier overrides beside it, rounded up the way every carrier rounds it. See docs\/boxes-and-packing.md\n    * **Packing is deterministic, and there is a test that proves it.** The same order packs the same way every single time: every sort breaks its ties on facts about the order \u2014 the line's id, the unit's place in its line, the box's code \u2014 and never on anything about the request. The suite packs one order a hundred times, reshuffling the lines each time, and compares the bytes. A merchant who sees two parcels on one page load and three on the next will never trust the number again, and nor should they\n    * **A store with no boxes still quotes.** One built-in box, every parcel flagged as an estimate, and a plain sentence saying the prices are approximate until real boxes are filled in. Refusing to price an order because nobody had typed a box in would be a worse answer than an approximate one. A line whose variant has no recorded size joins the pile of unknowns, lands in a box anyway, and the parcel says which lines it had to guess about \u2014 so the fix is visible rather than mysterious\n    * **Ship-from addresses.** A carrier will not quote, and certainly will not print a label, without knowing where the parcel starts, and core's only store address is `invoice.store_address`, a free-text block meant for the bottom of an invoice that no carrier API will take. The add-on keeps its own, structured, with the same column names `kahunacart_pickup_locations` uses so the two read alike. The first one added becomes the default, because a store with one address and no default cannot be quoted for and nothing on screen would say why\n    * **A connection check that presses the real key against the real account.** Not a non-empty-string test: a real call, reporting which account it reached and how many carrier accounts that account holds, which is the fact that actually predicts whether rates will appear. A key that works against an account with no carrier switched on is reported as a failure rather than as a pass with a warning nobody reads, because a store in that state quotes nothing. Two refusals happen before anything is sent \u2014 an empty token, and a token of the wrong kind. A live key pasted into the test field is caught by its prefix, which is a mistake somebody makes once and pays for when a test order buys a real label\n    * **An aggregator driver contract, with five optional interfaces declared beside it.** `AggregatorInterface` is the required half \u2014 the code, the name, the capabilities, the carrier list and the rates. `LabelPurchaseInterface`, `LabelVoidInterface`, `TrackingInterface`, `AddressValidationInterface` and `AggregatorWebhookSetupInterface` sit beside it, each small and each optional, and all five are implemented for Shippo. Every one of them is answered honestly in `capabilities()`, because a capability flag that lies turns a missing feature into a broken one \u2014 the admin card's matrix is built from what a driver says it can do, in a fixed order, rather than from a list somebody keeps in a template. `signed_webhooks` is in that matrix and Shippo's answer is no, because Shippo's only self-serve authentication on an inbound delivery is a secret in the URL and a published IP allowlist, and a merchant is entitled to know which they have. See docs\/development.md\n    * **Two permissions, not one.** `kahunacart.shipping.manage` covers carriers, boxes and ship-from addresses; `kahunacart.shipping.labels` covers buying and voiding, because that spends money \u2014 the same reason core separates `kahunacart.orders.refund` from `kahunacart.orders.manage`. A packer who prints labels all day should not also be able to repoint the store at a different carrier account. Reading a label rides on `kahunacart.orders.view`, which is the permission somebody answering \"where is my parcel\" already holds\n    * **Fourteen REST routes and an MCP tool for each**, under `\/kahunacart\/shipping`: the aggregator and its connection check, the carrier accounts and their refresh, the box catalogue, the ship-from addresses, and a packing preview that answers what an order \u2014 or a made-up basket \u2014 would pack into. The preview is the route behind **Test the packer**, and packing is the one part of this a merchant otherwise cannot see and therefore cannot trust. Every write honours `demo.readonly` the way core's do, and the manifest is checked against the routes in both directions, so a route added without a tool is a failing test rather than a 404 somebody finds in a month\n    * **Migration `shipping_0001_core`** adds `kahunacart_shipping_accounts`, `kahunacart_shipping_locations` and `kahunacart_shipping_boxes` through the base plugin's own migration runner. All three carry `store_id` the way core's tables do, and none of them is declared to `bin\/plugin kahunacart reset` \u2014 they are configuration, and a reset promises to keep the configuration\n    * **Buy a real postage label from an order, and print it.** One button on an order that has something left to ship: pick the parcels the packer worked out, pick a service, see the price, buy. What comes back is a label file, a tracking number and a shipment written onto the order through KahunaCart's own fulfilment service \u2014 so the fulfilment status moves, the shipped email goes out, and **the tracking link on it works**, which is the one thing a store cannot have without this. The purchase is seven steps in a fixed order and the order is the whole design: lock the order, work out a key, write the row **before any money is spent**, buy, settle the row, record the parcel, link the two. The step that writes first is what turns \"we never found out whether that worked\" into something a job can finish by itself. See docs\/labels.md\n    * **A double-click cannot buy two labels, and it is the database that says so.** The key is made from the store, the order, the service and what is actually in the parcels \u2014 never from anything about the request, never from the price \u2014 and a unique index on `(store_id, idempotency_key)` refuses the second write. A check-then-insert is a race; an index is not. Shippo has no idempotency mechanism of its own at all, so this is not a nice-to-have: the same transaction sent twice buys two labels and charges twice. There is a test that forks a second PHP process and races it against the first\n    * **A purchase whose answer never arrived is chased until it is settled.** A timeout leaves a `pending` row, not a failure, and `shipping.reconcile_label` asks Shippo every minute for six hours \u2014 by the plugin's own key, and through `GET \/transactions?rate=` which is the only way to ask \"did this rate ever become a label\". A purchase that did go through settles by itself and records the parcel; one that did not is marked failed. If six hours is not enough the merchant gets a **Label outcome unknown** notification, raised once per label for ever, and a banner on the order with **Record the parcel** on it. The one state this deliberately does not roll back is a bought label whose shipment write failed: throwing away paid-for postage to tidy up a database error is the wrong trade, so both facts go on the banner and the merchant picks\n    * **Void a label and get the postage back.** The refund is asked for, and then it is waited for honestly: a carrier takes up to fourteen business days to settle one, so `void_requested` is a normal state with a sentence rather than a spinner, and the reconcile job keeps asking. A request more than ninety days after the label was bought is refused here before anything is sent, naming the date, because nothing recovers it. A label the carrier has already scanned comes back `void_refused` and goes back to being what it is \u2014 a bought, used label\n    * **Tracking, two ways, because a store needs both.** An inbound webhook is prompt and a six-hourly poll is reliable, and **switching every webhook off and letting only the poll run still takes a parcel to delivered**. Every carrier's vocabulary is written down as one of seven statuses, a status nobody recognises is kept as `unknown` with the carrier's own words beside it rather than dropped, and the unique index on `(label_id, status, occurred_at)` is what makes a redelivered webhook and an overlapping poll write one event between them. The delivered email, the history line and the parcel-came-back notification all hang off that **write** rather than off the status, which is the whole of \"it goes out once, and once only\". See docs\/tracking.md\n    * **An inbound webhook route that brings its own body cap, delivery log and authentication.** Core's webhook door only opens for a payment provider, so this registers on the API plugin's public-route mechanism the way the Licenses add-on does. It refuses a body over 256 KB with a 413 **before** anything else happens, compares a per-store secret in the path with `hash_equals`, optionally checks the caller against Shippo's nine published addresses, and writes a row per delivery \u2014 kept thirty days \u2014 because \"the webhook never arrives\" and \"the webhook arrives and is refused\" are two completely different problems that look identical without one. A wrong secret is a **404**, so a scanner learns nothing; a body nobody can read is a **200**, because every aggregator treats a 4xx as a reason to retry for a week and the payload will not have become readable by then. **The endpoint writes no tracking at all** \u2014 it queues a job that re-reads the parcel with the store's own key \u2014 so a forged ping costs one API call and writes nothing, which is what makes an unsigned webhook acceptable here. It also keeps inside the three seconds Shippo allows a receiver, and there is a test asserting it makes no outbound call\n    * **The driver can set its own webhook up**, and for this release nothing calls it. `setupWebhook()` adopts an address it registered before rather than adding a second, and refuses anything that is not `https` or is over Shippo's 200-character limit before sending it \u2014 but no route exposes it, so there is no card and no button, and the four manual steps on the Instructions tab are what a merchant has. Written up as a finding in docs\/development.md rather than worked around\n    * **Customs declarations, built only when the parcel actually crosses a border.** Per line: a description, a quantity, a total weight, a total value and a country of origin \u2014 totals, not per-item, which is the thing everybody gets wrong once. The facts come from the product's own customs row, then the order line, then a store-wide default, most specific first and **field by field**, so a store that recorded a code on the product and an origin on one variant gets both. **Missing facts are named before the button, not by a carrier's error array after it**: \"three items need a country of origin\", with the products linked. Four rules are enforced here because the carrier's own wording for them is poor, including the one where Shippo accepts a harmonised code under two field names and silently prefers one. See docs\/customs.md\n    * **Buy a label automatically when an order is paid.** Off by default and worth leaving off until the packing looks right. It hangs off KahunaCart's compensating hooks rather than an event listener, and that buys two things a listener cannot have: an order that completes twice buys exactly one label, and a cancelled or refunded order **voids the label it bought** \u2014 only that one, because a label bought by hand was a decision and undoing somebody's deliberate action is not what this is for\n    * **The customer's tracking link points at the carrier, not at the aggregator.** A label bought through Shippo for USPS records the carrier as USPS on the shipment, so the link on the receipt goes to USPS's own page. A carrier KahunaCart has no code for is recorded as **Other** with the aggregator's own tracking URL kept, so the link still works, and a store can name it properly by adding it to core's own `shipping.carriers` with no change here\n    * **The label file is kept on this server.** Every aggregator's hosted label link is pre-signed and expires, so a merchant reprinting a label from March would get a 404 at the moment they need it most. The bytes are downloaded as the label is bought, sharded two levels deep so a shop printing forty a day does not end the year with fifteen thousand files in one directory, and kept for ever by default because a postage record is a financial record. If the file has gone, **Open the label** falls back to the aggregator's link and says so; if that has expired too, it says that too, which is the only honest answer left\n    * **A delivered email, in the order's own language**, carrying the carrier, the number, a working link and the date \u2014 and nothing about the label, the postage cost or the box. Four owner notifications beside it, each deduped to the thing that actually went wrong rather than to how many times it was retried: a failed purchase is once per order per day, an unresolved purchase is once per label for ever, an unreachable carrier is once per aggregator per day, and a parcel coming back is once per parcel\n    * **Migration `shipping_0002_labels`** adds `kahunacart_shipping_labels`, `kahunacart_shipping_tracking`, `kahunacart_shipping_item_customs` and `kahunacart_shipping_deliveries`. The first, second and fourth go with `bin\/plugin kahunacart reset --orders`, because they belong to the orders they describe; the customs facts stay, because they are catalogue configuration and a reset promises to keep it. Two unique indexes carry real design load rather than being belt and braces: `(store_id, idempotency_key)` on labels and `(label_id, status, occurred_at)` on tracking events\n    * **Checked against a real Shippo test account, not only against fixtures.** A live run bought a label, downloaded and stored it, tracked it to delivered through the inbound webhook, sent the delivered email and asked for the refund \u2014 and found three things a green suite could not: the driver was being built without the plain HTTP client, so no label file was ever kept; nothing at all enqueued the reconcile job, so a purchase whose answer never arrived would have sat pending for ever; and once it did, the job booked a second copy of itself on every run. All three are seams between pieces that were each correct on their own, all three are fixed, and each has a test that would have caught it\n    * **Ten more REST routes and an MCP tool for each** \u2014 quote, buy, void, record a parcel, list and read labels, serve a label file, read and refresh a parcel's tracking, read and write a product's customs facts, and check an address. Buying, voiding and recording sit behind `kahunacart.shipping.labels` because they spend the store's money; reading rides on `kahunacart.orders.view`, which the person answering \"where is my parcel\" already holds. The manifest is still checked against the routes in both directions\n    * **A Carriers screen, and it is called Carriers on purpose.** Core already owns two sidebar entries in the Setup band called *Shipping* and *Shipping profiles*, and a third one would be the worst thing on the screen \u2014 Carriers also reads as the sibling of *Payments*, which is exactly what it is: the place the accounts that move things live. Four tabs under one heading. **Carriers** holds the aggregator's card \u2014 the brand name bold, the buttons underneath, then a fixed check-and-cross matrix of the ten things a driver may or may not do, drawn from what the driver declares rather than from a list somebody keeps in a template \u2014 with the ship-from addresses and a card per carrier account under it, each with the switch that says whether this store offers it and its service codes as chips you can copy. **Boxes** is the catalogue and the packing rules. **Labels** is every label the store has. **Settings** is the plugin's own form, drawn inside the section so there is one place to paste a key rather than two. See docs\/getting-started.md\n    * **Test the packer, and see what a merchant otherwise cannot.** Pack a real order by its id, or a basket that does not exist yet, and read back the parcels the packer would make: the box each one landed in, what it weighs on the scale, what it weighs dimensionally, which of the two the carrier would bill and why, and every line whose size nobody has recorded. Packing is the one part of this that is invisible until a customer is charged for three parcels where two would have done, and a merchant who can look at the answer can trust it \u2014 or go and fix the box catalogue. The route behind it is a **GET** and writes nothing at all, which is what lets a read-only store show it\n    * **One status strip above the carrier cards, drawn only when something is wrong.** Live carrier rates switched off in KahunaCart, no fallback method nominated, a shipping method naming a service no enabled carrier offers \u2014 each one a sentence and a button that goes to the screen that fixes it. **It never fixes anything itself.** Core's configuration is core's to write, and a button on somebody else's screen that silently rewrites `kahunacart.yaml` is how a merchant loses an afternoon\n    * **The parcels of an order, beside the order.** A panel in the admin's own `order.details.side` zone: the carrier, the service, the tracking number as a link, what the postage cost, the buttons a bought label has, and the tracking events underneath with a place and a time on each. An order that has bought nothing yet draws **what the packer proposes instead**, so a merchant can see what the parcel will be before spending anything. An order with nothing physical on it draws nothing at all \u2014 a panel reading \"no parcels\" on every digital order is noise on every digital order\n    * **Buy label, in the order's action row, and a sheet that names the price on the button that spends it.** \"Buy this label for $9.41\", not \"Confirm\" \u2014 the same reason core's refund confirm says the amount. The sheet asks for a **fresh** quote rather than reusing checkout's, because that number can be fifteen minutes old and this one is what gets charged; it draws each parcel with its box, its billable weight and its contents, every service the carrier quoted with its price and its transit estimate, the ship-from address, and the delivery address exactly as it will be printed with the carrier's own validation messages under it. While it works the button is disabled and says so, because a double-click here buys two labels. **When it fails the sheet stays open** with the carrier's own words on it and the button live again \u2014 a sheet that closes on failure has thrown away everything the merchant chose. The service picked on the sheet is the service bought, even when the order's own method names none \u2014 a method set to take the first rate the carrier returns is the common case, and it must not be the one case a merchant cannot buy postage for. The name the carrier gave the service is kept with the label, so every screen afterwards says *Priority Mail Express* rather than `usps_priority_express`\n    * **Void asks a question that names the window and the wait.** \"This label was bought 3 days ago. A refund can be asked for up to 90 days after purchase and takes up to fourteen business days to settle, and it is refused outright if the parcel has already moved.\" What comes back is reported in the carrier's own words, and a refund the carrier has taken and not settled is drawn as exactly that rather than as a spinner. Fourteen days is not a loading state\n    * **Where the parcel is, on the receipt and in the account.** Two storefront panels and one partial, with an email twin beside it, both stamped so the drift report can see a theme copy fall behind. They print the carrier's name, the tracking number, its link, the dates and where the parcel has got to \u2014 and **nothing else, ever**: no cost, no label file, no box, no internal note. That is not a rule somebody has to remember, it is an allowlist with a test on it that fails if a key is added to one and not the other. A tracking status goes through a language key rather than the carrier's own English, so a store running in French reads French\n    * **The API token and the webhook secret are encrypted on save.** Core opened `onKahunaCartCollectEncryptedSettings` for any plugin to declare its own secrets \u2014 the gap this add-on wrote up in its first leaf \u2014 and this subscribes to it, handing over the driver's own declared list rather than a second copy of it. A `type: password` field added to the blueprint and forgotten in that list is a failing test rather than a key sitting in a config file in plaintext\n    * **A demo carrier, so the add-on can be seen working without a carrier account.** A driver that quotes, prints, tracks and refunds and never opens a socket: two invented carriers, five services, three distance bands and a whole parcel journey compressed into thirty-six minutes. It is off by default and it takes two switches to turn on \u2014 `aggregator: demo` names it and `demo_carrier.enabled` allows it, and with either off it registers nothing at all, exactly as a real driver with no key pasted does. It is deliberately **not** keyed off KahunaCart's `demo.readonly`, because those two switches mean different things: a staging box wants a read-only admin and still wants its real carrier, and a merchant who turned read-only on for an afternoon must not have had a simulation printing labels under a real carrier's methods for that afternoon. It is not a mode either \u2014 there is no flag anywhere that changes how the real driver behaves, every fake lives behind the aggregator interface, and nothing above it learns that a demo exists. See docs\/demo-carrier.md\n    * **The label it prints is a real PDF, and it says so across the top.** Rendered through KahunaCart's own PDF renderer \u2014 the one that already prints invoices and packing slips, with remote assets off \u2014 at 4x6 inches whatever the store's invoice paper is set to, carrying **SIMULATION \u2014 NOT VALID FOR POSTAGE**, the addresses as they would be printed, the parcel's billable weight and the tracking number. The barcode on it is decorative on purpose: a demo that printed a scannable code of an invented tracking number would be a demo that could produce a label somebody tried to post. The bytes go through the same store, the same authenticated route and the same retention sweep a real label's do, with no special case anywhere\n    * **A parcel that moves while you watch it, and one that goes wrong.** Six events from label to doorstep across the configured transit time, so a visitor who stays five minutes sees the parcel move and a demo store that rebuilds itself on the hour fits a whole journey inside one reset. A parcel going to postcode `00000` reaches *on its way*, then writes a failure with **action required** on it, then comes back to the sender \u2014 which is the same postcode the address check refuses outright, so there is one rule to remember and two places it shows up. It covers the two tracking statuses a happy path never reaches, and it is what gives the **Parcel came back** notification something to fire on\n    * **The clock runs on KahunaCart's own worker tick.** Core fires one after its queue drains on every pass, so a store that already runs the worker needs no cron line of its own and the demo moves on the store's clock rather than on one this add-on keeps. `bin\/plugin kahunacart-shipping demo:tick` is the same pass by hand, for a person watching a screen and for a read-only demo, where `demo.readonly` refuses every write through the admin and explicitly leaves the CLI open. Both write through the add-on's own tracking service \u2014 the same object the webhook job and the poll job write through \u2014 so what a demo parcel does is what a real one does. The tracking poll is deliberately not what does this: its one-hour floor exists to keep a real store inside a carrier's rate limits, and a setting invented to make a demo look good is how a real store ends up throttled\n    * **A tracking page the add-on serves itself.** A real label's link points at the carrier's own website; there is no carrier, so there is a bare page at `{api}\/kahunacart\/shipping\/public\/demo-track\/{number}` with its own markup, no theme, no assets and `noindex` on it \u2014 registered only when the demo carrier is the configured aggregator. It is public and unauthenticated, which is exactly what a tracking page is, and the price of that is an allowlist with a test on it: **a status, a time and a place, and never a name, an address, an order number or a price**\n    * **`bin\/plugin kahunacart-shipping seed` fills a store with parcels.** A ship-from address, four boxes, both carrier accounts written exactly as **Refresh carriers** would have written them, sizes onto the seeded catalogue \u2014 which carries a weight and no dimensions, so without them every parcel is an estimate \u2014 one oversized product that exists to be refused, shipping methods bound to the demo's services in the zones core's own seeder makes, and eleven orders carrying one part of the story each: delivered, out for delivery, in transit, one bought two minutes ago, two parcels on one order, an international parcel with a customs declaration, one that failed and came back, one refunded, one the carrier refused in its own words, one with no label yet so the packer's proposal is on screen, and one with nothing physical on it at all. Every label is bought through the add-on's own label service and every shipment written through KahunaCart's own fulfilment service, so a bug in the purchase path is a bug in the demo rather than something the demo papers over. Run it twice and the store is the same store; `--purge` asks first, defaults to no, and takes back exactly what it wrote and nothing else\n    * **One service exists to say no.** Driftwood Ground refuses a parcel over 900 mm on its longest side, one over 20 kg billable, and any destination abroad \u2014 because a store cannot demonstrate its refusal table with a carrier that says yes to everything, and \"the service you picked will not take a parcel this long\" is the most common real refusal there is. The seeder puts a 1,150 mm wall rack in the catalogue precisely so a visitor can add it to a cart and watch a method disappear from the checkout with a sentence naming why, and the other services stay priced. The carrier refuses at the counter what it refused at the quote, too, so a stale rate or a mis-picked service is a `failed` label carrying the carrier's own sentence rather than an exception nobody sees\n    * **The demo driver is also the worked example for anybody writing a second one.** It is the aggregator contract with all the API noise taken out of it: one class, every method, every honest refusal, and not a line of cURL in the way. It declares its capabilities honestly including the noes \u2014 no tracking webhook and no signed webhooks, because there is nothing on the other end to post one \u2014 and it takes no HTTP client at all, which is asserted by reading its constructor rather than promised in a comment. Its suites run the real label service, the real tracking service and the real ticker with it underneath, so a divergence between the demo and the product is a failing test rather than a demo that behaves differently from the thing being sold. See docs\/development.md\n    * **Documentation, end to end.** Twelve pages in `docs\/` for somebody reading on GitHub \u2014 getting started, carriers, boxes and packing, labels, tracking, customs, the demo carrier, configuration, a reference of every route, tool, permission, CLI command, job, table and event, troubleshooting, development and testing \u2014 and a whole Shipping chapter on kahunacart.com written for a merchant reading a website. `docs\/testing.md` carries the four layers the suite is built in, the real-key campaign in the order it has to be run, and the demo walk that needs no key at all\n    * **A `kc-shipping` test store**, served locally, that comes up with the demo carrier on and the seed applied, so every screen has something on it before anybody pastes a key, and puts itself back to a clean store in one command while keeping the catalogue, the zones, the boxes and any key that has been pasted\n\n1. [](#improved)\n    * **A store that installs this and pastes nothing is byte-identical to one that has not installed it.** No driver is registered until a token is configured, so no method is carrier-bound, nothing in the checkout path changes, and no call is ever made. The same is true of switching the plugin off: every bound method goes unavailable with a reason and the merchant's fallback carries checkout, which is core's own documented behaviour rather than anything this plugin invents\n    * **A carrier never blocks checkout**, and the suite asserts it rather than assuming it. A driver that throws, times out, is switched off or was never configured leaves every bound method shown unavailable with a sentence a customer can act on, and the storefront still renders\n\n1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **A label whose stored file has gone opens the carrier's own copy instead of a broken tab.** The file route has always answered the hosted link as JSON with a 200 on it rather than redirecting to a link that may itself have expired \u2014 and the parcels panel beside an order opened every answer as bytes, so what a merchant actually got was a tab of unreadable JSON. The worst possible way to be told a file is missing. The panel reads the content type now: the carrier's link opens in the new tab, and the card says the stored copy has gone and that those links expire, so the fix is to void the label and buy another. The thumbnail beside the details falls back to the drawn card as it always did\n    * **A delivery option no longer prints a line of codes under its own name.** A driver's rate carries two kinds of thing \u2014 Shippo's \"Delivery in 1 to 3 business days\", and the service code, the band and the reference a label is bought against \u2014 and all of it went onto the offer's one metadata map. A theme prints that map by looping it, so the checkout picker read `demo cached driftwood_ground Driftwood Ground 1215 USD 5 domestic 6000 1 1 demo_rate_8e6c5d` under the method name. KahunaCart 1.2.9 splits an offer's metadata in two \u2014 the half a person reads and the half a machine does \u2014 and both drivers now put only sentences in the first. Shippo sends its `duration_terms` and its `arrives_by`, and no longer sends the service zone or a One Rate variant's `terms`, which were codes and are already said by the service code. The demo driver sends nothing at all: its band, its billable weight, its parcel count and its `simulated` flag were its own working, and a customer choosing between two delivery options learned nothing from `domestic 6000 1`. The underlying carrier is not sent either \u2014 `usps_priority` already names it. What a label needs later still rides across: `rate_ref` and `carrier_account` travel under the names core reserves, land on the offer's `carrier` map, reach the order's audit trail, and are never printed\n    * **A customer no longer reads `%service% will not carry a parcel this long.` at checkout.** A driver refuses a service with its own language key and its own placeholders, core carries both halves \u2014 `detail_key` and `detail_params` \u2014 and the storefront theme prints the key with no arguments at all. Every parameterised refusal therefore reached a customer with the per cent signs still in it, the two the Shippo driver produces included: `%carrier%: %message%` when a carrier refused a service, and `The carrier quoted in %currency%` when a rate came back in the wrong money. All six now read correctly with nothing substituted into them, because the fact each would have named is already beside it \u2014 the method's own label is the service's name \u2014 and the carrier's own words still reach the merchant verbatim on the Labels tab. Found on the first live checkout with the oversized product in the cart. The other half is a theme that substitutes named parameters, and it is written up in docs\/development.md\n    * **The Carriers tab no longer tells a demo store to paste a carrier key.** `GET \/kahunacart\/shipping\/aggregators` answered `configured` from the Shippo token, whatever driver the store was actually running, and the whole tab is gated on that one boolean \u2014 so a store on the demo carrier, which has no token and never will, drew the empty state over a registered driver with two carrier accounts that was pricing a live checkout at that moment. It asks the registry now, which is the same question of Shippo and the right one of every other driver\n    * **The docs no longer describe two buttons that do not exist.** `docs\/tracking.md` and `docs\/configuration.md` both said the webhook secret is made by a **Generate** button on the Carriers screen and handed to Shippo by a **Register** one. The driver implements the whole webhook-setup contract and the plugin can build the address, but no route exposes any of it, so there is no card and there are no buttons. Both pages now carry the four manual steps \u2014 make a secret, save it, build the address, paste it at Shippo \u2014 and the Instructions tab carries them too, which is where the finding said they should be until the routes land\n    * **A refusal reaches the merchant in the carrier's own words rather than as \"HTTP 422\".** Buying, voiding and recording a parcel all answer an HTTP error when the carrier says no, and every fetch helper in the admin throws on a bad status and prints the error's message \u2014 so a body carrying `outcome` and `reasons` under `data` gave it nothing to print but the status number, and the branch meant to draw the carrier's sentence could never run. The refusal now answers a problem document with a title and the carrier's own sentence in it, which is what a thrown error carries. Found on a live store: a refund the demo carrier refused because the parcel had already been collected arrived as \"HTTP 422\"\n    * **A 403 on the buy sheet no longer blames the carrier.** A store with `demo.readonly` on, or an account without the labels permission, is the store refusing, not the carrier, and sending somebody to look at their carrier account over it wastes an afternoon. The sheet says **The store refused it** for a 403 and keeps core's own sentence underneath\n    * **The demo label's footer no longer falls off the right-hand edge of the page.** dompdf adds padding to a declared width rather than taking it out of it, so `width: 100%` plus side padding made the label's content box twenty points wider than the 4x6 page and the end of every long line was cut off. A block fills its parent on its own\n    * **`item_excluded` is produced for the first time.** Core reserved the reason code for an add-on and has never itself produced one. A comma-separated list of shipping classes this carrier will not take \u2014 empty by default, so a store that configures none behaves exactly as it did \u2014 takes every method bound to the carrier off the list and names the product, instead of leaving the customer looking at a shorter list with no explanation. The refusal is made in `supports()`, which core's runner asks before it spends any of the timeout, so it costs no network call; the exclusion event, which fires after the carrier has already been priced, is what rewords the reason so it says what actually happened"}}},"kahunacart-bookings":{"name":"KahunaCart Bookings","version":"1.1.14","description":"Appointments, classes and anything else with a time on it, for KahunaCart","description_html":"<p>Appointments, classes and anything else with a time on it, for KahunaCart<\/p>","description_plain":"Appointments, classes and anything else with a time on it, for KahunaCart","icon":"calendar-check","author":{"name":"Trilby Media, LLC","email":"hello@trilby.media"},"dependencies":[{"name":"grav","version":">=2.0.0"},{"name":"kahunacart","version":">=1.4.4"}],"compatibility":{"grav":["2.0"]},"homepage":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/add-ons\/bookings","docs":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/docs\/bookings\/overview","keywords":"grav, premium, commerce, ecommerce, bookings, appointments, scheduling, calendar, classes","bugs":"https:\/\/github.com\/getgrav\/grav-premium-issues\/labels\/kahunacart-bookings","license":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/license","tag_name":"1.1.14","date":"2026-10-26T03:50:44Z","release_type":"stable","zipball_url":"https:\/\/getgrav.org\/download\/plugins\/kahunacart-bookings\/1.1.14","repository":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/add-ons\/bookings","premium":{"vendor":"KahunaCart","validator":"kahunacart","license_product":"kahunacart-bookings","checkout_url":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/shop\/product\/kahunacart-bookings","button":"Buy Now","filename":"grav-plugin-kahunacart-bookings"},"slug":"kahunacart-bookings","install_path":"user\/plugins\/kahunacart-bookings","content_sha256":"4d936bc9e5e6239382ab1b0d73409a9a6a1445c59fa6179e94c147d336e85229","changelog":{"1.1.14":{"date":"10\/25\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **Buttons in these emails stay readable on a light button color.** The writing on a button was always white, so a store that picked a light Email Button Color got white on yellow. It now turns dark when the button is light, the same as KahunaCart's own emails. Needs KahunaCart 1.6.5 for the dark writing; an older KahunaCart keeps white\n"},"1.1.13":{"date":"10\/23\/2026","content":"1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **The front desk's Today is the shop's Today.** It was worked out from the first schedule's timezone, and a schedule saved without one is stored as UTC, so a Denver shop saw tomorrow's bookings from 6 PM. The desk now uses the store's own timezone, the same one the reports use (`reports.timezone`, then Grav's `system.timezone`). The theme helpers' \"today\" (the availability board, the day's lanes and the counts) and the timezone handed to Bookings modes use it as well; they only read `system.timezone` before\n"},"1.1.12":{"date":"10\/22\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **No empty Bookings tab.** On KahunaCart 1.6.0 with Hide Empty Tabs on (the default), a customer who has never booked doesn't get a Bookings tab in their account; the page still opens at its address. Older KahunaCart shows the tab as before\n    * **The booking form knows who's booking.** A signed-in customer finds their name, email and phone (from their default address) already in the details form, and a guest sees \"Have an account? Sign in\" above it, which comes back to the page after signing in. The picker asks a new public route, `GET \/kahunacart\/bookings\/public\/me`, which answers only about the browser session that asked and is never cached, so the picker still works on a cached page. The words are `WIDGET_HAVE_ACCOUNT` and `WIDGET_SIGN_IN`, and the line's class is `kcb-signin`\n"},"1.1.11":{"date":"10\/22\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Themes can list services and staff.** `kahunacart_bookings_services()` returns the appointments a shopper can book, each with its name, summary, link, price, length, whether it waits for a yes, its category and the people who do it. Ask for one `category` (child categories come with it), a list of `products` in the order you give, `group_by_category: true` to get `groups` in the category tree's order, or a `limit`. Where a service has options that differ, the price is the lowest with `from: true`, and the length is a range such as \"40 \u2013 55 min\". Lengths are written the way a salon says them: \"45 min\", \"1 hr 15\". `kahunacart_bookings_staff()` returns the people (never rooms or equipment), each with the services they take and a link that books them, and takes `products` or `category` to list only the people who can do those. Drafts, products the shopper may not see, switched-off services and classes are left out. So is any option the shopper couldn't buy, before the price and the length are worked out: one with no price in the shopper's currency, and one whose own rule is switched off (it doesn't fall back to the product's rule). A service with no option left isn't listed at all, and its people and their links come only from the options that are left. Both return an empty list on anything they cannot answer\n    * **A link can open a service with one person already chosen.** Add `?with=` and the person's slug to a service's address and the picker opens with them picked. If that person is switched off, is not a person, or cannot take that service on any option the shopper can buy, the picker opens as usual. Nothing changes when the address has no `with`\n    * **Themes can show what's free.** Four more template functions, for a theme that hires things out by the day. `kahunacart_bookings_lengths('trail-29')` lists the lengths a product is sold in, in option order, each with its price, a length such as \"4 hrs\", \"3 days\" or \"1 week\", and the times it can start at (\"8:00am\", \"1:00pm\"; none for an option a day or longer). `kahunacart_bookings_sizes('trail-29', { date, variant })` lists the equipment the product draws on, each with a label and note from the bookable's `size` and `note`, how many are free on that day and how many there are, a state of `open`, `low` or `full`, and a link that books that one. `kahunacart_bookings_next_free('trail-29')` gives the next day from tomorrow with something free: its date, \"tomorrow\" or a short day and date such as \"Fri, Oct 23\", and how many are free. `kahunacart_bookings_board({ products, category, days, from })` gives a week at a glance: the days, and for each product how many are free on each one, with a state of `open`, `low`, `full` or `closed` and a link that opens the product on that day. Free means the most that are free at any one start that day on the product's shortest option, worked out by the same engine the picker asks, so hours, days off, outside calendars, buffers and holds all count. Low means some are taken and two or fewer are left, or a quarter of the total or fewer. Days are the shop's days, not the visitor's. The new wording (`LENGTH_DAY`, `LENGTH_WEEK`, `NEXT_FREE_TOMORROW`, `DAY_DATE`) is in the language file. Each returns an empty list (or null) on anything it cannot answer\n    * **A link can open the picker on a day.** Add `?date=2026-10-25` to a product's address, or `date` to `kahunacart_booking()` or the shortcode, and the calendar opens on that day with its times showing. If the day has no times, is in the past or is not a date, the picker opens as usual. Nothing changes when the address has no `date`\n    * **`?with=` also picks a size.** The slug of a piece of equipment the customer chooses from (a bike size, say) preselects it, the same way a person's slug does. A switched-off one, one the product doesn't offer, and a room are ignored\n    * **A hire can only start while the shop is open.** A three-day or week-long hire runs on a schedule that is open round the clock, so it used to be offered on the day the shop is shut. Add `\"starts_in_hours\"` to the rule's `meta_json`, with a schedule's slug or id (`\"golden-hour-hours\"`) or `true` for the rule's location's hours, and a start is offered only inside those hours. The hire may still run on through closed time. Off unless a rule says so; see Schedules \u2192 Hires that run overnight\n    * **The picker draws a pool of sizes as sizes.** When the customer picks from equipment (a bike's S, M, L), the picker shows a row of sizes, \"Any\" first, each with its `size` label and its `note` under it. Once a day and a time are picked, each size says how many are free then (\"3 left\", \"None left\"), a size with none free is switched off, and a size running short is marked low. Picking a size narrows the times to that size's and keeps the time picked. The public availability answer carries `free` on every start of a pool the customer picks from, and the services answer carries `kind`, `size`, `note` and `units` on each piece of equipment; nothing changes for people or for a pool the shop picks from. New class names `kcb-sizes`, `kcb-sizelist`, `kcb-size`, `kcb-sizelabel`, `kcb-sizenote`, `kcb-sizefree`, with `kcb-selected`, `kcb-low` and `kcb-full`. The words are `WIDGET_SIZE`, `WIDGET_ANY_SIZE` (\"Any\", which a store can make \"Any size\") and `WIDGET_SIZE_NONE`; a pool of people still says \"Anyone\"\n    * **A by-the-night picker can be told which stay to open on.** `kahunacart_booking(product, { arrive: '2027-05-25', leave: '2027-05-30', guests: 2 })` and the shortcode's `arrive`, `leave` and `guests` write `data-arrive`, `data-leave` and `data-guests` on the mount point, where Rentals' picker reads them. A date that is not a date and a number that is not a positive whole one are dropped, the picker's own link (`?arrive=&leave=&guests=`) still wins, and nothing is written when a page gives none of them\n    * **Themes can draw today's book lane by lane, and count the openings left.** `kahunacart_bookings_day({ people: ['jo', 'mia'], resources: ['tub-1'], date, from, to })` lays one day out with a lane for each person and each tub (or other piece of equipment or room that is not a person). A lane is a list of blocks that run end to end across the day, each with its `start` and `end` in minutes after midnight, the same times as the store prints them (`start_label`, `end_label`), a `state` of `booked`, `free`, `closed` or `break`, a `service` and a `link`. Booked is a booking, a hold, a class or an outside calendar on that person or tub, with the buffers around it, and `service` is the service's title and nothing else: never the customer's name, the pet's, the details they filled in, their notes or contact details, and no id of any kind in the block or in a link. A booking on a service the shopper couldn't open has no title. Free is time that can really be booked: after the notice the service needs, inside the booking window, on the shop's grid of starts, and long enough for the shortest service the person or tub takes, so a quarter of an hour between two bookings is closed, not free. A free block's `link` opens that service's page on that person or tub, that day and that time (`?with=&date=&time=`, and `variant=` when the service has several options). Break is a gap in someone's hours, such as lunch. Closed is the rest: before they start, after they finish, a day away, time already gone. The answer also carries the day (`date`, `label`, `is_today`), `now` in minutes while it is today, and `open` and `close` (the hours of the lanes asked for, or the stretch given as `from` and `to`, as `HH:MM` or minutes). `date` is today in the store's timezone unless given. Name nobody and every person is a lane. A day nothing is open has `open` and `close` empty and no blocks. `kahunacart_bookings_counts({ people, resources, sessions: ['puppy-playgroup'] })` gives `openings_today` (the free blocks left today, the same ones the lanes draw), `resources_free_now` (how many of the named tubs are open and unused this minute for as long as their shortest service takes) and, for each class named, `places_left`, `capacity`, `starts` and a `label` such as \"Sat, Oct 24, 10:00am\" for its next date with a place left. A tub or a person is read from the same hours, days off, outside calendars, diary and holds the picker is. A service that needs more than the one lane (a groom that needs a tub too) is offered when that lane is free, and the picker the link opens checks the rest. Both return an empty answer on anything they cannot answer\n    * **A link can open the picker on an option and a time.** Add `?variant=` and an option's id to a service's address and the picker opens on that option (a size, a length), and `?time=14:30` beside a `?date=` picks that start on the day when it is free: it opens ready to confirm, and nothing is held until the customer confirms. A time the day doesn't have leaves the day open on its own times; an option the service doesn't offer, a time that isn't a time, and a time with no day leave the picker as it was. Times are read the way the picker prints them, in the visitor's own timezone, as `?date=` is. `kahunacart_booking()` and the shortcode take `variant` and `time` the same way, and a page that fills a mount point in itself can set `data-variant` and `data-time`\n1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **The booking picker no longer offers an option whose own rule is switched off.** An option with a rule of its own is governed by that rule, on or off; it used to fall back to the product's rule and be offered anyway. Now it is left out of the picker, asking for its times by hand is refused like any option that isn't offered, a time on it can't be held, and it can't be added to the basket as a booking (it says \"That option is not available to book right now.\"). The store's own option list under the picker is hidden whenever the picker has an option of its own, so a switched-off option can't be picked from there either. Moving a booking that was already made is not affected\n    * **Long hires are said in days.** The theme helpers (`kahunacart_bookings_lengths()` and the services helper's length and range) said a 58 hour hire was \"58 hrs\". A length of a day or more is now the calendar days it runs across from its start: out at 8:00, back by 6:00pm two days later is \"3 days\", 154 hours from 8:00 is \"1 week\", anything else \"N days\". The picker's option box says the same, through the services answer's new `length`. Under a day nothing changes\n    * **Prices follow Whole Amounts.** With KahunaCart 1.6.0's Whole Amounts on, the theme helpers' `price` and the picker's prices said \"$59.00\". They now print the way core prints its own: \"$59\", never rounding cents away, and a set printed together (a product's lengths, the picker's options) keeps its cents together or drops them together. The mount point carries `data-whole-amounts`, and the services answer `whole_amounts`, only when it is on. On an older core nothing changes\n    * **A hire that ends after the range asked about is still offered.** A three-day hire starting on the last day of a month view was left off the calendar, because the hours were read only to the end of the month. Every start inside the range is offered now, whatever day it ends. And a hire that starts every day at 8:00 still starts at 8:00 after the clocks change, rather than at 7:00\n    * **A page with service cards loads the picker.** Core asks for a booking product's buy field to decide whether a card gets a quick Add button, and throws it away; that used up the page's one copy of the picker's script and stylesheet, so a picker further down the page never loaded. The buy field no longer marks them printed, and a page that prints them twice has the second copy taken out on its way out\n    * **No crash with KahunaCart switched off.** With KahunaCart off and Bookings on, every page failed with \"Class KahunaCart not found\". Bookings now does nothing at all when KahunaCart is off\n    * **A mode's own picker prints amounts the way the store does.** The `price` a booking mode's picker is handed ignored Whole Amounts and printed \"$2,100.00\" where the rest of the page said \"$2,100\". It follows the store for the page's own currency now, and takes the other amounts printed beside it so a list keeps its cents together\n3. [](#improved)\n    * **A day with no times says \"nothing available\"** in the picker instead of \"nothing free\", so nobody reads it as a price.\n    * **Service lengths read in the store's language.** The theme helpers' \"45 min\", \"1 hr 15\" and \"40 \u2013 55 min\" are language strings now (`LENGTH_*`), so a German or French store can word them in Translations\n    * **Ships its license.** A `LICENSE` file now comes with the plugin, and the blueprint links to the KahunaCart License at kahunacart.com\/license instead of the Grav Premium license\n    * **With JavaScript off, a product sold by the night talks about nights.** The note says to turn JavaScript on to pick your nights (`WIDGET_NO_SCRIPT_NIGHTS`) instead of a time, and the store's quantity box and Add to cart button, which can do nothing without the picker, stay hidden\n\n"},"1.1.10":{"date":"10\/14\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Take payment at the till.** With the Point of Sale add-on installed, a booking whose order still owes money has a **Take payment** button on the front desk. It opens the till with that order ready to pay, for anyone allowed to use the till. The desk itself still takes no money; the till does. From the till, **Book next visit** opens the desk's New booking sheet for the same customer (`\/desk#\/new?customer=\u2026`)\n1. [](#improved)\n    * **A bookable service rung up at the till** is refused with a sentence pointing staff to the front desk to pick a time, rather than the shop's own \"Pick a time\" message\n"},"1.1.9":{"date":"10\/13\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Know when a customer moves their own booking.** **A customer moved their booking** (normal) goes to your channels with the service, the old and the new time, the customer's name and a link to the booking, once for every move. It is sent only when the customer did the moving, from their link or account, never when you or your desk did\n    * **An outside calendar that stops loading reaches your phone.** The dashboard alert is now also sent to your channels, once when a calendar stops loading and once when they all load again. Needs KahunaCart 1.5.3\n1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **The Open People and places button on the calendar alert goes to the right screen.** It pointed at an address from the old admin\n"},"1.1.8":{"date":"10\/11\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **A closed store takes no new booking.** With KahunaCart 1.5.0, a store that is coming soon or paused refuses the public book and hold routes with the store's message (503 `store_closed`), so free and pay-on-the-day services stop too, as do waiting-list places. Existing bookings can still be read, cancelled and rescheduled, and signed-in staff can still book\n1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **Works with a shop mounted at the site root.** With KahunaCart's `route: '\/'` (core 1.5.0) the product links in session lists, the customer's Bookings tab link in messages and the picker's cart form have one slash in them rather than two, and the picker's stylesheet loads only on the shop's own pages\n"},"1.1.7":{"date":"10\/08\/2026","content":"1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **The front desk works on a site installed in a subfolder.** On a store at an address like `example.com\/store`, the desk at `\/store\/desk` asked the domain root for its diary and showed an error, and its Refresh link, its sign-out and its home-screen icon pointed at the domain root too. It now uses the store's own address throughout, and follows a store that has moved or versioned its API\n2. [](#improved)\n    * The picker, the desk and the calendar feed links now take their addresses from KahunaCart's own `SiteUrl::api()` instead of each building one, so all three agree with every other add-on. Needs KahunaCart 1.4.4 or later\n"},"1.1.6":{"date":"10\/08\/2026","content":"1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **The booking picker works on a site installed in a subfolder.** On a store at an address like `example.com\/store`, every booking and rental product said \"That did not go through (404)\" where the calendar should be, because the picker asked the domain root for its times instead of the store. It now asks the store's own address, the same way every other storefront link does. A store at the root of its domain is unchanged\n"},"1.1.5":{"date":"10\/04\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Bookings sells in more than one currency.** On a store with several currencies enabled, the booking picker, the class list and the manage page price in the visitor's own currency with that currency's decimal places (12,000 yen, 55.00 euros), the held time is quoted in it, and the basket takes it only into a basket in the same currency. A time held in one currency and carried into a basket in another is refused with a sentence asking the visitor to pick it again, and a hold made before this version is in the store's own currency. A store with one currency sends and stores exactly what it did. Needs KahunaCart 1.4.0 or later\n    * **The front desk and the admin take bookings in a currency.** The New booking sheet shows a row of currency buttons on a store with more than one, starting on the store's own, and `desk\/services`, `POST desk\/bookings` and `POST \/kahunacart\/bookings` take a `currency`. A desk booking is collected in that currency (**To collect 9,000 yen** is 9,000), and one taken with payment from the admin builds its order in it\n    * **A booking mode says what currency it priced in.** `Offer` has a `currency`, a request for one carries `currency` when the booking is not in the store's own, and an offer that comes back in another currency or none is refused, so a mode that has not been taught about currencies is unavailable in them instead of selling at the wrong number. `Pricing\\BookablePrices` answers a bookable's price as an amount, free or not available\n    * **Every table in the Bookings section has a search box.** Type a few words and the rows that do not hold all of them drop away as you type, in any order and whatever the case: Sessions by class name, a class's dates by date, who is coming to a date and who is waiting by name or reference, Services by service or rule (a service keeps its options with it), People by name, hours or place, Hours by name or timezone, Places by name or address, and the Troubleshoot answer by date, status or reason. A short table inside a record, like a class with a few dates, gets its box once it has more than eight rows. The Bookings list keeps its Reference box as it was. Needs KahunaCart 1.3.11 or later, which draws the box\n2. [](#improved)\n    * Changing staff disables stale calendar choices while times load, and a day with no new times is cleared without losing the calendar\n    * Customer cancellations show the policy's refund explanation and use a booking mode's own cancellation words\n    * Moving a booking previews prices in its own currency, including after that currency is turned off, with the right decimal places\n    * Taking payment for an admin booking claims the staff hold even when the browser already has a visitor cookie, and releases the hold if the order cannot be built\n    * Booking and class-list shortcode pages render each visitor's currency instead of keeping the first visitor's prices in Grav's content cache\n    * Ordinary appointment and class holds also refuse a basket in another currency instead of silently changing their price\n    * Book and waiting-list links keep the currency shown in the class list\n    * Single-currency desk and picker translations keep their previous keys\n    * Existing bookings keep their own prices and desk balances after you turn their currency off\n    * **A price nobody set in a currency is never free.** An option with no amount in the visitor's currency says \"Not available in EUR\" in the picker and in the class list, cannot be held, and is refused at the desk and in the admin with the same sentence. A price of 0 with nothing else set is still free in every currency\n    * **Everything after a booking is paid is in the order's own currency.** The deposit, the balance, the \"paid so far\" lines on the receipt, the account tab, the manage page and the emails, the refund a cancellation offers and the price of a move are all in the currency the booking was paid in, whatever the visitor is browsing in now. A cancellation policy is told which currency the amount is in, and a fee with no amount in it does not apply\n    * **The widget's `price` helper and the manage page format an amount with the currency's own decimal places** instead of dividing by 100\n    * **A booking with no order remembers its currency.** A new column, `kahunacart_booking_bookings.currency` (migration `bookings_0009`), holds the currency of a booking that has no order behind it, and only when it is not the store's own\n\n3. [](#bugfix)\n    * The front desk reads payment status and currency together, avoiding an extra order lookup for every booking while retaining prices in a disabled currency\n"},"1.1.4":{"date":"10\/01\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * Help text in the Bookings section's forms now follows the admin's Help text setting, showing below the label or in a tooltip behind an info icon\n"},"1.1.3":{"date":"09\/30\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **The download no longer carries the plugin's tests.** `tests\/`, `phpunit.xml`, the desk app's source (`desk-src\/`; the built app in `assets\/desk` still ships) and `bin\/` stay in the repository and out of the release zip, 133 files fewer on every install. Nothing the plugin runs is affected\n"},"1.1.2":{"date":"09\/30\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Every table in the Bookings section sorts by its column headings.** Press a heading to sort by it, and press it again to turn the order round. The Bookings list sorts on the server, so a heading orders every booking the filters leave and not only the page on screen, and pressing one goes back to page one. `GET \/kahunacart\/bookings` takes `sort` and `dir` with `service`, `with`, `seats` and `order` added to the four it already had (`start_at`, `created_at`, `status` and `reference`), so a heading and the parameter are one control, and Export CSV follows the order on screen. Sessions, a class's dates and who is coming to each, Services, People, Hours, Places and the Troubleshoot answer sort in the browser. A date's waiting list stays in the order a place would be offered, and a service's options stay under their service. Needs KahunaCart 1.3.8 or later, which draws the headings\n"},"1.1.1":{"date":"09\/23\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **The test suite runs against KahunaCart 1.3, which bundles brick\/money and YetiSearch under its own namespace.** When it borrows the base plugin's dependencies it now also loads core's `vendor-prefixed\/autoload.php`. Against an older KahunaCart without that file nothing changes. Nothing in Bookings itself names brick\/money or YetiSearch, so the add-on needs no other change\n"},"1.1.0":{"date":"09\/22\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **New booking at the front desk picks a day off a month.** After the service, the sheet shows a month calendar with a bar under each day saying how much room it has, so a receptionist on the phone can say \"Thursday's nearly full, Friday's wide open\" without trying days one at a time. Closed, full and past days are greyed out, and it opens on the first day with room. Tapping a day lists its open times in one column per person or place, which is how the question is actually asked: \"who's free at two?\" On a tablet the month and the columns sit side by side\n    * **An appointment can be made longer or shorter at the desk.** A \u221215 \/ +15 control under **How long?**, with a \"usually 45 min\" link back to the service's own length, for the customer who always needs a bit more time. Every time offered is one that length fits, and the conflict guard checks the length that is booked. `desk\/slots` and `POST desk\/bookings` take `minutes`; only an appointment can be stretched, because a class runs as long as the class runs\n    * **Move and Cancel are one tap away on the booking sheet.** They sit under the main button instead of behind More. Move uses the same month and columns as New booking, opens on the booking's own day with its own person first, and can move it to somebody else or change its length on the way; the booking being moved no longer stands in its own way, so a half-hour nudge is offered. Cancel asks first, takes an optional reason for the trail, and keeps the **Let the customer know** tick, because a tablet on a counter gets tapped by accident\n    * **A double-booked booking can be put right at the desk.** Money that arrives after its time has gone makes a booking that holds no time, and until now nothing could clear that: the desk would not move it, and moving it anywhere else left it flagged and waiting. Moving it onto a free time now claims that time through the same guard, clears the flag, confirms it the way a paid booking of its service would be, and writes **resolved** in its trail. The person on the phone to the customer does that from the booking sheet; handing money back stays the owner's. The sheet says what to do instead of \"the owner has to sort this one out\", and its chip just says **Double-booked**\n    * **A customer sheet at the desk.** \"Patel rang, when was she last in?\" Find opens the customer rather than jumping to their next booking, and so does the name on any booking. The sheet has tap-to-call and tap-to-email, how often they come and how often they do not, their next and last bookings (each one opens), and **Book for them**, which starts New booking with them already filled in. A stylist sees only the bookings in her own column, the same rule the booking sheet follows. Still no address, no orders and no spend; those are the owner's, in the admin\n    * **The desk can put a customer's details right without ringing the owner.** **Edit details** on the customer sheet changes the name and the telephone number, and on an account that works the whole diary the email too \u2014 refused, and saying who, when another customer already has that address, because that is the same person twice and joining them up is the owner's. The sheet says the email is also how they sign in to the shop. Orders are filed by customer number, not by address, so nothing is split by changing it. Two new desk routes, `GET` and `POST desk\/customers\/{id}`, which the route test now lists\n    * **`desk\/slots` counts a range of days.** Asked about `from` \u2026 `to` it returns how many open times each day has in the store's timezone, for the month calendar, rather than a month of times; asked about one `date` it lists the times as before. `except` names a booking being moved. Still nine desk routes\n\n2. [](#improved)\n    * **The New booking sheet folds each answered question down to its answer.** Services and options are tiles, a picked one shows alone with **Change** beside it, and **Who with?** is a row of pills, so the calendar is on screen without scrolling past every service in the shop. The **Book it** button names what it books \u2014 day, time and who with \u2014 because the time picked is usually scrolled away by the time the customer is found\n    * **Check in is offered from the day a booking starts.** A booking for Thursday no longer has Check in as its big button on a Tuesday. A cancelled or turned-down booking no longer says \"Pay today\", and the sheet draws no buttons it has nothing behind\n\n3. [](#bugfix)\n    * **Saying yes to a double-booked booking confirmed an appointment nothing was keeping free.** The desk offered **Yes, book it** on one, and `approve` would have marked it confirmed while it claimed no time at all. The button is gone and the server refuses it: move it to a free time first, or cancel it\n    * **The desk offered a class at every half hour its teacher was free.** `desk\/slots` sent a class through the appointment scheduler, so a colour workshop that runs one Thursday evening was offered at eleven on a Saturday morning. It now answers with the dates the class actually runs, leaving out any that are full or past their registration deadline, the same way the storefront does\n    * **New booking hung on \"Loading\u2026\" in a store with a rule per variant.** `desk\/services` listed a product once for every rule it had, so a groom with a rule on Small and on Large came back twice and the sheet stopped with `each_key_duplicate`. Each product now comes back once, with its variants inside it as before\n"},"1.0.1":{"date":"09\/21\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **The docs and the diary seed say where the deposits switch is.** KahunaCart 1.2.13 moves **Deposits and part payment** from **Settings \u2192 Security** to **Settings \u2192 Commerce**. The massage clinic walkthrough named a tab that never existed (**Settings \u2192 Checkout**) and now names **KahunaCart \u2192 Settings \u2192 Commerce**, [Payments and deposits](payments-and-deposits.md#deposits) says where the switch is, and `seed diary` says the same when it skips the part-paid booking on a store with deposits off\n"},"1.0.0":{"date":"09\/21\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Bookings, the add-on.** Appointments, and everything else that takes somebody's or something's time. Core keeps the product, the price, the cart, the order, the money and the customer; this keeps what a product *means* as a booking \u2014 how long it takes, who or what it needs, and when it can be had. This first release is the engine and the tables underneath all of it, with no screens on top yet: what is here is installable, migrates clean, and answers \"when can a full groom be booked next Thursday, and if not, why not\"\n    * **One kind of record for anything that can be booked.** A stylist, a grooming tub, a range lane and a treatment room are all **bookables**, with a number of identical units on each \u2014 so ten lanes is one row with ten units, and the same engine books a person, a room and a machine without knowing which it is holding. That is the one modelling change over Schedule Pro, and everything else here follows from it\n    * **A service says what it needs, and a time is only offered when all of it is free.** A full groom needs one groomer from {Ann, Bo} with the customer choosing, and the tub with no choice. A range lane needs one unit from Lanes. A massage needs a therapist and a room. Candidate start times come from the first need; every other need has to have somebody or something free for the whole span, buffers included, before the time goes on the page. For each need the customer did not pick, the first free one by position takes it \u2014 and which one that is rides on the slot, so what a customer was shown and what gets claimed when they pay are the same answer rather than two computations hoping to agree\n    * **A blocked time says why, in words.** Too soon, too far ahead, full, booked, held by somebody else, time off, closed, busy on another calendar \u2014 and when a need is what failed, the reason names it: **\"Tub: booked\"**. That is one code path with the availability query, not a second one beside it, so the explanation cannot drift from what a customer is actually offered. \"Why can nobody book Thursday\" is the commonest question a booking system gets, and this is the answer to it\n    * **A service is a KahunaCart product of type Booking.** Registered as a product type with `ships: false` and `holdsStock: false`, and declared to core as a **service** rather than as a download \u2014 which is what keeps EU digital-services VAT off a haircut in Denver and keeps a digital-goods-only payment platform from being offered for one. Duration options are variants, so \"Small dog, 60 minutes, $55\" and \"Large dog, 90 minutes, $80\" are priced with nothing new built, and a rule written for a variant replaces the product's for that variant only\n    * **Working hours in three rungs, and time off where the person is.** A bookable's own schedule, else its location's, else the service's fallback \u2014 which is how a merchant thinks about it: a part-timer with no hours of her own is available when the shop is open. A weekly plan carries per-date overrides on the same record, so a bank holiday closes a day without needing a second kind of thing. Time off is whole days in the bookable's own timezone, because \"away on the 4th\" means the 4th where they are\n    * **The timezone and DST work came over from Schedule Pro untouched, matrix and all.** Spring forward, fall back, Kolkata's half hour and Auckland straddling the date line are the same tests they were, passing against the same code, with only the namespace line edited. Two bookables in two zones serving one service meet in the middle, and there is a test that says where\n    * **`bin\/plugin kahunacart-bookings slots`** prints the times for a product on a day, and `--explain` prints the blocked ones with the reason beside each. It is how this release is demonstrated with no screens at all, and it stays afterwards as the thing to run when a merchant says Thursday is empty. `seed` writes a whole business to point it at\n    * **Twelve tables, all created at once.** The seven this release writes to, and the five the next ones will \u2014 the bookings, the holds, the claims, the audit trail and the record of what has already been emailed. All of them now, so the leaves that follow add columns and never argue over who creates what. `reset --orders` takes the diary and leaves the salon standing, which is also what an hourly demo reset wants\n    * **Availability reads one table and nothing else.** A hold and a booking are both rows in `claims`, so they block in exactly the same way and turning one into the other changes no arithmetic anywhere. The reader behind it is an interface with one method on it, which is what lets the whole engine be driven from an array in a test \u2014 no database, no container, nothing on disk\n    * **Two customers cannot be sold the same chair.** Every path that takes a time \u2014 a visitor holding a slot, an order completing, a merchant booking somebody in over the phone, a customer moving Thursday to Friday \u2014 goes through one guard: a write lock on every bookable involved in ascending id order, a sweep of lapsed holds, a fresh look at what is free with this service's own buffers on it, and a unique key on the claim underneath all of it as the last word. No `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` anywhere, because KahunaCart does not take row locks that way and this does not start. There is a test with six real forked processes racing one SQLite file for the same two o'clock: one wins, five are told the time has gone, and against a bookable with two units, two win\n    * **A picked time is held while the customer finishes.** A hold is a claim like any other, so it blocks everybody else exactly as a booking does and becomes one without a single sum changing. Fifteen minutes in the cart, an hour once the payment page opens, and the longer of the two always wins \u2014 a payment page that outlived its own hold would be a customer paying for a time somebody else has taken. A hold is only ever read, resized or released by the browser that made it, so a guessed id frees nobody's Thursday. One hold per service and as many services as you like, which is a haircut and a massage in one basket\n    * **The appointment rides in the cart line.** The hold, the start, the timezone, the person and the words the customer saw, on core's own `line_data` \u2014 so two appointments for one service are two lines rather than one line of quantity two, the cart page prints \"Thu 25 Sep, 2:00pm with Ann\" under the product name, and the receipt says the same thing months later whatever anybody does to Ann's name in the meantime. A booking added with no time on it is refused with a sentence the customer can act on, and so is a hold that lapsed while they were deciding\n    * **The order and the diary are wired through core's compensating hooks.** An order completing makes the bookings, money landing confirms them, and cancelling or refunding gives the times back \u2014 each one idempotent per order and each one declaring how to undo itself, so a redelivered webhook makes no second appointment and a failed step leaves nothing half-done. The release deliberately has no undo: putting a cancelled booking back would claim a time the shop has already offered to somebody else\n    * **Money arriving for a time that has gone is somebody's job, not a swallowed error.** If a hold lapses on the payment page and the slot goes while the card is being typed, the booking is still made \u2014 pending, marked as conflicted, claiming nothing, with the whole story in its trail \u2014 and the shop gets the loudest notification this add-on sends. A person rings the customer and moves them or hands the money back. The alternative is selling one chair twice and finding out when both parties arrive\n    * **Some bookings have nothing to pay.** A store or a single service can take payment on the day, and a free consultation costs nothing wherever it is set; those go straight into the diary with no cart, no order and no checkout. Everything with a price on it goes through the basket, and a storefront that asks to book one directly is told to add it to the cart instead rather than shown an error\n    * **Deposits work, and what actually confirms a booking is money moving rather than an order being fully paid.** A part-paid order is correctly not paid as far as every report and provider check is concerned \u2014 but a shop that has taken a deposit for Thursday morning is busy on Thursday morning, and the diary says so. Building this found that core announces `payment_captured` only on the authorise-then-capture path, so bank transfers and marked-paid orders come in by a different door and are listened for there too. Without that, a booking paid by transfer would have sat waiting for ever with nothing saying why\n    * **Cancelling never moves money on its own.** It frees the time, writes the trail, tells the shop and opens a refund request for a person to look at, because what a customer gets back is a merchant's policy and a merchant's money. A full refund cancels the bookings on the order; a part refund is a note in the trail and nothing more, because a goodwill gesture is not a cancellation\n    * **A calendar link per person, and outside calendars that block their time.** A stylist opens one link on her phone and every booking arrives on its own: moves follow the appointment rather than duplicating it, cancellations disappear, and there is nothing to install and no admin login to give out. What goes in it is a choice \u2014 everything, initials only, or the word \"Busy\" and the time \u2014 because a calendar link ends up in places nobody planned, and one person can answer differently from the rest of the shop. Pointed the other way, the evenings somebody has blocked in their own Google, iCloud or Outlook calendar stop being offered to customers, with no OAuth and nothing to keep signed in\n    * **A second calendar link, for Airbnb, VRBO and Booking.com.** A **channel link** per property, at a privacy level below all three of the others: the summary is \"Not available\", there is no guest, no service, no notes, no telephone number and no way back into the shop, and there is no setting anywhere that opens it up \u2014 because that link is pasted into somebody else's website and read by their machines. It is a separate token on a separate button, so leaving a channel does not take everybody's phone down with it. It carries the weeks somebody is **paying for right now** as well as the weeks already sold, since the gap between two polls is exactly when the same week gets sold twice; a basket left open is not in it, because baskets are abandoned all day long\n    * **Whole dates, counted where the property is.** A booking of a mode that books by the day goes out as `DTSTART;VALUE=DATE` and an exclusive `DTEND`, which is all a channel reads \u2014 and the dates come off the **property's own clock** rather than Greenwich, which is the difference between a right answer and one day out for anywhere west of UTC. The channel form leaves the day a booking ends on free, because somebody arrives that afternoon; a guest's own invitation still draws the banner across the days they are away, which is a different question with a different answer\n    * **An all-day event from a channel blocks the days it covers and not one more.** Read literally, \"the 9th to the 16th\" is midnight to midnight and takes the night before the arrival off the market as well \u2014 a genuinely sellable night refused, every time, at every property. A mode that counts days now says what its days run between, and the same event becomes check-out on the first date to check-in on the last: exactly the days sold, with both changeover days still bookable. An appointment book still reads an all-day entry as \"away all day\", because that is what it means there. A whole date with no end on it is one whole date, the way the standard says, instead of an hour nobody would notice\n    * **A cached calendar is only good for the zone and the dates it was fetched for.** Two things fell out of reading whole dates that were survivable while everything was hours and are not now. A `VALUE=DATE` value carries no timezone of its own, so the instants a parse produces belong to wherever the fetch was made for \u2014 two properties in two zones sharing one Airbnb address, or one property read once through the **Test** button and once through the calendar, were a whole day apart, with the night before an arrival refused and the last night the channel had sold quietly back on the market. And a copy is clipped to the dates it was asked about, so a month's answer was being served for every other month and pressing Test wrote thirty days over a year. The zone is in the cache key now, a copy carries the range it covers, and nothing narrower ever replaces something wider\n    * **`DURATION:P7D` on a whole-date reservation is seven days and not 604,800 seconds.** The week the clocks go back is 169 hours long; adding a fixed number of seconds lands an hour the wrong side of midnight, the exclusive end reads as the day before, and the last night goes back on the market. Real exporters write a reservation both ways and the two now agree\n    * **A channel that echoes our own bookings back at us is ignored.** Give Airbnb our link, then add Airbnb's link here, and every stay would otherwise arrive back as somebody else's reservation and block itself \u2014 and the worse half is the cancellation, where a stay we let go of would be held for ever by its own shadow at the other end. Our own ids are recognised on the way in and dropped before anything is cached, holds included\n    * **A calendar that will not load never closes the diary.** The last copy we got is used for up to a day, then the feed is skipped and the shop carries on selling \u2014 because one bad address emptying a whole salon is a worse mistake than one double booking against somebody's dentist. What is new against Schedule Pro is that the merchant is *told*: a dashboard warning names the calendar and the day it stopped, and it goes away on its own when the feed comes back. A blocked time names the calendar too, so a stylist with three feeds knows which one took the evening\n    * **A merchant typing a URL into a box is how a server-side request forgery starts, so that box is guarded.** `https` only, `webcal:\/\/` rewritten to it, the host resolved and every address it answers to checked against private, loopback, link-local and reserved ranges, at most two redirects with each hop checked again, and the address that passed pinned onto the connection so a name cannot resolve to something else a millisecond later. Two megabytes, no cookies, no sign-in. All of it refuses with a sentence a merchant can act on, and all of it is tested with a fake resolver rather than by firing requests at real private addresses\n    * **Our own bookings never come back at us.** Subscribe to a KahunaCart calendar link in Google, then point the add-on at that same Google calendar as a busy source, and nothing breaks: our own events are recognised and ignored. Without that every appointment would block itself, the diary would look full, and moving one would be refused by its own shadow\n    * **Repeating events in somebody else's calendar block every week they repeat.** Schedule Pro read `RRULE` as an unknown property and blocked the first occurrence only, so \"Tuesdays, six to eight, every week\" cost one Tuesday. Deleted occurrences are skipped, an event marked *free* in the owner's own calendar does not block anything, and the expansion is capped so a daily rule with no end cannot run away\n    * **Seventy-one endpoints, and the eleven public ones are public on purpose.** The storefront asks what is bookable, what times are on offer, takes a hold, books, and lets a customer see, move or cancel their own appointment with the token they were emailed \u2014 no login, no session, an origin check and a honeypot on every write, and a token pattern strict enough that a booking id in the URL is a 404 rather than somebody else's afternoon. Fifty of the fifty-one admin ones are MCP tools \u2014 the one exception is the CSV export, which answers a file rather than JSON \u2014 and neither the eleven public ones nor the front desk's nine is a tool; the reason for each exclusion is written down where a test can fail if a new one turns up without one\n    * **A booking picker inside the store's own buy form, on whatever theme the shop already uses.** A month calendar showing only the days with something free, the times on the day a customer picks, an option selector when the service has variants and a \"who with\" selector when it lets them choose \u2014 all of it arriving through core's own buy-button extension point, so a shop that installs this and does nothing else has a working booking storefront. Picking a time holds it, and from there one of two things happens, asked of the store rather than guessed: with money to pay, no details are asked for, the five `line_data` fields are filled in and the store's own quantity box and Add to cart button appear, so the customer goes through core's cart, core's checkout and core's provider and gives their details once; with nothing to pay, a short form and straight into the diary. **Add to cart stays hidden until a time is held**, and that is done from the script rather than from a stylesheet on purpose \u2014 a store that has turned `builtin_css` off does not load this add-on's CSS either, and a plain Add to cart button on a booking product is a cart line the store refuses by name\n    * **The same picker anywhere else on the site.** `[kahunacart-booking product=\"full-groom\" \/]` and `{{ kahunacart_booking('full-groom') }}`, on a landing page, in a sidebar, on a blog post. Off a shop route the shortcode brings its own script and stylesheet with it, once per request, and every colour in that stylesheet falls back to something \u2014 the store's `--kc-*` tokens are only defined on KahunaCart's own routes, and a calendar in no colours at all is what happens without it\n    * **The appointment on the receipt, in the order email, and in the account.** A panel on the receipt page with Move it and Cancel it on it, a matching block in the order email, a Bookings tab listing what is coming up and what already happened, and a card on the account overview showing the next appointment and nothing else. The email panel **holds the receipt back** until the booking exists rather than sending one that says nothing about the appointment the customer just paid for \u2014 except after a conflict, where there is nothing left to wait for and a person has to ring them. Both writes go through the store's own account form with core's nonce and login check on them, and a booking that is not this customer's is refused in exactly the same words as one that does not exist\n    * **A page a guest changes their own appointment on.** `[kahunacart-booking-manage \/]` on a page of the shop's own making, named in the settings \u2014 and until it is named, no message carries a link at all, because a link to a page nobody has made is worse than no link. The token is never rendered into the page: the script reads it off the query string, since a page Grav has cached would otherwise hand out whoever's booking was drawn first. It is checked against its pattern before anything is looked up, so a guessed token never reaches a query\n    * **Eight emails to the customer, and a paid booking gets one of them.** Request received, you're booked in, the calendar invitation, cancelled, moved, declined, the reminder and the thank-you \u2014 every one a switch a merchant can turn off and a template pair a theme can replace. The rule underneath is that a customer who paid gets **one** receipt: core's own already names the appointment in its panel, so a booking that rode in on an order never gets the long confirmation. It gets two sentences and the `.ics` file, and turning that off leaves exactly one email. A request waiting for a person to approve it beats both, because \"have they said yes\" is the one thing the customer is actually wondering\n    * **A calendar invitation that replaces the entry instead of adding a second one.** `REQUEST` to create and update, `CANCEL` to remove, and a sequence number off the booking that rises on every move \u2014 which is what makes Apple, Google and Outlook move the appointment rather than quietly keep the old one. The port from Schedule Pro changed exactly one thing: the line folding walks back to a character boundary, so a service name with an accent in it no longer hands somebody's calendar a file it cannot read\n    * **Reminders that go once, whatever the store is running.** A day before and an hour before out of the box, as many or as few as a shop wants. The row saying a reminder went is claimed **before** the message is sent, not after \u2014 two workers that pick up the same booking race on one insert, one wins and sends and the other does nothing. The other way round has both of them send. An appointment that moves earns its reminders again, because the row is about the booking and its lead time and not about the time it is at\n    * **A thank-you afterwards, and nothing in it.** Two hours after the appointment ends, on by default, with a lower bound of a week so that switching it on does not mail everybody who has ever been to the shop. No discount code and no review request: those are a merchant's decision and belong in a campaign\n    * **Five messages to the shop, deduped by cause.** Created, waiting for a yes, cancelled, a class selling out, and the conflict \u2014 the last two of those loud, because a request nobody has answered and money arrived for a time the shop no longer has are the two where doing nothing costs something. Created, needs-approval and cancelled are one message per booking however many times a webhook is delivered; the conflict is one per **order**, because three late lines is one mess for one person and three high-priority messages about it is two too many. **A payload carries a reference, a service, a time and a status, and never a name, an email address or a telephone number** \u2014 these go to Slack and to somebody's phone, and there is a test asserting it on the key names\n    * **A customer with an appointment next week cannot be forgotten yet.** Core's erasure asks every add-on whether it holds anything, and this refuses until the appointment is over or called off: erasure rewrites a name in place, and doing it to somebody who is coming in on Thursday leaves the shop holding an hour for a person it can no longer ring. The refusal names the reference and the date and **never the service**, because a blocker's label goes on an admin screen and into a log. Once it is allowed, past bookings lose their notes and their form data and keep their times, because an hour the shop was busy is a fact about the shop\n    * **A front desk for the people at the counter, at `\/desk` on your own site.** A tablet app, signed in with the Login plugin, on its own screens: Today with what is next and who has arrived, a Diary you press and hold to drag an appointment to another time, and Find for when the telephone rings. One big button for whatever comes next \u2014 Check in, then Done \u2014 and the other six actions behind More, because a thumb finds one big button and hunts for six. The app is Svelte and the build output is committed, so an installed add-on needs no Node and no build step\n    * **The desk cannot leak a number it was never handed.** It is not the admin with the figures hidden: it is nine routes of its own that have never carried an order id, an order line, a total, a payment provider, a refund or a coupon, and a test walks every payload they can return looking for those keys. What a stylist may know is decided once, on the server, by which routes exist. Money on the tablet is one line and nothing to press \u2014 **Paid online**, **Deposit paid \u2014 $25.00 to pay today**, **Pay today: $80.00**, **Free** \u2014 and cancelling something that was paid for opens a refund request for a person to look at rather than moving anybody's money from a counter\n    * **Two permissions, and `kahunacart.bookings.desk.own` means one column.** A stylist linked to her bookable by the account field sees her own day and nothing else: the diary hands her one column whatever she asks for, an appointment that is not hers answers `404` with the same sentence an id that does not exist gets \u2014 so the desk never confirms somebody else's afternoon is there \u2014 and booking into another column is refused before anything is written rather than after. A desk account with nothing else ticked reaches no admin screen and no other API route, and there is a test that says so\n    * **Writes carry a desk nonce, because a session cookie on its own is not a defence.** The desk is authenticated by a cookie, and a cookie rides along with a cross-site request; `desk\/me` mints a nonce from the session and the username, every write sends it back in `X-Desk-Nonce`, and one without it is refused with a sentence telling the person to reload. Only a request that arrived with a session cookie is asked for one, so an API key or a token client needs nothing new. Admin Next was checked first and is not a precedent: it signs with a JWT and never rides the session\n    * **The sign-in page is the desk's own, not the shop's.** The Login plugin does the authenticating either way, but a tablet screwed to a counter should not get the shop header, the menu, the cookie banner and the footer on the way in. The whole app extends no theme template and loads no theme asset, so a shop that buys a new theme gets the same desk on Monday morning as it had on Friday night. It carries a web manifest and an icon, so it adds to a home screen and opens without browser furniture, and there is a Sign out within reach because the tablet is shared\n    * **Booking somebody in at the counter is allowed inside the shop's own notice period.** A customer ringing at ten to ask for eleven is exactly what a shop wants to say yes to, so the desk ignores minimum notice and the booking window \u2014 and nothing else. The conflict guard, the working hours, the time off and every need are checked exactly as they always are, and there is no flag anywhere in this add-on that turns those off\n    * **Arrivals are recorded.** A new `arrived_at` column, stamped by Check in, and only ever the first time \u2014 pressing it twice does not rewrite when somebody actually walked in. It is what lets Today say who is here and who is five minutes late without asking anybody to keep a list\n    * **An appointment no longer stands in its own way when you move it.** Pushing somebody back half an hour \u2014 the commonest thing a front desk does all day \u2014 was refused, because the availability check in front of the move counted the appointment's own claim as somebody else's. The store underneath had always excluded it when it wrote the new time; the check in front of it had never been told. Now it is, so a nudge shorter than the appointment's own length works, from the diary, from the sheet and from the admin\n    * **Classes and workshops, as a service in session mode.** A Tuesday evening bootcamp that repeats for eight weeks, a Saturday safety course with twelve seats, a colour workshop on one afternoon. It is an ordinary KahunaCart product with a recurrence rule on its booking rule, so its price, tax, picture, category, coupons and translations are the product's like anything else the shop sells \u2014 no second kind of record, no separate price, no separate catalogue. A date is identified by its own UTC start and there is no occurrence table: editing the rule changes next month's dates and leaves last month's bookings exactly where they were\n    * **Six o'clock stays six o'clock.** A weekly class is expanded from a **local** first date in the host's own timezone, so a Tuesday evening bootcamp is at six in the evening in March and at six in the evening in November and the clocks changing in between moves its UTC start by an hour instead of moving the class. Dates can be skipped or **moved to a different time**, both written against the day as a person says it, and both survive the clocks changing for the same reason\n    * **Sell each date, or sell the whole course.** One purchase buys a place on one date, or a place on every date that is left under one course number. The places left on a course is the fewest left on any of its dates, because a place on the course is a place on every date of it \u2014 and a course is held whole while somebody pays for it, so nobody is gazumped on week four while they are still typing their card number\n    * **The host cannot be in two places at once, on a day nobody has registered yet.** A class takes its trainer and its room for the hour the moment the merchant says it runs, which Schedule Pro never guaranteed \u2014 an event there blocked its staff member only through the bookings on it, so an empty class was an empty diary and the first person to find out was whoever turned up. There is no row behind any of it: the occurrences are expanded on demand and handed to the availability walk and the claim guard as the claims they both already read. Troubleshoot says **\"Trainer: teaching a class\"**, and a second class on the same person at the same time is refused when it is saved, naming the one it clashes with and the date it first happens on\n    * **One seat guard, not two.** Places on a class are counted off the same claims, under the same row lock, inside the same transaction as everything else this add-on writes \u2014 the guard from the release before this one, doing what it already did for a group service. A second guard on a different row would be two locks protecting one number, and the day they disagreed somebody would be standing in a room with no chair for them. There is a test with six real forked processes going for the last two places on a twelve-seat class: two win, four are told it is full, and the class sells exactly twelve\n    * **A full class takes names, and a place that comes free is offered rather than given.** A waitlisted booking has no order, no money and no seat, and joining is free even on a class that is otherwise paid for in the basket, because nothing is charged for a place in a queue. When a place opens up on a **free** class the first person waiting simply gets it. On a **paid** one they are offered it: a place is held in their name for twelve hours, they get a link that puts it in their basket, and paying turns the booking they already have into the confirmed one \u2014 same reference, one booking, not two. Schedule Pro confirmed these outright, which on a paid class hands somebody a seat they have not paid for. An offer that runs out goes to the next person, and the one who let it lapse keeps their place in the queue for the next. Three emails go with it \u2014 on the list, a place is being held for you, and the place is yours \u2014 and only the last carries a calendar invitation, because a place in a queue is not an appointment and a place held while somebody pays for it is not theirs yet\n    * **Registration deadlines, checked three times.** How many hours before a class the doors shut, honoured when somebody picks a date, when they add it to the basket and again at the checkout button \u2014 because a basket can sit open for an hour and a class two hours away with a two-hour deadline closes while it does. It reads **\"Registration for that date has closed. Pick another one.\"**, which is deliberately not the same sentence as a hold lapsing\n    * **The register, and calling a date off.** Who is coming on one date and who is waiting behind them, with Offer and Promote on each; the whole room marked as having come or not having come in one go, because thirty names should not be thirty clicks; and **Cancel this date**, which cancels everybody on it, lets the queue go and hands back the list of orders that were paid so a person can refund each one. It refunds nothing by itself, which is the same rule the rest of this add-on follows\n    * **`[kahunacart-sessions \/]` and `kahunacart_sessions()`** put the upcoming classes on any page or in any template, drawn on the server so the dates are in the HTML a search engine reads and still there with JavaScript switched off. Every date reads as one of three things and there is no fourth: places left with a Book button, full with a Join the waiting list button, or full or closed with no button at all \u2014 because offering one would be offering something the server is going to refuse\n    * **The owner's section, inside the KahunaCart admin.** Nine screens under a Bookings tab \u2014 no second app, no second login, and the same stylesheet, pills, row menus and empty states as every other screen in the admin. Core puts every add-on's section in its own band below its own screens, so Bookings does not sit between Orders and Customers; it is **first among the add-ons**, which is as near to Orders as core lets a section get. It is one plain JavaScript custom element with no imports and no build step, the way an add-on section is meant to be\n    * **An overview calendar, read-only on purpose.** A week draws a row per person, room and machine with their opening hours shaded, time off hatched and every booking sitting at the time it really occupies, in that person's own colour; a month draws a cell a day with a count, a bar of how full it is and a flag where something is waiting or clashing. Nothing on it can be dragged. Moving an appointment is a decision with a customer on the other end of it, and it is made on the booking's own screen where the thing being moved is named\n    * **Bookings in conflict have a row of their own.** A booking paid for after its time had gone holds nothing at all, so it belongs in nobody's column \u2014 it used to be on the calendar's numbers and on none of its squares. There is a **Holding nothing** row above everything else now, and it appears only when something is in it\n    * **Money is a link on every screen in this section, and never a figure.** The strip above the calendar counts bookings, how full the span is, who is waiting, the no-shows and the cancellations, and where takings would go there is a way through to core's own Reports. A booking's Payment panel says how it was paid for and links to the order. What was taken, what is owed and what a refund would do are the order's questions and the order's screen answers them; a copy here would be a second number to keep in step with the first\n    * **A booking in conflict is not offered Approve.** Approving one would confirm an appointment that holds no time, which fixes nothing. Moving it to a time that is free is the primary instead, and turning it down and handing the money back through the order is the other way out\n    * **Troubleshoot answers \"why can nobody book Thursday\" on a screen.** Pick a service, a day and optionally one person, and every start time the engine considered is listed with what stopped the ones it did not offer. It asks the engine the same question the storefront asks, so what it says is what the customer was told. Where a whole day was refused for one reason it is said once and the times fold away behind it, rather than printing fourteen identical cards\n    * **Four setup screens, and a service that is not set up says so.** Services lists every booking product with its rule and its options; an option with no rule of its own follows the product's, the way a price list does. People and places is who and what a booking occupies, with their hours, their timezone, the account they sign in with and the days they are away. Schedules is the named weekly hours with per-date overrides. Locations is where it all happens\n    * **The plugin's settings are drawn on its own Settings tab** rather than on a page of its own in the Grav admin, through core's settings home. On a core that does not host them the tab says so and links straight to the page that does\n    * **Two panels elsewhere in the admin, and both draw nothing when there is nothing.** An order that has a booking grows an Appointment panel beside its details; a customer who has ever booked grows a Bookings panel with their next appointment and how many they have had. An order without one and a customer who never booked grow nothing at all\n    * **Two dashboard alerts that land on the answer.** Bookings waiting for a yes, and bookings in conflict \u2014 each a link into the list already filtered to exactly the ones it is about, so \"three are waiting\" is one press from the three\n    * **Export CSV** gives whatever the filters leave on screen, in the same order, with the cells hardened so a reference that starts with an equals sign is not a formula when somebody opens it in a spreadsheet\n    * **Five new admin endpoints behind those screens** \u2014 the overview, troubleshoot, the CSV, the booking products and the site's accounts. The accounts one answers a username and a name and nothing else, because it exists so the admin can offer a picker rather than so anything can enumerate a site's users\n    * **The library it needs travels with it.** `rlanvin\/php-rrule` is what reads a repeat rule, and `vendor\/` was in `.gitignore` \u2014 so an add-on installed the way every customer installs one, as a folder of files, had no RRULE library at all. It is committed now, the runtime half of it only, and a test reads what is actually in the repository and fails if a dependency is added without its vendor folder or a development install is committed over the top\n    * **A missing part of this add-on is loud.** Reading a repeat without the library used to answer \"no dates\", which is exactly what a course that has finished looks like \u2014 a shop could lose its Tuesday evening class and have nothing anywhere to go on. Both places that expand a repeat now stop by name, with a sentence saying which library is missing and how to put it back, and the dashboard carries a red alert saying the same thing for somebody who does not read logs\n    * **The guest manage page serves itself, at `\/booking`.** It used to be a page the merchant had to make, and until they made one no message carried a link at all \u2014 so a guest, the one customer with no account to fall back on, got an email pointing at the account area. The shop answers that route itself now, out of the box, and every message carries the absolute `https:\/\/your-shop\/booking?token=\u2026` link for a guest and an account holder alike. It wears the shop's own theme, unlike the front desk, because it is a page a customer opens from an email. A page of the merchant's own at that route still wins and the `[kahunacart-booking-manage \/]` shortcode still works on it, and clearing the setting switches the whole thing off and leaves the old behaviour standing\n    * **The two-bookable race runs on MariaDB and PostgreSQL too, and so does a new one.** The lock ordering \u2014 ascending bookable id, which is what stops two bookings that both want Ann and the tub from each taking one and waiting for ever for the other \u2014 is the only part of the guard that can fail on one engine and not another, and it was only ever raced on SQLite, which serialises writers anyway and cannot show the bug. Both servers now run the two-bookable race, and a third arrangement where all six racers want the same two bookables and half of them name the two the other way round. Checked by breaking the guard on purpose: PostgreSQL killed a racer with a deadlock it could not retry its way out of, and the fixed guard is clean on three consecutive runs of all three engines\n    * **One place decides what goes in a calendar file.** The feed used to scrub control characters out of a customer's note itself, as a belt to the builder's braces, from back when a lone carriage return could still get past the escaping. The builder was fixed and the belt has come off with it, so a note is escaped in exactly one place and the feed is tested against that rather than against a second copy of the rule\n\n    * **A Sessions tab, because a class is not an appointment and should not read like one.** Every class the shop runs with the dates coming up and the places left on the next one; one class with every date it runs on, when registration shuts on each and which are taking names; and one date's **register** \u2014 who is coming, who is waiting and in what order, Offer the place, Give them the place, Take them off the queue, and marking the whole room as having come or not, because thirty names is not thirty clicks. **Calling a date off refunds nothing**, cancels everybody on it, lets the queue go, and then names every order that had money on it with a link to each, so a person refunds them where refunds live\n    * **The Services editor knows what a class is.** It had no mode control anywhere in it, so a class read as an appointment and the six columns that say *when it runs* could not be seen or set from any screen: the first date, how it repeats, the dates that are skipped or moved, the places on each, when registration shuts, the waiting list, and whether one purchase buys a date or the whole course. The first date is written and shown in the shop's own time and never converted, which is the whole reason a Tuesday evening class is still a Tuesday evening class in November. Turning an appointment into a class, or back, while people are still booked on it is refused with a sentence and a count rather than done quietly\n    * **Somebody waiting for a place had no buttons at all.** None of the states a booking's record knew about was a waitlisted one, so the screen drew the status correctly and then offered nothing \u2014 not even a way off the queue. It offers the place, gives the place, takes them off, and links to the whole register for their date\n    * **The calendar link and the outside calendars are on the person's own screen now.** Make a link, copy it, make a new one with a confirm that says in plain words that the old one stops working, take it away, and choose what it says about each booking for this one person. Underneath it, the calendars that block their time: each one with its host and the last six characters of its address \u2014 never the address again, because it is a password that happens to look like a link \u2014 when it last answered, a Test that fetches it there and then and prints what came back, and a box to paste a replacement into\n    * **The Troubleshoot screen names what is in the way.** A class says *which* class (\"Teaching Tuesday bootcamp\") and an outside calendar says *which* calendar (\"busy on Ann's Google calendar\"), because a stylist with three feeds and two classes needs to know which one took the evening, not that something did\n    * **The front desk button comes from the store's settings.** It used to guess the address and ask the server whether anything was there, which was wrong twice over: a shop that had moved the desk got a button to a page that is not there, and a shop that had switched the desk off still got the button\n\n    * **Five businesses, each one command.** `seed salon`, `groomer`, `trainer`, `massage`, `range` \u2014 and `all`, which writes the five and then a fortnight of diary across them. Each writes its own products, people, hours, rules and needs and shares nothing with the others, everything in the **store's** timezone, and every one of them is idempotent: running it twice changes nothing, because everything is found by slug before it is written. Between them they cover what the model is actually for \u2014 a colour that waits for a yes, a groom that needs a person and a tub, a course sold whole, a treatment that asks for a deposit, and ten range lanes as one record with ten units on it\n    * **`seed diary` puts a fortnight on the board.** A week behind and a fortnight ahead, in every status a shop actually sees: one waiting for a yes, one that arrived after its time had gone and holds nothing, one part paid with a balance owed, a class full with two people waiting, and somebody away next week. It is what makes a screenshot, a demo and an afternoon of testing possible without booking forty things by hand, and the times come out of the same availability walk the storefront uses, so nothing in it is a time the shop would not have offered\n    * **A whole documentation set, written for the person who cuts hair rather than the person who wrote the plugin.** Twenty-two pages under `docs\/`: getting started in ten minutes, the same ten minutes written five times over for a salon, a groomer, a trainer, a clinic and a range \u2014 each one matching its seed recipe step for step \u2014 every setup screen, the front desk, a single page to print and stick by the tablet, payments, the messages, the storefront, calendar sync, twenty-four things that can go wrong with the sentence you are looking at, and the two reference pages for anybody building on it\n    * **One way of writing a time, and one setting to change it.** A time of day is written `6:00pm` \u2014 minutes always, because `6 pm` reads well in a sentence and badly in a column \u2014 and a shop that thinks in 24-hour time says so once under **How times are written** and gets `18:00` everywhere instead. Everywhere is the whole of it: the storefront picker, the basket line, the receipt, the account, the eight emails, the calendar message, the admin diary and its hour gutter, Troubleshoot's reasons, the panels beside an order and a customer, the front desk, the `slots` command and the export. There is one formatter on the server and one in each front end, each fed the setting by the server rather than guessing at the browser's locale, so the owner reading the diary from an airport sees the same clock the customer got in their email\n    * **The picker is something a page of your own can be written against.** A salon's own site with a Book button that opens a dialog used to have nothing to hold: no event said a booking had been made, so the dialog could not close itself; closing it could not give the held time back; and a picker put in the page after load could only be started by re-appending the script tag. Now there are five bubbling `CustomEvent`s on the mount point \u2014 `kahunacart-booking:ready`, `:held` with the expiry, `:booked` with the reference and whether it is still waiting for a yes, `:released` and `:error` \u2014 and one namespace, `window.KahunaCartBookings`, with `mount(element)` to boot a picker whenever it arrived and `release(element)` to hand a held time back the moment a dialog closes. The customer's manage token is never in any of it. The two-column layout now follows the **picker's** own width rather than the window's, so a picker in a 480px dialog on a wide screen stacks instead of overflowing, `{{ kahunacart_booking('cut', { boot: false }) }}` renders one as a template a page can copy rather than as a picker that draws itself into hidden markup, and `Seed\\Catalog::product()` takes a `visibility`, so a showcase can seed its services `unlisted` in one write. `docs\/storefront.md` has the whole contract with an example\n    * **A third kind of booking can come from another plugin.** Appointments and classes are Bookings' own and are unchanged in every way; beside them there is now one narrow seam another plugin registers a **mode** through \u2014 a way of booking with its own idea of what a unit of time is, its own rules table, its own availability payload and its own words for a booking. A mode answers one method with an offer, and that offer goes into exactly the same claim every appointment goes through: the same write lock in ascending order, the same sweep, the same recheck, the same unique key. Nothing a mode returns can bypass the double-booking guard, and there is no way to ask it to. It touches the service editor, the picker's availability, the hold, booking straight away, booking at the front desk, moving a booking, Troubleshoot, the reason codes, the cart line, the receipt, the emails, the calendar invitation, the manage page, the account tab, the admin record and the desk \u2014 and every one of those is exactly what it was for a service that is not one. A calendar invitation can now be dates rather than instants, which is what a week in a cottage is. `nightly` stops being a word refused by name and becomes the first registered mode, in the KahunaCart Rentals add-on; a service set to a mode nothing installed provides is still listed and still editable, and is refused at the picker and at the cart door with a sentence naming the add-on that would bring it, the way core treats a product of a type nothing declares. A word that is neither built in nor registered is now refused when it is saved rather than quietly turned into an appointment. `docs\/development.md` has the whole contract with a mode in about two hundred lines beside it\n\n1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **A class could not be bought from its own page.** Availability answered a class the way it answers a haircut \u2014 every half hour its trainer was free \u2014 and every one of those times was then refused at the hold with \"The class does not run on that date\", so the Book button on the classes list led to a page that could not take the booking. It answers the dates the class runs on now, one time on each, with the places left on it, and a date that is full or whose registration has shut is not offered at all\n    * **The times a shop offers are its own, whatever moment it is asked from.** Candidate starts used to be counted out from the instant the availability walk was handed, so a question asked at twenty past nine was answered with twenty past nine, ten to ten, twenty past ten. The seeded diary hit it twice, and so \u2014 every time, unnoticed \u2014 did any customer whose own midnight falls in the middle of the shop's working day: a customer in Mumbai and a customer in Denver were offered the same salon's times half an hour out of step with each other. The grid belongs to the shop's opening hours now and the window only filters, which also closes a way the front desk could book a minute the shop never offers\n    * **A customer offered a place on a paid class can take it.** The route that hands the held place over had been there since the classes leaf and nothing in the front end called it, so the email told people to open a link and put the place in their basket, and the page it opened drew nothing at all. It draws it now: what is being held, until when in their own timezone, what the whole thing costs, and one button that hands the hold to their browser and drops the line in the shop's own basket. Paying turns the booking they already have into the confirmed one \u2014 same reference, one booking, not two. A place with no offer on it says where they stand in the queue and lets them leave it, and leaving gives back a place that was being held for them instead of letting it sit there for twelve hours. A place that has already been offered and lapsed says so. The Bookings tab in the account says all of the same things, and a place on a waiting list is listed under what is coming up rather than under what has already happened\n    * **A whole course reads as a whole course.** Eight Tuesdays bought in one purchase is one order line and eight bookings, and the receipt drew the one the line pointed at \u2014 so the customer who had just bought eight evenings was shown the first Tuesday and nothing else. It is one card saying *8 Tuesdays, 6:00pm, the first on 22 September*, on the receipt and in the order email, with a link to where every date is listed and each one moved or called off on its own. The weekday is only named when every date falls on one; a course that jumps around says how many dates instead. A guest's own booking page now lists the whole course too, each date with its own status and its own link, which is where that link lands when there is no account behind it\n    * **A class's own page reads like a class.** It used to draw an appointment's furniture \u2014 \"60 minutes \u00b7 $160\" over a month calendar with four of its thirty squares live and \"Pick a day to see the times\" underneath \u2014 for an eight-week bootcamp. It lists the dates the class runs on now, the next two months of them, each row saying when it is, how many places are left and a button to take one; the line under the name says what one purchase actually buys, \"for the whole course, whichever date you start on\" or \"for one date\"; and there is a **How many places** box above the dates. That number is now the number the shop is asked about, which it never was \u2014 a date with two places left used to say \"Book it\" to a party of three and then refuse the hold. A full date says **Full** and offers **Join the waiting list** where the class keeps one, and is left off the page where it does not, because a button that cannot do anything is worse than no button\n    * **A booking that is for more than one person can say so.** A range lane takes four, a studio takes six, a tasting room takes eight \u2014 and there was nowhere to write that down, so the range's own documentation told merchants to put it in the product description and hope. Services now have **Party size up to**, the picker asks **How many people** when it is more than one, and the number rides on the booking, the basket line (*Thu 25 Sep, 2:00pm \u00b7 3 people*), the receipt, the emails, the admin record and the front desk (*Lane hire \u00b7 3 people*). It changes nothing the engine counts: four friends hiring a lane hire **one lane**, and a range with ten of them still sells ten at two o'clock. That is the difference between a party and a class, and confusing the two is what made the first draft of the range recipe cap the whole range at four hires an hour. Per-person pricing is deliberately not part of it: a lane costs what a lane costs whoever is standing on it\n    * **The places a customer asked for never reached the question.** The picker's own box asked how many, and then asked the shop what was free for **one** \u2014 so a class with two places left drew a Book button for a party of three and refused the hold a moment later. The number is part of the availability question now, and the page draws its button off the answer\n    * **A service whose lengths are an option had no names on it.** Build \"30 min \/ 60 min\" as a product option, press Generate, and core writes the variants with no title of their own \u2014 the name lives on the option values and is worked out when it is printed. This add-on read the title straight off the row, so the Bookings \u2192 Services list showed two blank rows to write a rule against, the storefront picker offered two blank buttons at two different prices, the front desk's service list had two blank options on it, and every email about the appointment named the service and nothing else. Only the seeded shops looked right, because a seed writes the titles out by hand. There is one answer to \"what is this variant called\" now, it is core's own rule, and every screen asks it\n    * **A class on a pool everything else uses took a lane per player.** A class writes one block claiming the units its needs declared \u2014 four of the venue's eight lanes \u2014 and every registration then writes a claim of its own carrying the places it took, so seven people on league night read as eleven lanes gone out of eight and Tuesday between 7:00pm and 9:00pm had nothing left to hire at all. The number that decided it was how many had signed up, which is not a number the merchant set anywhere. A class takes exactly what it declared now, for its whole span, whether twelve people are coming or nobody is; the other four lanes are there to be hired, a hire of five is refused and what it is told names the class \u2014 **\"Lanes: teaching Ironbark League\"** \u2014 rather than the unhelpful \"booked\". Nothing about the class's own places changed: they are still counted one per person against its capacity. It only ever bit a class sharing a pool, which is the ordinary case for a venue and no case at all for a salon\n    * **A page of your own can dress the month grid and say things in its own words.** Round days were written into the stylesheet rather than taken from a token, so setting a radius to zero still got circles, and a free day's ten per cent tint of the accent is nothing at all on a dark panel \u2014 the first host page built against this overrode nine rules to get a legible calendar. The month grid reads `--kc-day-radius`, `--kc-day-free-bg`, `--kc-day-free-border`, `--kc-day-off-opacity` and the rest now, its own rules are one class each so a host's two-class rule wins whatever order the stylesheets loaded in, a shut day clears 3:1 on a dark ground and on white, and `--kc-card`, `--kc-input` and `--kc-surface` fall back to a faint tint of the text colour rather than to `transparent`. `kahunacart_booking()` and the shortcode also take `places_first`, which puts **How many people** above the option box for a business whose first question is the party, and `i18n`, which hands one picker its own words for as many strings as the page names \u2014 so a venue whose two options are already called *An hour* and *Ninety minutes* can empty `minutes` and have the picker leave the digits out rather than say the same thing twice. And \"We will email you as soon as the shop says yes\" is now \"as soon as it is confirmed\", which is true of a venue, a clinic and a studio as well as a shop\n    * **The picker asks how many people first, and names the option it is pricing.** Three decisions about what every shop gets out of the box rather than what a page of somebody's own can ask for. **How many of you** is now above the length box, because how many are coming is the thing somebody already knows when they open the page and the length is what they work out from it \u2014 a page that really does sell a length first writes `places_first: false` and gets the old order. The line under the service's name says which option is being priced, so a groomer reads *Large dog \u00b7 90 minutes \u00b7 $80.00* rather than *90 minutes \u00b7 $80.00* with nothing saying which dog; a service with one option is left exactly as it was, because one option is not a choice anybody made and its name is very often the product's own. And a service can say **the option names already say how long**, which takes the digits out for a venue whose two options are called *An hour* and *Ninety minutes* \u2014 a tick on the rule rather than a guess at English, off out of the box so nothing ever loses its length by accident, and it works on the shipped product page where there is no template of yours to put an override in. The approval sentence and its neighbours lost the word shop while we were in there: a status now reads *Waiting to be confirmed*, a cutoff says *Give us a ring*, and a clinic or a studio reading any of them is not being told it is a shop\n    * **A booking mode names its own price, and the store still owns the money.** A mode quoting a week in a beach house answers with a figure and the rows behind it, Bookings hands both to core through its line-price contract, and core makes the line: the unit price is the quote, the breakdown prints under the product name, the quantity box is gone and a coupon skips the rows that say they are not to be discounted. **Nothing here computes or stores what was paid.** An appointment and a class are untouched by every word of this \u2014 they are still priced by the product and its option the way they always were, and there are tests asserting the same line data, the same events and the same totals as before\n    * **The price a guest was shown is the price they pay.** The quote is frozen onto the hold the moment they take it, and the cart reads back the frozen figure rather than asking again \u2014 so an owner raising the rate while somebody is at the checkout does not reprice the basket under them. The hold is the credential for it as well: a line pointing at a hold that has lapsed, that belongs to another browser, or that was taken for a different product is refused by name, and so is a hold carrying no price the store will take \u2014 because a line nobody prices is a line charged at the catalogue price, and the catalogue price of a holiday cottage is one night. A request that names no visitor at all now only gets a hold that names none either, so clearing a cookie and guessing an id buys nobody somebody else's week. A mode's booking can only ever be bought one at a time, because a quantity of two on a line priced for exactly one set of dates is two things sold and one delivered\n    * **Paying a deposit now and the rest later, decided by the booking and carried out by the store.** A mode says \"30% down, the rest a fortnight before arrival\" and Bookings answers core's payment-schedule contract with one figure for the whole order: its total, less what the bookings on it defer. Always a figure and never a percentage \u2014 the store applies a percentage to the **order**, so a $1,125 stay at 30% with $200 of sales tax on it would ask for $406.50 today while the guest had been quoted $337.50 for the stay, and the same stay beside a t-shirt would ask for a different number again. One rule means the same stay contributes the same amount whatever else is in the basket. A basket nobody asked to defer gets no schedule at all, the checkout page gets the same answer the payment does, and the listener never throws: a refusal is a line in the log and a normally-paid order, not a checkout that will not finish\n    * **A deposit confirms the booking, and giving it all back releases the dates.** Core announces a part payment instead of a payment, so without this a stay bought with 30% down would sit pending for ever \u2014 and refunding the whole of a deposit is, as far as the order is concerned, a *partial* refund, which used to leave a confirmed cottage held for somebody who had paid nothing. The receipt, the order email, the account tab and the manage page all say what has been paid, what is still owed and the date it is owed by, in a sentence rather than a table \u2014 *Paid so far $760.20 \u00b7 Balance $1,773.80 due by Sat 25 Jul 2026* \u2014 and the front desk's one money line grew a date with it: **Deposit paid \u2014 $1,773.80 due by 25 Jul**\n    * **A balance that goes unpaid is a person's decision, not an automatic cancellation.** When core says a balance is overdue it goes on the booking's own trail, the owner gets a high-priority message with what is owed and how late it is, and the booking's mode is asked whether it has anything to add. Nothing is cancelled, nothing is refunded and nobody's dates are given away \u2014 the shop has already asked the customer once and what happens next is theirs to choose\n    * **Cancelling asks the booking what it is worth back.** A mode with a cancellation policy is handed what the shop is holding **against that booking's own line** \u2014 so two stays in one basket is two answers rather than one offer of the whole basket \u2014 answers with a figure, and the refund request a person opens carries that figure in its own sentence. The amount goes on the booking's trail whatever anybody does with the request afterwards. A mode with no policy, an appointment and a class are exactly as they were: the request is opened with no number on it, because what a customer gets back has always been the merchant's call\n    * **Moving a booking a mode priced is refused when the new dates cost something different.** The order was taken for one figure and a guest cannot move themselves onto a more expensive week for free, so the answer is a sentence with a person in it \u2014 *Those dates come to a different amount from the ones you booked. Get in touch with us and we will sort it out for you.* Dates at the same price move as normal, and an appointment moves the way it always has\n<!-- \u2500\u2500 nightly N4: the owner's screens and the desk \u2500\u2500 -->\n    * **An add-on can draw its own screens inside the Bookings section.** Two named slots, and nothing else: a panel in the service editor that appears the moment the way of booking is set to one that add-on brought, and a tab of its own in the strip along the top. The add-on declares where a file is and which custom element it defines; the section fetches it through its own authenticated route, waits for the definition, and draws it with the store's own stylesheets handed over so it looks like the rest of the admin rather than like a plugin. A script that will not load leaves one sentence naming the add-on that should have drawn there \u2014 never an empty panel, because an empty panel is a screen nobody can debug. The service editor drops the questions that mean nothing to a mode \u2014 how long it takes, start times every so often, buffers \u2014 and keeps the ones that still do: notice, how far ahead, workflow, where, hours and who or what it needs. What the panel holds rides out on Bookings' own Save, in the same transaction as the service row\n    * **A booking that runs over days is drawn as one.** A mode can now say that its bookings are runs of dates rather than times of day, and saying so is all it takes: the admin's overview gains a **Stays** view \u2014 bookables down the side, a month across, one bar per booking, drawn from the middle of the day somebody arrives to the middle of the day they leave so that a changeover day reads as one bar ending and another beginning rather than as a clash \u2014 and the front desk gains the same strip over a fortnight or a month. Nothing in either of them knows what a night is: the dates, the words and the noun all come from the booking's own mode, so a cottage counts nights, a kayak counts days and neither says the other's word. \"How full\" changes with the view, because dates taken out of dates there were is the number an owner wants and a percentage of opening hours is not \u2014 a holiday let has no opening hours\n    * **The front desk knows who is arriving and who is leaving.** Today opens on two lists above everything else, with the check-in time, the nights, the guests and what is still owed on each row, and the button on a stay says **Check in** and then **Check out** rather than Start and Complete. Troubleshoot answers over a **range** of dates for a booking of that kind, one row a date with the mode's own reason beside it, rather than asking about one day at a time. A booking's own mode puts the dates into words everywhere else too: on the list, on the record, on the panels beside an order and a customer, and on the sheet at the desk\n    * **A mode can teach Bookings its own messages.** A registered mode can add messages of its own \u2014 a note before somebody arrives, a thank-you after they leave \u2014 and they go out through Bookings' own mailer, which means the order's language, the customer lookup that respects an erasure, the audit trail, the order's timeline and the claim that stops two workers sending the same thing twice. A mode says which stays are due one by asking Bookings for the ones starting or ending in a window, so the sweeping and the timing are the mode's own business and the sending is not\n<!-- \u2500\u2500 end nightly N4 \u2500\u2500 -->\n<!-- \u2500\u2500 nightly N3: the range picker \u2500\u2500 -->\n    * **A booking mode can bring its own picker.** The storefront picker draws appointments and classes itself, and now stops at anything else and hands the mount element to a **face** the mode's own plugin registered \u2014 `window.KahunaCartBookings.registerFace('nightly', factory)` \u2014 with everything the built-in two use: the rule and the options as the services route answered them, the mode's own translated words, how this shop writes a time of day, the availability payload, holding and booking and releasing, the countdown, the store's own Add to cart with its six line keys spelled in one place, the `--kc-*` tokens and the same five host-page events. A face never opens a route of its own, never learns a line-data key and never reaches back into the picker. A service whose mode nothing installed can draw says so in a sentence \u2014 the server's own, naming the add-on that would bring the calendar \u2014 and leaves Add to cart hidden rather than inviting somebody to press it so the shop can refuse them\n    * **A face's script and stylesheet reach the page from the server.** `onKahunaCartBookingsRegisterPickerFaces` hands a mode's plugin a registry to append a script, a stylesheet and its own translated strings to, and they go out beside the picker's own two files \u2014 through Grav's asset pipeline on the shop's routes and as tags beside the calendar anywhere else, because a page's processed content is cached and anything a shortcode enqueued is missing on the second request. Both deferred and the face's second, so a face is never run by a browser that has not got `registerFace` yet. A mode's words travel under `<mode>.<key>` and the picker takes the prefix off, so two modes may both have an `arrival` and neither can take the other's\n    * **A mode that books whole days is held by its dates rather than by a start time.** Check-in is a wall time where the property is, and a browser working out the instant for itself is how a calendar ends up a day out twice a year \u2014 so a registered mode may be posted a hold with the dates it deals in and no `start`, and computes the instant on the server in the property's own zone. An appointment and a class are still held at a start time, required by name, word for word as before\n    * **Moving one of them may change its length.** A reschedule now carries whatever else the caller posted through to the mode, under the five things Bookings decides for itself \u2014 so a guest moving a week in a cottage picks a new arrival and a new departure rather than shifting a fixed span, and a caller still cannot say which booking is being moved, who it is with or how many places it takes. The manage page hands a registered mode's booking to its own face, and prints the mode's own words for when a stay is \u2014 *Wed 21 Oct to Tue 27 Oct, 6 nights* \u2014 where it used to print a start time and a finish time with no week between them\n<!-- \u2500\u2500 end nightly N3 \u2500\u2500 -->\n<!-- \u2500\u2500 nightly N7: the follow-up \u2500\u2500 -->\n    * **A booking that lasts days can be taken at the front desk.** Somebody rings the owner and wants the second week of June, and until now there was no way to write it down: the sheet asked for a time of day, a service booked from a date to a date offered none, and Book it never lit up. The New booking sheet now asks a service of that kind for the day they arrive, the day they leave and how many people, and prints what the mode says it comes to: the dates in its own words, the check-in and check-out lines, every row behind the price and the total, all of it exactly as the server gave it and none of it worked out on the tablet. A refusal is the mode's own sentence \u2014 *3 nights minimum*, *Arrivals on Saturdays*, *That would leave nights nobody could book* \u2014 because a counter told \"that time has just been taken\" about a minimum stay rings the owner. Everything goes through the same conflict guard every other door does, and a desk user who only works her own column is still told whose it is before anything is written\n    * **And the desk says what there is to collect.** A booking taken at the counter is the no-order path: **no payment schedule is opened, no balance job is queued and there is no order** \u2014 the whole amount is taken across the counter when the guest turns up, and the sheet and the docs say so in those words. So the quote the mode gave is written down at the moment it is given, the same way it is already written onto a hold, and the desk reads it back as **To collect $1,714.00**. It is its own word rather than \"Pay today\", which about a fortnight in June would have a receptionist asking for it in March. A booking nobody quoted \u2014 seeded into the diary, imported from an outside calendar \u2014 still says nothing at all about money, because a number that is wrong by a factor of the nights is worse than no number, and so does one whose add-on has been switched off since\n    * **How wide the picker may get is a token.** `--kc-picker-max` on anything wrapping the picker, defaulting to the 44rem it always was. It is the one dimension a page argues with: 44rem is right beside a product's price and dead space in a section of a page's own, and until now the only way round it was an override with three classes in it. Setting it to `none` also lets the panel's own 900px container query put the summary beside the months instead of under them\n    * **A mode's panel in the service editor opens on what is actually saved.** `GET setup\/services` answers each service with the mode's own fields on it, so the component mounted in the editor is handed the values the contract promises it rather than an empty object. Anything that trusted the contract was drawing its own defaults over a merchant's settings and only got away with it by fetching its own rule behind the section's back\n    * **The picker inside a buy form brings its own script and stylesheet.** On the shop's own product page they arrive through Grav's pipeline and nothing changes. On a page of your own that draws the store's buy fields itself, the markup used to land with no JavaScript behind it and simply do nothing \u2014 the only way to get them was to render an availability calendar you might not want. Whatever emits the field now makes sure the assets are there, once\n    * **The front desk can be narrowed to the part of the shop somebody works in.** A store with a salon, a lane hire and a holiday cottage on it is one diary with all of them in one flat row: six stylists, two rooms, a tub, ten lanes and a cottage, and the only way to get to the salon was to tap six names one at a time. There is now a row above the names for what kind of thing it is \u2014 People, Rooms and spaces, Equipment, Properties, the same four words People and places uses \u2014 and a row of places under it when the shop has more than one address. Each is drawn only when there is more than one to choose between, and each is drawn from the whole list rather than from the diary's answer, so narrowing to one person can never hide the way back out. What you picked is remembered with the view and the names, and somebody who only works her own column sees none of it\n    * **And the Stays view is a picture of stays again.** It was drawing a row for every bookable in the shop and a round dot with one letter in it for every appointment, nine of them stacked in a Tuesday under one stylist's name. Rows are now only the things one of the shop's own day-unit services actually needs \u2014 asked of the services and their needs, never of the word \"property\" \u2014 and bars are only bookings that last longer than a day. The Stays button is not drawn at all in a shop that lets nothing by the day. The day and week grids do the opposite and leave those things out to begin with, because a cottage booked for five nights would otherwise be one block sitting in a Tuesday column at check-in time; tap its kind, or its name, and it comes back\n    * **`seed diary` says what is missing instead of saying `skipped`.** Two of its bookings go through core's own checkout, and both of them quietly skipped on almost every store \u2014 one because deposits are switched off, which is a setting and not a fault, and one because the cart door refused the hold the seed had just minted. Now each says which: *skipped \u2014 this store takes the whole amount at checkout. Switch on checkout.deposits.enabled to see a part-paid one*, or *skipped \u2014 there is no free time in the week after next to put it in*. The refusal is fixed as well: the console counts as the shop's own staff, because somebody with a shell on the server is further inside the shop than `admin.super` is, so the seed can buy its own hold and the diary gets its paid booking back\n    * **A booking made at the counter could be sold twice on MySQL.** Found by racing a stay taken at the front desk against a week somebody was holding in a basket: on MariaDB two of the six won and the house was let twice, on SQLite and PostgreSQL one did. The guard was right and its lock was right \u2014 what was wrong was the **order of two statements**. MySQL's default isolation fixes a transaction's whole view of the world at its first plain `SELECT`, so reading how many units a booking wants *before* taking the write lock left the re-check reading the world as it was before this booking queued for the lock \u2014 blind to the claim the racer ahead of it had just committed. Both wrote. Every read in the guard now happens after the lock, a caller that looks something up before claiming takes the lock itself first, and there is a test on the order of the statements as well as the race, because a bug that only appears on one engine under load is not one a test that passes locally will ever find again. It reached anything that books without going through a basket: the front desk, a merchant booking somebody in, a booking being moved, and a place given away on a full class\n<!-- \u2500\u2500 end nightly N7 \u2500\u2500 -->\n    * **An account that may only look is no longer shown buttons it cannot use.** Somebody with `kahunacart.bookings.view` and not `kahunacart.bookings.manage` was shown Add somebody or something, New hours, Add a place, every row's Edit, Switch off and Remove, the approve, move and cancel buttons on a booking, the register's Everybody came and Call this date off, and every editor's Save, and the server refused each one with a 403. The settings answer now says `can_manage`, worked out through the same check the writes go through, and the section leaves out everything that writes for somebody who may only look. They still read every screen, open every booking and every editor \u2014 with the boxes read-only \u2014 and export the list. The order panel's Cancel it follows the same answer on the bookings list, and a component another add-on mounts in the section is handed `canManage` on its context\n    * **A store with deposits off no longer hears from Bookings about them.** A booking that asked for a deposit used to answer core's payment schedule question even when `checkout.deposits.enabled` was off, and core, which charges the whole order with the switch off, ignored the answer and wrote a line in the log naming this plugin for every checkout page and every order. Bookings now stays quiet while deposits are off and answers as before the moment they are switched on."}}},"kahunacart-rentals":{"name":"KahunaCart Rentals","version":"1.0.9","description":"Nightly stays and day rentals for KahunaCart, on top of the Bookings add-on","description_html":"<p>Nightly stays and day rentals for KahunaCart, on top of the Bookings add-on<\/p>","description_plain":"Nightly stays and day rentals for KahunaCart, on top of the Bookings add-on","icon":"bed","author":{"name":"Trilby Media, LLC","email":"hello@trilby.media"},"dependencies":[{"name":"grav","version":">=2.0.0"},{"name":"kahunacart","version":">=1.4.0"},{"name":"kahunacart-bookings","version":">=1.1.5"}],"compatibility":{"grav":["2.0"]},"homepage":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/add-ons\/rentals","docs":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/docs\/rentals\/overview","keywords":"grav, premium, commerce, ecommerce, rentals, nightly, stays, holiday let, vacation rental, bnb, campsite, bookings","bugs":"https:\/\/github.com\/getgrav\/grav-premium-issues\/labels\/kahunacart-rentals","license":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/license","tag_name":"1.0.9","date":"2026-10-26T03:50:57Z","release_type":"stable","zipball_url":"https:\/\/getgrav.org\/download\/plugins\/kahunacart-rentals\/1.0.9","repository":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/add-ons\/rentals","premium":{"vendor":"KahunaCart","validator":"kahunacart","license_product":"kahunacart-rentals","checkout_url":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/shop\/product\/kahunacart-rentals","button":"Buy Now","filename":"grav-plugin-kahunacart-rentals"},"slug":"kahunacart-rentals","install_path":"user\/plugins\/kahunacart-rentals","content_sha256":"1ec6c452eb61049b1112c4f03c4c879367a69ce630fc1629e7f8722c930eff1f","changelog":{"1.0.9":{"date":"10\/25\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **Buttons in these emails stay readable on a light button color.** The writing on a button was always white, so a store that picked a light Email Button Color got white on yellow. It now turns dark when the button is light, the same as KahunaCart's own emails. Needs KahunaCart 1.6.5 for the dark writing; an older KahunaCart keeps white\n"},"1.0.8":{"date":"10\/22\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **The gap rule is off by default.** A new property takes a stay even when it leaves a night or two too short to sell beside it, because guests often can't move their dates and a stay with a gap is better than no stay. Turn on **Do not take a stay that would leave a gap too short to sell** on a property that wants it. Properties already saved keep the setting they have, and the beach house recipe now leaves it off too\n    * **The stay request form knows who's booking.** With Bookings 1.1.12 or later, a signed-in guest finds their name, email and phone already filled in, and a guest who isn't signed in sees \"Have an account? Sign in\" above the form. An older Bookings leaves the form as it was\n1. [](#new)\n    * **Themes can show the rates and the house rules.** `kahunacart_rentals_rates('beach-house')` lists the rate bands still to come in date order, each with its dates, nightly and weekend price as the store prints money, minimum stay and arrival days worded (\"Saturday to Saturday\"), and `kahunacart_rentals_house('beach-house')` answers the check-in and check-out times, the stay limits, the fees, the deposit, the cancellation steps as plain lines, the lowest price a night and the next night a stay could start on. Both answer in the currency the shopper is browsing in, follow Whole Amounts, and answer an empty list or nothing for anything they cannot\n    * **The calendar can open on the next free month and start a stay.** `kahunacart_availability()` and the shortcode take `from` (`2026-08`, or `next` for the first month with a night a stay could start on) and `pick`, which lets a click on such a night become the arrival in the picker for the same property on the page, scrolled into view and focused. The calendar stays read-only when the page has no picker\n    * **A link can open the picker on a stay.** `?arrive=2026-08-01&leave=2026-08-08&guests=4` on any page with the nightly picker opens it with those nights picked and the price showing, or, when they cannot be booked, on the arrival's month with the reason the picker already gives. A page can set the same three as `data-arrive`, `data-leave` and `data-guests` on the picker's mount point\n2. [](#improved)\n    * **The picker opens on a month you can book.** With no stay in the address, a picker whose first months have no night a stay could start on (a closed winter, say) opens on the first month that has one, the same search the calendar's `from: 'next'` does and through the same route. A link's own arrival still opens on its month, and the page where a stay is being moved looks where it already is\n    * **The picker says the minimum stay.** A row of small pills under the months, there from the first draw, gives the house's minimum (and maximum), its arrival days and any rate band still to come that asks for more nights or other arrival days than the house does, with its dates: \"5 to 28 nights\", \"Arrivals on Saturdays\", \"High summer \u00b7 3 Jul \u2013 27 Aug \u00b7 7 nights minimum, Saturday to Saturday\". Once an arrival is picked the prompt says the earliest day to leave (\"Now pick the day you leave: at least 7 nights, so Sat 24 Jul or later\") using the minimum of the band the arrival is in, where it used to check the house's, and the days short of it are dimmed with the reason on them until the pick is cleared. The availability answer carries them as `stay_terms` and a night in a band with its own minimum says `min_nights`. Not on the read-only calendar\n    * **Ships its license.** A `LICENSE` file now comes with the plugin, and the blueprint links to the KahunaCart License at kahunacart.com\/license instead of the Grav Premium license\n\n3. [](#bugfix)\n    * **A night is only offered as an arrival if a stay can start there.** The calendar marked every night somebody could sleep in as one a stay could start on, and the picker then refused a Tuesday inside a band that takes arrivals on Saturdays only. The availability answer now honors the band's own arrival days (or the property's where the band has none), and a band's own minimum stay where the free nights after a date run out before it, so the read-only calendar's `pick`, the picker and the shop agree. A night that can be slept in and not started on says why in `arrival_reason`, with `arrival_days_say` or `arrival_min_nights`, and the picker words it (\"Arrivals on Saturdays\", \"7 nights minimum\")\n    * **Amounts in the picker follow Whole Amounts.** With the store's Whole Amounts on, the quote read \"$2,100.00\", \"$630.00 is due today\" and \"$800.00\" for the damage deposit while the rest of the page said \"$2,100\". The quote's lines, its total, the damage deposit and the sentence about what is due today now print the way the store does, keeping their cents together when any of them has some. What is frozen on a hold, and so the receipt, keeps the full amount. Needs Bookings 1.1.11\n"},"1.0.7":{"date":"10\/11\/2026","content":"1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **Works with a shop mounted at the site root.** With KahunaCart's `route: '\/'` (core 1.5.0) the listing link in a stay's messages is `\/product\/{slug}` rather than `\/\/product\/{slug}`\n"},"1.0.6":{"date":"10\/04\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Rentals sells in more than one currency.** On a store with several currencies enabled, a stay is quoted, held, paid and refunded in the currency the guest is browsing in. The property's own price a night comes from the product's amount in that currency, each rate band has a nightly and a weekend price in each other currency, and the cleaning fee, the extra-guest rate, the damage deposit and the monthly rate have one each too, typed in that currency's own decimal places on the booking rule and on the **Rates** tab. A store with one currency draws none of it, stores none of it and runs the same queries it did. Needs KahunaCart 1.4.0 and Bookings 1.1.5 or later\n    * **A night with no price in a currency is not available in it.** The calendar and the picker say \"Not available in EUR\" and a stay across it is refused with those words, while the same night stays bookable in your own currency. A rate band that covers a night but has no price in the currency makes the night unavailable and is not skipped, because skipping it would lose its minimum stay and arrival days along with its price. A fee with no amount in a currency is no such charge there, and is never the figure from your own currency under another code. New reason code `currency`\n    * **`--currency` on `bin\/plugin kahunacart-rentals nights`, and `currency` on the quote route and the `quote_rental_stay` tool.** The rules and rates routes and tools carry and take `currency_amounts` in core's format on a store with more than one currency, and the quote says which currency it is in. The **Rates** tab says which bands are missing which currency, and its try-it box prices a stay in a chosen one\n    * **The beach-house showcase is priced in euros and pounds** on a store that sells in them, and only in the currencies the store has turned on\n2. [](#improved)\n    * **Everything after a stay is booked is in its order's currency.** The deposit, the balance and its reminder, the damage deposit in the receipt and the pre-arrival email, the refund a cancellation offers and the price of a move are all in the currency the stay was paid in, whatever the guest browses in later. A property with no damage deposit in that currency says nothing about one\n    * **Amounts are said with the currency's own decimal places.** The command line, the quote sentences and the cancellation sentence no longer divide by 100, so yen reads 35,000 and not 350.00\n    * **`PriceBook::nightlyMinor()` takes a currency and answers a `PriceAnswer`** (an amount, free or not available), and `StayMessages::install()` takes two more optional callables for tests. A store with one currency is served exactly what it was\n\n3. [](#bugfix)\n    * Stores with no extra currencies enabled avoid currency-table queries when removing band prices or deleting bands and rental rules, while existing foreign-currency stays still read their own prices\n    * Concurrent rate-band edits clear the latest fixed prices when switching to a percentage, so an old foreign price cannot return when the band switches back\n    * Customer cancellation calls a stay a stay and explains the refund policy in its own currency when the refund request is raised\n    * A paid property without its own price in a currency is unavailable in the calendar and quote, even when rate bands have prices there, so the picker agrees with checkout\n    * Existing stays keep reading their own currency's rate bands and damage deposit after that currency is turned off, including in the pre-arrival message\n"},"1.0.5":{"date":"10\/01\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * Help text on the rental rates and property screens now follows the admin's Help text setting, showing below the label or in a tooltip behind an info icon\n"},"1.0.4":{"date":"09\/26\/2026","content":"1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **Seeding a rental works again with Bookings 1.1.1.** Bookings 1.1.1 gave its seed recipes an optional clock of their own, under the same name the rental recipes already used for theirs, and PHP refuses to set that property twice. So `bin\/plugin kahunacart-rentals seed` and every showcase built on a rental recipe stopped with \"Cannot modify readonly property\". The rental recipes now keep their clock under a name of their own and work with any Bookings version\n"},"1.0.3":{"date":"09\/23\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **The test suite runs against KahunaCart 1.3, which bundles brick\/money and YetiSearch under its own namespace.** When it borrows the base plugin's dependencies it now also loads core's `vendor-prefixed\/autoload.php`, and Bookings is still borrowed alongside it as before. Against an older KahunaCart without that file nothing changes. Nothing in Rentals itself names brick\/money or YetiSearch, so the add-on needs no other change\n"},"1.0.2":{"date":"09\/21\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **A monthly rate.** A property let by the month can now say so. A new **Monthly rate** on the booking rule, under **Who it sleeps, and what they pay**, prices a stay of a month or more a whole month at a time, counted from the arrival date, and the nights after the last whole month by the rate card as before. 15 January to 20 March at $1,200 a month and $60 a night is two months and five nights, $2,700, and the receipt says \"2 months at $1,200.00 a month\" and then \"5 nights\". A month is the same date in the next month and the 31st stays the 31st where it can, so from 31 January one month is 28 February and two is 31 March. **A rate band does not reach inside a month**, because a monthly price is the price for the month whatever season it is in, and bands only change the leftover nights. A stay shorter than a month, and every property with no monthly rate, is priced exactly as it was. The cleaning fee, extra guests, the deposit, the balance date and cancellation work as they do on any stay, and the quote label says \"2 months and 5 nights\" instead of 64 nights. `monthly_minor` on the rule, the API and the MCP tools, and \"Month 1\" beside each night in `bin\/plugin kahunacart-rentals nights --quote`; migration rentals_0003. A property's longest stay still defaults to 28 nights, so raise it, or set it to 0, before letting by the month. See [Rates and seasons \u2192 A monthly rate](rates-and-seasons.md#a-monthly-rate)\n"},"1.0.1":{"date":"09\/21\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **The \"Deposits are off\" note points at the right tab.** KahunaCart 1.2.13 moves **Deposits and part payment** from **Settings \u2192 Security** to **Settings \u2192 Commerce**, so the note under a property's **Paying** box and the [Payments and cancellation](payments-and-cancellation.md) page now send you to **KahunaCart \u2192 Settings \u2192 Commerce**. On an older KahunaCart the switch is still under Security\n"},"1.0.0":{"date":"09\/21\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **KahunaCart Rentals, the add-on.** Nightly stays and day rentals, on top of KahunaCart Bookings. A holiday let, a cabin, a B&B room, a campsite pitch, a kayak for the afternoon and a camera kit for three days are all the same thing \u2014 something booked by the day rather than by the hour \u2014 and this is where that lives. Bookings keeps everything that is true of any booking: bookables, needs, claims, holds, the double-booking guard, orders, deposits, emails with calendar entries, the manage page and the front desk. This keeps what is only true of a stay. It installs, migrates clean, and answers \"which nights of August can the beach house be slept in, which days can a stay start or end on, and what do the 9th to the 16th cost for six people\"\n    * **A night is named by the date it starts on, in the property's own timezone.** The night of the 9th is the night you go to bed on the 9th and get up on the 10th, and a stay from the 9th to the 16th is seven nights with the 16th a departure rather than a night. Everything else falls out of saying it that way: the claim runs from check-in on the arrival date to check-out on the departure date, four in the afternoon to ten in the morning, and the six hours in between belong to nobody\n    * **The shared changeover day, which is the case home-made calendars get wrong.** One party leaves the 10th at ten in the morning and another arrives the 10th at four in the afternoon. Both hold a real claim, the two claims do not touch, and the date shows as both a departure and an arrival. It is not an edge case: on a property that is doing well it is what every Saturday in August looks like\n    * **Five states per date, not two.** A rental calendar is not a grid of free and busy squares. A date carries two facts at once \u2014 whether you could sleep there tonight and whether you could have slept there last night \u2014 and the four combinations of those are the square, the square with a diagonal one way, the square with a diagonal the other way and the filled square that every rental site in the world draws. `free`, `arrival_only`, `departure_only`, `taken`, and `blocked` for a property that is shut rather than booked\n    * **Clocks going forward and back stop being interesting.** Every conversion happens through the property's own zone on the date in question, never by adding 86,400 to yesterday's answer \u2014 so the night before the clocks go forward is seventeen hours, the night before they go back is nineteen, a calendar day is 23 or 25, and a week across either is still a week. Tested in Denver, in Berlin and in Auckland, which is east of UTC and over the date line and where the transitions happen the other way round\n    * **What a run of nights costs, band by band.** A rate card is dated bands laid over the property's own price, and they overlap on purpose: \"Winter\" across five months with \"Christmas week\" laid over the top of it is how anybody would write it down, and the higher position wins per night. A band can be a price a night or a percentage of the property's own price, can charge a different rate for Friday and Saturday nights, and can carry a minimum stay and a set of arrival days that override the property's for a stay starting inside it \u2014 which is how \"seven nights, Saturday to Saturday, in August\" is one band with two numbers on it. **A property priced one way all year needs no bands at all**: a night no band covers costs the option's own price, which is the number the merchant already typed into the shop\n    * **The orphan-night rule, on by default.** A stay that would leave a run of free nights too short for anybody to book is refused rather than sold, because a calendar full of two-night gaps under a three-night minimum is accurate and unsellable. It looks both ways, it is fine with no gap at all, and it names the night that would have been stranded \u2014 \"that would leave nights nobody could book \u2014 nobody could book the night of Wed 12 Aug\" \u2014 because \"no\" on its own is what makes an owner turn a good rule off\n    * **Airbnb, VRBO and Booking.com, both ways.** Give each of them one channel link and paste each of theirs back, and a week sold anywhere stops being offered everywhere. Nothing in this engine changed for it: Bookings reads their whole-date reservations as the nights those dates really cover, and the night states fall out of the arithmetic that was already here. What did have to be said is what a night runs between \u2014 four in the afternoon to ten in the morning, or whatever this property says \u2014 because \"the 9th to the 16th\" read literally is midnight to midnight and takes the night of the **8th** off the market, which is a night that is genuinely still for sale and which no owner would ever notice they were losing\n    * **Both changeover days stay on the market.** A channel's week from the 9th to the 16th blocks the nights of the 9th to the 15th: somebody already in the house still leaves on the 9th, and somebody else can still arrive on the 16th. Proved east and west of UTC, across the clocks going forward and back, and against a stay of our own sharing the changeover day with a channel's\n    * **`docs\/channels.md`, written for an owner**, with where to paste each link, how often each site looks, and the limit said plainly rather than buried: every iCal sync polls, so two sites can sell the same night in the gap between two looks. It is rare, it is the same with every plugin that works this way, the only cure is a channel manager and this is not one \u2014 and there is a numbered list of what to do on the day it happens to you\n    * **Seven new reasons, in the same words as Bookings' own eight.** `min_nights`, `max_nights`, `arrival_day`, `departure_day`, `guests`, `gap` and `past` are taught to Bookings' `Reason` when the mode registers, so Troubleshoot, the command line and the picker all print them beside \"Booked\" and \"Too soon\" with no second lookup table anywhere. `past` is the one that replaces a Bookings answer rather than adding to it: a night that has already started is certainly less than the notice period away, but *Too soon* reads as \"come back later\" on a date nobody can ever book again, so a guest looking at last August is told it is last August\n    * **It reads nothing of Bookings' own.** Which properties could take this service, how many of each there are, when they are open, the days the owner is away, what is already booked or held or blocked by an Airbnb feed \u2014 every one of those arrives through the narrow set Bookings hands a registered mode. Not one query in this add-on touches a table of Bookings', and there is a test that fails if one ever does. That is what keeps this engine and Bookings' own availability walk from drifting: they are the same three readers underneath\n    * **A stay goes through the same guard a haircut goes through.** The mode answers with an interval, a property and one place, and Bookings claims exactly that through the write lock, the sweep, the recheck and the unique key it has always used. Nothing this add-on returns bypasses the double-booking check and there is no way to ask it to. A stay and an hour-long booking on the same property refuse each other both ways round, which is what a venue hired by the day and also by the hour needs\n    * **Four identical doubles are one record with four units.** The calendar says \"two left\" rather than making a guest work out which of Room 1, Room 2, Room 3 and Room 4 is free, and the owner has one row to edit instead of four. A claim from an outside calendar takes the whole property rather than one unit, because there is no sensible reading of \"Airbnb has one of your four identical doubles\"\n    * **Two tables and nothing on anybody else's.** `kahunacart_rental_rules` is one row per nightly property \u2014 arrival and departure times, the shortest and longest stay, which weekdays a stay may start and end on, how many guests the rate covers, the fees, the deposit terms and the cancellation policy. `kahunacart_rental_rates` is the rate card. Nothing is added to a Bookings table and nothing to a core table, so switching this add-on off leaves every service, booking, hold and claim in the store reading exactly as it did\n    * **`bin\/plugin kahunacart-rentals nights`** prints the month as a table with what each date is for, and `--quote 2026-08-09..2026-08-16` prints a stay as a breakdown with the rate band beside every night. It is how this release is demonstrated with nothing to click, and it stays afterwards as the thing to run when an owner says the calendar is wrong \u2014 it answers out of the same engine the picker will, so it is not a second opinion\n    * **`bin\/plugin kahunacart-rentals seed rental`** writes the beach house: sleeps eight, $249 a night, $289 at weekends, a summer rate at $329 with a seven-night Saturday-to-Saturday minimum, a Christmas week, a $140 cleaning fee, $25 a night for each guest over six, a $500 damage deposit, 30% down with the rest a fortnight before arrival, a cancellation policy in three steps \u2014 and three stays already in the book, two of them sharing a changeover day. `seed bnb` writes four identical doubles as one record with breakfast as an option. Both are idempotent and both are written in the store's own timezone, and the rate bands are worked out from today so a store seeded in September still has a calendar worth looking at in March\n    * **Every time of day is written the way the rest of the shop writes it.** A store that says `6:00pm` on a class card says `4:00pm` on the stay it sold, and a store set to 24-hour time says `16:00` in both places. It goes through Bookings' one class for that rather than a second opinion here, on the receipt, in the order email, on the account tab, on the manage page and in the command line's own banner\n    * **No `composer install` and no `vendor\/`.** This add-on has no run-time package of its own: it reads KahunaCart's database through the base plugin and Bookings' engine through the mode seam, and both are installed on any store that can run it. The autoloader is four lines of PSR-4 in the plugin file, and a GPM install is a folder of files that works as it lands\n    * **No permissions of its own, and no product type of its own.** A stay is a Bookings booking and a property is a Bookings bookable, so `kahunacart.bookings.view` and `kahunacart.bookings.manage` already cover everything here \u2014 a separate one would mean an owner who ticked \"Manage Bookings and Setup\" still could not edit the rate card of the property they had just set up\n    * **What a stay costs, as a breakdown a guest can read.** The quote a property answers with is a list of rows \u2014 *7 nights at $329.00*, *2 extra guests, 4 nights*, *Cleaning* \u2014 and those rows become the cart line, the receipt and the order email, adding up to the figure the guest was shown at every step. The cleaning fee is marked as not discountable, so a 10% coupon takes 10% off the nights and nothing off the cleaning, which is what every owner means by a discount. The damage deposit is **not** in the total: it is a fact printed beside the stay, because it is money held and given back rather than money taken\n    * **30% now and the rest a fortnight before you arrive.** The property's own deposit terms go to the store through Bookings' payment-schedule seam, as a figure and in the property's own words \u2014 *Of the $2,534.00 for this stay, $760.20 is due today and the rest \u2014 $1,773.80 \u2014 by Sat 25 Jul 2026.* It says what it is a share of because it is a share of the **stay**: a hamper bought alongside the week, and any sales tax, are due in full today, so a checkout's \"Due today\" can be more than the deposit on the quote. A stay booked after the balance date is due in full today and says so rather than asking for a deposit on money that was owed last week, a property with no deposit set is paid for in one go, and a stay with nothing to pay skips all of it\n    * **A balance nobody paid does not cost anybody their holiday.** The guest is emailed a pay link three days before the date \u2014 a number an owner can change \u2014 and the owner is told the moment the date passes with the money still not in, with a sentence from the property itself naming what is owed and how much warning the guest had. The three days are there so that the owner's \"not paid\" message does not arrive on the same morning the guest was first asked. The dates stay held and the deposit stays where it is: a holiday cancelled automatically at one minute past midnight over a card that expired is how a rental business loses a guest it already had\n    * **The cancellation policy is read when somebody cancels, and it answers with a figure.** The three-step policy the property was set up with \u2014 full refund far enough out, half inside that, nothing at the last minute \u2014 is applied against what the guest has actually paid **for that stay**, so cancelling one of two weeks in one basket offers back one of them rather than the basket, and the amount lands on the refund request a person opens. **The policy is read as it stands today rather than as it stood when the stay was sold**, which is written down on the settings, in the docs and in the code, because there is nowhere on a booking to freeze terms and an owner who rewrites their policy mid-season should know which stays it now covers\n    * **A stay with nothing to pay, and a stay paid for on arrival.** A free night, a comp, a room the owner takes payment for at the door \u2014 each of them goes straight into the diary with no basket and no checkout, the same as any other booking with no money on it, and the quote still prints the breakdown so everybody can see what is being given away\n    <!-- \u2500\u2500 nightly N4: the owner's screens \u2500\u2500 -->\n    * **The owner sets a property up from the Bookings section, on two screens.** Setting the way of booking to **Nightly stay** draws this add-on's own panels straight into the service editor: when people can arrive and leave and on which weekdays, the shortest and longest stay, who it sleeps and what an extra guest costs, the cleaning fee, the damage deposit, the deposit and when the balance falls due, the cancellation policy as a repeater that reads itself back in a sentence \u2014 *100% up to 30 days before, then 50% up to 14 days before, then 0% after that* \u2014 and the orphan-night rule. It saves on Bookings' own Save button, in the same write as the service\n    * **A Rates tab that draws the year.** A month ruler, a line across the top showing what a night actually costs, and one lane per band underneath drawn to scale in the order they are laid down \u2014 so a gap in March, a band that runs past new year and two bands over the same week are all things you can see rather than things you work out. Arrows on each lane decide which band wins where two of them overlap, a band opens into an editor for its dates, its price or its percentage, its weekend rate, its own minimum stay and its own arrival days, and underneath it all is a box that prices a real stay through the same engine a guest's calendar uses \u2014 pick two dates, press **Price it**, and read the nights, the extra guests and the cleaning with the deposit due today under them. Nothing is held and nothing is booked\n    * **Two messages a stay sends of its own.** A few days before somebody arrives they get the note the owner wrote for that property \u2014 directions, parking, the door code \u2014 with their dates, their check-in and check-out times and the damage deposit beside it; a day after they leave they get a thank-you with a link back to the property. Both have a switch of their own on the store's messages screen and both are ordinary Twig templates a theme can override by copying one file. Neither is sent for a stay that was cancelled, and the note is not sent to somebody whose balance fell due and was never paid. **The note is the only place a door code is ever printed**: it is not on the receipt, not in the confirmation, not in the calendar entry, not on the front desk and not in any calendar the shop publishes, and there is a test for every one of those clauses\n    <!-- \u2500\u2500 end nightly N4 \u2500\u2500 -->\n    <!-- \u2500\u2500 nightly N3: the range picker \u2500\u2500 -->\n    * **The calendar a guest actually uses.** Two months side by side where there is room and one where there is not, an arrival, a departure, and the nights between them drawn as one band. It is registered as a **face** on Bookings' picker rather than being a storefront of its own, so it arrives inside the store's own buy form, on whatever theme the shop already uses, in the store's own `--kc-*` colours, and the store's own quantity box and Add to cart button appear underneath once the dates are held \u2014 the same ones a jar of shampoo goes in with\n    * **Five states per date on the page, not two.** The states the engine has always answered with are finally drawn: a free night tinted, a booked night filled, a night that has gone struck through, and a **changeover day** split on the diagonal with a line through the middle \u2014 half of it spoken for and half of it not. On a property that is doing well it is what every Saturday in August looks like, and it is the one thing a home-made calendar never has\n    * **A date that cannot be chosen says why, in a sentence.** *3 nights minimum*, *Arrivals on Saturdays*, *Booked*, *That date has been and gone* \u2014 on the line under the calendar when a pointer crosses the date, and on the date itself for anybody reading with a keyboard or a screen reader. The minimum stay is enforced while the range is being drawn, so a date that would make too short a stay stops being pressable rather than being refused after somebody has pressed it. A refused date is `aria-disabled` rather than disabled, because a date nobody can explain is worse than a date nobody can book\n    * **Every figure on the summary is the shop's own answer.** Each rate band as its own row, the extra guests, the cleaning fee, the total, what is due today, when the balance falls due, the damage deposit as a stated amount and the cancellation policy in sentences \u2014 all of it comes back from the same quote the hold, the cart line and the receipt are built from. **Nothing in the page adds two numbers together**, so there is no version of this where the calendar and the checkout disagree, and a rate card edited this morning cannot be stale in a browser left open since yesterday\n    * **The dates are what is held, and the instant is worked out where the property is.** The browser posts *the 21st to the 27th* and never an instant, because check-in is four in the afternoon where the cottage is and a browser in another timezone doing that arithmetic is how a calendar ends up a day out twice a year. Dates are read and written as plain `YYYY-MM-DD` throughout the script for the same reason\n    * **It works with a keyboard, and it says what it is doing.** Arrow keys a day at a time, Page Up and Page Down a month at a time, Home and End to the ends of a week, Enter to choose and Escape to start again, with one tab stop into the grid and the focus kept on the same date when the calendar redraws. The chosen range, the nights, the price and every refusal are announced through a live region, so the summary is not something you have to go and look at\n    * **One month or two, decided by the space it has rather than by the size of the window.** The picker measures its own container, so the same calendar is two months wide in a page's main column, one month in a sidebar and one month in a phone's width, and a theme that puts it in a narrow box gets a calendar that fits rather than one that scrolls sideways. It is measured again when the box changes size, and the months are added and removed rather than hidden, so nothing keyboard-reachable is ever drawn where it cannot be seen\n    * **Moving a stay, from a guest's own link.** The manage page opens the same range picker with the guest's **own** booking left out of the reckoning, so the week they are moving off does not read as taken by themselves. The booking is named to the availability route by the token the guest was emailed and never by its id, so there is nothing in that request a stranger could guess\n    * **A read-only calendar for a listing page.** `[kahunacart-availability product=\"beach-house\" months=\"2\" \/]` and `{{ kahunacart_availability('beach-house') }}` draw a month or two of free and taken nights anywhere on the site with nothing to press. It is the same script reading the same public route the picker does, so a listing page and a booking page can never disagree about which week in August has gone\n    * **Every word on it is a language key with English behind it.** Fifty-odd of them, sent to the browser by the server as this mode's own strings \u2014 so a store with no translation reads properly, a store with one reads in its own language with no JavaScript changed, and there is a test that fails if the script ever draws a word the server does not send or the server sends one the script has no English for\n    <!-- \u2500\u2500 end nightly N3 \u2500\u2500 -->\n    <!-- \u2500\u2500 nightly N6: the docs \u2500\u2500 -->\n    * **Eleven pages of documentation, written for somebody with a cottage to let.** Two getting-started walkthroughs that end with a page a guest can book on \u2014 one for a whole property, one for four rooms sold as one record with breakfast as an option, each with a `seed` command that writes the same thing in one line \u2014 then what a night is and what each state on the calendar means, the rate card with the warning that a band's percentage is a multiplier and not an uplift, the deposit and the balance and what a cancellation actually gives back, Airbnb and VRBO and Booking.com with the polling limit said plainly, and a whole page of why a night is not on offer with every reason code, its sentence and what to do about it. The limits are in the prose where an owner meets them rather than in a list at the end: channels poll, an outside calendar on a property with four units blocks all four, a minimum stay that applies only at weekends cannot be written down, and a stay cannot be booked at the front desk\n    <!-- \u2500\u2500 end nightly N6 \u2500\u2500 -->\n    <!-- \u2500\u2500 nightly N7: the follow-up \u2500\u2500 -->\n    * **A stay can be taken at the front desk.** Somebody rings and wants the second week of June. Open `\/desk`, tap New booking, tap the property, and the sheet asks for the day they arrive, the day they leave and how many guests \u2014 then prints the same rows and the same total the guest would have read on the property's own page: the dates written out, check in and check out, every rate band as its own row, the extra guests, the cleaning and the total. Not the deposit, the due-today figure or the balance date, because none of those happens on this path. If those days cannot be had it says why in the same words the picker uses, so you can read the reason down the telephone. **Nothing is taken and nothing is scheduled**: a booking made at the counter has no order behind it, so there is no deposit, no balance date and no reminder, and the desk says *To collect $1,714.00* until you have. Put it through the property's own page instead if you want the deposit taken now\n    * **A seeded stay can have somebody in it.** `stay()` takes an optional customer \u2014 an id, or a name and an email to find or make one from \u2014 so a recipe writing a real property's book gets a diary worth looking at rather than a calendar of anonymous blocks. Neither of the two shipped recipes passes one; it is there for the recipes other people write, which had to copy the whole method to add one key\n    * **The read-only calendar measures itself, like the picker beside it.** `months` on `[kahunacart-availability]` is the **most** it will draw rather than the number it must, so a phone gets one month with seven readable columns instead of two squeezed ones \u2014 and two calendars in the same column stop disagreeing about how many months fit, which reads as a broken page rather than as two different controls\n    * **A refusal no longer sits under a range somebody has chosen.** Crossing a night the property is shut on wrote that night's reason onto the line under the calendar and left it there, because the line was only cleared while a range was half drawn. A guest who had just picked a perfectly good week read *Closed* under it. Once both dates are chosen the line says the range and nothing else\n    * **A stay with no dates on it is refused rather than priced from 1970.** Both doors into the nightly mode send a start of nought where nobody named one, and reading that as an instant put the arrival on the first of January 1970 \u2014 so the quote walked fifty-six years of rate card before anybody was told they had forgotten a date\n    * **How wide the calendar may get is a token here too.** `--kc-picker-max`, set on whatever wraps the picker, now lifts this face's own two caps as well as the panel's \u2014 so a page whose whole middle is the calendar says so once rather than reaching past three stylesheets with a selector long enough to beat them. The defaults are unchanged: 58rem for the picker, 44rem for the read-only calendar\n    * **A stay taken at the desk races a stay in somebody's basket, on all three engines.** Six processes going for two overlapping weeks in one house, half of them through the storefront's hold and half of them straight off the front desk, all jumping at the same wall-clock moment. Exactly one of them gets the week. It is what found the ordering bug in Bookings' own guard that let MariaDB sell the house twice\n    <!-- \u2500\u2500 end nightly N7 \u2500\u2500 -->\n    * **The Rates tab and the stay rules panel respect an account that may only look.** Somebody with `kahunacart.bookings.view` and not `kahunacart.bookings.manage` reads the whole year, opens a band to see its weekend rate and arrival days, and prices a stay with the try-it box, but is not offered Add a band, the reorder arrows, or a band's Save and Remove, all of which the server refuses. The stay rules inside Bookings' service editor draw read-only for them, with no cancellation step to add or take off. The properties list, the rate card and the rule each carry `can_manage`, worked out through the same check the writes go through\n\n1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **A property only asks for a deposit when the store takes deposits.** A property with a deposit percentage on a store where `checkout.deposits.enabled` is off still quoted \"30% due today and the rest by \u2026\" in the picker, the Rates preview and the command line, while the checkout took the whole stay. Every quote now follows the store switch: with deposits off the whole stay is due when it is booked, with no deposit line, no balance date and nothing handed to Bookings to schedule. The percentage stays saved and editable, and the Paying box under the property's settings says deposits are off for this store and where to switch them on. The damage deposit is the owner's own and is still stated either way."}}},"kahunacart-print-on-demand":{"name":"KahunaCart Print on Demand","version":"1.0.3","description":"Sell products Printful, Printify and Gelato make and ship, from your KahunaCart store","description_html":"<p>Sell products Printful, Printify and Gelato make and ship, from your KahunaCart store<\/p>","description_plain":"Sell products Printful, Printify and Gelato make and ship, from your KahunaCart store","icon":"shirt","author":{"name":"Trilby Media, LLC","email":"hello@trilby.media"},"dependencies":[{"name":"grav","version":">=2.0.0"},{"name":"kahunacart","version":">=1.4.7"}],"compatibility":{"grav":["2.0"]},"homepage":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/add-ons\/print-on-demand","docs":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/docs\/print-on-demand\/overview","keywords":"grav, premium, commerce, ecommerce, print on demand, printful, printify, gelato, merch","bugs":"https:\/\/github.com\/getgrav\/grav-premium-issues\/labels\/kahunacart-print-on-demand","license":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/license","tag_name":"1.0.3","date":"2026-10-23T03:22:49Z","release_type":"stable","zipball_url":"https:\/\/getgrav.org\/download\/plugins\/kahunacart-print-on-demand\/1.0.3","repository":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/add-ons\/print-on-demand","premium":{"vendor":"KahunaCart","validator":"kahunacart","license_product":"kahunacart-print-on-demand","checkout_url":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/shop\/product\/kahunacart-print-on-demand","button":"Buy Now","filename":"grav-plugin-kahunacart-print-on-demand"},"slug":"kahunacart-print-on-demand","install_path":"user\/plugins\/kahunacart-print-on-demand","content_sha256":"4a567f5e1ada75dec69abe3b1a16ff0e461cf17071c3e696f42dbc86456d7868","changelog":{"1.0.3":{"date":"10\/22\/2026","content":"1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **The Review tab shows an order that arrived while the section was open.** Review and Orders each read their list once and kept it, so an order paid and handed over for review after the section opened showed as waiting on the Orders tab while Review still said Nothing waiting. Both lists are now read again each time their tab is opened\n"},"1.0.2":{"date":"10\/21\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Themes can say a product is printed for you.** `kahunacart_pod_product(product)` answers `printed`, `print_days` (the store's Production days for the provider that makes it) and `label`, a sentence like \"Printed for you in about 3 days, then shipped\" that never names the provider or the cost. Anything that isn't a printed product gets an empty answer, and it never throws\n1. [](#improved)\n    * **Ships its license.** A `LICENSE` file now comes with the plugin, and the blueprint links to the KahunaCart License at kahunacart.com\/license instead of the Grav Premium license\n"},"1.0.1":{"date":"10\/13\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **You hear when a print provider's webhook is turned away.** A delivery refused because the secret in the address is wrong or missing, or because its signature does not check out, now goes to KahunaCart's Webhook rejected message under the provider's name, once a day per provider and reason (KahunaCart 1.5.3 or later)\n    * **The review queue can reach your phone.** The \"Print on demand has things waiting for you\" alert gets a row of its own under Notifications \u2192 Store owner, sent when the queue first has something in it and again as \"Cleared\" when it is empty (KahunaCart 1.5.3 or later)\n"},"1.0.0":{"date":"10\/11\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * The add-on itself: the plugin, its settings with a Printful tab and step-by-step instructions, two permissions (one for setting up, one for sending orders, because sending spends money), and the `pod_0001_core` migration with the links and imports tables\n    * The provider contract every print-on-demand company plugs into, with the Providers card's capability matrix built from what each driver actually declares, and room for more than three providers without a migration\n    * A **Print on demand** product type: it ships, holds no stock, and is refused at the cart door if the add-on is switched off. It is registered only once a provider is connected, so a store that connects nothing is the same as one without the add-on\n    * Printful connection check: a real call that names the store the token reaches, lists its scopes, says which of `orders`, `sync_products\/read` and `webhooks` are missing, and says \"expired or revoked\" when Printful refuses the token\n    * Printful import: list what you designed at Printful, see each product's variants with their cost, retail price and availability, and import one as a draft product with Color and Size options, one variant per variant switched on at Printful, its mockups copied into the product's media and attached to the right variants, its own shipping profile, and a link from every variant to Printful's two references. Prices are the ones you confirm on the import sheet; nothing is priced without your word, and a second import of the same product is refused as already in your store\n    * Refresh: bring Printful's cost, availability and SKUs in again, add new variants switched off for you to price, and mark variants Printful dropped as unavailable without deleting them. Title, description, prices and pictures change only when you ask\n    * A variant Printful no longer makes, or whose provider is not connected, is refused at the cart door with a sentence the customer can read\n    * Command line: `check printful`, `products printful`, `import printful <id> [--markup=100] [--dry-run]`, and `record printful` for refreshing the test fixtures from a real store (reads only, never orders)\n    * Printful's live shipping rates at checkout: a carrier driver per connected provider, quoting every Printful service for a parcel in one call, in the order's currency, with \"Arrives in about N days\" counting Printful's production days (a new setting, 3 by default) and a line saying import duties may be due when a parcel crosses a border\n    * `shipping printful --zones=1,2` and `POST \/kahunacart\/pod\/providers\/{code}\/shipping-methods` (MCP `add_pod_shipping_methods`): add one method per Printful service to each zone named, bound to Printful, without touching any method that is already there\n    * A parcel Printful makes is offered only Printful's methods, so your own flat rate never pays Printful's postage, and a mixed cart is two parcels charged separately and added up. With KahunaCart 1.4.6 this holds for a cart of only printed goods; KahunaCart 1.4.7 passes the parcel to `onKahunaCartShippingMethodExcluded`, and a mixed cart is guarded parcel by parcel. Switch it off with **Printed goods ship at the printer's rates** to charge your own rates instead. When Printful cannot carry a parcel, your own methods carry it\n    * Address checks before payment: a state for the US, Canada and Australia, and a CPF or CNPJ for Brazil, each refusal naming the field\n    * `record printful` also records Printful's rates for a US and a German address, through the one POST the recorder allows, which writes nothing\n    * Custom products: **Made by** on any product sets it to be made by Printful from Printful's own blanks. Search Printful's catalogue (\"Bella Canvas 3001\", \"hoodies\", \"embroidery\"), match the product's own colours and sizes to a blank's (Grey and Gray, XXL and 2XL read as the same), fix the misses by pasting a blank id, and save; nothing is saved while a variant has no blank. The product becomes a Print on demand product with Printful's shipping profile, and **Made by: nobody** puts its own type, profile and stock counting back\n    * Print files: upload a PNG, JPEG or PDF per print area, for the whole product or one variant. Checked from the bytes, not the name, and against the size limit before it is read; the pixel size is read and a file that would print under 150 dpi says so. Files are kept privately and Printful fetches them from a public address with a token of their own, which changes every time a file is replaced. A print area Printful cannot name is refused when you save, and a custom variant without a print file is refused at the cart door\n    * Assign by SKU: a SKU prefix or a numeric range (\"7000 to 7999\") links every variant it catches to Printful, with one blank for all, blanks matched by each product's options, or each variant's own, and optionally a print file for all of them. A dry run lists what it will link, what it will replace and what it will skip and why, before anything is written\n    * Links CSV: import a spreadsheet of `sku, provider, mode, blank, file:<print area>` rows with a dry run that names every bad line and why, then write the good rows in the background, fetching each file once however many rows share it; export the store's setup the same way. Every bulk run can be undone, and an undo is refused, naming the variants, when something has changed them since\n    * Command line: `links:import <file> [--commit]`, `links:export [--provider=] [--output=]`, `links:undo <run>`\n    * The `pod_0002_custom` migration, with the artwork and link-runs tables\n    * Orders: once an order is paid, each provider's share of it is created at Printful as a draft named by your order number on the packing slip, with the cost shown when Printful has worked it out and the margin against what the customer paid. Review mode waits for **Send to Printful**; auto mode sends by itself once the cost is known\n    * A Printful order is never made twice: the order is looked up by its reference before every attempt, and an order Printful already has is carried on with\n    * Send again, Check again and Cancel at provider on the order: a draft is deleted, a pending order is cancelled with its cost going back to the Printful wallet, and one Printful has started says so on the order and in a notification\n    * Tracking: each parcel Printful sends becomes a shipment on the order with its carrier, tracking number and link, and KahunaCart's shipped email goes out. Partial parcels, replacements and deliveries are recorded once, from Printful's webhooks or a poll every four hours for up to 60 days\n    * Webhooks: **Register webhook** (and `webhook printful --url=`) gives Printful a secret address and keeps the signing secret it answers with. Deliveries are checked by size, address and signature, logged, and read back from Printful in the background\n    * The review queue and a dashboard alert: orders waiting to be sent, orders that need attention, auto-synced drafts and cost increases. Daily reminders for orders waiting and a token about to expire\n    * Shipping methods are added for the services Printful actually offers each zone, found by quoting the store's own products to the zone's first country\n    * Command line: `orders [order-id] [--sync] [--cancel=<id>]` and `webhook <provider> [--url=] [--reset]`\n    * The `pod_0003_orders` migration, with the provider orders, parcels and webhook log tables\n    * Gelato: a Gelato tab with the API key, mode, production days and step-by-step instructions, and `check gelato`, which makes one read to prove the key works\n    * Gelato products: import a Gelato template with `import gelato <template id>` (its sizes and colours become options, with Gelato's print areas and cost on every variant), or set any product to **Made by: Gelato** and paste a product UID or search Gelato's catalogue (\"posters A3\", \"framed\"). Either way it is your own print files Gelato prints, fetched from a private address only an order names\n    * Gelato's shipping at checkout: Economy, Standard and Express, priced from every facility Gelato would make the parcel in and added up, offered only when every facility can do it, in the order's currency, with the duties line when the parcel crosses a border and the method does not include duties\n    * Gelato orders: a paid order becomes a Gelato draft named by your order number, with its cost and margin, and is sent when you press Send, or at once in auto mode, after the draft is read back from Gelato and found to be the order as it stands. The order is looked up by its reference before every attempt, so a timeout never makes a second one. An order Gelato splits across facilities is one order here, with every parcel tracked onto it, and cancel works across all of them\n    * Gelato's address rules at checkout: its name and address lengths, an email, a local-script address for China, Japan, Korea and Russia, and a product Gelato does not make for the customer's country, each named so the customer knows what to change\n    * A Gelato parcel becomes a shipment on the order only once its items have left, not when Gelato first gives it a tracking code\n    * Gelato stock: products that can run out (frames, say) are checked once a day and go unavailable while Gelato is out of them\n    * Gelato webhooks: Gelato does not sign them and has no API to add them, so **Register webhook** (and `webhook gelato --url=`) makes a secret address and lists the steps to add it in Gelato's dashboard, with the secret hidden; `webhook gelato --reveal` prints the whole address to paste. Events from your other Gelato sales channels are answered and ignored\n    * Printify: a Printify tab with the token, the shop, the billing currency, mode, production days and step-by-step instructions. **Printify cannot be switched on until you confirm the shop is set to Manual order approval**, because a Printify shop left on its default sends every order to production by itself after 24 hours and charges you; saving it on without that is refused with the reason, and `check printify` lists the shops the token reaches and says which scopes are missing\n    * Printify products: import what you designed in Printify (its enabled variants, Color and Size options, mockups and costs), refreshed daily, and Printify's Publish button can bring products in as drafts and is always answered, so a product is never left locked in Printify\n    * Printify's shipping at checkout: Standard, Priority, Express and Economy from one quote, Express and Economy only when every line can have them, in the currency Printify bills you in, with Printify's address rules (lengths, a phone outside the US, Latin letters) checked before payment\n    * Printify orders: a paid order is made on hold at Printify under your order number, with its cost and margin, and sent when you press Send or at once in auto mode, after the order is read back and found to be the order as it stands. A retry never makes a second one. Cancel works while Printify still allows it and says so by name when it does not; an order Printify puts back on hold after it was sent needs attention, with the reason in plain words\n    * Printify tracking: each parcel onto the order by the SKUs Printify names, from signed webhooks (or, if Printify does not sign, deliveries checked by reading the order back), and Printify disconnecting the shop switches Printify off and tells you\n    * `record printify` for putting real Printify answers beside the documented fixtures (reads only, never an order)\n    * `record gelato --template=<id>` records Gelato's catalogue, a template, its products and prices, shipment methods and two errors from a real account, with GET calls only\n    * A changed order whose draft cannot be edited at the provider is remade: the draft is cancelled and the new lines are sent as the next attempt\n    * Kahuna Print Co., a built-in demo provider for demo stores and for walking the screens before you have an account: three invented products (a tee in four colours and five sizes, a mug, a poster) with mockups drawn for it, Standard and Express postage worked out from the country and the item count, and orders that go from submitted through in production and shipped (two parcels for a mixed order) to delivered on a clock, with generated tracking numbers and a public tracking page. It never opens a socket, and it is off unless `demo_provider.enabled` is on\n    * `seed` and `seed --purge --yes`: import, price and publish the three demo products, add Standard and Express to every shipping zone, and place two orders in different states. Idempotent and reversible\n    * `demo:tick [--advance=N]`, and the same pass on the job worker's tick, to move the demo printer's orders on\n    * A demo store that is read-only shows \"Demo store \u2014 this is read-only\" where a Send, Check, Cancel or Refresh button would fail, and hides what a simulation cannot fake. The orders list, review list and providers payloads gain `can_send` and `readonly` for it\n    * `docs\/demo-provider.md`, with the checklist for putting it on a demo box\n    * The `pod_0004_order_claims` migration: a claim on each provider order, so two workers, a button and a job never act on one at the same time, and what was last sent to the provider, kept for the retry that finds an order by reference\n    * The documentation in `docs\/`: getting started, a page for each of Printful, Printify and Gelato, products and import, custom products, moving from another platform, shipping, orders, tracking, configuration, the reference and testing\n2. [](#improved)\n    * The docs say Gelato makes clothing, hats and mugs as well as wall art and cards, and that the add-on needs KahunaCart 1.4.7 or later\n    * **A cart of printed goods shows the printer's options and nothing else.** For a parcel the printer makes all of, the store's own shipping methods were listed under the printer's Standard and Express, each turned down with a sentence (\"Made by our print partner\") or with another carrier's \"No rate is available for this order right now\". Every one of those refusals is now marked hidden, so with KahunaCart 1.4.9 or later checkout leaves them out and the shopper picks between the printer's own options. A method already refused for its own reason keeps it. On an older KahunaCart they are listed as before\n3. [](#bugfix)\n    * An order with lines from two providers no longer fails on its second provider: each provider's order reference now ends with the provider's name\n    * **A Gelato mug, bottle or tote asks for the right size of print file.** Importing a Gelato template took the product's own dimensions as its print area for anything printed on one side, which is right for a poster or a card (the sheet is what gets printed) and wrong for a mug, a bottle or a tote bag, where Gelato's dimensions are the object itself: a 10 oz mug says 105 \u00d7 104 mm. The product panel then asked for a print file that size and could warn about a good file printing soft. Only `flat_` products now take their sheet as the print area; everything else keeps the size of the area its template uses. Nothing sent to Gelato changed. Clothing was never affected, and a real Gelato tee's record is now in the tests to keep it that way\n    * **The demo printer and the shipping add-on's demo carrier no longer share a code.** Kahuna Print Co. was registered as the carrier `demo`, which is also the shipping add-on's demo carrier, and core keeps one carrier driver per code. On a store running both, like demo.kahunacart.com, whichever registered second quoted nothing: the shipping add-on's Kahuna Ground and Priority methods said \"No rate is available\" for every parcel. The printer is now `kahunaprint`, and migration pod_0005 moves what an earlier version stored under `demo`: its links, imports, orders, parcels and webhook log, and the Standard and Express shipping methods its seeder made. The shipping add-on's own methods are left alone"}}},"kahunacart-affiliates":{"name":"KahunaCart Affiliates","version":"1.1.3","description":"Affiliate program for KahunaCart: referral links and partner coupons, commissions that follow refunds, recurring commissions on subscriptions, and payouts by store credit, PayPal or by hand","description_html":"<p>Affiliate program for KahunaCart: referral links and partner coupons, commissions that follow refunds, recurring commissions on subscriptions, and payouts by store credit, PayPal or by hand<\/p>","description_plain":"Affiliate program for KahunaCart: referral links and partner coupons, commissions that follow refunds, recurring commissions on subscriptions, and payouts by store credit, PayPal or by hand","icon":"handshake-o","author":{"name":"Trilby Media, LLC","email":"hello@trilby.media"},"dependencies":[{"name":"grav","version":">=2.0.0"},{"name":"kahunacart","version":">=1.5.18"}],"compatibility":{"grav":["2.0"]},"homepage":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/add-ons\/affiliates","docs":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/docs\/affiliates","keywords":"grav, premium, commerce, ecommerce, affiliates, referrals, commissions, payouts","bugs":"https:\/\/github.com\/getgrav\/grav-premium-issues\/labels\/kahunacart-affiliates","license":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/license","tag_name":"1.1.3","date":"2026-10-26T03:50:38Z","release_type":"stable","zipball_url":"https:\/\/getgrav.org\/download\/plugins\/kahunacart-affiliates\/1.1.3","repository":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/add-ons\/affiliates","premium":{"vendor":"KahunaCart","validator":"kahunacart","license_product":"kahunacart-affiliates","checkout_url":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/shop\/product\/kahunacart-affiliates","button":"Buy Now","filename":"grav-plugin-kahunacart-affiliates"},"slug":"kahunacart-affiliates","install_path":"user\/plugins\/kahunacart-affiliates","content_sha256":"291df09cebee878e2034cc96a66c0c0571b2b0e457ecdb68647789f59ea33eeb","changelog":{"1.1.3":{"date":"10\/25\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **Buttons in these emails stay readable on a light button color.** The writing on a button was always white, so a store that picked a light Email Button Color got white on yellow. It now turns dark when the button is light, the same as KahunaCart's own emails. Needs KahunaCart 1.6.5 for the dark writing; an older KahunaCart keeps white\n"},"1.1.2":{"date":"10\/22\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **Ships its license.** A `LICENSE` file now comes with the plugin, and the blueprint links to the KahunaCart License at kahunacart.com\/license instead of the Grav Premium license\n"},"1.1.1":{"date":"10\/19\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **A settled dispute settles the commission.** A commission held by a dispute now follows the outcome when KahunaCart reports it (`onKahunaCartDisputeClosed`, from Stripe and PayPal, or set on the order by hand). **Won** lets the hold go, as **Release the dispute** does, with a line on the commission's note. **Lost** takes the commission back as a full refund would, because the platforms report a lost dispute as its outcome and not as a refund: an unpaid commission is reversed, and a paid one gets a clawback off the partner's next payout, with the **Affiliate commission taken back** message saying the buyer won a dispute. Setting a dispute back to open by hand holds the commission again. A closing that names a different dispute from the one holding the commission leaves it held. Needs KahunaCart 1.5.18\n"},"1.1.0":{"date":"10\/17\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Partners choose their code.** The application form, signed in and public, has an optional **Your code** field that shows the link it makes. Blank still makes a code from their name. A code that's taken, kept for the store or badly formed sends the form back with every answer kept and the sentence under the field; it's never quietly changed into another one. On the public form the code waits in the unconfirmed application, and if somebody takes it before the email is confirmed, even in the very moment of confirming, confirming still works: they get a code made from their name, and the application in the admin says which code they asked for. A code made from a name leaves out **Blocked Words in Codes**, so **Approve Automatically** never hands out your brand\n    * **Live check as they type.** Every code field asks a small public endpoint whether the code is free a moment after the typing stops, and says so under the field with the same sentence the form would give. It's only a hint, since the form checks again when it's sent, and it's rate limited in KahunaCart's limiter (`affiliate_code_check`, 30 a minute per visitor by default, `security.rate_limits.affiliate_code_check` to change it) with the visitor's address hashed before it's counted\n    * **Blocked Words in Codes** (`program.blocked_codes`, on the Program tab, empty by default): words partners may not use in a code they ask for, such as your brand or a competitor's. A word blocks any code that contains it, so `official` also blocks `theo-official`. Checked on applications and change requests (again when you approve one, so a word you block later still counts), never on a code you type yourself, and it matters most with **Approve Automatically** on\n    * **Partners can ask for a new code.** The Overview page in the Affiliate tab, which every partner has (coupon-only ones too), shows the partner's code and a **Change code** form (new code with the live check, an optional reason). It works with JavaScript off. One request waits at a time, even when Send is pressed twice at once, and while it waits the page says what they asked for and when, with a **Withdraw** button. Only active partners can ask. Account API routes `GET`, `POST` and `DELETE \/kahunacart\/affiliates\/account\/code-request` do the same\n    * **You approve or reject it.** A waiting request shows on the partner's page and on the Applications tab with **Approve\u2026** and **Reject\u2026**. Approving checks the code again (if somebody took it meanwhile, or it now has a blocked word in it, it says so and you reject or edit by hand), gives the partner the new code and emails them their new link and that their old code and coupon keep working (`affiliates.code_changed`). Rejecting emails them your message (`affiliates.code_request_rejected`). Admin API routes: `GET \/kahunacart\/affiliates\/code-requests`, `POST \u2026\/code-requests\/{id}\/approve` and `\u2026\/reject`\n    * **Also make a coupon for the new code.** Approving a request, and changing a code yourself on the partner's page, offers a coupon with the new code in capitals at the discount of their first enabled percent coupon (on when they have one). If that coupon code already exists, even if it was made a moment earlier, the code still changes, the partner is still emailed, and you're told why no coupon was made. The old coupon is never disabled or renamed\n    * **The owner hears about requests.** A new row on Notifications \u2192 Store owner, **Affiliate asked for a new code** (`affiliates.owner_code_request`), names the partner, the code they asked for and their reason, with a link to their page, and a dashboard alert stays up while requests are waiting\n    * **Old codes on the partner's page.** The Details panel lists a partner's old codes, all of which still credit them\n    * **New events.** `onKahunaCartAffiliateCodeChanged` fires after every code change has committed, yours or an approved request's, with `affiliate`, `old_code`, `new_code`, `actor`, `request` (null for your own edit) and `coupon`. `onKahunaCartAffiliateCodeRequested` (`affiliate`, `request`) and `onKahunaCartAffiliateCodeRequestRejected` (`affiliate`, `request`, `message`, `actor`) go with the requests\n    * Every code a partner has had (table `kahunacart_affiliate_codes`) and their code requests travel in store exports with the partner in the **Affiliates** set, and `reset` clears them. A merge never gives a code that belongs to one partner here to another (it says so in the report), and a partner whose code is one of a partner's old codes here is matched to that partner\n1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **Changing a partner's code no longer breaks their links.** The old code used to stop working, so every link the partner had already printed credited nobody, and the code was free for somebody else to take, who would then get the credit for the first partner's links. Now every code a partner has had, their current one included, is kept in one table whose unique index decides who has a code, written in the same transaction as the change: links with an old code still credit the same partner, nobody else can ever take it, not even in the same moment (applications, requests, your own edits and store imports all go through it), and a partner can take their own old code back. Partners already in the store have their current code recorded when the migration runs. Erasing a partner keeps their old codes, like their code, and blanks what they wrote when asking for a new one\n"},"1.0.2":{"date":"10\/13\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * Reply-To Address (`notifications.reply_to`, on the Notifications tab): where a partner's reply to an affiliate email goes, such as affiliates@yourstore.com. It covers every email to partners and the confirmation email for a public application, and nothing else the store sends. Blank, or an address that isn't an email address, uses the Email plugin's `reply_to` as before, and without that replies go to the From address.\n    * Spam check on the public form (`program.captcha`, on the Program tab, on by default): the public application form carries Cap, the Form plugin's invisible proof-of-work check, the same one Grav's contact and newsletter forms use. The visitor's browser does a moment of work before the form is sent, with no puzzle and no third party. A form sent without a valid answer, from a browser with JavaScript off for example, is sent back with the answers kept and a sentence asking to send it again. Scripts caught by the hidden field or the fill time still hear nothing. Without the Form plugin there is no check rather than a form nobody can send.\n    * **The owner hears about payouts, tax forms and commissions.** Notifications \u2192 Store owner has five new Affiliates rows. **Affiliate payouts due** says how many partners have reached the payout minimum and how much is owed, once a month, on the first day anyone is over it. **Affiliate payout problem** (high priority) says when one payout fails, comes back or waits for the partner to claim it, or when a PayPal batch is stuck or its outcome isn't known, with the partner's name, the amount and the reason in plain words. **Affiliate tax form received** names the partner and the form (W-9, W-8BEN or W-8BEN-E) and never anything written on it. **Affiliate commission earned** (\"Theo earned $14.70 on order #1042\") and **Affiliate commission taken back** start switched off, because a busy program would send one for every sale. None of them ever includes a partner's email address\n    * **PayPal Payouts not switched on and tax forms needing attention reach your channels.** Those two dashboard alerts now have their own rows, sending one message when they appear and a \"Cleared\" one when they go (needs KahunaCart 1.5.3)\n    * **Email on Each Commission says what it does.** Its help text now says it emails the partner, and that the store-owner messages are on the Notifications screen\n    * **New event.** `onKahunaCartAffiliatePayoutProblem` fires when a PayPal batch is stuck or its outcome is unknown, with `payout`, `kind` and `reason`. `onKahunaCartAffiliateCommissionReversed` now also carries `why`\n"},"1.0.1":{"date":"10\/12\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * Partners in a customer group: pick one of the store's customer groups as **Customer Group** on the Program tab and every customer with an active or paused partner is put in it, so Customers can be filtered to partners and a page can be locked to them with `site.customer_group.<code>`. A partner customer whose partners are all applied, rejected, closed or erased is out of it, even if they were put in by hand, so the group should be one just for partners. Customers who have never been partners are never touched, and moving a partner to another customer moves the membership with them.\n    * Changing the group takes partner customers out of the old one and into the new one, and clearing it takes them out. The add-on remembers the group it last filled, so this works however the setting was changed. A code that isn't a group adds nobody and breaks nothing.\n    * Approving, pausing, closing or linking a partner changes the group straight away. Saving the settings, a daily worker job and `bin\/plugin kahunacart-affiliates groups:sync [--dry-run]` bring every partner customer into line, putting right anything an import, a restore or a customer merge changed.\n    * Apply without an account: with **Apply Without an Account** on (`program.public_apply`, on by default), people with no customer account apply from a public form at `\/affiliate\/apply` under the store. They confirm their email from a link before anything is filed, and the page says \"Check your email\" whatever the address, so the form never tells anyone whether an address has an account. The program block's Apply button opens the form, and a visitor who is signed in is sent to their Affiliate tab.\n    * The confirmation link (`affiliates.application_confirm`, a new row on the Notifications screen) works once, only from the newest email, until the store's `account.invite_expiry_days`, and only its hash is stored. Opening it shows a button, and only pressing the button files the application, so a mail scanner that opens links files nothing. Unconfirmed applications are deleted by the worker once their link expires, and they stay out of store exports and restores.\n    * The account is made on approval: an address that belongs to a customer is attached to them, anything else becomes a new customer with a sign-in and no password through KahunaCart's own repositories, and the approval email carries a link to choose a password. Rejecting makes nothing. With **Approve Automatically** on, confirming is the approval.\n    * The public form has a hidden field for scripts, a minimum fill time, and a rate limit per client and per address in KahunaCart's limiter (`affiliate_apply`), with the client address and the email hashed before they're counted.\n    * The signed-in form says who is applying (\"Applying as\" with the account's name and email, and a way to sign out), and the public form invites people with an account to sign in and apply with it. Both are one shared template, `apply-form.html.twig`.\n    * Applications are marked **Existing customer**, **Matches a customer** or **No account yet**, so the merchant knows whether approving makes an account, and approving one with no account says so.\n    * Quick reasons for rejecting: the reject dialog has a placeholder and reasons a click puts into the message, from **Quick Reasons for Rejecting** (`program.reject_reasons`), a list on the Program tab the merchant can add to, edit, reorder or empty.\n    * The rejection email says what happened, gives a general reason when the merchant didn't write one, asks the applicant to reply if something has changed, and says it changes nothing about their account (or that none was made). The confirmation, received and approved emails end with how to get help and the store's sign-off, and partner emails take the Email plugin's `reply_to` when one is set.\n2. [](#bugfix)\n    * A customer whose address sent a public application before they signed in sees it waiting in their Affiliate tab rather than a second form, and can't apply twice.\n    * The payout line in the program's terms ends with a full stop before the store credit bonus sentence.\n    * The tax disclaimer and the program's intro no longer join clauses with a semicolon or promise a commission on every order (some products may earn nothing): \"on the orders you bring in\".\n    * The program block's line under Apply only mentions making an account when the public form is on.\n    * The accept-the-terms sentence keeps \"program terms.\" together: the checkbox labels' words are one item in their row.\n    * The approval email only carries a link to choose a password when the account behind it still has the address the applicant confirmed. If that customer's address was changed, or the partner was moved to another customer, before the email was built, it goes without the link and tells them to sign in as usual.\n    * The public form's minimum fill time and its hashed rate-limit keys are signed with KahunaCart's own site secret. They were using Grav's `security.salt`, which Grav 2 doesn't have, so the key was empty and anyone could make a valid signature. With no secret the form turns every submission away (still answering \"Check your email\") and logs why, once.\n    * Pressing a confirmation link takes the waiting application out only if the link's token is still the current one and hasn't expired. Sending the form again, or a newer email, in the middle of a press can no longer let the old press delete the new answers and file the old ones.\n    * Filing a confirmed application is one database transaction with taking it out, including the customer and sign-in an automatic approval makes. If it fails partway, nothing is lost: the application is still waiting, the same link still works, no customer is left behind and no email goes out. The applied event and the emails follow only once it has committed.\n    * The confirmation email and the welcome email's password link are built on a site address the store trusts (`security.site_urls` or `system.custom_base_url`), the way KahunaCart's own sign-in and password emails are. With none, the email isn't sent and the job tries again once one is set, instead of sending a link with no host in it, and nothing is minted first.\n    * A retried confirmation email keeps its identity: the job now gets its queue id, so every attempt is one entry in the email log and a retry that finds the email already sent doesn't send it again or replace the link in it.\n    * An application from an address a Grav account holds no longer becomes a partner nobody can sign in as. Approving it makes and attaches no customer, the application is marked **Sign in needed**, and the approval email tells them to sign in with the account they have and send the form from the Affiliate tab, which connects the account to the approval they already have.\n    * Approving a public application from the admin is one database transaction, including the customer and sign-in it makes. If saving the partner fails, no customer is left behind and the application is still waiting. The customer group and the status event follow only once it has committed. Approvals that are refused, like a customer who already has a partner, still say so and change nothing.\n    * Every partner email (the approval, rejection, payout and statement emails and the rest) keeps its identity when it is retried: the job now gets its queue id, so every attempt is one entry in the email log and the logged mailer can tell a retry that already went out. Before, each retry got a new message id and could send the email twice.\n"},"1.0.0":{"date":"10\/12\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * First release.\n    * A paid order earns its referring partner a commission once, whether it was paid at checkout or later, worked out on each line after its share of discounts with no tax in it (tax-inclusive prices are taken apart per tax class), without shipping unless the program pays on it, and without gift card purchases.\n    * Rates resolve from a product override for the partner, to one for everyone, to a category override (the highest of a product's categories wins), to the partner's own rate, to the program default; flat amounts are per unit on products and categories and once per order otherwise, and never more than what they are paid on.\n    * Every line's exact share is kept and the total is rounded half up once, so a partial refund takes back exactly what the refunded lines and units earned, and a refund delivered twice takes back once.\n    * Commissions wait out the holding period and are approved by the worker every quarter hour, or wait for the merchant when approval is required; disputes hold an unpaid commission until a refund settles it or the merchant releases it.\n    * A refund or reversal after payment leaves the paid commission alone and writes a negative clawback that nets off the next payout; balances can go negative and the merchant can add signed adjustments with a note.\n    * Subscription renewals of a referred order earn renewal commissions (all, a set number, or none, with a renewal rate), and a renewal order never earns a second time as a sale.\n    * Admin API for commissions (filter, sort, search, page, bulk approve and reject, release a dispute, adjustments, credit an order set by hand), rate overrides, balances and the overview numbers; account API for a partner's own commissions and totals, with orders shown by number and date only.\n    * Partner links: a visit on `?ref=<code>` for an active partner tracked by link is remembered in a signed first-party `kc_ref` cookie for the program's window, counted on the partner's daily click counter (bots skipped, nothing about the visitor stored), and sent on to the same address without the parameter.\n    * Consent: the cookie is declared to the consent plugin as marketing. Without marketing consent the referral is held in the session for the visit (`consent_mode: session`) or not at all (`strict`), and a shopper who withdraws consent loses the cookie straight away.\n    * Last-click or first-click attribution, the link referral stamped on the order at every checkout attempt, and at payment the partner decided between link, partner coupon (coupon wins by default) and the merchant's own choice, with self-referrals and inactive partners flagged and not credited.\n    * Partner coupons: assign an existing core coupon to a partner, or make a new single code for them; every code stays an ordinary core coupon.\n    * Admin API for partners, applications, coupons, the order panel's referral (set, change or remove, written to the order's history) and click charts, behind the view and manage permissions.\n    * Account API for the signed-in partner: their status and code, their link, a link builder for any page of the store, their coupon links, their clicks, and the application to join the program.\n    * Tax details in the partner's account: a W-9 for US persons, a W-8BEN for individuals and a W-8BEN-E (signed PDF) for entities elsewhere, with a typed signature and certify box. KahunaCart collects and totals this information; it doesn't file or give tax advice.\n    * Full tax ID numbers, foreign tax IDs and dates of birth are stored only when the server has a `KAHUNACART_TAX_KEY` environment key, encrypted with it; without one a W-9 keeps the last four digits plus the signed PDF. Tax IDs are masked in every screen and API response.\n    * A Tax ID key field on the Tax settings tab says whether the key is set, how to set it in the root `.env`, and generates a new key that is shown once and saved nowhere.\n    * `bin\/plugin kahunacart-affiliates tax:rekey --from=<old key>` moves every encrypted value to the current key, and `tax:status` reports the key and how many forms need filling in again. A form the key can't open is shown as needing re-entry, never as garbage.\n    * Signed PDFs are checked by their first bytes, stored encrypted with `KAHUNACART_TAX_KEY` when it is set, kept under random names in `user\/data\/kahunacart\/affiliates-tax` or a folder outside the web root (`tax.documents_path`), and served only to admins with the tax permission.\n    * W-8 forms expire at the end of the third calendar year after signing; partners are told 60 days before (`onKahunaCartAffiliateTaxExpiring`) and the form is marked expired on the day (`onKahunaCartAffiliateTaxExpired`). Merchants can verify a form or ask for it again (`onKahunaCartAffiliateTaxRequested`).\n    * The payout tax gate follows `tax.require_before_payout` for the payout batch builder: nobody, US partners only, or every partner.\n    * Year-end report per calendar year in the store's time zone: paid totals per partner by method and currency, the form on file, and a 1099-NEC flag for W-9 partners at the threshold (other currencies listed, never converted). Exports: the IRS IRIS 1099-NEC CSV (full tax IDs only with the key), the full table CSV, and a statement PDF per partner. Every export is written to a history the admin can see.\n    * Erasing a partner after the retention period also deletes their tax form and its PDF.\n    * Payout batches: pick store credit, paid by hand, PayPal, or each partner's own choice (one batch per method) and a currency; the preview lists every partner with an approved balance and says why anyone is left out (below the minimum, a negative balance, no PayPal address or customer account, the method switched off, a currency PayPal cannot send, or the tax gate), and the merchant can untick anyone.\n    * Creating a batch works every amount out again inside one transaction from the commissions it locks, so a refund between preview and click is counted and two people building batches at once can never pay the same commission twice.\n    * Store credit posts each partner's payout to their credit account through the store credit ledger, once (a repeated run is absorbed by the item's own key, and a run cut off part way is finished by running it again or with Resume, paying only what is still pending), with an optional bonus percent for choosing credit, recorded apart on the item.\n    * Paid by hand: the batch waits while the merchant pays outside the store and marks items or the whole batch paid with an optional reference, or marks an item as not paid; CSV files for a PayPal Payouts upload, a Wise batch payment (Wise emails each partner for their bank details) and a plain one with the payout details.\n    * PayPal Payouts with the add-on's own client (works without PayPal at checkout), borrowing the PayPal payment plugin's keys when it is set to the same mode; one request per batch, kept before it goes, under ids made from the batch's own random reference so stores sharing a PayPal account never clash; a send that got no answer is sent again exactly as it was for up to 72 hours, a batch PayPal already has (or shows when polled) is taken only when every item matches what was sent, and when the store can no longer learn the outcome the batch says so, keeps every commission claimed, and the merchant marks each item paid or not paid.\n    * PayPal outcomes are polled every ten minutes: paid, unclaimed (the partner is reminded a week before PayPal's 30 days run out), returned and failed, with PayPal's reason kept; a failed or returned payout always hands its commissions back to the partner's balance, and a failed item is retried in a new batch, never sent again under its old id.\n    * PayPal connection check when the settings are saved and from a Check button on the PayPal tab, which says plainly when the PayPal account still needs Payouts switched on and links PayPal's page for it; new settings for the subject and message of PayPal's payout email.\n    * Admin API for payouts behind the Pay Affiliates permission (preview, create, run, resume, cancel a draft, mark paid, retry, poll now, CSV files, connection check, a partner's payout settings), and account API for a partner's own payouts and payout settings, with manual payout details encrypted at rest, shown masked to the partner and in the merchant's editor, and opened only in the plain CSV or through a separate reveal that is written to the log with who asked.\n    * Affiliates section in the KahunaCart admin with tabs for Overview, Affiliates, Applications, Commissions, Payouts, Tax, Creatives and Settings; every screen has its own address, so a reload, a pasted link or the Back button lands where it says, and tabs and buttons follow the view, manage, payouts and tax permissions.\n    * Overview: a needs-attention strip (applications waiting, partners due a payout, failed or unclaimed payouts, tax forms), clicks, referred orders and earnings for 30 days, where the money stands per currency, and the top partners.\n    * Partners: a searchable, sortable list with balances and tax form state, a form for a new partner tied to a customer with an optional coupon, and a partner's own record with details, rate overrides, coupons, how they're paid (stored bank details shown only on request), tax form, application, clicks chart, recent commissions and history, saved with one button and guarded against leaving with unsaved changes.\n    * Applications to approve or reject with a message, commissions with filters, bulk approve and reject, dispute release and signed adjustments, the payout builder with a preview of who is paid and who is left out and why, and a batch's own page to run, resume, mark paid, ask PayPal, settle an outcome-unknown PayPal batch item by item, retry failures and download the bulk files.\n    * Tax: the year-end report with the IRIS and full table files, export history, statement PDFs, and each partner's form to verify, ask for again or download.\n    * Creatives: banners from the site's media, text snippets and links for partners, with ordering, editing and switching off; new admin API for them under `\/kahunacart\/affiliates\/creatives`.\n    * An Affiliate panel beside an order (the partner credited, how, and the commission, with change and take off) and beside a customer (their partner record and since when).\n    * Dashboard alerts for applications waiting, failed payouts, PayPal Payouts switched off on the account, and tax forms that need attention, each opening the tab that deals with it.\n    * A new `\/kahunacart\/affiliates\/attention` endpoint gives the section its counts and the permissions held; commission and rate reads now accept the view or the manage permission, and the partner list can be filtered by customer.\n    * Affiliate tab in the customer account: while the program is open, a customer who isn't a partner gets the application form (website, where they'll share the store, how they'll promote it, payout method and PayPal address, the program terms and an agree box); after applying they see a thanks note, and a turned-down applicant one short line with no form. With the program closed only partners see the tab.\n    * Active partners get Overview (ready to pay, waiting, paid and earned per currency, clicks and referred orders, when the next payout comes), Links with a copy button and a builder for any page of the store, Coupons, Commissions and Payouts with paging, payout settings, Tax details and Banners and text. Paused partners keep their history, payout settings and tax form; closed partners keep their history. Nothing for sending people is shown to either.\n    * Tax details asks one question (US person, someone outside the US, or a business outside the US) to pick the W-9, W-8BEN or W-8BEN-E, shows what's on file masked, and says plainly when the store has asked for the form again, when it's about to run out or has run out, and, without a tax ID key, that only the last four digits are kept with the signed PDF. A refused form keeps the partner's answers, never their tax ID or date of birth.\n    * Every portal form is a plain POST with the store's nonce, so it works without JavaScript; the link builder and copy buttons add to that when it runs. The styling uses only KahunaCart's `--kc-*` tokens, follows light and dark, works at phone width and is skipped when `builtin_css` is off.\n    * One account resolver (`Account\\AccountCustomer`) finds the signed-in customer for the tracking, commission, payout and tax account APIs and the tab, refuses a customer merged into another, and only creates a customer record when someone applies.\n    * Partner emails on the Notifications screen, each one switchable and re-pointable: application received, approved, rejected, new commission (off by default), payout sent, payout failed, unclaimed reminder, tax form requested, expiring and expired. They're queued as jobs, read what's true when they go, and go through the store's mailer onto its email log.\n    * Owner messages through the store's channels: a new application, and a payout batch finished, with failed and unclaimed payouts called out.\n    * Monthly statement (`notifications.statements`) for partners with no customer account who had commissions or payouts last month, booked once in the first week of the store's month.\n    * Public program block with the program's terms and an Apply button, for any page: the `[kahunacart-affiliate-program]` shortcode, the `kahunacart_affiliate_program()` Twig function, or the `partials\/kahunacart-affiliates\/program.html.twig` partial."}}},"kahunacart-point-of-sale":{"name":"KahunaCart Point of Sale","version":"1.0.2","description":"A till on your own site for KahunaCart. Sell at a counter with cash and card readers, run shifts and cash counts, print receipts and take returns","description_html":"<p>A till on your own site for KahunaCart. Sell at a counter with cash and card readers, run shifts and cash counts, print receipts and take returns<\/p>","description_plain":"A till on your own site for KahunaCart. Sell at a counter with cash and card readers, run shifts and cash counts, print receipts and take returns","icon":"cash-register","author":{"name":"Trilby Media, LLC","email":"hello@trilby.media"},"dependencies":[{"name":"grav","version":">=2.0.0"},{"name":"kahunacart","version":">=1.6.5"}],"compatibility":{"grav":["2.0"]},"homepage":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/add-ons\/point-of-sale","docs":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/docs\/point-of-sale\/overview","keywords":"grav, premium, commerce, ecommerce, pos, point of sale, till, cash register, card reader, kahunacart","bugs":"https:\/\/github.com\/getgrav\/grav-premium-issues\/labels\/kahunacart-point-of-sale","license":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/license","tag_name":"1.0.2","date":"2026-10-26T03:50:17Z","release_type":"stable","zipball_url":"https:\/\/getgrav.org\/download\/plugins\/kahunacart-point-of-sale\/1.0.2","repository":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/add-ons\/point-of-sale","premium":{"vendor":"KahunaCart","validator":"kahunacart","license_product":"kahunacart-point-of-sale","checkout_url":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/shop\/product\/kahunacart-point-of-sale","button":"Buy Now","filename":"grav-plugin-kahunacart-point-of-sale"},"slug":"kahunacart-point-of-sale","install_path":"user\/plugins\/kahunacart-point-of-sale","content_sha256":"0bb208c1d4009a2d53c9d4b52c93603c0817a0a20bbd67b38bb011e477956ea4","changelog":{"1.0.2":{"date":"10\/25\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Sell a product's add-ons at the counter.** A product with add-ons now asks for them before its line goes in, on one sheet: dropdowns, pills and swatches as big buttons, text boxes, text areas and tick boxes, each choice priced in the sale's currency, and Add to sale waits for the required ones. A second product with add-ons tapped or scanned while a picker is open waits its turn and opens next. Until now the till added such a product without its add-ons, and couldn't add one with a required add-on at all. A choice that sells another product (a lamp's \"Add a warm white LED tealight\") shows what the sale will charge for it (the customer's own price list, quantity tiers, sales), is grayed out with the reason when it can't be had, and puts the product in the sale as a line of its own, as the product page does. When it can't be added after all, the lamp goes in without it and the till says so: \"LED tealight is out of stock, so it wasn't added.\" The sale lists each line's answers the way the receipt names them (\"Need an LED?: Warm white\"), and a line an add-on sold says which product it came with. A product without add-ons still adds in one tap. Needs KahunaCart 1.6.5\n1. [](#improved)\n    * **The emailed till receipt uses your email colors.** It was drawn in fixed grays; it now takes its background, card, text and line colors from KahunaCart's email settings (Settings \u203a Notifications \u203a Customer Emails), like every other email the store sends. Needs KahunaCart 1.6.5\n"},"1.0.1":{"date":"10\/22\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **Ships its license.** A `LICENSE` file now comes with the plugin, and the blueprint links to the KahunaCart License at kahunacart.com\/license instead of the Grav Premium license\n"},"1.0.0":{"date":"10\/14\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **A till on your own site.** `\/pos` sells at a counter from a tablet, a laptop or a phone, behind the site's own sign-in. Scan or tap products, attach a customer, take cash with the change worked out, take cards on a card reader, split a payment, send a pay link as a QR code, print or email the receipt, and take things back by scanning the order number. Every sale is an ordinary KahunaCart order on the same stock, tax, coupons and customers as the online shop. Add it to a tablet's home screen and it opens full screen\n    * **Card readers** through KahunaCart's card reader drivers: Stripe Terminal (Stripe 1.2.10), Square Terminal (Square 1.1.7) and SumUp Solo (SumUp 1.1.6). A payment the till can't confirm shows as checking until the platform answers, and a manager can give up on it\n    * **Your store's look on the till.** A brand colour, from which the till works out its buttons, the text on them (kept readable to WCAG AA), the selected tint and a dark-mode version; a logo, with an optional one for dark mode, on the sign-in and Who's serving? screens and in the top bar; and light, dark or follow the device, set before the page paints. The home-screen icon and the browser's bar colour follow the colour\n    * **Registers and paired devices.** A register sells at one of the store's pickup locations, with its own card reader and receipt setting. A manager pairs a browser to it once with a PIN, and a lost tablet is revoked from the admin\n    * **Staff PINs.** The tablet stays signed in as one account and the person serving signs in with a PIN. Every action is checked against that person's own permissions. Wrong PINs lock the person, then the device, and the till locks itself after a few idle minutes\n    * **Manager overrides.** A discount over the limit, a return by somebody without refunds, or pairing a browser asks for a manager's name and PIN on the spot, and the override is written on the order\n    * **Shifts.** Open with a float, put cash in or take it out with a reason, and close by counting the drawer note by note in the store's currency. The X and Z reports give sales, tenders, refunds, discounts, tax, sales per person, payments on earlier orders and the cash expected. A drawer that is out by more than the store's limit sends the owner a message\n    * **Take a payment on an earlier order.** Find an order by number, email or name and take what it still owes at the till: a booking's balance, or an order placed online and paid in the shop. The Bookings desk's **Take payment** button opens the same screen, and the Paid screen offers **Book next visit**\n    * **A Point of sale section in the KahunaCart admin** with Registers, Staff, Favourites, Shifts, Reports (by register, staff, location or tender, with CSV) and the settings. A counter sale's order shows its register, who served and its shift, and the manager's routes are also MCP tools\n    * **No double charges.** Charging and tendering carry an idempotency key, so a double tap or a retried request records one order and one payment, and two tablets opening a shift on one register leave one open"}}},"kahunacart-mollie":{"name":"KahunaCart Mollie","version":"1.1.8","description":"Mollie payments for KahunaCart \u2014 hosted checkout, webhooks, refunds, and manual capture","description_html":"<p>Mollie payments for KahunaCart \u2014 hosted checkout, webhooks, refunds, and manual capture<\/p>","description_plain":"Mollie payments for KahunaCart \u2014 hosted checkout, webhooks, refunds, and manual capture","icon":"credit-card","author":{"name":"Trilby Media, LLC","email":"hello@trilby.media"},"dependencies":[{"name":"grav","version":">=2.0.0"},{"name":"kahunacart","version":">=1.4.5"}],"compatibility":{"grav":["2.0"]},"homepage":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com","docs":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/docs\/providers\/mollie","keywords":"grav, premium, commerce, ecommerce, payments, mollie, checkout, ideal, twint","bugs":"https:\/\/github.com\/getgrav\/grav-premium-issues\/labels\/kahunacart-mollie","license":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/license","tag_name":"1.1.8","date":"2026-10-22T23:33:21Z","release_type":"stable","zipball_url":"https:\/\/getgrav.org\/download\/plugins\/kahunacart-mollie\/1.1.8","repository":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com","premium":{"vendor":"KahunaCart","validator":"kahunacart","license_product":"kahunacart-mollie","checkout_url":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/shop\/product\/kahunacart-mollie","button":"Buy Now","filename":"grav-plugin-kahunacart-mollie"},"slug":"kahunacart-mollie","install_path":"user\/plugins\/kahunacart-mollie","content_sha256":"04bdd79d0c42fbef719e5d0bee4f646782057b05e500804b06a8e096ce7b917f","changelog":{"1.1.8":{"date":"10\/22\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **Ships its license.** A `LICENSE` file now comes with the plugin, and the blueprint links to the KahunaCart License at kahunacart.com\/license instead of the Grav Premium license\n"},"1.1.7":{"date":"10\/13\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **A chargeback on a Mollie payment is written on the order and tells you.** The order's history says \"Dispute opened at Mollie\" with the amount (and the bank's reason code on SEPA Direct Debit), and KahunaCart sends its Dispute opened message to the channels you have ticked. Affiliates holds the partner's commission on it. The payment is still recorded as a reversal as before, the order itself is not changed, and each chargeback is reported once however many later notifications arrive for the payment, including one whose payment is still pending\n    * **You are told when the rest of a hold was not released.** After a partial capture the store asks Mollie to let go of the rest of the hold. When Mollie refuses, or the store cannot read the payment back to check, **Part of a Mollie hold was not released** goes to your notification channels with the order number, what may still be held and a link to the order, once per payment\n"},"1.1.6":{"date":"10\/08\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Check connection.** The Payments page can now check a Mollie key: whether it is a test or live key, which website profile it belongs to, and which methods Mollie will offer in each currency the store sells in, for example `CHF: TWINT, Card`. It fails when nothing on the profile takes the store's base currency, so a merchant finds out before a shopper does. Read-only calls only\n1. [](#improved)\n    * Every webhook the driver refuses or ignores now says why in the webhook log (`mollie_auth`, `mollie_unavailable`, `mollie_rate_limited`, `mismatched_payment`, `bad_id`, `unknown_payment`, `status_open` and so on) and names the payment, instead of every Mollie failure reading \"signature or payload rejected\" for a provider that signs nothing\n    * A refund Mollie refuses because it has less left than the store thinks (usually a refund made in the Mollie Dashboard) says how much Mollie has left and to record the outside refund, and raises the store's refund-balance notice. Mollie's one-hour rule against two refunds of the same amount on one payment is explained in a sentence instead of \"A duplicate refund has been detected\". No message anybody reads carries a schema field name any more\n    * A shopper who let Mollie's page time out or left it is told so plainly, not that their payment was declined, and a shopper never reads Mollie's or curl's own text at checkout. The detail goes to the Grav log\n    * Currency codes on webhook payloads are uppercased\n    * README and Instructions: all six capabilities listed, Klarna plainly not offered in this release (Mollie needs order lines and a billing address the driver does not send yet), and TWINT and pay-by-bank on a manual-capture store come back as an ordinary paid sale rather than being refused\n1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **Refunds that finish together are all recorded.** Mollie settles refunds on a payment in batches and its notification names only the payment, so two line refunds made a second apart finished together and the store only ever recorded the newer one; the older one stayed pending for ever, and an older refund made in the Mollie Dashboard was never recorded at all. Every finished refund is now reported on every notification, oldest first, and the store's reference matching makes repeats harmless. **Needs KahunaCart 1.4.5**\n    * **A captured order can be refunded.** A capture reported Mollie's `cpt_\u2026` id, which the refund call then refused, so every refund of an order captured from the admin failed with \"That transaction has no Mollie payment to refund against\"\n    * **A partial capture releases the rest of the hold.** Mollie kept the remainder of a partly captured Klarna hold on the customer's account for 28 days. The driver now asks Mollie to release it after any capture for less than the held amount, and if Mollie refuses, the capture still stands and the merchant is told the remainder is still held\n    * **A held payment is recorded at what Mollie captured,** not at the amount that was held, when Mollie later reports it paid\n    * **A hold released at Mollie closes the order.** A hold cancelled in the Mollie Dashboard or left to lapse was read as a shopper walking away, because Mollie drops `authorizedAt` on a cancel, and the order sat at `authorized` with Capture and Void buttons Mollie refused. Holds are now recognised from the payment's own `captureMode`, never from the store's current setting, so switching the store back to automatic no longer strands a hold either\n    * **Chargebacks reach the store** as a reversal of the settled payment: a line in the order's history and a notice for the merchant, without completing the order again. A finished refund no longer hides one\n"},"1.1.5":{"date":"10\/04\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Mollie is no longer offered for a currency it cannot take.** The driver now tells KahunaCart the 28 currencies on Mollie's multicurrency page, so checkout and the pay link leave Mollie out of an order in KWD, INR, CLP or any other currency off that page instead of letting the shopper pick it and fail on Mollie's own page. The list is every currency any Mollie payment method takes. Which of them your profile can take depends on the methods you have switched on, and one that none of yours takes still fails at Mollie as before. **Needs the base plugin at 1.4.0**, which is where the question is asked\n1. [](#improved)\n    * A refund the driver submits is reported with the currency Mollie says it is in, so core can compare it with the order's\n    * The README and the Instructions tab say which currencies Mollie takes and which of them need PayPal switched on\n"},"1.1.4":{"date":"09\/25\/2026","content":"1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **The download no longer carries PHPUnit.** Every release until now committed a development `vendor\/`: PHPUnit and about thirty packages only the test suite uses, some 1,700 files in a plugin that needs no Composer package at run time. `vendor\/` is now a `--no-dev` install holding just the autoloader, and `psr\/http-message` is declared under `replace` because Grav core already supplies it. A test fails if a development install is ever committed again\n"},"1.1.3":{"date":"09\/23\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * Composer's installed-package record carries the plugin's current package name, `kahunacart\/grav-plugin-kahunacart-mollie`, instead of the old `trilbymedia\/grav-plugin-kahunacart-mollie`\n"},"1.1.2":{"date":"09\/10\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * A payment that names a Mollie customer reports the id back to the store, so it is on record the next time somebody needs it. Nothing new goes out and no customers are created: a one-off Mollie payment has no customer object behind it, Mollie's customers are for recurring billing, and opening one per checkout would fill the merchant's dashboard with records nothing ever charges again\n"},"1.1.1":{"date":"09\/08\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * An **Instructions** tab in the plugin settings, after General, that walks a merchant through setup in order: the account, which credential to copy and from where, saving it, why there is no webhook to register, test cards, and what to check afterwards\n\n1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **A refund event named the payment on `payment_id`, and the base plugin only reads `payment_ref`.** The value was always right, so this cost nothing on a refund the store submitted itself \u2014 that one is matched by its own refund id. It cost the other route entirely: a refund issued in the Mollie Dashboard arrives with no pending row to confirm, and the base plugin can only open one from the payment the refund belongs to, which it was never handed. `payment_ref` is now the name. The list the base reads is `payment_ref`, `order_id`, `charge_id`, `charge`, and it is closed \u2014 drivers are corrected rather than the list grown\n"},"1.1.0":{"date":"09\/01\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * Two-step capture. `capture_mode: manual` sends `captureMode: manual` on the payment, so the customer's payment stops at Mollie's `authorized` status and the order stands at `authorized` until the merchant presses Capture or Void. `MollieProvider` implements `AuthorizeCaptureInterface`: `capture()` posts to `\/v2\/payments\/{id}\/captures` with an idempotency key, and `void()` cancels the payment, because Mollie has no void endpoint \u2014 releasing an authorization *is* cancelling it\n    * `authorized` and `canceled` are read against the declared mode, since Mollie reuses the payment's own status rather than sending an event. On a manual store `authorized` is the hold, and a `canceled` payment carrying an `authorizedAt` is the release \u2014 this store's own Void, a cancellation in the Mollie Dashboard, or an authorization Mollie let expire. A `canceled` payment with no `authorizedAt` is still a customer who walked away, and an automatic store keeps exactly the reading it always had\n    * The README says plainly what manual capture costs: it has to be enabled on the Mollie account, and only some methods can honour it \u2014 cards and Klarna can, iDEAL and Bancontact and the other bank-transfer methods settle immediately and cannot be held. Mollie refuses a payment it cannot hold, and that refusal reaches the merchant in Mollie's own words rather than in a sentence this plugin invented\n    * `completeAuthorize()` asks Mollie nothing and answers \"not yet\", because Mollie appends no payment id and no status to the redirect URL and says the redirect is not a signal. It is a real method rather than an omission: a driver that guessed the state from the customer's return is the failure the contract exists to prevent\n    * A capture Mollie answers `pending` is reported as the capture. The contract has no answer between captured and failed, and telling a merchant their capture failed when it did not is the worse of the two mistakes \u2014 the payment's own webhook settles the row when Mollie finishes\n    * `encryptedSettings()` names the API key, so KahunaCart stores it encrypted against a key that lives in the store's database and not in any file. It is the only credential this plugin holds \u2014 there is no webhook secret beside it, because Mollie does not sign its webhooks at all\n    * A reference that is not a Mollie payment id is refused before any call is made, for a capture and a void as it already was for a refund \u2014 an id that does not match the format is a probe, not a payment, and it never reaches a request path\n\n1. [](#improved)\n    * The README says what this driver does not claim. Mollie can keep a payment method through customers and mandates and it has Apple Pay, and neither `Tokenization` nor `ExpressPay` is declared because neither is implemented \u2014 KahunaCart draws its admin controls from these flags, so a flag claiming something that is not there would put a button in the admin that could never work\n"},"0.1.0":{"date":"08\/19\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * Initial release: first-party Mollie provider for KahunaCart\n    * Hosted checkout on Payments API v2 \u2014 `initiatePayment()` creates a Mollie payment and redirects to `_links.checkout`, with the order hash and transaction hash carried in the payment's metadata\n    * Verify-by-fetch webhooks, which is Mollie's documented model: the posted body is mined for a single `id=tr_\u2026`, the id's format is checked before it reaches a URL, and every fact acted on comes back from an authenticated `GET \/v2\/payments\/{id}?embed=refunds`. Nothing in the request body is ever trusted\n    * Deliberate webhook failure paths \u2014 a body with no usable id is a 400, a well-formed id Mollie has never issued is answered 200 so the status code cannot be used to probe which ids exist, and a rejected key or unreachable API is a 400 that leaves Mollie to redeliver\n    * Status mapping: `paid` completes the order, `failed`, `canceled`, and `expired` fail it with Mollie's own `statusReason` where there is one, and `open`, `pending`, and `authorized` are ignored\n    * Refund reconstruction: Mollie has no refund webhook, so a refund state change re-fires the payment's own webhook with nothing to say which refund moved \u2014 the plugin embeds the refunds and reports the most recently created one that has *finished*, leaving `queued`, `pending`, and `processing` alone until the refund has actually resolved\n    * Async refund lifecycle: a refund Mollie answers `queued` or `pending` is reported as pending and settled later by the payment's own webhook, keyed on the same `re_\u2026` reference the refund call returned \u2014 `refunded` confirms it, and `failed` or `canceled` releases the held amount for a retry. Cancellation is the one transition Mollie does not announce, so a canceled refund is released the next time any notification arrives for that payment\n    * Full and partial refunds through `\/v2\/payments\/{id}\/refunds`, including refunds issued in Mollie's dashboard, which arrive with the order hash and amount the ledger needs to record a credit it never initiated\n    * `Idempotency-Key` on payment creation, derived from the transaction hash, so a double-submitted checkout returns the same Mollie payment rather than creating a second one \u2014 and deliberately no key on refunds, where a replayed response would read as a second refund that never happened\n    * No mode toggle: a Mollie API key is prefixed `test_` or `live_` and the key alone decides, so the provider registers on the key and nothing can contradict it\n    * Exact integer and string conversion between minor units and Mollie's decimal amounts at each currency's own precision \u2014 EUR as `\"10.00\"`, JPY and ISK as `\"1000\"` \u2014 with HUF and TWD following Mollie's two-decimal handling, and unrecognised formats rejected rather than rounded\n    * Unit test suite covering webhook verify-by-fetch and every reject path, status and refund mapping, payment and refund body building, decimal amounts across exponents, idempotent key derivation, and transport failure"}}},"kahunacart-paddle":{"name":"KahunaCart Paddle","version":"1.1.9","description":"Paddle Billing payments for KahunaCart \u2014 merchant-of-record hosted checkout, webhooks, and refunds","description_html":"<p>Paddle Billing payments for KahunaCart \u2014 merchant-of-record hosted checkout, webhooks, and refunds<\/p>","description_plain":"Paddle Billing payments for KahunaCart \u2014 merchant-of-record hosted checkout, webhooks, and refunds","icon":"credit-card","author":{"name":"Trilby Media, LLC","email":"hello@trilby.media"},"dependencies":[{"name":"grav","version":">=2.0.0"},{"name":"kahunacart","version":">=1.5.18"}],"compatibility":{"grav":["2.0"]},"homepage":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com","docs":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/docs\/providers\/paddle","keywords":"grav, premium, commerce, ecommerce, payments, paddle, checkout, merchant-of-record","bugs":"https:\/\/github.com\/getgrav\/grav-premium-issues\/labels\/kahunacart-paddle","license":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/license","tag_name":"1.1.9","date":"2026-10-22T23:33:16Z","release_type":"stable","zipball_url":"https:\/\/getgrav.org\/download\/plugins\/kahunacart-paddle\/1.1.9","repository":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com","premium":{"vendor":"KahunaCart","validator":"kahunacart","license_product":"kahunacart-paddle","checkout_url":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/shop\/product\/kahunacart-paddle","button":"Buy Now","filename":"grav-plugin-kahunacart-paddle"},"slug":"kahunacart-paddle","install_path":"user\/plugins\/kahunacart-paddle","content_sha256":"4f3539797082f9c9c4683de9589e78de9899a692993c701964e4a6fd2dfffcaa","changelog":{"1.1.9":{"date":"10\/22\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **Ships its license.** A `LICENSE` file now comes with the plugin, and the blueprint links to the KahunaCart License at kahunacart.com\/license instead of the Grav Premium license\n"},"1.1.8":{"date":"10\/19\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Check connection says whether Paddle's notification destination is right.** After the key checks out, the driver looks up the destination at this store's address, changing nothing, and the Payments card warns when there is none, when it is switched off, when it is missing events the driver handles, or when its secret key is not the one stored here. **Set up webhook** fixes each of these\n2. [](#improved)\n    * Needs KahunaCart 1.5.18\n"},"1.1.7":{"date":"10\/13\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **The plugin serves the payment page itself.** Paddle has no hosted payment page for websites, so every store had to build a page that loads Paddle.js and paste its client-side token into a template. Paste the token into the new **Client-side Token** setting instead, and the plugin answers `{store route}\/paddle\/pay`, sends every transaction there, opens Paddle's checkout on it, and takes the shopper to their receipt when they have paid (or back to the checkout if they close it). No default payment link is needed in Paddle. A store with its own page keeps it by leaving the token empty, and **Payment Link Override** still wins\n    * **Paddle sets up its own webhook.** A **Set up webhook** button creates this store's notification destination in Paddle with the five events the plugin handles and saves its secret. A destination already at this address is adopted rather than duplicated, switched back on and given any missing events, so pressing twice is safe. **Reset webhook** rotates the secret. Other destinations on the account are never touched, and only the same path on another host is reported as stale\n    * **Check connection.** Tests the API key with one read, catches a sandbox key or token in live mode and the other way round before any request, and says whether the webhook secret and the payment page are in place\n1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **Refunds give back Paddle's tax too.** Paddle adds its tax on top, and its refund amounts include it, but the store's amounts went over unscaled: refunding a $24.00 order that Paddle charged $25.24 in two parts gave the customer back $24.00 and left $1.24 with Paddle. The net amount is now scaled by the transaction's own ratio, and the whole net is sent as a full refund\n    * **The payment is recorded at the store's net.** The completed payment reported Paddle's tax-inclusive total as its amount, so the ledger, balances and reports counted Paddle's tax as the store's takings. It now reports the net, with Paddle's tax and the card total beside it as core 1.5.1 reads them for receipts, and refunds do the same\n    * A refund refused because Paddle is still reviewing an earlier one says to wait instead of quoting Paddle's error\n1. [](#improved)\n    * **Out of testing.** Checkout on the plugin's own payment page, the webhook, partial and full refunds with Paddle's tax, and Check connection were all run end to end against Paddle's sandbox, so this is the first stable release\n    * The Instructions tab and README describe the new setup: API key, client-side token, domain approval, Check connection, Set up webhook. They no longer claim sandbox accepts any domain; it needs the same website approval as live, which sandbox grants at once\n"},"1.1.6":{"date":"10\/13\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **A chargeback at Paddle is written on the order and tells you.** When a customer's bank charges a payment back, the order's history says \"Dispute opened at Paddle\" with the amount, and KahunaCart sends its Dispute opened message to the channels you have ticked. Affiliates holds the partner's commission on it. The order itself is not changed, and a chargeback is reported once however often Paddle sends the created and updated events for it, including one that arrives before its payment is recorded. Nothing new to subscribe to in Paddle\n"},"1.1.5":{"date":"10\/04\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Paddle is only offered for the currencies Paddle takes.** On a store that sells in more than one currency, checkout and the pay link leave Paddle out for an order in a currency outside its 33, instead of letting the shopper pick it and fail at the last step. The list is the one the plugin already carried, checked against Paddle's supported currencies page on 2026-10-01. Needs KahunaCart 1.4.0.\n    * The README and the settings screen's Instructions tab say which currencies Paddle takes.\n"},"1.1.4":{"date":"09\/25\/2026","content":"1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **The download no longer carries PHPUnit.** Every release until now committed a development `vendor\/`: PHPUnit and about thirty packages only the test suite uses, some 1,700 files in a plugin that needs no Composer package at run time. `vendor\/` is now a `--no-dev` install holding just the autoloader, and `psr\/http-message` is declared under `replace` because Grav core already supplies it. A test fails if a development install is ever committed again\n"},"1.1.3":{"date":"09\/23\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * Composer's installed-package record carries the plugin's current package name, `kahunacart\/grav-plugin-kahunacart-paddle`, instead of the old `trilbymedia\/grav-plugin-kahunacart-paddle`\n"},"1.1.2":{"date":"09\/10\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * Paddle is told which customer it is looking at. A transaction for somebody Paddle has already billed now carries its own customer id, so the checkout opens with the address and country Paddle holds instead of asking for them again, and the id Paddle names on the completed transaction goes back to the store so it is there for the next order. An id Paddle no longer recognises, after a move between the sandbox and production or after the customer was archived, is dropped and the transaction goes again without it\n"},"1.1.1":{"date":"09\/08\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * An **Instructions** tab in the plugin settings, after General, that walks a merchant through setup in order: the account or sandbox, which credential to copy and from where, saving it, the webhook (button or manual steps with the endpoint URL and the events), test cards or sandbox accounts, and what to check afterwards\n"},"1.1.0":{"date":"09\/01\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * Initial release: first-party Paddle Billing provider for KahunaCart, targeting the current platform rather than Paddle Classic\n    * Hosted redirect flow \u2014 `initiatePayment()` creates a Paddle transaction and redirects to the checkout payment link, with the order hash and transaction hash on the transaction's `custom_data`\n    * Non-catalog pricing: the whole cart is one ad-hoc item with an inline price wrapping an inline product, so any cart checks out with an empty Paddle catalog \u2014 no carrier product, no product mapping, no sync\n    * `MerchantOfRecord` capability: Paddle computes and remits tax at checkout, so KahunaCart's own tax estimate steps aside\n    * Self-contained webhook verification \u2014 HMAC-SHA256 over `{ts}:{raw body}` against the notification destination secret, verified on the raw bytes before any decoding, with a configurable replay window, constant-time comparison, and support for the multiple `h1` signatures Paddle reserves for secret rotation\n    * Event mapping for `transaction.completed` and `transaction.paid` (both complete the order, deduped by the ledger), `transaction.payment_failed` (with Paddle's decline code as the reason), and `adjustment.created` \/ `adjustment.updated`\n    * Full and partial refunds through the adjustments API, allocating a partial amount across the transaction's `txnitm_` line items and sending a whole-total refund as `type: full`\n    * Refund approval handled honestly: a `pending_approval` adjustment is reported as pending rather than settled, and only an approved refund is ever recorded as money returned \u2014 credits and chargebacks are not\n    * Async refund lifecycle: a refund left pending in Paddle's review holds its amount against the order's refundable balance until `adjustment.updated` settles it under the same `adj_\u2026` reference the refund call returned \u2014 `approved` confirms it, `rejected` releases it for a retry, and `reversed` is answered too so a contradiction of an already-confirmed refund reaches the log instead of vanishing\n    * Refunds issued in Paddle's own dashboard are traced back through the originating transaction, so they land on the right KahunaCart order\n    * Sandbox\/live toggle selecting the API root, with the provider registering only when enabled and an API key is set\n    * Configurable customer-facing label, ad-hoc product name, Paddle tax category, payment link override, and signature tolerance\n    * Amounts pass between KahunaCart and Paddle in minor units with no conversion, guarded by Paddle's own currency table \u2014 CLP, JPY, KRW, and VND carry no minor unit, no three-decimal currency is supported, and a currency Paddle does not settle in is refused before the request goes out\n    * Unit test suite of 242 tests covering signature verification, the behavior matrix, transaction creation, webhook event mapping, refund mapping, amount and exponent handling, the contract declarations, and transport-failure degradation\n    * `captureMode()` is declared explicitly as `Automatic`, and there is deliberately no setting to change it. Everything Paddle Billing does to a transaction is list it, create it, read it, preview it, update it, invoice it and revise the customer on it \u2014 no authorisation resource, no capture call, no void call \u2014 and the charge happens on Paddle's own checkout, so by the time a transaction reads `completed` the money has moved and the tax has been computed and remitted against it. `Authorize`, `Capture` and `Void` are undeclared with it, so KahunaCart never draws a Capture or Void button on a Paddle order\n    * Paddle's own `collection_mode: manual` is explicitly *not* read as a capture mode, and a test asserts it is neither sent nor read. It is a different mechanism with an unfortunately similar name \u2014 bill the customer by invoice rather than collect at checkout \u2014 and it moves when the customer pays rather than when the merchant takes money already committed. Deriving `CaptureMode::Manual` from it would tell the base plugin to wait for an `authorize()` this driver has no endpoint to implement, and every checkout would stall\n    * `encryptedSettings()` names the API key and the webhook signing secret, so KahunaCart stores both encrypted against a key that lives in the store's database rather than in any file. The key can move money out of the account and the secret would let anyone holding it sign a notification this plugin cannot tell from a real one. A value written in plaintext before this reads correctly and is rewritten encrypted on the next save, with nothing for a merchant to do. `payment_link` is left in the clear on purpose: it is a URL on the merchant's own approved domain, Paddle prints it into its own dashboard, every customer who checks out sees it, and a merchant has to compare it against Paddle's Checkout settings page\n    * The README says plainly what this driver does not claim. Paddle keeps a customer's card for the subscriptions it bills itself, but exposes no vault to store a method against and no way to charge one off-session; its overlay checkout takes Apple Pay and Google Pay, but only once Paddle.js has opened it on the merchant's payment link after the redirect, so there is no wallet button to mount on a KahunaCart cart"}}},"kahunacart-lemonsqueezy":{"name":"KahunaCart Lemon Squeezy","version":"1.1.8","description":"Lemon Squeezy payments for KahunaCart \u2014 hosted checkout, merchant of record tax, webhooks, and refunds","description_html":"<p>Lemon Squeezy payments for KahunaCart \u2014 hosted checkout, merchant of record tax, webhooks, and refunds<\/p>","description_plain":"Lemon Squeezy payments for KahunaCart \u2014 hosted checkout, merchant of record tax, webhooks, and refunds","icon":"lemon-o","author":{"name":"Trilby Media, LLC","email":"hello@trilby.media"},"dependencies":[{"name":"grav","version":">=2.0.0"},{"name":"kahunacart","version":">=1.5.18"}],"compatibility":{"grav":["2.0"]},"homepage":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com","docs":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/docs\/providers\/lemonsqueezy","keywords":"grav, premium, commerce, ecommerce, payments, lemonsqueezy, checkout, merchant-of-record","bugs":"https:\/\/github.com\/getgrav\/grav-premium-issues\/labels\/kahunacart-lemonsqueezy","license":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/license","tag_name":"1.1.8","date":"2026-10-22T23:33:27Z","release_type":"stable","zipball_url":"https:\/\/getgrav.org\/download\/plugins\/kahunacart-lemonsqueezy\/1.1.8","repository":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com","premium":{"vendor":"KahunaCart","validator":"kahunacart","license_product":"kahunacart-lemonsqueezy","checkout_url":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/shop\/product\/kahunacart-lemonsqueezy","button":"Buy Now","filename":"grav-plugin-kahunacart-lemonsqueezy"},"slug":"kahunacart-lemonsqueezy","install_path":"user\/plugins\/kahunacart-lemonsqueezy","content_sha256":"f5856005311c2604ec7c9d275cd539a971c2ea590289dd50fefcb373477865cf","changelog":{"1.1.8":{"date":"10\/22\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **Ships its license.** A `LICENSE` file now comes with the plugin, and the blueprint links to the KahunaCart License at kahunacart.com\/license instead of the Grav Premium license\n"},"1.1.7":{"date":"10\/19\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Check connection says whether Lemon Squeezy's webhook is right.** After the key checks out, the driver looks up the webhook at this store's address in the current mode, changing nothing, and the Payments card warns when there is none or when it is missing events the driver handles. **Set up webhook** fixes both\n2. [](#improved)\n    * Needs KahunaCart 1.5.18\n"},"1.1.6":{"date":"10\/13\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Lemon Squeezy sets up its own webhook.** A **Set up webhook** button registers this store's webhook in the mode the plugin is in, with the two events it handles and a signing secret it generates and saves. Lemon Squeezy never shows a secret twice, so a webhook already at this address is adopted with a fresh secret and any missing events rather than duplicated. **Reset webhook** replaces it. Other webhooks on the store are never touched\n    * **Check connection** finds the store from the API key, lists your products with their variant ids when no Carrier Variant ID is set, and checks that the carrier is published and in the same mode as the key\n    * **Store ID can be left empty.** The plugin uses the one store the API key can see, which is nearly every account; several stores are listed by name and id. The plugin now registers as soon as the API key is saved, and is offered at checkout once the carrier variant is set\n1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **The payment is recorded at the store's net.** The completed payment reported Lemon Squeezy's tax-inclusive total as its amount, so the ledger counted Lemon Squeezy's tax as the store's takings. It now reports the net, with the tax and the card total beside it as core 1.5.1 reads them for receipts, and refunds report the same way\n    * **Partial refunds give back their share of the tax.** A partial amount went out unscaled, so two partials making up the whole order left the tax with Lemon Squeezy. They are now scaled by the order's own ratio\n    * **The API key is encrypted on the first save.** Core only knew to encrypt the key once the provider was registered, which needed the store and variant too, so a key pasted first was saved in plain text. The plugin now declares its secrets up front\n1. [](#improved)\n    * **Out of testing.** Check connection, Set up webhook, checkout, the webhook, partial and full refunds, and forged webhooks were run end to end against Lemon Squeezy's test mode, so this is the first stable release\n    * The Instructions tab and README describe the shorter setup\n"},"1.1.5":{"date":"10\/04\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Lemon Squeezy is only offered for an order in the store's currency.** On a store that sells in more than one currency, checkout and the pay link leave Lemon Squeezy out for an order in any other currency than the **Store Currency** saved in the settings, instead of letting the shopper pick it and fail at the last step. The settings, the instructions and the README now say to fill in Store Currency on a multi-currency store: while it is empty Lemon Squeezy is offered for every currency, and a mismatch is still refused when the shopper pays, as before. The payments a Lemon Squeezy store reports already carried their currency. Needs KahunaCart 1.4.0.\n"},"1.1.4":{"date":"09\/25\/2026","content":"1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **The download no longer carries PHPUnit.** Every release until now committed a development `vendor\/`: PHPUnit and about thirty packages only the test suite uses, some 1,700 files in a plugin that needs no Composer package at run time. `vendor\/` is now a `--no-dev` install holding just the autoloader, and `psr\/http-message` is declared under `replace` because Grav core already supplies it. A test fails if a development install is ever committed again\n"},"1.1.3":{"date":"09\/23\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * Composer's installed-package record carries the plugin's current package name, `kahunacart\/grav-plugin-kahunacart-lemonsqueezy`, instead of the old `trilbymedia\/grav-plugin-kahunacart-lemonsqueezy`\n"},"1.1.2":{"date":"09\/10\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * The paid order reports Lemon Squeezy's own customer id back to the store, so its record of who this shopper is on the platform is complete. Nothing new goes out: Lemon Squeezy's checkout has no parameter for an existing customer and it keys customers by email itself, so a shopper who buys twice was already one customer on its side\n"},"1.1.1":{"date":"09\/08\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * An **Instructions** tab in the plugin settings, after General, that walks a merchant through setup in order: the account or sandbox, which credential to copy and from where, saving it, the webhook (button or manual steps with the endpoint URL and the events), test cards or sandbox accounts, and what to check afterwards\n"},"1.1.0":{"date":"09\/01\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * Initial release: first-party Lemon Squeezy provider for KahunaCart\n    * Hosted checkout flow \u2014 `initiatePayment()` creates a JSON:API checkout priced with `custom_price` equal to the order total, so any cart checks out against a single carrier variant without per-product mapping\n    * `MerchantOfRecord` capability: Lemon Squeezy computes and remits tax at checkout, so KahunaCart's own tax estimate steps aside\n    * Currency guard \u2014 a Lemon Squeezy store charges in one currency and a checkout has no currency field, so a cart priced in anything else is refused with a message naming both currencies before any request goes out; the store currency is configurable or read from `GET \/v1\/stores\/{id}` once per request\n    * Both KahunaCart hashes travel in `checkout_data.custom` and come back as `meta.custom_data`, which is the only link between a checkout and the order it produces\n    * Webhook verification against the `X-Signature` HMAC-SHA256 hex digest of the raw body, with an unset secret failing closed, `hash_equals` comparison, and the event name read from the signed body rather than the `X-Event-Name` header\n    * Event mapping for `order_created` (gated on the order's own `status`, so `paid` completes, `failed` and `fraudulent` fail, and anything else is ignored) and `order_refunded` (full and partial, with the running `refunded_amount` folded into the reference so successive partial refunds stay distinct in the ledger)\n    * Full and partial refunds through `POST \/v1\/orders\/{id}\/refund`, with a full refund sent without an amount so Lemon Squeezy returns the tax it collected alongside the pre-tax total KahunaCart priced\n    * Refunds are synchronous here, so submission is confirmation: the endpoint answers with the order, refund attributes already applied, and there is no pending state to wait on. Documented alongside the limitation it comes with \u2014 Lemon Squeezy publishes no event for a refund failing or being reversed, so a refund undone at their end cannot reach the ledger\n    * Test-mode toggle alongside the mode-scoped API key, and registration only when the API key, store id, and variant id are all present\n    * Exact integer and string rendering of amounts at each currency's own precision for the one line of copy the customer reads at checkout \u2014 zero-decimal (JPY, KRW\u2026), two-decimal, and three-decimal (KWD, BHD\u2026) currencies all render correctly, with no float arithmetic anywhere\n    * Unit test suite covering signature verification and tamper rejection, JSON:API request construction, the currency guard, webhook event mapping, refund body building and reference agreement, the capability matrix, money rendering, and transport-failure degradation\n    * `captureMode()` is declared explicitly as `Automatic`, and there is deliberately no setting to change it. Everything Lemon Squeezy offers for a one-time sale is create a checkout, read an order back and refund it \u2014 no authorisation resource, no capture call, no void call \u2014 and the charge happens on Lemon Squeezy's own page, so by the time an order exists to read back the money has moved and the tax has been computed and remitted against it. `Authorize`, `Capture` and `Void` are undeclared with it, so KahunaCart never draws a Capture or Void button on a Lemon Squeezy order\n    * `encryptedSettings()` names the API key and the webhook signing secret, so KahunaCart stores both encrypted against a key that lives in the store's database rather than in any file. The key can move money out of the store and the secret would let anyone holding it sign a webhook this plugin cannot tell from a real one. A value written in plaintext before this reads correctly and is rewritten encrypted on the next save, with nothing for a merchant to do. The store id, the variant id and the store currency are left in the clear on purpose \u2014 none is a secret, and all three have to be read back and checked against the Lemon Squeezy dashboard\n    * The README says plainly what this driver does not claim. Lemon Squeezy keeps a customer's card for the subscriptions it bills itself, but exposes no vault to store a method against and no way to charge one off-session; its checkout takes wallets, but only after the redirect, so there is no SDK to mount an express button with"}}},"kahunacart-square":{"name":"KahunaCart Square","version":"1.1.9","description":"Square payments for KahunaCart \u2014 hosted Payment Links, Square Terminal card readers, webhooks, and refunds","description_html":"<p>Square payments for KahunaCart \u2014 hosted Payment Links, Square Terminal card readers, webhooks, and refunds<\/p>","description_plain":"Square payments for KahunaCart \u2014 hosted Payment Links, Square Terminal card readers, webhooks, and refunds","icon":"credit-card","author":{"name":"Trilby Media, LLC","email":"hello@trilby.media"},"dependencies":[{"name":"grav","version":">=2.0.0"},{"name":"kahunacart","version":">=1.5.18"}],"compatibility":{"grav":["2.0"]},"homepage":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com","docs":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/docs\/providers\/square","keywords":"grav, premium, commerce, ecommerce, payments, square, checkout","bugs":"https:\/\/github.com\/getgrav\/grav-premium-issues\/labels\/kahunacart-square","license":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/license","tag_name":"1.1.9","date":"2026-10-22T23:32:59Z","release_type":"stable","zipball_url":"https:\/\/getgrav.org\/download\/plugins\/kahunacart-square\/1.1.9","repository":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com","premium":{"vendor":"KahunaCart","validator":"kahunacart","license_product":"kahunacart-square","checkout_url":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/shop\/product\/kahunacart-square","button":"Buy Now","filename":"grav-plugin-kahunacart-square"},"slug":"kahunacart-square","install_path":"user\/plugins\/kahunacart-square","content_sha256":"4675a41b39ebf1f9ec36bfae6eede010984fa9c8df83a109a7eea4e8b5e5676c","changelog":{"1.1.9":{"date":"10\/22\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **Ships its license.** A `LICENSE` file now comes with the plugin, and the blueprint links to the KahunaCart License at kahunacart.com\/license instead of the Grav Premium license\n"},"1.1.8":{"date":"10\/19\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Check connection says whether Square's webhook subscription is right.** After the token checks out, the driver looks up the subscription at this store's address, changing nothing, and the Payments card warns when there is none, when Square has switched it off, when it is missing events the driver handles, when no signature key is stored, or when the Notification URL setting differs from the address the subscription calls. **Set up webhook** fixes each of these\n2. [](#improved)\n    * Needs KahunaCart 1.5.18\n"},"1.1.7":{"date":"10\/14\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Card readers with Square Terminal.** With KahunaCart 1.5.7 and the Point of Sale add-on, a counter sale can be paid on a Square Terminal. Pairing works the way Square does it: the till shows a code, you type it into the Terminal, and the Terminal appears as a reader. A payment goes to the Terminal under the store's own idempotency key and settles from Square's `terminal.checkout.updated` webhook or the till's status check, whichever comes first, exactly once, and refunds go to the Square payment as usual. A cancel from the customer or the till, and a timeout, are told apart. A timeout is only treated as final once the Terminal has moved on to a later checkout, because Square documents that a timed-out checkout can still complete; until then the till shows it as checking and staff can give up on it. A payment sent again after a lost answer finds the checkout the first attempt made, even days later. In the sandbox, Square's test devices are listed as readers ready to use. Press **Set up webhook** once on an existing store to add the Terminal event. Interac, tipping on the Terminal and delayed capture are not supported yet. Needs KahunaCart 1.5.7\n"},"1.1.6":{"date":"10\/13\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Square sets up its own webhook.** A **Set up webhook** button on the Square card registers this store's webhook subscription with Square, subscribed to the four events the plugin handles, and saves the signature key for you. A subscription already at this store's address, including one added by hand in the Developer Dashboard, is adopted rather than duplicated: switched back on if it was disabled, given any missing events while keeping the ones it had, and its key read back, so pressing the button twice is always safe. **Reset webhook** deletes this store's subscription and registers a fresh one with a new key. Subscriptions left at an old address by a tunnel or a staging copy are reported and never touched. A token Square will not let manage subscriptions gets a sentence pointing at the manual steps\n1. [](#improved)\n    * **Out of testing.** Checkout, webhooks, partial and full refunds, and a refund issued in the Square Dashboard were all run end to end against Square's sandbox, so this is the first stable release\n    * With Notification URL blank, signatures are checked against the same address Set up webhook registers, built from the site's base URL, instead of the address the request appears to arrive on, which a proxy or tunnel can change\n    * Check connection notes when no webhook signature key is stored yet\n    * The Instructions tab and README describe the button first and the manual subscription as the fallback, and the sandbox steps describe Square's testing panel (Next, then Test Payment) instead of a test card\n"},"1.1.5":{"date":"10\/04\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Square is offered only for the currency its location takes.** A Square location has one currency and Square charges in it, and which currency that is belongs to your account, so the plugin carries no list of its own. A new **Location Currency** setting on the API Credentials tab takes it, and from KahunaCart 1.4.0 checkout and the pay link leave Square out of an order in any other currency instead of sending the shopper to a Square page that fails. Leave it blank and Square is offered for every currency, as before. **Needs the base plugin at 1.4.0**, which is where the question is asked\n    * **Square has a Check connection button.** It reads the location with one `GET \/v2\/locations\/{id}`, shows its name, country and currency, tells you what to enter as Location Currency, and fails when the currency you entered disagrees with Square's or the location is not active. A token that is not allowed to read locations still passes, with a note, because it can take payments all the same\n1. [](#improved)\n    * A refund the driver submits is reported with the currency Square says it is in, so core can compare it with the order's\n    * The README and the Instructions tab explain Location Currency and the check\n    * An order hand-posted to Square in another currency than the location's is refused with a sentence before any request goes out\n"},"1.1.4":{"date":"09\/25\/2026","content":"1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **The download no longer carries PHPUnit.** Every release until now committed a development `vendor\/`: PHPUnit and about thirty packages only the test suite uses, some 1,700 files in a plugin that needs no Composer package at run time. `vendor\/` is now a `--no-dev` install holding just the autoloader, and `psr\/http-message` is declared under `replace` because Grav core already supplies it. A test fails if a development install is ever committed again\n"},"1.1.3":{"date":"09\/23\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * Composer's installed-package record carries the plugin's current package name, `kahunacart\/grav-plugin-kahunacart-square`, instead of the old `trilbymedia\/grav-plugin-kahunacart-square`\n"},"1.1.2":{"date":"09\/10\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **The same buyer is one Square customer now, instead of a new profile on every order.** A `quick_pay` payment link has nowhere to name a customer, so Square opened a fresh profile for every payment and a shopper who bought three times arrived in the seller's Customer Directory three times with their history split across all three. KahunaCart 1.2.3 keeps Square's own id per customer and hands it back on `PaymentContext::$providerCustomerRef`, so when the store holds one the link is built on the `order` branch of `CreatePaymentLink` carrying `customer_id`. That is the only branch Square allows it on, and Square says so in its own words: *\"At this time adding a known customer to a quick pay link isn't currently available. You'll need to add the customer in an order.\"* A store that has never been told an id stays on `quick_pay` exactly as before, one line item for the exact total, and `pre_populated_data.buyer_email` is sent either way because it prefills the form rather than identifying anybody\n    * The id is reported back wherever Square names it: `payment.customer_id` on the completed payment, which is the first and only place a `quick_pay` checkout ever names one; the order Square echoes alongside a link created on the `order` branch; and the payment a completed refund reads back for its hashes, which was being fetched anyway\n    * **A customer id Square no longer knows falls back instead of stranding the shopper.** A stored id stops being real when a merchant moves a store from the sandbox to production or connects a different Square account, and Square answers `NOT_FOUND`. The link is created once more without the id, under an idempotency key of its own because the retry is a different request, and the new profile's id replaces the old one from the payment webhook. One line goes into the Grav log. Once, deliberately: a second refusal is Square saying something else and is returned as the failure it is, and a `NOT_FOUND` that names anything other than the customer, a wrong location id say, is never retried at all\n    * The base plugin dependency requires KahunaCart 1.2.3, where `PaymentContext::$providerCustomerRef` and `WebhookResult`'s `providerCustomerRef` were added\n"},"1.1.1":{"date":"09\/08\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * An **Instructions** tab in the plugin settings, after General, that walks a merchant through setup in order: the account or sandbox, which credential to copy and from where, saving it, the webhook (button or manual steps with the endpoint URL and the events), test cards or sandbox accounts, and what to check afterwards\n\n1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **A refund event named the payment on `payment_id`, and the base plugin only reads `payment_ref`.** The value was always right \u2014 a completed refund spends a `GET \/v2\/payments\/{payment_id}` precisely to recover it \u2014 so this cost nothing on a refund the store submitted itself, and everything on the route that lookup exists for: a refund issued in the Square Dashboard arrives with no pending row to confirm, and the base plugin can only open one from the payment it belongs to, which it was never handed. `payment_ref` is now the name. `payment_id` stays beside it, because it is Square's own word for the same id \u2014 on the refund object and in the refund request \u2014 and a merchant reconciling by hand reads the ledger row rather than the provider contract\n"},"1.1.0":{"date":"09\/01\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * `encryptedSettings()` names the access token and the webhook signature key, so KahunaCart stores both encrypted against a key that lives in the store's database and not in any file. The access token is the whole Square application \u2014 payments, catalog, customers, refunds \u2014 and the signature key is what lets a forged webhook mark an order paid. The location ID and the notification URL are deliberately excluded: neither is a secret, and a merchant has to be able to read both back to check them against the Developer Dashboard, which is the first thing to do when a signature will not verify\n    * `captureMode()` is declared explicitly as `automatic`. It is what the base plugin would assume anyway, and saying it means the next person reading this driver does not have to work out whether the silence was a decision\n\n1. [](#improved)\n    * The README says why two-step capture is not here, with Square's own words rather than a hand-wave. Square has delayed capture \u2014 `CreatePayment` takes `autocomplete: false` and `CompletePayment` and `CancelPayment` finish it \u2014 but it lives on the Payments API and needs a card token minted in the buyer's browser by the Web Payments SDK. Payment Links cannot use it: *\"At this time delaying the capture of the payment isn't currently possible with the payment links today.\"* So `Authorize`, `Capture` and `Void` are not declared, because KahunaCart draws the Capture and Void buttons from those flags and a button here could never work\n    * Same for `Tokenization` and `ExpressPay`. Square can store a card on file and it has Apple Pay and Google Pay, and all three want the same on-site flow, so they arrive together or not at all\n    * The base plugin dependency requires KahunaCart 1.1.0 rather than 1.0.0, because `captureMode()` and `encryptedSettings()` do not exist in 1.0.x\n"},"0.1.0":{"date":"08\/19\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * Initial release: first-party Square provider for KahunaCart\n    * Hosted redirect flow on the Checkout API's Payment Links \u2014 `initiatePayment()` creates a payment link for the order total with `quick_pay` and redirects to Square's hosted checkout page\n    * Both hashes round-trip through `payment_note`, which Square copies onto the resulting `Payment`, so a `payment.updated` webhook always names the transaction and the order it belongs to; `payment.reference_id` is read as a second source\n    * The ledger reference starts as the payment link's Square order id and becomes `payment.id` when the payment completes, because that is the only id a refund can be issued against\n    * A completed `refund.updated` resolves its hashes with one `GET \/v2\/payments\/{payment_id}`, which is what makes a refund issued inside the Square Dashboard land on the right order\n    * Webhook verification on the raw request bytes: base64 HMAC-SHA256 of the notification URL concatenated with the body, compared with `hash_equals`, failing closed on an unset signature key and never producing an outbound call for a request that fails\n    * The notification URL is configurable because Square signs it as part of the payload, so a proxy that rewrites scheme, host, or port would otherwise break every signature\n    * Event mapping for `payment.created`, `payment.updated`, `refund.created`, and `refund.updated`, with `COMPLETED` completing, `FAILED` and `CANCELED` failing, and everything in flight ignored\n    * Async refund lifecycle: a refund Square answers `PENDING` \u2014 the common case, and one it documents as lasting up to 14 days on card and bank transfer payments \u2014 is reported as pending and settled later by `refund.updated`, keyed on the same refund id the refund call returned; `REJECTED` and `FAILED` release the held amount for a retry, and neither costs a payment lookup, since a failed refund has no ledger row to trace back to\n    * Full and partial refunds through `\/v2\/refunds`, with the idempotency key derived from the payment id, amount, and currency to fit Square's 45-character cap without letting a retry refund twice\n    * Tipping turned off explicitly on every link, because Square's default is the seller's country default \u2014 a 15% auto-tip in the US and Canada \u2014 which would ask the customer for more than the total the base plugin finalised\n    * The API version is pinned rather than left to whatever the access token's application defaults to\n    * Sandbox and production API bases with a single toggle; the provider registers only when the access token and location ID are both set\n    * Amounts stay integer minor units end to end \u2014 Square's `Money.amount` and KahunaCart's `*_minor` are the same unit, so JPY, KWD, and USD all cross untouched, and amounts arriving in any other form are refused rather than cast\n    * Unit test suite covering signature verification (including notification-URL mismatch and body tampering), payment-link request construction, event mapping and the payment\/order correlation, refund idempotency-key derivation, the behavior matrix, money handling, and transport failure"}}},"kahunacart-sumup":{"name":"KahunaCart SumUp","version":"1.1.7","description":"SumUp payments for KahunaCart \u2014 hosted checkout, SumUp Solo card readers, webhooks, and refunds","description_html":"<p>SumUp payments for KahunaCart \u2014 hosted checkout, SumUp Solo card readers, webhooks, and refunds<\/p>","description_plain":"SumUp payments for KahunaCart \u2014 hosted checkout, SumUp Solo card readers, webhooks, and refunds","icon":"credit-card","author":{"name":"Trilby Media, LLC","email":"hello@trilby.media"},"dependencies":[{"name":"grav","version":">=2.0.0"},{"name":"kahunacart","version":">=1.5.7"}],"compatibility":{"grav":["2.0"]},"homepage":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com","docs":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/docs\/providers\/sumup","keywords":"grav, premium, commerce, ecommerce, payments, sumup, checkout","bugs":"https:\/\/github.com\/getgrav\/grav-premium-issues\/labels\/kahunacart-sumup","license":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/license","tag_name":"1.1.7","date":"2026-10-22T23:33:04Z","release_type":"stable","zipball_url":"https:\/\/getgrav.org\/download\/plugins\/kahunacart-sumup\/1.1.7","repository":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com","premium":{"vendor":"KahunaCart","validator":"kahunacart","license_product":"kahunacart-sumup","checkout_url":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/shop\/product\/kahunacart-sumup","button":"Buy Now","filename":"grav-plugin-kahunacart-sumup"},"slug":"kahunacart-sumup","install_path":"user\/plugins\/kahunacart-sumup","content_sha256":"0aa2506723ea3e9658e9fb0abede9d24f726e8b47a9bdb9a16100e763c5a1550","changelog":{"1.1.7":{"date":"10\/22\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **Ships its license.** A `LICENSE` file now comes with the plugin, and the blueprint links to the KahunaCart License at kahunacart.com\/license instead of the Grav Premium license\n"},"1.1.6":{"date":"10\/14\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Card readers with SumUp Solo.** With KahunaCart 1.5.7 and the Point of Sale add-on, a counter sale can be paid on a SumUp Solo. Pair a Solo with the code it shows, and send the payment to it from the till. A payment settles from SumUp's callback or the till's status check, whichever comes first, exactly once, and refunds go to the SumUp transaction as usual. A decline, a cancel from the till or the Solo, and a payment the reader never took are told apart. SumUp has no idempotency key for reader payments, so a payment is sent to the Solo once; if its answer is lost, the till finds the payment in SumUp's history rather than sending it again. When SumUp can't confirm how a payment ended, the till shows it as checking and staff can check the SumUp app and give up on it. Live SumUp accounts need an Affiliate Key for Solo payments (Settings, then Instructions step 8). Tips, instalments and SumUp Go terminals aren't supported yet. Needs KahunaCart 1.5.7\n2. [](#bugfix)\n    * Removed a `curl_close()` call that PHP 8.5 reports as deprecated\n"},"1.1.5":{"date":"10\/13\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Out of testing.** Tested end to end against a SumUp sandbox merchant: Hosted Checkout payments with the success and 3D Secure test cards, the webhook and the return to the store, partial and full refunds, and forged and replayed webhooks\n    * **Check connection** on the Payments card reads the merchant the API key belongs to and shows its name, merchant code, country, currency and whether it is a sandbox, and says so when the Merchant Code field names a different merchant\n    * **The Merchant Code can be left empty.** The plugin uses the merchant the API key belongs to, so a key is all SumUp needs\n2. [](#bugfix)\n    * **Refunds work.** They were sent to SumUp's `\/v1.0\/merchants\/\u2026\/payments\/\u2026\/refunds`, which refuses a decimal amount with \"Invalid amount\". They now go to `\/v0.1\/me\/refund\/{id}`, the endpoint SumUp documents\n    * **The API key is encrypted from the first save.** It used to be saved in plain text until the merchant code was filled in as well, because KahunaCart only learned which settings to encrypt from a provider that was already set up\n"},"1.1.4":{"date":"10\/04\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **SumUp is no longer offered for an order in a currency it cannot take.** The plugin now tells KahunaCart which sixteen currencies SumUp's Checkouts API accepts, so on a store that sells in more than one currency checkout and the pay link leave SumUp out of an order in yen or dinars instead of letting the shopper pick it and fail. The list is the one on SumUp's Create a checkout page, checked on 10\/01\/2026, and it does not depend on the account. The refusal when a payment is started stays as a second line of defence. A store that sells in one currency sees no change. Requires KahunaCart 1.4.0\n2. [](#improved)\n    * A refund reports the currency of the SumUp transaction it was made against, so KahunaCart can tell a refund in another currency from the order's own\n"},"1.1.3":{"date":"09\/25\/2026","content":"1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **The download no longer carries PHPUnit.** Every release until now committed a development `vendor\/`: PHPUnit and about thirty packages only the test suite uses, some 1,700 files in a plugin that needs no Composer package at run time. `vendor\/` is now a `--no-dev` install holding just the autoloader, and `psr\/http-message` is declared under `replace` because Grav core already supplies it. A test fails if a development install is ever committed again\n"},"1.1.2":{"date":"09\/23\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * Composer's installed-package record carries the plugin's current package name, `kahunacart\/grav-plugin-kahunacart-sumup`, instead of the old `trilbymedia\/grav-plugin-kahunacart-sumup`\n"},"1.1.1":{"date":"09\/08\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * An **Instructions** tab in the plugin settings, after General, that walks a merchant through setup in order: the account, which credential to copy and from where, saving it, why there is no webhook to register, test cards or a sandbox merchant, and what to check afterwards\n"},"1.1.0":{"date":"09\/01\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * `encryptedSettings()` names the API key, so KahunaCart stores it encrypted against a key that lives in the store's database and not in any file \u2014 a config file copied to staging no longer carries a bearer token for the whole SumUp account. Nothing about how a merchant enters it changes, and a key already saved in plaintext is rewritten encrypted on the next save. The merchant code is deliberately excluded: it is an identifier rather than a credential, it travels in the URL of most of these calls, and it is the field a merchant checks against their dashboard when one is refused\n\n1. [](#improved)\n    * `captureMode()` is declared explicitly as automatic with the reason written out, rather than inherited silently. SumUp's Checkouts API cannot hold money without taking it: the create-checkout body carries no intent, a checkout's status enum is `PENDING`, `FAILED`, `PAID` and `EXPIRED` with no member for a hold, and there is no capture or void endpoint to move or release one with. `purpose`, the only field that changes what a payment means, chooses between a payment and collecting a card for later. A provider that cannot authorize declares automatic and says why \u2014 it is never special-cased in the base plugin \u2014 and `Authorize`, `Capture` and `Void` stay undeclared so KahunaCart draws no button with nothing behind it\n    * The README says plainly what this driver does not claim and why, for both two-step capture and saved cards. SumUp does tokenize cards, but its own guide documents that flow for the Payment Widget rather than for the Hosted Checkout this plugin redirects to, and a `Tokenization` flag with no way to store a method would give a customer a saved-methods page that never fills up\n"},"0.1.0":{"date":"08\/19\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * Initial release: first-party SumUp provider for KahunaCart\n    * Hosted checkout on the Checkouts API \u2014 `initiatePayment()` creates a checkout with `hosted_checkout.enabled` and redirects to the `hosted_checkout_url` SumUp answers with, so card details never touch the Grav server\n    * Correct handling of SumUp's two URL fields, which is the detail that silently breaks integrations: `return_url` is SumUp's *server-to-server* callback and carries the webhook URL, while `redirect_url` is the *browser* redirect and carries the return URL\n    * Verify-by-fetch webhooks, which is SumUp's documented model rather than a workaround: SumUp signs nothing and its entire webhook body is `{\"event_type\": \"CHECKOUT_STATUS_CHANGED\", \"id\": \"\u2026\"}`, so the body is mined for one id, the id's format is checked before it reaches a URL, and every fact acted on comes back from an authenticated `GET \/v0.1\/checkouts\/{id}`. Nothing in the request body is ever trusted \u2014 SumUp's own documentation is explicit that an application \"must always verify if the event really took place, by calling a relevant SumUp's API\"\n    * Deliberate webhook failure paths \u2014 a body with no usable id is a 400, an event type SumUp adds later is answered 200 before any API call is spent on it, a well-formed id this merchant has never issued is answered 200 so the status code cannot be used to probe which ids exist, and a rejected key or unreachable API is a 400 that leaves SumUp to redeliver\n    * A checkout belonging to a different merchant code is ignored rather than acted on, so a key covering more than one merchant cannot land another merchant's payment on this store's ledger\n    * Status mapping across the whole of SumUp's checkout enum: `PAID` completes the order, `FAILED` and `EXPIRED` fail it, and `PENDING` is ignored\n    * The ledger reference becomes the *transaction* id once a checkout is paid, because the transaction id is the only identifier SumUp's refund endpoint accepts \u2014 a completed payment that reported the checkout id would be unrefundable\n    * Both hashes ride in `checkout_reference`, which is the only merchant-controlled field on a SumUp checkout that survives to the webhook; a reference this plugin did not write reads as \"no hashes\" rather than as two wrong ones\n    * That same reference is the idempotency key SumUp does not otherwise offer: SumUp has no `Idempotency-Key` header anywhere in its API but refuses a duplicate `checkout_reference` with `409 DUPLICATED_CHECKOUT`, so a double-submitted checkout recovers the existing checkout by listing the reference back instead of billing a second one against one order\n    * Full and partial refunds through `POST \/v1.0\/merchants\/{code}\/payments\/{txn}\/refunds`, with the refund read back out of the transaction's `REFUND` events \u2014 SumUp answers an accepted refund `201` with a body of `{}`, so there is no refund id in the response to key a ledger row on, and the event list is the only place one exists\n    * Refund events are matched by amount rather than by being newest, so a concurrent partial refund against the same transaction cannot be mistaken for this one\n    * Honest refund semantics: a refund SumUp reports as `REFUNDED` or `SUCCESSFUL` is confirmed, a `FAILED` one is failed, and one still `PENDING` \u2014 or one that could not be read back at all \u2014 is reported pending with the ledger row held rather than recorded as money that has moved\n    * SumUp emits no refund webhook and this plugin does not pretend otherwise. `CHECKOUT_STATUS_CHANGED` is its only event and a refund does not change a checkout's status, so `refund()` reading the transaction back at submission time is the entire settlement mechanism; the README says plainly that a refund left pending is the merchant's to check\n    * Exact conversion between minor units and SumUp's decimal major-unit amounts with no float ever constructed on the way out \u2014 the amount is built as an exact decimal string with integer arithmetic and unquoted into the JSON so it reaches SumUp as a number, and the noise `json_decode` introduces on the way back is rounded off at the currency's own precision\n    * Currency validation against SumUp's own sixteen-member enum, refused locally with a message naming the currency rather than sent and rejected as an unreadable API error, and CLP handled as the zero-decimal currency ISO 4217 says it is\n    * All three of SumUp's error shapes are read \u2014 the Checkouts API's `error_code`\/`message`\/`param`, the differently spelled `error_message` on a refused checkout, and RFC 9457 problem details from the gateway and the newer endpoints \u2014 with a refund's nested `errors[]` preferred over the outer `detail`, so \"Amount exceeds the refundable amount\" reaches the merchant rather than \"Refund failed.\"\n    * No mode toggle: a SumUp sandbox is a separate merchant account with its own code and key on the same host, so the credentials carry the mode and a switch could only contradict them\n    * No SumUp SDK is vendored \u2014 four JSON endpoints are cheaper to write than to depend on\n    * Unit test suite covering webhook verify-by-fetch and every reject path, checkout status mapping, checkout and refund body building, the refund read-back including its async and unreadable cases, decimal amount handling in both directions, duplicate-checkout recovery, transport failure, and packaging consistency"}}},"kahunacart-moneris":{"name":"KahunaCart Moneris","version":"1.0.1","description":"Moneris payments for KahunaCart. Moneris Checkout on the store&#39;s own payment page, orders confirmed with Moneris, and refunds.","description_html":"<p>Moneris payments for KahunaCart. Moneris Checkout on the store&#39;s own payment page, orders confirmed with Moneris, and refunds.<\/p>","description_plain":"Moneris payments for KahunaCart. Moneris Checkout on the store&#39;s own payment page, orders confirmed with Moneris, and refunds.","icon":"credit-card","author":{"name":"Trilby Media, LLC","email":"hello@trilby.media"},"dependencies":[{"name":"grav","version":">=2.0.0"},{"name":"kahunacart","version":">=1.4.0"}],"compatibility":{"grav":["2.0"]},"homepage":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com","docs":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/docs\/providers\/moneris","keywords":"grav, premium, commerce, ecommerce, payments, moneris, canada, checkout","bugs":"https:\/\/github.com\/getgrav\/grav-premium-issues\/labels\/kahunacart-moneris","license":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/license","tag_name":"1.0.1","date":"2026-10-22T23:32:42Z","release_type":"stable","zipball_url":"https:\/\/getgrav.org\/download\/plugins\/kahunacart-moneris\/1.0.1","repository":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com","premium":{"vendor":"KahunaCart","validator":"kahunacart","license_product":"kahunacart-moneris","checkout_url":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/shop\/product\/kahunacart-moneris","button":"Buy Now","filename":"grav-plugin-kahunacart-moneris"},"slug":"kahunacart-moneris","install_path":"user\/plugins\/kahunacart-moneris","content_sha256":"4b5105ae401c2229d04ee4ab354bfe82d2381721bb7818716f82c08c71cfb35b","changelog":{"1.0.1":{"date":"10\/22\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **Ships its license.** A `LICENSE` file now comes with the plugin, and the blueprint links to the KahunaCart License at kahunacart.com\/license instead of the Grav Premium license\n"},"1.0.0":{"date":"10\/07\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * First release. Moneris payments for KahunaCart through Moneris Checkout, on a payment page the plugin serves on your own store. The shopper is sent there from checkout, pays in Moneris's card panel, and lands on KahunaCart's normal receipt page\n    * Payments are confirmed with a server-to-server receipt request to Moneris, never from the browser. The page's \"done\", a revisit of the page and a worker check of every open payment all ask Moneris, so a shopper who pays and closes the tab still gets their order confirmed\n    * A payment is never failed just because its Moneris ticket expired. The worker keeps asking Moneris every 5 minutes for an hour after expiry and then hourly, for up to 24 hours, with the schedule kept on disk so a restart picks it up. If Moneris never says paid or declined, the payment stays pending. A checkout where the shopper never pressed Pay is then closed quietly; one where they did gets a Notices queue item, a line in the order's history and a warning in the Grav log, each with the Moneris order number to look up\n    * A shopper who comes back to an expired payment page is checked with Moneris first, then told the page timed out with a link back to checkout to try again\n    * The order's items go to Moneris with each payment, with SKU, quantity and unit price, plus shipping and tax, so they are kept with the transaction. They are only sent when they add up to the amount charged, with an \"Other charges\" line for a fee and no items at all for a discounted order or a deposit\n    * The payment page loads Moneris's current `chkt_v3.00.js` script, and each of Moneris's double-fired panel events is acted on once\n    * Full and partial refunds from the order in the KahunaCart admin, through the Moneris Gateway\n    * Canadian dollars by default, or US dollars for a store ID Moneris set up for them. Moneris is only offered for orders in that currency\n    * Check connection on the Payments page, which opens a real 1.00 payment with your credentials and checkout profile\n    * An Instructions tab that walks through the Merchant Resource Center, the checkout profile, test cards and going live"}}}}
